window.SEARCH=[{"t":"Met 1. Atmosphere","s":"What air is made of, how it is layered from the surface to space, why the tropopause matters, and the ISA numbers every altimeter is built on.","h":"chapters/met-01.html","k":"Composition · layers · ISA "},{"t":"New to the atmosphere? Start here","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#start","k":"The atmosphere is a thin skin of gases held by gravity, heated from the ground up, and stacked in four layers that are told apart by what the temperature does with height. Start here "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What is air made of?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#composition","k":"Nitrogen 78.09%, oxygen 20.95%, argon 0.93%, CO₂ 0.035%. N₂ : O₂ is 4 : 1 by volume and 3 : 1 by weight.  \"78-21-1\": nitrogen, oxygen, everything else. Weight drops the 4 to a 3 because oxygen is heavier."},{"t":"Which gas does what?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#gases","k":"Ozone absorbs UV (warms the stratosphere); greenhouse gases trap long-wave heat; particles become condensation nuclei.  "},{"t":"Who heats the atmosphere?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#heating","k":"The sun heats the earth; the earth heats the air from below. Most of that heat (77%) moves as latent heat, only 23% as sensible heat.  Air is like water in a pan: the flame (ground) is underneath."},{"t":"How much air is below each height?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#mass","k":"Half the atmosphere is below 6 km (about 18,000–20,000 ft), three quarters below 10 km, 99% below 35 km.  6 – 10 – 35: half, three-quarters, nearly all."},{"t":"What are the layers, from the ground up?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#layers","k":"Troposphere (falls) → tropopause → stratosphere (rises, ozone) → mesosphere (falls, coldest top at 80 km) → thermosphere (rises).  \"The Strong Man Throws Eggs\": Tropo, Strato, Meso, Thermo, Exo."},{"t":"How does temperature change with height?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#profile","k":"Falls to the tropopause, stays at −56.5°C, rises through the stratosphere to about 0°C at 50 km, falls to about −100°C at 80 km, then rises.  Zig-zag: down, flat, up, down, up."},{"t":"Which layer is it in?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#which-layer","k":"Troposphere: all the weather and nearly all the water vapour. Stratosphere: the ozone layer and nacreous clouds. Mesosphere: meteors, noctilucent clouds and the coldest air. Thermosphere: ionised air that reflects radio.  Weather low, ozone next, meteors burn, radio bounces."},{"t":"Why is the tropopause higher over the equator?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#tropopause","k":"Hot rising air pushes it up: 16–18 km and −70 to −75°C over the equator, 8–10 km and −40 to −45°C over the poles. It breaks at about 40° and 60°, and jets sit at the breaks.  High = cold: the higher the tropopause, the colder its top."},{"t":"What changes the height of the tropopause?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#tropo-controls","k":"Surface temperature, latitude, season, land–sea distribution and the synoptic situation. Warmer surface → higher tropopause; winter → lower.  "},{"t":"What are the ISA numbers?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"Dry air, 15°C, 1013.25 hPa, 1225 g/m³ at MSL; −6.5°C/km to 11 km; −56.5°C from 11 to 20 km; +1°C/km from 20 to 32 km (−44.5°C at 32 km).  15 · 1013 · 6.5 · 11 · −56.5 — the ISA \"phone number\"."},{"t":"MSL temperature: 15°C","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"288.15 K"},{"t":"MSL pressure: 1013.25 hPa","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"= 760 mm = 29.92 in"},{"t":"MSL density: 1225 g/m³","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"1.225 kg/m³"},{"t":"Lapse rate to 11 km: 6.5°C/km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"1.98°C per 1000 ft (≈2)"},{"t":"ISA tropopause: 11 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"36,090 ft"},{"t":"11–20 km: −56.5°C","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"isothermal; 20 km = 65,617 ft"},{"t":"20–32 km: +1°C/km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"0.3°C/1000 ft; −44.5°C at 32 km (104,987 ft)"},{"t":"Gravity: 980.665 cm/s²","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":""},{"t":"Air in ISA: Dry","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"no water vapour"},{"t":"JSA lapse rate: 2°C/1000 ft","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa","k":"no tropopause"},{"t":"Slide up through the ISA","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-sim","k":"ISA: 15 °C and 1013.25 hPa at MSL, falling 1.98 °C per 1000 ft to −56.5 °C at 36,090 ft, then constant. A hotter-than-ISA day raises density altitude.  "},{"t":"How do I work out ISA temperature and deviation?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-calc","k":"ISA temp = 15 − 2 × (height in thousands of feet), or 15 − 6.5 × km, down to −56.5°C. Deviation = actual − ISA.  Deviation is always \"what I see minus what ISA says\"."},{"t":"ISA temperature (feet)","s":"Met 1 · formula","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-calc","k":"Valid to 36,090 ft; above that −56.5°C."},{"t":"ISA temperature (km)","s":"Met 1 · formula","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-calc","k":""},{"t":"ISA deviation","s":"Met 1 · formula","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-calc","k":""},{"t":"Pressure deviation","s":"Met 1 · formula","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-calc","k":""},{"t":"Which standard is which?","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#isa-jsa","k":"ISA has a tropopause at 11 km (then −56.5 °C); JSA keeps 2 °C per 1000 ft all the way up with no tropopause. Nacreous clouds sit in the stratosphere, noctilucent ones higher in the mesosphere.  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Atmosphere thickness: ≈ 480 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"80% within 16 km"},{"t":"Homosphere top: 80 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tropopause, equator: 16–18 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"−70 to −75°C"},{"t":"Tropopause, poles: 8–10 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"−40 to −45°C"},{"t":"Tropical tropopause, India: 16–16.5 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"≈100 hPa"},{"t":"Middle tropopause, India: ≈11.5 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"≈200 hPa, −45°C"},{"t":"Polar tropopause: ≈300 hPa","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tropopause breaks: 40° and 60°","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"40° more prominent"},{"t":"Tropopause lapse rate: 1–2°C/km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Stratosphere top: 50 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ozone maximum: 20–25 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"appreciable 10–50 km"},{"t":"Mesopause: 80 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"−100°C (173 K), coldest"},{"t":"Ionosphere: above 60 km","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Supplementary O₂: above 10,000 ft","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Max WV: 4% by volume","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":"tropics"},{"t":"Latent : sensible: 77 : 23","s":"Met 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks for this chapter","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check: think like the examiner","s":"Met 1 · Atmosphere","h":"chapters/met-01.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Composition and characteristics (p.1–3)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Variable gases and greenhouse gases (p.2)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Water vapour, dry and saturated air (p.2)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"CO₂, ozone and particles (p.3)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vertical distribution of air mass (p.3)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"How the atmosphere is heated (p.4)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Layers (thermal structure) (p.4–7)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tropopause details (p.5–6)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"International Standard Atmosphere (ICAO ISA) (p.7)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Jet Standard Atmosphere (JSA) (p.7)","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 1 · notes","h":"chapters/met-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 2. Atmospheric Pressure","s":"Pressure as the weight of the air above, how fast it falls with height, the pressure settings on your altimeter, and the high-to-low traps that make it lie.","h":"chapters/met-02.html","k":"Altimetry · QNH · QFE · QNE "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Why is pressure higher aloft over warm air?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#columns","k":"Pressure falls faster with height in cold (dense) air. So every pressure level sits lower over a cold column, and at any height the warm column has the higher pressure.  Cold air is \"squashed\": its pressure levels sit closer together."},{"t":"How many feet is one hectopascal?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#per-hpa","k":"About 27 ft at sea level (exam often uses 30 ft), 50 ft at 20,000 ft and 100 ft at 40,000 ft. More in warm air, less in cold.  \"27 at the beach, 100 at the jets.\""},{"t":"Feet per hPa","s":"Met 2 · formula","h":"chapters/met-02.html#per-hpa","k":"T in kelvin, p in hPa."},{"t":"hPa to inches","s":"Met 2 · formula","h":"chapters/met-02.html#per-hpa","k":""},{"t":"Elevation from QNH and QFE","s":"Met 2 · formula","h":"chapters/met-02.html#per-hpa","k":""},{"t":"QFE from QNH","s":"Met 2 · formula","h":"chapters/met-02.html#per-hpa","k":""},{"t":"Alticor (rough)","s":"Met 2 · formula","h":"chapters/met-02.html#per-hpa","k":"Add when MSL pressure > 1013 or air warmer than ISA; subtract when lower/colder."},{"t":"Why does pressure rise and fall twice a day?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#diurnal","k":"Semi-diurnal variation: maxima about 1000 and 2200 h, minima about 0400 and 1600 h local time. Biggest in the tropics (3–5 hPa), tiny at the poles.  10 and 10 are the highs (10 am, 10 pm); 4 and 4 are the lows."},{"t":"What does each pressure setting make the altimeter show?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#settings","k":"QFE: zero on the runway (height above the aerodrome). QNH: aerodrome elevation (altitude above MSL). QNE: what it reads on the ground with 1013.25 set (pressure altitude).  QFE = Field Elevation is zero. QNH = Nautical Height (sea). QNE = Nineteen-ninety-two (29.92)."},{"t":"Play with it: what does each setting read?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#altim-sim","k":"The altimeter only measures pressure. The subscale tells it which pressure to call zero: QFE makes the runway zero, QNH makes sea level zero, 1013 makes the 1013.25 hPa surface zero (pressure altitude / flight level).  Three floors: runway (QFE), sea (QNH), 1013 surface (QNE)."},{"t":"QFE, QNH, QFF, QNE: which is which?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#settings-table","k":"QFF uses the real temperature (for synoptic charts); QNH assumes ISA (for flying). Hotter than ISA → QFF < QNH; at MSL QFF = QNH.  "},{"t":"Where do I change from QNH to 1013?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#transition","k":"At or below the transition altitude you fly altitudes on QNH. At and above the transition level you fly flight levels on 1013.2. The gap between them is the transition layer.  Climbing: set 1013 passing the TA. Descending: set QNH passing the TL."},{"t":"Will the altimeter over-read or under-read?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#over-under","k":"High to low, or warm to cold: the altimeter over-reads (you are lower than it shows). Low to high, or cold to warm: it under-reads.  \"High to Low, look out below.\" Cold is the same as low."},{"t":"See \"high to low, look out below\" happen","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#hilo-sim","k":"Fly from high to low pressure, or into colder air, without resetting the subscale and the aircraft sinks while the altimeter stays the same: it over-reads, and terrain clearance is less than you think.  High to low or hot to cold: look out below."},{"t":"Which pressure level is at which height?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"850→5,000 · 700→10,000 · 500→18,000 · 400→24,000 · 300→30,000 · 200→38,000 · 100→53,000 ft.  5-10-18-24-30-38-53."},{"t":"850 hPa: 5,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL50"},{"t":"700 hPa: 10,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL100"},{"t":"500 hPa: 18,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL180 · half the atmosphere"},{"t":"400 hPa: 24,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL240"},{"t":"300 hPa: 30,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL300 · ≈9,160 m"},{"t":"200 hPa: 38,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL380"},{"t":"100 hPa: 53,000 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#levels","k":"FL530"},{"t":"What do lows, highs, troughs, ridges and cols look like?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#patterns","k":"Low: anticlockwise, converging, rising air, bad weather but good visibility. High: clockwise, diverging, sinking air, fair weather but poor visibility. Trough is V-shaped; ridge is rounded; col sits between two highs and two lows.  Lows are Lousy weather but Look clear; Highs are Happy but Hazy."},{"t":"Low or high?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#pattern-sort","k":"Lows and troughs: converging air, anticlockwise winds (NH), bad weather but good visibility. Highs and ridges: subsiding air, clockwise winds, fair weather but haze. A col sits between two highs and two lows with light, variable winds.  Low: lousy weather, long views. High: haze, but fine."},{"t":"What do the lines on upper-air charts mean?","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#charts","k":"Contours are heights of a pressure surface (40 gpm on 700/500 hPa, 80 gpm on 300/200 hPa). Thickness lines are isotherms of layer mean temperature. Isallobars join equal pressure tendency.  "},{"t":"Pairs that catch students out","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#pairs","k":"QNH is reduced to MSL with ISA, QFF with the actual temperature. Isobars join equal pressure, isallobars equal pressure change. Transition altitude is the top of QNH flying; transition level the bottom of flight levels.  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Standard MSL: 1013.25 hPa","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":"760 mm · 29.92 in"},{"t":"hPa → in: × 0.02953","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Half pressure: 6 km","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Feet per hPa at MSL: 27 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":"exam often uses 30"},{"t":"at 20,000 ft: 50 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"at 40,000 ft: 100 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Semi-diurnal max: 1000 & 2200 h","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Semi-diurnal min: 0400 & 1600 h","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Amplitude, equator: 3–5 hPa","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Contour interval 700/500: 40 gpm","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Contour interval 300/200: 80 gpm","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Isobar interval: 2 hPa","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tendency period (India): 24 h","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":"3 h in higher latitudes"},{"t":"Regional QNH: hourly","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":"valid 1 h"},{"t":"Temp. error: 1% per 3°C","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flight level spacing: 500 ft","s":"Met 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check: think like the examiner","s":"Met 2 · Atmospheric Pressure","h":"chapters/met-02.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Static and dynamic pressure (p.12)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure as the weight of air above (p.12)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vertical variation of pressure (p.13)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Warm and cold columns (p.14)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Semi-diurnal variation (p.14–15)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Measurement (p.15)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter correction (Alticor / D value) (p.15–16)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Contours and thickness (p.16)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure gradient, tendency, isallobars (p.16–17)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Altimetry definitions (p.17)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure settings (p.18)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Worked examples (p.18–19), using 1 hPa = 30 ft (or 27 ft)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Standard isobaric levels (Table 2.1)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Over-reading and under-reading (p.19–20)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure patterns (p.21–22) (isobars at 2 hPa)","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 2 · notes","h":"chapters/met-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 3. Temperature","s":"Scales and conversions, how heat moves, the radiation laws, the earth's heat budget and albedo, and why the day's coldest moment comes after sunrise.","h":"chapters/met-03.html","k":"Heat · radiation · diurnal range "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How do I convert °C, °F and K?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#convert","k":"F = 9C/5 + 32, C = 5/9 (F − 32), K = C + 273. Water freezes at 0°C = 32°F = 273 K and boils at 100°C = 212°F = 373 K. −40 is the same in both scales.  Double it, take a tenth off, add 32 (°C → °F, close enough)."},{"t":"°C → °F","s":"Met 3 · formula","h":"chapters/met-03.html#convert","k":""},{"t":"°F → °C","s":"Met 3 · formula","h":"chapters/met-03.html#convert","k":""},{"t":"°F → K","s":"Met 3 · formula","h":"chapters/met-03.html#convert","k":""},{"t":"Absolute zero","s":"Met 3 · formula","h":"chapters/met-03.html#convert","k":"Book: −273.16°C. Molecular motion almost stops."},{"t":"Heat or temperature? Specific or latent?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#heat-temp","k":"Heat is the total energy of the molecules; temperature is their average. Water has a high specific heat (1) and soil a low one (0.2), so land heats and cools faster than the sea.  Heat is the sum, temperature the average."},{"t":"How does heat move in the atmosphere?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#transfer","k":"Conduction works only right at the ground; convection lifts heat up; radiation needs no medium and plays the biggest role; advection carries it sideways.  "},{"t":"Why does the angle of the sun matter?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#insolation","k":"Insolation depends on the obliquity of the rays: overhead sun puts the same energy on a smaller area. Most at noon and in the tropics, least at sunrise/sunset and at the poles.  Torch straight down = bright spot; torch at a slant = dim smear."},{"t":"What do the radiation laws say?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#laws","k":"Stefan–Boltzmann: energy ∝ T⁴ (hot sun radiates intensely). Wien: peak wavelength ∝ 1/T (hot sun → short waves; cool earth → long waves).  Stefan = Strength (T⁴). Wien = Wavelength (1/T)."},{"t":"Where does the sunlight go?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#budget","k":"30% is reflected back to space (the albedo), 19% is absorbed by the atmosphere, 51% is absorbed by the earth's surface.  30 bounces, 19 stays up, 51 lands."},{"t":"When is the day coldest and hottest?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#diurnal","k":"The ground keeps cooling until incoming equals outgoing radiation just after sunrise: the screen minimum comes ½–1 h after dawn. The ground is hottest at noon; the air maximum comes 2–3 h later.  Coldest just after dawn, hottest after lunch."},{"t":"Why are cloudy nights warmer?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#cloudy-night","k":"Cloud absorbs the earth's outgoing long-wave radiation and its base radiates heat back down. The lower and thicker the cloud, the smaller the night-time cooling.  Cloud is a blanket at night and a parasol by day."},{"t":"Big or small day–night range?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#range","k":"Biggest range: dry land, desert, clear sky, calm wind. Smallest: sea, overcast low cloud, strong wind, wind off the sea.  "},{"t":"How is temperature measured?","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#instruments","k":"Thermometers sit in a Stevenson screen 1.25 m above the ground, in shade. Mercury everywhere except the minimum thermometer (alcohol); thermographs use bi-metallic strips.  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Freezing: 0°C · 32°F · 273 K","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Boiling: 100°C · 212°F · 373 K","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Equal point: −40°C = −40°F","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Specific heat: water 1 · ice 0.5 · soil 0.2","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Vaporising boiling water: > 5× heat","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":"vs heating ice-cold water to boil"},{"t":"Sun surface: ≈ 6000°C","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Solar mix: IR 46 · Vis 45 · UV 9 %","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Albedo: 30%","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Absorbed by atmosphere: 19%","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Absorbed by surface: 51%","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Reaching surface, clear day: 5/6","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Snow reflects: ≈ 80%","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Diurnal range, sea: < 1°C","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Diurnal range, land: ≈ 20°C","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Minimum temperature: ½–1 h after dawn","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Maximum temperature: 2–3 h after noon","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Screen height: 1.25 m (4 ft)","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Friction layer: ≈ 1 km","s":"Met 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check: think like the examiner","s":"Met 3 · Temperature","h":"chapters/met-03.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Scales (p.27)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Instruments (p.28)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Definitions (p.28)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Heat transfer (p.29–30)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Insolation and radiation laws (p.30)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Solar, terrestrial and nocturnal radiation (p.31)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Heat budget and albedo (p.31–32)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Diurnal variation of surface temperature (p.32–33)","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 3 · notes","h":"chapters/met-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 4. Air Density","s":"What density is, why hot, high and humid air hurts performance, density altitude (120 ft per °C), and how density changes with height and latitude.","h":"chapters/met-04.html","k":"Density altitude · ρ = P/RT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What does low density do to an aircraft?","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#performance","k":"Hot, high and humid air is thin: less lift and thrust, higher take-off and landing speeds, slower climb, longer runways.  \"Hot, High, Humid = Horrible performance.\""},{"t":"How do I find density altitude?","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#da","k":"Density altitude = pressure altitude + 120 ft for every °C above ISA (subtract when colder). In ISA they are equal.  1 degree = 120 feet."},{"t":"Density altitude","s":"Met 4 · formula","h":"chapters/met-04.html#da","k":""},{"t":"Gas law","s":"Met 4 · formula","h":"chapters/met-04.html#da","k":"At constant pressure ρ ∝ 1/T. ISA sea level → 1225 g/m³."},{"t":"Moist air","s":"Met 4 · formula","h":"chapters/met-04.html#da","k":"Water vapour is lighter than dry air, so moist air is less dense."},{"t":"1% density rule","s":"Met 4 · formula","h":"chapters/met-04.html#da","k":"Any one of these changes density by about 1%."},{"t":"How fast does density fall with height?","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#height","k":"About 3% per 1000 ft near the ground. Half at 6 km, a quarter at 11 km, an eighth at 17 km.  6-11-17: halve, halve, halve."},{"t":"Where is air densest: equator or pole?","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#latitude","k":"Below about 8 km the air is densest over the cold poles; above 8 km the order reverses and the equator has the densest air. Density altitude equals pressure altitude only in ISA.  Eight kilometres: the density flip."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"ISA MSL density: 1225 g/m³","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DA per °C: 120 ft","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Per 1000 ft: −3%","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"1% change: 10 hPa · 3°C · 300 ft","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Half density: 6 km","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quarter: 11 km","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Eighth: 17 km","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Uniform-density atmosphere: 8 km deep","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Atmosphere extends: ~800 km","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":"drag still felt at 240 km"},{"t":"Humid take-off: +10%","s":"Met 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 4 · Air Density","h":"chapters/met-04.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Why it matters (p.37)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Definition and density altitude (p.37)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Gas law (p.38)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Factors affecting density (p.38)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Surface variations (p.39)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Variation with height (p.39)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Latitudinal variation (p.39)","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 4 · notes","h":"chapters/met-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 5. Humidity","s":"How much water vapour air holds, the words used to describe it, wet bulb vs dew point vs frost point, and the cloud-base rule of 400 ft per degree.","h":"chapters/met-05.html","k":"RH · dew point · mixing ratio "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which humidity word means what?","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#terms","k":"Absolute humidity is per volume (g/m³); mixing ratio is per mass of dry air (g/kg) and stays constant when air rises; RH compares what is there with what could be there.  "},{"t":"Dry bulb, wet bulb, dew point: how are they ordered?","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#three-temps","k":"In unsaturated air T > Tw > Td. When saturated (fog, rain) all three are equal and the spread is zero.  Dry ≥ Wet ≥ Dew: alphabetical order backwards (D, W, D)."},{"t":"Wet bulb vs dew point vs frost point","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#wb-dp","k":"The wet bulb is cooled by evaporating water into the air; the dew point is how far you must cool the air (no water added) to saturate it. Frost point is the same for ice. Heating changes RH but not the dew point.  Wet bulb adds water; dew point takes away heat."},{"t":"Why does ice grow at the expense of water drops?","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#svp","k":"Below 0°C the saturation vapour pressure over water is higher than over ice, so in a mixed cloud the drops evaporate and the ice crystals grow. This is how rain forms in clouds above the freezing level.  "},{"t":"Work it out","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#calc","k":"Convective cloud base is about (temperature − dew point) × 400 ft. RH = actual vapour content ÷ saturation content × 100.  Spread times four hundred."},{"t":"Cloud base","s":"Met 5 · formula","h":"chapters/met-05.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"Relative humidity","s":"Met 5 · formula","h":"chapters/met-05.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Cloud base factor: 400 ft/°C","s":"Met 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Supercooled limit: −40°C","s":"Met 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-05.html#numbers","k":"−45°C in CB"},{"t":"Max WV (tropics): 4% by volume","s":"Met 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Humid take-off: +10% distance","s":"Met 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 5 · Humidity","h":"chapters/met-05.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Water in the air (p.41)","s":"Met 5 · notes","h":"chapters/met-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Terms (p.41–42)","s":"Met 5 · notes","h":"chapters/met-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Measurement (p.42)","s":"Met 5 · notes","h":"chapters/met-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wet bulb, dew point, frost point (p.42)","s":"Met 5 · notes","h":"chapters/met-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Key facts (p.43)","s":"Met 5 · notes","h":"chapters/met-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 5 · notes","h":"chapters/met-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 6. Winds","s":"What drives the wind, the balance of forces behind geostrophic and gradient winds, friction, gusts and squalls, wind shear, local winds and the thermal wind.","h":"chapters/met-06.html","k":"Coriolis · geostrophic · local winds · shear "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How is wind measured and reported?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#basics","k":"Direction is where it comes FROM (degrees true), speed in knots, measured at 10 m and averaged over 10 minutes (2 minutes for take-off and landing).  A westerly hits you in the face when you look west."},{"t":"Where is the low pressure?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#buys-ballot","k":"NH: back to the wind, low is on your LEFT. SH: on your right. Facing the wind, swap sides.  NH: \"Back to wind, Low on Left\"."},{"t":"What is the Coriolis force?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#coriolis","k":"An apparent force from the earth's rotation that turns moving air right in the NH and left in the SH. 2ΩρV sin φ: largest at the poles, zero at the equator.  Coriolis \"Comes alive at the poles, Collapses at the equator\"."},{"t":"What is the geostrophic wind?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#geostrophic","k":"The frictionless wind that blows along straight isobars when the pressure gradient force exactly balances Coriolis, with low pressure on the left in the NH. It fails near the equator; good beyond about 30°.  Geo-strophic = earth-turned."},{"t":"Why is the wind weaker round a low than round a high?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#gradient","k":"On curved isobars a third force (centripetal) is needed. Round a low the wind is sub-geostrophic; round a high it is super-geostrophic.  Lows are Less, Highs are Higher."},{"t":"Which balanced wind is which?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#winds-table","k":"Geostrophic: pressure force = Coriolis on straight isobars. Gradient adds curvature. Cyclostrophic: pressure force = centripetal, in a cyclone core. Inertial: no pressure force, always anticyclonic. Thermal: the vector difference between levels, cold air on the left (NH).  Straight, curved, spinning, coasting, thermal."},{"t":"What does friction do to the wind?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#friction","k":"Within the friction layer (about 1 km) the wind slows and turns towards the low: about 15° and ⅔ speed over the sea, about 30° and ⅓–½ speed over land.  Sea 15, Land 30."},{"t":"How does the surface wind change from day to night?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#diurnal-wind","k":"By day thermals mix the strong upper wind down, so the surface wind veers and strengthens (NH). At night the mixing stops: the surface wind backs and drops. A gust lasts seconds, a squall at least a minute, a gale is a steady 34 kt or more.  Day veers and blows; night backs and slows."},{"t":"What makes a microburst so dangerous?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#shear","k":"A concentrated thunderstorm downdraught about 4 km across lasting 1–5 minutes. An aircraft first meets a headwind (gains IAS), then the downdraught, then a tailwind (loses IAS and sinks).  Headwind lies, downdraught pushes, tailwind kills."},{"t":"What causes wind shear?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#shear-causes","k":"Thunderstorms (gust fronts and microbursts), low-level night inversions, gusty winds, strong heating, terrain and buildings, fronts and jet streams.  "},{"t":"How do sea and land breezes work?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#breezes","k":"By day the land heats, so a cool, moist sea breeze blows onshore (temperature falls, humidity rises). At night the land cools, and a weaker, shallower land breeze blows offshore.  Sea by day, land by night: the wind blows towards the warmer side."},{"t":"Anabatic, katabatic or Föhn?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#slopes","k":"Anabatic: warm, up the slope by day. Katabatic: cold, down the slope at night (frost and fog in hollows). Föhn: warm and dry down the lee after the air rained out on the windward side.  ANA goes up the stairs; KATA comes down; FÖHN is the hairdryer."},{"t":"Which local wind is it?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#local-stamp","k":"Anabatic winds blow up sunny slopes; katabatic winds drain down cold slopes at night (Bora, Mistral). Föhn is the warm dry wind on a mountain's lee (Chinook). Sea breeze by day, land breeze by night.  Ana goes up, kata comes down."},{"t":"Named winds of the world","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#named","k":"Bora and Mistral are cold katabatic winds; Chinook and Föhn are warm dry lee winds; Haboob, Harmattan and Khamsin carry dust and sand. Trades blow at 10°–30°, the doldrums sit at 5°N–5°S, the Roaring Forties at 35°–60°.  Cold falls (Bora, Mistral), warm dries (Föhn, Chinook)."},{"t":"What is the thermal wind?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#thermal","k":"The vector you add to the lower geostrophic wind to get the upper one: thermal wind = upper − lower. It blows along the isotherms with cold air on the left (NH).  Thermal wind keeps the Cold on its Left (NH)."},{"t":"Thermal wind sums","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#thermal-calc","k":"To subtract a wind, reverse its direction (add 180°) and add. Same-line winds just add or subtract speeds.  "},{"t":"Upper wind","s":"Met 6 · formula","h":"chapters/met-06.html#thermal-calc","k":""},{"t":"Thermal wind","s":"Met 6 · formula","h":"chapters/met-06.html#thermal-calc","k":""},{"t":"Vector calculator","s":"Met 6 · formula","h":"chapters/met-06.html#thermal-calc","k":""},{"t":"How do winds change with height?","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#upper-winds","k":"Temperature falls towards the poles, so the thermal wind is westerly and westerlies grow with height. Over India in the monsoon, easterlies dominate above 500 hPa with the Tropical Easterly Jet near 13°N at ~15 km.  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Calm: 0–2 kt","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Speed conversion: 1 m/s ≈ 2 kt","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Anemometer height: 10 m","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Averaging: 10 min / 2 min","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":"reports / take-off & landing"},{"t":"Squall: +16 kt to ≥ 22 kt","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":"lasting ≥ 1 min"},{"t":"Gale: ≥ 34 kt","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":"persistent mean, Beaufort 8"},{"t":"Hurricane (Beaufort 12): ≥ 64 kt","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Friction layer: ≈ 1 km","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Over sea: 15° · ⅔ Vg","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Over land: 30° · ⅓–½ Vg","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Microburst: ≈ 4 km · 1–5 min","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":"up to 90 kt difference"},{"t":"Gust front: ≈ 30 km · 6,000 ft","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sea breeze reach: 15–25 km inland","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":"up to 3,000–5,000 ft deep"},{"t":"Föhn warming: ≥ 10°C","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Geostrophic valid beyond: ≈ 30° latitude","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TEJ: 13°N · 15 km","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Trades: 10°–30°","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Doldrums: 5°N–5°S","s":"Met 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"The Beaufort scale","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#beaufort","k":"Beaufort force 8 is a gale (34–40 kt) and force 12 a hurricane (64 kt or more). Speeds are measured at 10 m.  Eight is a gale at 34; twelve a hurricane at 64."},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check: think like the examiner","s":"Met 6 · Winds","h":"chapters/met-06.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Basics (p.45)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Crosswind and instruments (p.46)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Gust, lull, squall, gale (p.46–47)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Backing and veering, Buys Ballot (p.47)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Coriolis (geostrophic) force (p.48)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Geostrophic wind (p.49–50)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cyclostrophic, gradient, isallobaric, inertial winds (p.50–51)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Friction (p.52)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turbulence (p.52–53)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Diurnal variation of surface wind (p.53–54)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wind shear (p.54–55)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Local winds (p.55–58)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Sea and land breeze (p.57–58)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Thermal wind (p.58–59)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Contours and upper winds (p.59–60)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vorticity (p.61)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Named winds (p.61–62)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Beaufort scale (wind at 10 m)","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 6 · notes","h":"chapters/met-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 7. Visibility and Fog","s":"How visibility and RVR are defined and measured, what cuts visibility (haze, mist, fog, dust, ash), slant visibility, and how each kind of fog forms and clears.","h":"chapters/met-07.html","k":"RVR · haze · mist · fog types "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Haze, mist or fog: where are the limits?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#limits","k":"Fog: below 1000 m (RH ~100%). Mist: 1000–5000 m (RH ~100%). Haze: 5000 m or less, made of dry particles.  Fog under 1, Mist 1 to 5, Haze is dry."},{"t":"What exactly are visibility and RVR?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#definitions","k":"Visibility is the greater of the black-object distance (against a bright background) and the 1000-candela-light distance (against an unlit background). RVR is what a pilot on the centre line can see of the runway.  "},{"t":"Why can I see the runway overhead but lose it on final?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#slant","k":"Looking straight down you see through a thin fog layer; on approach you look through a long slant path, so slant visibility is much worse than vertical visibility.  Down is thin, slant is long."},{"t":"How does radiation fog form and clear?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#radiation","k":"A clear, nearly calm night over moist land: the ground radiates, cools the air to its dew point; fog is thickest around sunrise and lifts into stratus 2–3 hours after.  Clear + Calm-ish + Cold ground + Close to saturation = fog."},{"t":"Radiation, advection or steaming?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#fog-types","k":"Radiation: the ground cools at night. Advection: warm moist air moves over a cold surface (land or sea, any time). Steaming: cold air over warm water.  Advection = Arrives warm. Steaming = Sea steams under cold air."},{"t":"Every fog type side by side","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#fog-table","k":"Radiation fog forms over land on clear calm nights; advection fog when warm moist air crosses a cold surface, by day or night, over land or sea. Steaming fog is cold air over warm water; frontal fog forms ahead of a warm front in continuous rain.  Radiation needs a night; advection needs a journey."},{"t":"Will radiation fog form tonight?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#fog-flow","k":"Radiation fog needs moist air, a clear sky and a light wind of about 3–7 kt under a high or col. Dead calm gives dew or shallow patches; a strong wind lifts the cooling into low stratus.  Moist, clear, light breeze."},{"t":"What else ruins visibility?","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#obscure","k":"Besides fog, visibility is spoilt by dust haze (north India in summer), smoke and smog under inversions, volcanic ash (engine-damaging; Barren Island is India's active volcano) and sea spray.  Dust, smoke, ash and spray."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Fog: < 1000 m","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mist: 1000–5000 m","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Haze / dust storm: ≤ 5000 m","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RVR reported below: 1500 m","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Visibility lights: ≈ 1000 cd","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fog wind: 3–7 kt","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fog depth: ≤ 1500 ft","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":"max ~1 km"},{"t":"Clears: 2–3 h after sunrise","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Dust haze depth: 6–8 km","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Moderate rain: 1–3 km","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Drizzle: ≤ 3 km","s":"Met 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 7 · Visibility and Fog","h":"chapters/met-07.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Definition and reporting (p.67)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"RVR (p.67–68)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Obscurations (p.68–69)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vertical and slant visibility (p.70)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Radiation fog (p.70–71)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other fogs (p.72)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fog dispersal (p.72)","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 7 · notes","h":"chapters/met-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 8. Vertical Motion and Clouds","s":"What makes air rise and sink, convergence and divergence, the ten cloud types and their weather, how cloud is reported, and when contrails form.","h":"chapters/met-08.html","k":"Convergence · 10 genera · oktas · contrails "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Convergence or divergence?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#vertical","k":"Air piling in at the surface (convergence) has to rise: lows and troughs bring cloud and rain but good visibility. Air spreading out (divergence) sinks: highs and ridges bring fair weather but haze.  Converge → climb → cloud. Diverge → descend → dry."},{"t":"What makes air go up or down?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#causes","k":"Air is lifted by local eddies and thermals, by terrain (up the windward slope, down the lee, sometimes as mountain waves), by fronts (gradual at warm, sharp at cold fronts) and by shear near jet streams.  Heat, hills, fronts and shear."},{"t":"Which cloud lives where?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#atlas","k":"High (ice): CI, CS, CC. Medium: AC, AS. Low: ST, SC, NS. Vertical: CU and CB. In the tropics high clouds sit at 6–18 km, medium at 2–8 km, low below 2 km.  Cirro- = high, Alto- = middle, plain Stratus/Cumulus = low, Nimbo-/-nimbus = rain."},{"t":"Meet all ten clouds","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#gallery","k":"Learn each cloud by three things: height band, what falls from it (nothing, drizzle, continuous rain, showers/hail) and the optical clue (halo = CS, corona = AS).  "},{"t":"Where do they appear? Heights from pole to tropics","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#bands","k":"The low band is up to 6,500 ft everywhere. The medium and high bands rise towards the equator: in the tropics medium is 6,500–25,000 ft and high 20,000–60,000 ft.  Warmer, deeper troposphere → higher cloud bands."},{"t":"Read the sky as a warm front (or WD) approaches","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#warmfront","k":"CI → CS (halo) → AS (ground-glass sun, corona) → NS (continuous rain) → ST/fractus: the cloud lowers and thickens as the front comes closer.  "},{"t":"Which cloud is it?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#identify","k":"Work down from the obvious: anvil and hail = CB; flat base and cauliflower top = CU; high icy cloud = CI, CS (halo) or CC; ground-glass sun = AS; continuous rain from a dark layer = NS; drizzle from a grey layer = ST.  Tower, heap, ice, middle, low."},{"t":"What each cloud does to an aircraft","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#hazards","k":"High ice clouds are harmless (except CAT near jets). AS and NS bring long rain and icing below 0 °C; SC and AC bring bumps; CU and above all CB bring the worst turbulence, icing and visibility.  The taller the cloud, the bigger the hazard."},{"t":"Species and special features","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#species","k":"Lenticularis = mountain waves; castellanus = instability aloft; capillatus = CB with anvil; mammatus = severe turbulence; virga = rain that evaporates (dry microburst risk).  "},{"t":"Sort the clues into the right band","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#sort","k":"High clouds give halos and fine fibres; medium clouds give a ground-glass sun, coronas and lenticular lenses; low clouds give drizzle, bumps and continuous rain; vertical clouds give anvils, hail and cauliflower tops.  "},{"t":"What weather does each cloud give?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#genera","k":"Each genus has a signature: CS → halo, AS → corona and steady rain when thick, ST → drizzle, SC → bumps, NS → continuous rain or snow, CU → bumps and showers, CB → thunderstorms, hail and squalls.  Halo high, corona middle, drizzle low."},{"t":"Name that cloud","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#which-cloud","k":"Read the clue word: halo → CS, drizzle → ST, steady rain → NS, hail and anvil → CB, ground-glass sun → AS, lens over a ridge → AC lenticularis, feathery strands → CI.  "},{"t":"Pairs that catch students out","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#special","k":"Halo (22°, refraction in ice, CS) is not a corona (≤ 5°, diffraction in droplets, AS). Nacreous clouds are stratospheric; noctilucent clouds, in the mesosphere, are the highest of all. Castellanus turrets warn of instability aloft.  "},{"t":"FEW, SCT, BKN or OVC?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting","k":"Sky in eighths (oktas): 1–2 FEW, 3–4 SCT, 5–7 BKN, 8 OVC. The ceiling is the lowest layer below 20,000 ft covering 5/8 or more (BKN or OVC).  Few, Some, Broken, Over: 2-4-7-8."},{"t":"Reporting numbers","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting-rules","k":"  "},{"t":"Ceiling: ≥ 5/8","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting-rules","k":"lowest layer below 6000 m / 20,000 ft"},{"t":"Cloud of operational significance: < 1500 m / 5000 ft","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting-rules","k":"or below highest MSA, whichever greater"},{"t":"Base in METAR/TAF: AGL","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting-rules","k":""},{"t":"Base on area charts: AMSL","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting-rules","k":""},{"t":"Turbulence around active CB: 10–20 km","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#reporting-rules","k":""},{"t":"When does an aircraft leave a contrail?","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#contrails","k":"No contrails below the mintra level (about the −45°C level for jets) or above the maxtra level (in the stratosphere). Above the drytra level (≈2 km over mintra) they form even in dry air.  MINtra = MINimum level; MAXtra = MAXimum."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"High cloud (tropics): 6–18 km","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":"20,000–60,000 ft"},{"t":"Medium (tropics): 2–8 km","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":"6,500–25,000 ft"},{"t":"Low: < 2 km","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":"< 6,500 ft"},{"t":"Genera: 10","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Noctilucent: 80–85 km","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ice pellets: < 5 mm","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Exhaust trails from: ≈ 9 km","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mintra estimate: ≈ −45°C","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Drytra: ≈ 2 km above mintra","s":"Met 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 8 · Vertical Motion and Clouds","h":"chapters/met-08.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Vertical motion (p.75–76)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Convergence and divergence (p.76–78)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Clouds: formation and classification (p.77)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The ten genera (p.79–80)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reporting (p.80–82)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flying conditions (p.81–82)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Species (p.83)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Condensation trails (p.83–84)","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 8 · notes","h":"chapters/met-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 9. Stability and Instability","s":"Adiabatic cooling, the three lapse rates, inversions, and how comparing the environment with a lifted parcel tells you whether the air is stable, unstable or conditionally unstable.","h":"chapters/met-09.html","k":"DALR · SALR · ELR · inversion · tephigram "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How do DALR, SALR and ELR compare?","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#rates","k":"A dry parcel cools at 9.8°C/km, a saturated one at about 5°C/km (latent heat offsets the cooling). The environment's actual rate (ELR) decides what happens to the parcel.  Dry drops ~10, Saturated ~5, ISA 6.5 in between."},{"t":"Test any atmosphere","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#parcel","k":"Compare the ELR with DALR and SALR. Below SALR: absolutely stable. Above DALR: absolutely unstable. In between: conditionally unstable (unstable only once saturated).  ELR is the referee: lower than both → stable; higher than both → unstable."},{"t":"Stability tester","s":"Met 9 · formula","h":"chapters/met-09.html#parcel","k":""},{"t":"Dry parcel temperature","s":"Met 9 · formula","h":"chapters/met-09.html#parcel","k":""},{"t":"Per 1000 ft","s":"Met 9 · formula","h":"chapters/met-09.html#parcel","k":"Per 1000 ft."},{"t":"The stability rules in one table","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#criteria","k":"Compare the environmental lapse rate (ELR) with the DALR and SALR: less than both = absolutely stable; between them = conditionally unstable (unstable only once saturated); more than both = absolutely unstable. Isothermal layers and inversions are very stable.  Below both stable, between conditional, above both unstable."},{"t":"Name the stability","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#stamp","k":"Put the ELR between the two adiabats: ELR 6.8 with DALR 9.8 and SALR 5 is conditionally unstable; a super-adiabatic desert surface is absolutely unstable; any inversion or isothermal layer is absolutely stable.  "},{"t":"What does an inversion do?","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#inversion","k":"Temperature rising with height (negative lapse rate) makes the layer very stable: no mixing, smooth air, but smoke, haze and fog are trapped beneath.  An inversion is a lid."},{"t":"How is the tephigram read?","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#tephi","k":"Plot temperature and dew point from a radiosonde. Lift the parcel: DALR from the dry bulb, SALR from the wet bulb, saturation mixing-ratio line from the dew point all meet at the Normand point — the LCL, i.e. cloud base.  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"DALR: 9.8°C/km","s":"Met 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-09.html#numbers","k":"≈ 3°C/1000 ft"},{"t":"SALR (low levels): ≈ 5°C/km","s":"Met 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-09.html#numbers","k":"≈ 1.5°C/1000 ft"},{"t":"ISA ELR: 6.5°C/km","s":"Met 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"SALR → DALR below: −40°C","s":"Met 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 9 · Stability and Instability","h":"chapters/met-09.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Processes (p.87)","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The three lapse rates (p.88)","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Inversion (p.88)","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Stability ideas (p.88–89)","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Stability criteria (p.89)","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tephigram (p.90–91)","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 9 · notes","h":"chapters/met-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 10. Optical Phenomena","s":"Rainbows, halos, coronas, mirages, glories and aurora: what causes each, which cloud it comes from, and what it tells a pilot about icing.","h":"chapters/met-10.html","k":"Halo · corona · rainbow · mirage · aurora "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Every optical effect, drawn","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#gallery","k":"Ice + refraction = halo (22°, CS). Small water drops + diffraction = corona (≤ 5°, AS) and glory. Raindrops + refraction and internal reflection = rainbow (42°, sun behind you).  "},{"t":"Which phenomenon am I seeing?","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#which","k":"Sun behind you: rainbow (on rain) or glory (round your shadow). A ring round the sun: 22° in an icy veil = halo; a small ring = corona. Displaced images = mirage. Colours on cloud edges = irisation.  Rainbows and glories face away from the sun."},{"t":"Halo or corona?","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#halo-corona","k":"Halo: big 22° ring from refraction in ice crystals of cirrostratus — little icing risk. Corona: small coloured rings from diffraction in water droplets of altostratus — icing possible above the freezing level.  Halo is High and has a big Hoop; Corona is Close and cold-wet."},{"t":"Every optical effect side by side","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#compare","k":"Rainbows come from refraction and reflection in raindrops (42°, red outside); halos from refraction in ice crystals (22°); coronas, glories and Bishop's rings from diffraction by small droplets or dust; mirages from refraction in layers of different density.  Refraction makes big rings, diffraction small ones."},{"t":"How does a rainbow form?","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#rainbow","k":"Each raindrop refracts sunlight in, reflects it once off the back and refracts it out: the primary bow at 42° with red on the outside. A fainter secondary bow at about 52° has its colours reversed.  "},{"t":"Inferior or superior mirage?","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#mirage","k":"Inferior mirage over hot surfaces (steep lapse rate) puts an image below the object. Superior mirage over cold surfaces (inversion) lifts it above.  Hot = image dips (inferior); Cold = image climbs (superior)."},{"t":"Aurora and lightning facts","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#aurora","k":"Aurora borealis is the northern lights, australis the southern, both near the magnetic poles. St Elmo's fire (a blue-green glow on the airframe in CU/CB) warns of a strong electric field: lightning may follow.  Boreal is north."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Primary rainbow: 42°","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Secondary rainbow: ≈ 52°","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Small halo: 22°","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Large halo: 46°","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":"book key option 42°"},{"t":"Corona radius: ≤ 5°","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Bishop's ring: ≈ 22°","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aurora: 70–1000 km","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":"peak ≈ 100 km"},{"t":"Fair-weather field: 100 V/m","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ball lightning: 10–20 cm","s":"Met 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 10 · Optical Phenomena","h":"chapters/met-10.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Rainbow (p.95–96)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Halo (p.96)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Corona (p.96–97)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mirage (p.97)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other phenomena (p.97–98)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aurora (p.98–99)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Atmospheric electricity and lightning (p.99–100)","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 10 · notes","h":"chapters/met-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 11. Precipitation","s":"Drizzle, rain, showers, hail and snow; the three theories of how cloud drops grow big enough to fall; which cloud gives what; and India's rainfall timing.","h":"chapters/met-11.html","k":"Bergeron · coalescence · rainfall terms "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How big is each kind of precipitation?","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#sizes","k":"Drizzle 0.2–0.5 mm, rain 0.5–5 mm, showers > 5 mm, hail 5–50 mm or more. Sleet is rain mixed with snow.  Drizzle is dust-sized, rain is a pea, hail is a marble."},{"t":"How do cloud drops grow big enough to fall?","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#theories","k":"Cold clouds (tops above freezing): ice crystals grow at the expense of supercooled drops because SVP over ice is lower (Bergeron). Warm clouds: drops collide and merge (coalescence). Over the sea, big salt nuclei start drops early (giant nucleus).  Bergeron = Berg (ice). Coalescence = Collide."},{"t":"Which cloud gives which precipitation?","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#cloud-type","k":"Drizzle comes from ST; continuous rain or snow from NS (and thick AS); showers, hail and snow showers from TCU and CB. Fair-weather CU, AC, CI and CC give nothing that reaches the ground.  Drizzle from stratus, showers from heaps."},{"t":"When does it rain most?","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#when","k":"Inland tropical stations get most rain in summer afternoons (convection); coasts and NE India valleys at night and early morning (breezes and slope winds); temperate places and the western Himalaya in winter (depressions and Western Disturbances).  "},{"t":"Numbers and terms","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":"  "},{"t":"Rainy day: ≥ 2.5 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":"in 24 h"},{"t":"Light rain: < 7.5 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Moderate: 7.5–35.5 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Rather heavy: 35.6–64 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Heavy: 65–84.9 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Very heavy: ≥ 85 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Exceptionally heavy: ≥ 250 mm","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Snow/sleet if surface below: ≈ 4°C","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":"and cloud-to-ground mean < 0°C"},{"t":"Seeding result: 15–20 min","s":"Met 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-11.html#terms","k":""},{"t":"Pairs that catch students out","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#pairs","k":"A cloudburst is the rain (very heavy, small area, short time); a flash flood is what the river does. Hills wring rain out on the windward side and leave a rain shadow behind, as east of the Western Ghats.  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 11 · Precipitation","h":"chapters/met-11.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Types and sizes (p.102)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"How precipitation forms (p.102–103)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cloud → precipitation (Table 11.1)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Snow, sleet and terms (p.104)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Diurnal and seasonal variation (p.104)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cloud seeding (p.104–105)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Classification of rainfall (24 h)","s":"Met 11 · notes","h":"chapters/met-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 12. Ice Accretion","s":"How ice builds up on an aircraft, the three kinds of airframe ice, the temperatures and clouds that bring the worst of it, what it does to performance, and engine/carburettor icing.","h":"chapters/met-12.html","k":"Rime · glaze · hoar frost · carb icing "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Rime, glaze or hoar frost?","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#types","k":"Hoar frost: vapour deposits as feathery crystals in clear air on a cold airframe. Rime: small supercooled droplets freeze instantly (white, porous). Glaze: large drops spread before freezing (clear, heavy, sticky) — the most dangerous.  Small drops freeze where they hit (Rime); big drops Run back (glaze)."},{"t":"The three ices compared","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#type-table","k":"Hoar frost forms in clear air by deposition; rime from small supercooled droplets (white, brittle); clear ice from large supercooled drops or freezing rain (glassy, heavy, the most dangerous).  Small drops rime, big drops clear."},{"t":"At which temperatures is icing worst?","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#temps","k":"Outside CB, severe icing is likeliest from 0 to −7°C, moderate to −12°C, light to −20°C and very light below that, because supercooled water becomes scarce as it gets colder.  0-7-12-20-40: Severe, Moderate, Light, Very light."},{"t":"Which clouds ice up most?","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#clouds","k":"Ice-crystal clouds (CI, CS, CC) hardly ice. Layer clouds give light to moderate. Big convective clouds (TCU, CB) can give severe icing down to the −20°C level.  "},{"t":"What does icing do to the aircraft?","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#effects","k":"More weight and drag, less lift, a higher stall speed; plus vibrating propellers, jammed control hinges, a false ASI (pitot ice) and poor radio (aerial ice).  "},{"t":"Freezing level over India","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#fl-india","k":"The freezing level over India is lowest in winter in the north (about 700 hPa) and highest in the SW monsoon (500–550 hPa), when warm moist air fills the troposphere.  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 12 · Ice Accretion","h":"chapters/met-12.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Types of icing (p.107–108)","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Temperature ranges (except CB) (Table 12.1)","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Icing by cloud (p.108)","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Effects of airframe icing (p.109)","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Engine icing (p.109)","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Freezing level over India (pressure levels, Table 12.2)","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 12 · notes","h":"chapters/met-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 13. Thunderstorms","s":"How a thunderstorm forms, lives and dies, its types, every hazard it throws at an aircraft, microbursts, dust storms, Nor'westers and tornadoes.","h":"chapters/met-13.html","k":"CB life cycle · hazards · microburst · Nor'westers "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What are the three stages of a thunderstorm?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#lifecycle","k":"Cumulus: updraughts only. Mature: updraughts and downdraughts side by side, with rain, hail, lightning and a gust front (20–40 min, the most violent). Dissipating: downdraughts only.  Up → Up & Down → Down."},{"t":"Each stage in detail","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#stages","k":"Cumulus stage: updraughts only, up to 30 m/s. Mature stage: rain starts, downdraughts and the gust front arrive, the anvil spreads; the worst hazards, for 20–40 min. Dissipating stage: downdraughts everywhere and the cloud dies, leaving the anvil.  Up, up-and-down, down."},{"t":"Will a thunderstorm form?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#conditions","k":"A thunderstorm needs three things: a deep unstable layer (lapse rate steeper than the SALR), plenty of moisture, and a trigger. Take away the moisture aloft and you get a dust storm or weak CB instead.  Instability, moisture, trigger."},{"t":"Which kind of thunderstorm is it?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#types","k":"Air-mass storms are random and weaker; frontal storms and squall lines are more violent; MCCs and supercells (strong shear, rotating updraught) bring the giant hail, hurricane-force gusts and most tornadoes.  Shear turns an ordinary storm into a supercell."},{"t":"What can a thunderstorm do to an aircraft?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#hazards","k":"Turbulence and icing are the worst; then hail (even under the anvil in clear air), wind shear and microbursts, squalls, lightning (+10 to −10°C), heavy rain, darkness, and an over-reading altimeter.  Keep 10–20 km away: TS bites outside the cloud too."},{"t":"How close to a CB is too close?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#rings","k":"The danger does not stop at the cloud edge: turbulence reaches 10–20 km around a CB in clear air and 25–30 km downwind, with hail possible under the anvil. Underneath, squalls, microbursts and huge draughts. How far to stay away Twenty around, thirty downwind."},{"t":"Every hazard with its numbers","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#hazard-list","k":"Avoid by 10–20 km (more downwind): turbulence is around, above and below the cell, icing from 0 to −30 °C, hail up to 45 000 ft and under the anvil, lightning mostly between +10 and −10 °C, draughts of 3000–5000 ft.  "},{"t":"Microburst: why only seconds to react?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#microburst","k":"A concentrated downdraught under 4 km across (macroburst 4 km or more), lasting 1–5 minutes. On approach: headwind (go high) → downdraught → tailwind (sink). Recognition time is only 5–15 s, so avoid flying under CB, in heavy rain or under virga.  Micro < 4 km, Macro ≥ 4 km; 1–5 minutes; 5–15 seconds to react."},{"t":"Nor'westers, Andhi and tornadoes","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#india","k":"Nor'westers (Kalbaishakhi) hit east and NE India on pre-monsoon afternoons with squalls and hail; Andhi are dry dust storms of NW India. A tornado hangs from a CB; a waterspout is its sea cousin; a dust devil is a small hot-spot whirl.  Nor'westers wet in the east; Andhi dusty in the north-west."},{"t":"How are dust storms classed?","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#ds-class","k":"Dust storms are graded by wind and visibility: light up to 21 kt (500–1000 m), moderate 22–40 kt (200–500 m), severe above 40 kt (below 200 m).  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"CB cell diameter: 1–10 km","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Updraught (cumulus): up to 30 m/s","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":"≈ 60 kt"},{"t":"Mature stage: 20–40 min","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"MCC life: 6–24 h","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Moderate TS wind: 15–40 kt","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Severe TS wind: > 40 kt","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Squall: up to 100 kt","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Microburst: < 4 km · 1–5 min","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Microburst reaction time: 5–15 s","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TS producing microbursts: ≈ 5%","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Icing range: 0 to −30°C","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Lightning band: +10 to −10°C","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hail damage up to: 45,000 ft","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Radar for precipitation: 3–20 cm","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":"10 cm common; X 3 cm, S 10 cm"},{"t":"Tornado winds: > 150 kt","s":"Met 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-13.html#numbers","k":"≤ 200 m wide"},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 13 · Thunderstorms","h":"chapters/met-13.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"What it is (p.112)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Types (p.112–113)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Classification by structure (p.113)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Conditions and triggers (p.114)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Life cycle (p.114–116)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Intensity (p.117)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Diurnal and seasonal variation (p.117)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hazards (p.117–120)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Radar detection (p.120)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Air pockets, downbursts, microbursts (p.121–123)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Dust storms (p.123–124)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nor'westers (p.124)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tornado, waterspout, dust devil (p.124–125)","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 13 · notes","h":"chapters/met-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 14. Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","s":"The six air masses and which reach India, how cold, warm and occluded fronts look and what weather each brings, and the Western Disturbances that drive India's winter weather.","h":"chapters/met-14.html","k":"Pc · Tm · Em · cold/warm/occluded fronts · Western Disturbances "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which air mass is which?","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#masses","k":"First letter = temperature from latitude (Polar / Tropical / Equatorial); second = moisture from surface (continental = dry, maritime = moist).  c = continent = crusty dry; m = maritime = moist."},{"t":"Which air masses reach India?","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#india-masses","k":"In winter India is under continental tropical (Tc) air from the Siberian high: cold, dry, hazy. In the monsoon, equatorial maritime (Em) air covers the south, with the monsoon trough over the Gangetic plain between the moist and dry air.  Winter continental and dry; monsoon maritime and wet."},{"t":"What does a cold front look like?","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#cold","k":"Cold air wedges under warm air on a steep slope (≈1:50 ana to 1:120 kata), moving 20–30+ kt. A narrow band of CB, showers and squalls; line squalls may run 100–300 km ahead; skies clear quickly behind.  Cold front = Coming fast, Cumulonimbus, Clears quickly."},{"t":"What does a warm front look like?","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#warm","k":"Warm air glides up over cold air on a shallow slope (1:150–1:180) at 10–15 kt. Cloud thickens over 1000 km: CI → CS → AS → NS → ST, with long steady rain, drizzle, low stratus, frontal fog and freezing rain ahead.  Warm front cloud alphabet: Cirrus, Cirrostratus, Altostratus, Nimbostratus, Stratus — \"Cats Can Always Nap Soundly\"."},{"t":"Cold vs warm front: before, during, after","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#compare","k":"A cold front is steep and fast: a narrow band of CB, showers and squalls, a sharp veer and a quick clearance. A warm front is shallow and slow: a wide belt of layer cloud, steady rain, poor visibility and freezing rain ahead.  Cold is quick and violent; warm is wide and wet."},{"t":"Cold or warm occlusion?","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#occlusion","k":"The cold front moves about twice as fast and catches the warm front. Whichever cold air mass is colder ends up underneath: colder air behind → cold occlusion (summer); colder air ahead → warm occlusion (winter).  Name the occlusion after the air that does the undercutting."},{"t":"Where do Western Disturbances come from and go?","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd","k":"Extra-tropical lows from the Mediterranean, Caspian and Black Seas that reach India occluded, moving W→E north of 20°N at 250–300 km/day, 5–7 a month in winter. Rain/snow along the Himalayan foothills; fog and cold waves after they pass.  WD = Winter Disturbance from the West."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Cold front slope: 1:50 (ana) – 1:120 (kata)","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cold front speed: 20 kt (kata) / > 30 kt (ana)","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Warm front slope: 1:150 – 1:180","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Warm front speed: 10–15 kt","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"CI ahead of warm front: ≈ 1000 km","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"AS/NS ahead: 400–500 km","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Line squall ahead of cold front: 100–300 km","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ana cold front belt: 100–150 km","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"WD frequency: 5–7 / month","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":"winter"},{"t":"WD speed: 250–300 km/day","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"WD latitude: north of 20°N","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Monsoon Em limit: south of 25°N","s":"Met 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-14.html#wd-numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 14 · Air Masses, Fronts and WDs","h":"chapters/met-14.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Air masses (p.129–130)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Air masses over India (p.130–131)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fronts (p.131–132)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cold front (p.132–133)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Warm front (p.133–134)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Occlusions and others (p.134–135)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fronts in India and Western Disturbances (p.136–137)","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 14 · notes","h":"chapters/met-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 15. Jet Streams","s":"What a jet stream is, the polar-front, subtropical and tropical easterly jets, their positions over India month by month, the cloud that marks them and where the turbulence hides.","h":"chapters/met-15.html","k":"STJ · PFJ · TEJ · LLJ · CAT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What does a jet stream look like in cross-section?","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#section","k":"A flattened tube of fast wind (core ≥ 60 kt) just below the tropopause break. Shear is strongest vertically (≈5 m/s per km). Cirrus streaks mark the warm side; the worst CAT sits just below the core on the cold, low-pressure side.  Cold side = CAT side."},{"t":"Which jet is which?","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#types","k":"Over India two jets matter: the westerly subtropical jet (about 12 km, near 27°N, October to May, up to 200 kt) and the easterly tropical jet (15–16 km, near 13°N, June to August). The polar-front jet stays further north.  Winter westerly STJ, monsoon easterly TEJ."},{"t":"Which jet sits over India, and when?","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#india","k":"Winter half (Oct–May): the westerly STJ at ~12 km, ~27°N, reaching 22°N in February, fastest in January. Monsoon (Jun–Aug): the easterly TEJ at 15–16 km near 13°N, plus a low-level jet over the Peninsula.  Winter West (STJ), Monsoon east (TEJ)."},{"t":"The STJ through the year","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#stj-year","k":"The STJ arrives over India in October, is fastest in January (100–120 kt), reaches furthest south (22°N) in February, weakens and leaves by the end of May, and the TEJ takes over for the monsoon.  Arrives October, peaks January, south in February, gone by June."},{"t":"STJ details the exam likes","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#stj-facts","k":"The STJ core is 3–4 km below the tropopause; the worst CAT is above the core and on its north (cold) side, where the horizontal shear is strongest. It splits round the Himalaya and strengthens downstream.  Shear is strongest above and to the north."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Jet minimum: 60 kt","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":"30 m/s"},{"t":"Vertical shear: 5 m/s per km","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Depth: < 5 km","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"PFJ height: 9–12 km","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"STJ over India: ~12 km · 27°N","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"STJ southernmost: 22°N (Feb)","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"STJ months: Oct–May","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"STJ fastest: Jan 100–120 kt","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TEJ: 13°N · 15–16 km","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TEJ months: Jun–Aug","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TEJ speed: 60–80 kt (max 150)","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"LLJ: 1–3 km","s":"Met 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 15 · Jet Streams","h":"chapters/met-15.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Definition (p.140)","s":"Met 15 · notes","h":"chapters/met-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Types (p.141)","s":"Met 15 · notes","h":"chapters/met-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"STJ over India (p.141–143)","s":"Met 15 · notes","h":"chapters/met-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tropical Easterly Jet (p.143)","s":"Met 15 · notes","h":"chapters/met-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Jet stream weather and CAT (p.144)","s":"Met 15 · notes","h":"chapters/met-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 16. CAT and Mountain Waves","s":"Turbulence you cannot see: where clear-air turbulence hides near jets and the tropopause, and how strong wind over a ridge sets up lee waves, lenticular and rotor clouds.","h":"chapters/met-16.html","k":"Clear air turbulence · lee waves · rotors "},{"t":"Both chapters on one page","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where does clear air turbulence hide?","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#cat","k":"High up (23,000–39,000 ft) near the tropopause, on the fringes of jet streams and upper fronts where wind shear and a steep lapse rate combine; also above and downwind of mountains and CB tops.  CAT lives on the edges of the jet, not in its heart."},{"t":"What happens when strong wind crosses a ridge?","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#wave","k":"With wind within 30° of perpendicular (≥ 7 m/s small hills, ≥ 15 m/s big ranges), increasing with height, and a stable layer at ridge level, standing waves form downwind: cap cloud on the top, lenticulars at the crests, rotors beneath them.  Rotor = rollercoaster: the worst ride."},{"t":"What happens downwind of a ridge?","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#wavefly","k":"A strong wind across a ridge with a stable layer near the top sets up standing waves far downwind. Cap clouds and lenticulars mark them; the rotor under the first crest holds the worst turbulence. Mountain wave, animated Lens means wave; roll means rotor."},{"t":"Will mountain waves form?","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#wave-flow","k":"Mountain waves need a wind within 30° of perpendicular to the ridge, strong enough (7 m/s for hills, 15 m/s for ranges) and increasing with height, and a stable layer near ridge level with less stable air above.  Across, strong, stable."},{"t":"Wave clouds and what they warn of","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#clouds","k":"Cap clouds sit on the summit; lenticulars mark wave crests downwind (smooth if their edges are clean); rotor clouds below the crests mark the worst turbulence, especially under the first wave.  Lens means wave; roll means rotor."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"CAT band: 23,000–39,000 ft","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CAT patch: 120 × 250 km","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":"~2,000 ft deep"},{"t":"Wave wind angle: ≤ 30° from ⊥","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Wave wind speed: 7 m/s small · 15 m/s large","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Vertical currents: 10–25 m/s","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Rotor g: 2–4 g (7 g)","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Downwind extent: 100–200 km","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":"expect turbulence 20–30 NM"},{"t":"Wavelength: ≈ 10 km","s":"Met 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 16 · CAT and Mountain Waves","h":"chapters/met-16.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Clear Air Turbulence (p.147–148)","s":"Met 16 · notes","h":"chapters/met-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mountain waves — definition and conditions (p.149)","s":"Met 16 · notes","h":"chapters/met-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vertical currents and clouds (p.150–151)","s":"Met 16 · notes","h":"chapters/met-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turbulence and altimetry (p.151)","s":"Met 16 · notes","h":"chapters/met-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Variation and extent (p.152)","s":"Met 16 · notes","h":"chapters/met-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 16 · notes","h":"chapters/met-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 18. Tropical Systems","s":"The ITCZ, easterly waves, peninsular discontinuity, surges and shear lines, the low-pressure ladder from low to super cyclone, monsoon depressions and tropical cyclones over the Indian seas.","h":"chapters/met-18.html","k":"ITCZ · easterly waves · depressions · cyclones "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where is the ITCZ through the year?","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#itcz","k":"Where the NE and SE trades meet. It follows the sun: to about 20°N in July (merging with the monsoon trough), south of the equator to Australia in January. Active stretches of 75–150 km have heavy showers and CB.  ITCZ follows the sun like a sunflower."},{"t":"Easterly wave, peninsular discontinuity, surge, shear line","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#disc","k":"An easterly wave is a trough in the easterlies with the weather behind it; the peninsular discontinuity is a dry line in April–May bringing afternoon storms; a surge is a speed-up within one air mass; a shear line separates opposing winds.  "},{"t":"From low to super cyclone: the wind ladder","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#ladder","k":"Low < 17 kt · depression 17–27 · deep depression 28–33 · cyclonic storm 34–47 · severe 48–63 · very severe 64–119 · super cyclone ≥ 120 kt (winds over sea; at 900 m over land).  17 · 28 · 34 · 48 · 64 · 120 — \"Sweet 17, Gale 34, Hurricane 64\"."},{"t":"Where is the weather in a monsoon depression?","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#md","k":"Forms over the head Bay (N of 18°N) in Jun–Aug, moves NW inland, lasts 3–5 days. Heaviest steady rain in the SW sector (≈400 km belt); at recurvature it shifts to the N–NE sector. The system slopes SW with height.  Depression rain is South-West; it Slopes South-West too."},{"t":"What is inside a tropical cyclone?","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc","k":"A calm, clear, warm eye (30–65 km) ringed by the eye wall of CB with > 64 kt winds — the most dangerous part — then the outer storm area to ~400 km with gales and spiral bands. In the NH the right-forward sector is worst.  Eye = peace, wall = war."},{"t":"Can a tropical cyclone form here?","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-needs","k":"A tropical cyclone needs sea warmer than about 26 °C, a latitude of roughly 5°–25° (enough Coriolis to spin, warm enough sea), weak vertical shear and humid air through the middle troposphere.  Warm sea, off the equator, little shear."},{"t":"Cyclone numbers","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":"  "},{"t":"Minimum TC wind: 34 kt","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":"IMD: 3-min mean"},{"t":"SST: > 26°C","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":"to ~60 m"},{"t":"Latitude: 5°–25°","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Shear: < 20 kt","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":"850–200 hPa"},{"t":"Eye: 30–65 km","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Eye wall: 50–150 km","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":"> 64 kt"},{"t":"Outer area: ≈ 400 km","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Size: 150–800 km","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Speed: 300–500 km/day","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Central pressure: 1000–900 hPa","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Steep gradient within: 100 km","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Circulation top: ≈ 40,000 ft","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Indian TC life: 3–5 days","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":"max 6–7"},{"t":"Per year: Bay 4.5 · AS 1.1","s":"Met 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-18.html#tc-facts","k":""},{"t":"Cyclones and India","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#avoid","k":"Indian cyclones peak in October–November (May–June is the other season) and are weaker for their short sea tracks. In the NH, with strong wind from port the centre is ahead: turn until the wind is from starboard and dropping.  Wind on the port side: the storm is ahead."},{"t":"Pairs that catch students out","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#pairs","k":"Tropical cyclones are non-frontal, warm-core and form over warm sea at 5°–25°; extra-tropical depressions are frontal and travel west to east. A surge is a speed-up inside one air mass; a front separates two.  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 18 · Tropical Systems","h":"chapters/met-18.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Tropical discontinuities (p.154)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ITCZ (p.154–155)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Easterly waves (p.155–156)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Peninsular discontinuity (dry line) (p.156)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Surge and shear line (p.157–158)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Classification of lows (winds over sea; over land at 900 m) (p.158)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Monsoon depressions (p.158–159)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tropical cyclones (TC / TRS) (p.159–162)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Indian cyclones (p.162–163)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Frontal (extra-tropical) depressions (p.163–164)","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 18 · notes","h":"chapters/met-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 19. Climatology of India","s":"India's four seasons as a pilot sees them: pressure, winds, weather and hazards of winter, the pre-monsoon storm season, the SW monsoon (onset, trough, breaks) and the retreating monsoon, plus route winds.","h":"chapters/met-19.html","k":"Seasons · monsoon · route winds · El Niño "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Each season at a glance","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#seasons","k":"Winter (Jan–Feb): clear and dry, with fog, CAT and cold waves after Western Disturbances. Summer (Mar–May): the most hazardous, with storms, dust and squalls. SW monsoon (Jun–Sep): widespread cloud and rain. Post-monsoon (Oct–Dec): cyclones.  Fog, dust, rain, cyclones."},{"t":"When does the monsoon reach each place?","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#onset","k":"Andaman Sea ~20 May, Kerala 1 June, Mumbai 15 June, Delhi 1 July, whole country 8 July. It arrives in two branches (Arabian Sea and Bay of Bengal) and withdraws from Rajasthan on 1 Sep, from almost everywhere by 15 Oct.  1 June Kerala, 15 June Mumbai, 1 July Delhi, 8 July everyone."},{"t":"Monsoon calendar","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#onset-tl","k":"The monsoon reaches the Andamans about 20 May and Kerala on 1 June, Mumbai by mid-June, Delhi by 1 July and the whole country by about 8 July. It starts withdrawing from Rajasthan on 1 September and is gone by mid-October.  Kerala 1 June, Delhi 1 July, all India 8 July."},{"t":"Active monsoon or break?","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#break","k":"Rain follows the monsoon trough. Along the Gangetic plains it rains widely; when it moves to the Himalayan foothills (a break) pressure rises, the plains dry out with dust haze and strong westerlies over E UP/Bihar, while the foothills flood.  Break = trough goes to the hills; rain goes with it."},{"t":"How is monsoon strength described?","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#strength","k":"Monsoon strength is graded by rain over land and wind over sea: weak (< ½ normal, ≤ 12 kt), normal, strong, vigorous (> 4 × normal, > 32 kt).  Vigorous: four times the rain, over 32 kt."},{"t":"Which wind will you meet on this route?","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#routes","k":"From October to May expect westerlies above about 3 km (the STJ at 12 km). From June to September expect easterlies above about 6 km (the TEJ at 15 km) and W–SW winds below.  Winter west aloft; monsoon east aloft."},{"t":"Route winds in one table","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#route-table","k":"Upper winds over India flip with the seasons: westerly above 3 km from October to May, easterly above 6 km from June to September. Low-level winds follow the monsoon: NW–W in the north in winter, W–SW in the south in summer.  "},{"t":"El Niño vs La Niña and other systems","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#enso","k":"El Niño (a warm eastern Pacific) tends to weaken the monsoon and brings India's droughts; La Niña tends to strengthen it. Mid-tropospheric cyclones over Gujarat and monsoon depressions from the head Bay bring the heaviest rains.  El Niño: a nino-sized monsoon."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"Monsoon share of rain: ≈ 75%","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":"Jun–Sep"},{"t":"Mawsynram: 1187.3 cm","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cherrapunji: 1177.7 cm","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"W Rajasthan: < 13 cm","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Winter WDs: 5–7 / month","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cold wave: ≥ 4°C below normal min","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cold day: max ≤ 16°C","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Max diurnal range (summer): 18°C","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":"Gujarat, MP"},{"t":"Dust-raising winds: 30–50 kt","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":"0900–1700 h"},{"t":"Monsoon low: 994 hPa","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":"Baluchistan"},{"t":"Monsoon low cloud: 150–200 m","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TN NE-monsoon share: ≈ 48–60%","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sea breeze inland (post-monsoon): 160 km","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Forecast errors: Apr 5% · Jun 4%","s":"Met 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 19 · Climatology of India","h":"chapters/met-19.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Overview (p.169–170)","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Seasons","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Winter (Jan–Feb) (p.171–173)","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Summer / pre-monsoon (Mar–May) (p.173–175)","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SW monsoon (Jun–Sep) (p.175–180)","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Post-monsoon / NE monsoon (Oct–Dec) (p.180–182)","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Route winds","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Monsoon forecasting (p.182–183)","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 19 · notes","h":"chapters/met-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 20. General Circulation","s":"Why the atmosphere circulates at all, the three-cell model, the planet's pressure belts and wind belts, upper westerlies and easterlies, and standing and Rossby waves.","h":"chapters/met-20.html","k":"Hadley · Ferrel · polar cells · pressure belts "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 20 · General Circulation","h":"chapters/met-20.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How does the three-cell model work?","s":"Met 20 · General Circulation","h":"chapters/met-20.html#cells","k":"Hadley cell: air rises at the ITCZ, flows poleward aloft, sinks at ~30° (subtropical high, deserts) and returns as the trade winds. Ferrel cell: surface westerlies to ~60°, rising at the polar front. Polar cell: air sinks at the pole and spreads out as polar easterlies.  H-F-P from equator to pole: Hot Hadley, Fair Ferrel, Polar."},{"t":"The planet's belts","s":"Met 20 · General Circulation","h":"chapters/met-20.html#belts","k":"From the equator outwards: doldrums (ITCZ, rising air and rain), trade winds (0–30°), horse latitudes (30–40°, sinking air and deserts), westerlies (35–60°), the polar front (about 60°) and the polar easterlies.  Rain, trades, deserts, westerlies, fronts, polar."},{"t":"Which way do weather systems move?","s":"Met 20 · General Circulation","h":"chapters/met-20.html#steer","k":"Weather systems are steered by the upper winds: mid-latitude lows move west to east with the westerlies; tropical disturbances drift west in the easterlies and recurve when they reach the westerlies.  Upper winds steer the weather."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 20 · General Circulation","h":"chapters/met-20.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Why there is a general circulation (p.187)","s":"Met 20 · notes","h":"chapters/met-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Three-cell model (p.188–190)","s":"Met 20 · notes","h":"chapters/met-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Surface features","s":"Met 20 · notes","h":"chapters/met-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Upper winds (p.190)","s":"Met 20 · notes","h":"chapters/met-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Standing and Rossby waves (p.191–192)","s":"Met 20 · notes","h":"chapters/met-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 20 · notes","h":"chapters/met-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 21. Met Services for Aviation","s":"Who provides aviation weather (WMO, WAFS, IMD and its AMOs, AMSs and MWOs), how much notice operators must give, the reports and forecasts on offer, SIGMET and AIRMET, aerodrome and wind-shear warnings, and how messages are exchanged (VOLMET, ROBEX, ATIS).","h":"chapters/met-21.html","k":"WMO · WAFS · IMD offices · forecasts · SIGMET · warnings · VOLMET "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Indian aviation met offices","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#offices","k":"India has 6 regional met offices, 18 aerodrome met offices (AMOs, 4 of them MWOs), and 54 aerodrome met stations (AMSs). The 4 MWOs (Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai) watch the FIRs and issue SIGMETs; WAFCs London and Washington make the global charts.  Six regions, 18 offices, 54 stations, 4 watch offices."},{"t":"Which office sends you which warning?","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#board","k":"The four MWOs (Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai) watch their FIRs and issue SIGMETs; Mumbai and Kolkata also broadcast HF VOLMET; Chennai compiles the national SIGWX charts; WAFCs London and Washington issue the global charts. Who issues what Four watch offices, two VOLMETs, one compiler."},{"t":"How much notice for a non-scheduled flight?","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#notice","k":"A non-scheduled flight must give the AMO 3 hours' notice (12 h at other AMOs for international flights outside the 9 main airports) and an AMS 18–24 hours.  Three hours for an office, a day for a station."},{"t":"Every product: validity and issue","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#products","k":"METAR every half hour with a 2-hour TREND; SPECI at once when things worsen; TAF valid 9 to 30 hours; local and area forecasts valid 12 hours, issued three times a day.  TREND 2 h, TAF 9–30 h, area forecast 12 h."},{"t":"Forecast ranges","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#range","k":"Nowcasts cover less than a day, short-range forecasts 1–3 days, medium-range 4–10 days, and long or extended range beyond 10 days.  Now, short, medium, long: 1, 3, 10 days."},{"t":"SIGMET or not?","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#sigmet","k":"SIGMETs warn of en-route weather that can endanger any aircraft: obscured, embedded, frequent or squall-line thunderstorms, severe turbulence, severe icing, severe mountain waves, dust/sand storms, volcanic ash and tropical cyclones. Rain and isolated storms are not on the list.  Severe, embedded, frequent or volcanic: SIGMET."},{"t":"ISOL, OCNL, FRQ","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#ts-cover","k":"Thunderstorm coverage words: ISOL under half the area, OCNL 50–75% (well separated), FRQ over 75% (little separation). Only FRQ (with OBSC, EMBD and SQL) is used in SIGMETs.  Isolated, occasional, frequent."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Aerodrome warning wind: ≥ 30 kt","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Wind direction change (20 kt wind): 45°","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Light aircraft warning: ≥ 17 kt","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Wind shear warning: up to 500 m (1600 ft)","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"TC in SIGMET: ≥ 34 kt 10-min mean","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"SEV MTW: downdraught ≥ 3.0 m/s","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"AIREP data link: 15 min en route · 30 s climb (first 10 min)","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"AIREP exempt: ≤ 2 h flight · ≤ 1 h to land · < 1500 m · no RNAV","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"OLBS winds/temps: FL50–FL530","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"WAFC products: 4/day, within 6 h","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"VOLMET slot: 5 min per station","s":"Met 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"VOLMET, ROBEX, ATIS, and the acronyms","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#exchange","k":"VOLMET broadcasts weather for many aerodromes to aircraft en route (HF, Mumbai and Kolkata, every half hour); ATIS broadcasts one aerodrome's current weather. ROBEX exchanges reports internationally; AIRMET and GAMET serve low-level flights.  VOLMET for the route, ATIS for the airfield."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 21 · Met Services for Aviation","h":"chapters/met-21.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Purpose and organisations (p.194–195)","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aviation met offices in India","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Notification by operators (p.196) — non-scheduled flights, before ETD","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Observations and reports","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Types of forecast by range","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aviation forecasts (p.197–199)","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SIGMET (p.199)","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Warnings (p.200)","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exchange of met messages (p.201)","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 21 · notes","h":"chapters/met-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 22. Weather Radar and Met Satellites","s":"How ground and airborne weather radars see cloud and rain, which radar band does what, how to read echoes for severe weather, airborne radar colours, and the polar-orbiting and geostationary satellites (INSAT, Kalpana, METEOSAT, NOAA) that feed forecasters.","h":"chapters/met-22.html","k":"Radar bands · DWR · echoes · airborne radar · satellites "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which radar band does what?","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#bands","k":"Shorter wavelengths see smaller drops: X band (3 cm) is the airborne weather radar that sees cloud and storms; S band (10 cm) sees through heavy rain to track cyclones (Cyclone Detection Radar).  X for aircraft, S for storms at sea."},{"t":"PPI or RHI? Polar or geostationary?","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#scopes","k":"A PPI shows echoes in plan (bearing and range); an RHI shows their height. A bright band at the freezing level marks steady stratiform rain; sharp-edged echoes mark convection.  P for plan, H for height."},{"t":"What does this echo tell you?","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#echo","k":"Echo shapes give the game away: hooks and protuberances mean hail, a figure-6 means a tornado, long-lived spiral bands mean a tropical cyclone, a bright band means a weak storm, slanting rain shafts mean wind shear.  Hook for hail, six for a twister."},{"t":"Radar numbers and facts","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":"  "},{"t":"Max detection range: 500 km","s":"Met 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":"good resolution to 250 km"},{"t":"Normal display: 25 NM","s":"Met 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":"long range to 75 NM"},{"t":"Return ∝: D⁶","s":"Met 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":"so drizzle is invisible"},{"t":"Range limited by: rain attenuation + earth curvature","s":"Met 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":""},{"t":"Indian X-band Multi Met Radar: since 1975","s":"Met 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":""},{"t":"Quantitative radar: 10 cm","s":"Met 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-22.html#dwr","k":""},{"t":"Reading the airborne radar","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#airborne","k":"Airborne weather radar is X-band (3 cm), beam about 3° wide, scanning ±70° of heading. Colours go green (light, 1–4 mm/h) → yellow (moderate, 4–12, avoid) → red (> 12 mm/h) → magenta (TS, hail, severe turbulence). Always avoid red and magenta.  Go, Yield, Run, Must-avoid."},{"t":"Two kinds of weather satellite","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#sats","k":"Polar orbiters (NOAA) fly at 650–1500 km and see the whole earth twice a day. Geostationary satellites (INSAT, METEOSAT, GOES) sit ~36 000 km above the equator, always over the same spot; five cover the globe.  "},{"t":"Indian met satellites","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#india-sats","k":"India's met satellites are geostationary: Kalpana-1 (74°E), INSAT-3A (93.5°E), INSAT-3D (82°E) and INSAT-3DR (74°E); together 3D and 3DR give fresh images every 15 minutes.  "},{"t":"Reading a satellite picture","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#images","k":"On an infrared picture the coldest (highest) cloud tops are the whitest; on a visible picture thick water cloud is brightest. Long cirrus streaks mark jet streams; ash is brown, dust tan, smoke grey.  Infrared: cold is white."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 22 · Weather Radar and Met Satellites","h":"chapters/met-22.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Weather radar (p.206)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wavelength bands (Table 22.1)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Doppler Weather Radar (p.207–208)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cloud echoes (p.208–209)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airborne weather radar (p.209)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met satellites (p.210–213)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reading satellite images (p.212–213)","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 22 · notes","h":"chapters/met-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 23. Met Instruments","s":"One table: every weather element, the instrument that measures it and the unit it is reported in.","h":"chapters/met-23.html","k":"What measures what, and in which unit "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 23 · Met Instruments","h":"chapters/met-23.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Element → instrument → unit","s":"Met 23 · Met Instruments","h":"chapters/met-23.html#table","k":"Each weather element has its instrument: barometers for pressure, max (mercury) and min (alcohol) thermometers, wet and dry bulbs for humidity, rain gauges, wind vanes and anemometers (wind from true north), ceilometers for cloud base and transmissometers for RVR.  Mercury for the max, alcohol for the min."},{"t":"Can you name the instrument?","s":"Met 23 · Met Instruments","h":"chapters/met-23.html#cards","k":"Learn instruments by their roots: baro = pressure, anemo = wind, hygro = humidity, hyeto = rain, nepho = cloud movement, ceilo = cloud base, transmisso = visibility. \"-graph\" means it records on a chart. Instrument cards -meter measures, -graph records."},{"t":"Which element does it measure?","s":"Met 23 · Met Instruments","h":"chapters/met-23.html#match","k":"Anemo- is wind, hyeto- is rain, nepho- and ceilo- are cloud, transmisso- is visibility, baro- and aneroid are pressure.  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 23 · Met Instruments","h":"chapters/met-23.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 23 · notes","h":"chapters/met-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 24. Station Models","s":"How a surface observation is plotted around a station circle: wind barbs, temperature, dew point, visibility and weather codes, QFF, pressure change, cloud types, amounts and heights.","h":"chapters/met-24.html","k":"Reading a plotted weather observation "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What does each position around the circle mean?","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#layout-hs","k":"Left column: temperature, visibility and present weather, dew point. Right column: QFF, 24-hour change, past weather. Above: high and medium cloud. Below: low cloud, its amount/base, and rain. The wind shaft points to where the wind comes from. Tap the plot Left is what it feels like; right is what the pressure did."},{"t":"Where each element sits","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#layout","k":"Left: TT, VV ww, TdTd. Right: PPP (QFF), 24-h change, past weather. Above: high and medium cloud. Below: low cloud, Nh/h, RRR. Wind shaft points to where the wind comes from; full barb 10 kt, half barb 5 kt.  "},{"t":"The book's worked example","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#example","k":"998 means QFF 999.8 hPa (add 9 or 10 in front, tenths last). VV 93 = 500 to < 1000 m. h code 2 = base 100–199 m.  "},{"t":"Visibility and cloud-base codes","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#codes","k":"Visibility code VV and cloud-base code h grow in steps: code 0 is under 50 m visibility or a base under 50 m; code 9 is 50 km or more, or no cloud below 2500 m.  Nought is nil, nine is clear."},{"t":"Which ww group is it?","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#ww","k":"ww 00–19: no precipitation at the station now; 20–29: weather in the last hour but not now; 40–49: fog now; 50–59 drizzle; 60–69 rain; 70–79 snow; 80–89 showers; 90–99 thunderstorms.  Twenties are the past hour; nineties are thunder."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 24 · Station Models","h":"chapters/met-24.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Layout (Table 24.1, inland plain station)","s":"Met 24 · notes","h":"chapters/met-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Codes","s":"Met 24 · notes","h":"chapters/met-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 25. METAR, SPECI and TREND","s":"How an aerodrome weather report is coded: wind, visibility, RVR, weather, cloud, temperature, QNH and supplementary groups; when a SPECI must be issued; and the TREND landing forecast appended to it.","h":"chapters/met-25.html","k":"Decode every group · SPECI criteria · TREND "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Tap each group of a SPECI","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#decode","k":"Read a METAR or SPECI left to right: place, day-time Z, wind (with gusts and variation), visibility (and a second direction), RVR, weather, cloud, temperature/dew point, QNH, recent weather, then the TREND.  Where, when, wind, view, runway, weather, cloud, temps, pressure."},{"t":"And the question METAR","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#decode2","k":"In the question METAR: wind 300/05 varying 290–050, minimum visibility 1500 m south with 5000 m north, RVR above 1500 m and rising, mist, cloud from FEW020 to BKN300 including FEW CB at 2500 ft, 32/29 and QNH 1003.  "},{"t":"Visibility, RVR and cloud reporting steps","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#steps","k":"Visibility is reported in 50 m steps up to 800 m, 100 m steps to 5 km, 1 km steps to 9 km, then 9999. RVR goes in 25, 50 and 100 m steps. Cloud bases in 100 ft steps up to 10 000 ft.  50, 100, 1000, then 9999."},{"t":"How is it coded?","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#round","k":"Temperatures round to the nearest whole degree (M for minus); QNH is always rounded down; an unsteady light wind is VRB; 100 kt or more is coded P99.  QNH always rounds down."},{"t":"Cloud amounts and which layers are reported","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#cloud","k":"FEW 1–2 octas, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8. Report the lowest layer whatever its amount, the next only if 3/8 or more, the next only if 5/8 or more, and CB or TCU always.  Any, three, five, and always the CB."},{"t":"Weather terms people mix up","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#wx","k":"Fog (FG) is visibility under 1000 m, mist (BR) 1000–5000 m. Hail (GR) is 5 mm or more, small hail (GS) less. Drifting (DR) is raised under 2 m, blowing (BL) 2 m or more. VC means within 8 km of the station.  Under a kilometre is fog; five millimetres is hail."},{"t":"When can you say CAVOK?","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#cavok","k":"CAVOK replaces visibility, RVR, weather and cloud only when visibility is 10 km or more, there is no cloud below 5000 ft (or the highest MSA) and no CB/TCU, and no significant weather.  Ten kilometres, nothing below five thousand, nothing happening."},{"t":"When must a SPECI be issued?","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#speci","k":"A SPECI is issued when conditions cross set thresholds: visibility through 800, 1500, 3000 or 5000 m; RVR through 150, 350, 600 or 800 m; a big wind change; cloud bases through 100–1500 ft; a temperature rise of 2 °C; and the onset or end of significant weather.  Cross a limit, send a SPECI."},{"t":"BECMG or TEMPO?","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#trend","k":"BECMG is a lasting change between stated times; TEMPO is fluctuations each under an hour, in total under half the period. A TREND is valid 2 hours, NOSIG means no change expected, and PROB is never used in a TREND.  Becoming stays; tempo goes away."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 25 · METAR, SPECI and TREND","h":"chapters/met-25.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"The reports (p.227)","s":"Met 25 · notes","h":"chapters/met-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reporting rules","s":"Met 25 · notes","h":"chapters/met-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SPECI criteria (p.230–231) — deterioration at once, improvement after 10 min","s":"Met 25 · notes","h":"chapters/met-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"TREND (landing forecast) (p.233–235)","s":"Met 25 · notes","h":"chapters/met-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Worked example (book)","s":"Met 25 · notes","h":"chapters/met-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 25 · notes","h":"chapters/met-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 26. TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","s":"How aerodrome forecasts (TAF) and the figure-coded area and route forecasts (ARFOR, ROFOR) are written: validity groups, change groups, and the 7/6/5/4/2 indicator groups for freezing level, icing, turbulence, winds and tropopause, plus jet and maximum-wind sections.","h":"chapters/met-26.html","k":"Aerodrome, area and route forecasts "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Tap each group of the book's TAF","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf","k":"A TAF reads like a METAR with a validity period: place, issue time, valid-from/to, wind, visibility, weather, cloud, then change groups (BECMG for lasting changes, TEMPO for short ones) with their times.  Same order as a METAR, plus valid times and change groups."},{"t":"TAF numbers","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":"  "},{"t":"Validity: 9–30 h","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":""},{"t":"9-h TAF issued: every 3 h","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":"domestic + VOLMET only"},{"t":"12–30 h TAF issued: every 6 h","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":"from 0000 UTC"},{"t":"PROB: 30 or 40 %","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":""},{"t":"TX42/10Z: max 42 °C at 1000 Z","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":""},{"t":"TN05/01Z: min 5 °C at 0100 Z","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":""},{"t":"IST: UTC + 5:30","s":"Met 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-26.html#taf-rules","k":""},{"t":"What does each indicator figure introduce?","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#indicators","k":"In ROFOR/ARFOR the first figure tells you the group: 7 freezing level, 6 icing, 5 turbulence, 4 wind and temperature at a height, 2 tropopause; 11111 starts the jet-stream section and 22222 the max-wind and shear section.  Seven freezes, six ices, five bumps, four blows."},{"t":"Turbulence, icing and weather code figures","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#codes","k":"Turbulence and icing code figures run from 0 (nil) upwards in severity; the repeated-figure weather groups are worth memorising: 111 TS, 222 tropical cyclone, 333 severe line squall, 444 hail, 555 marked mountain waves, 666 sandstorm, 777 duststorm.  Triple figures: one thunder, two cyclone, four hail."},{"t":"Decode the book's ROFOR","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#rofor","k":"A ROFOR gives a route forecast by quadrant: cloud amounts and heights, the freezing level (7 group), then winds and temperatures at each height (4 groups), plus any turbulence and icing groups.  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 26 · TAF, ARFOR and ROFOR","h":"chapters/met-26.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"TAF — Aerodrome Forecast (p.242–244)","s":"Met 26 · notes","h":"chapters/met-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ARFOR — Area Forecast (p.247–249)","s":"Met 26 · notes","h":"chapters/met-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ROFOR — Route Forecast (p.250–251)","s":"Met 26 · notes","h":"chapters/met-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 26 · notes","h":"chapters/met-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 27. Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","s":"How a ground radar report (RAREP) describes echoes, how a SIGMET message is laid out, and what a satellite bulletin says.","h":"chapters/met-27.html","k":"RAREP · SIGMET format · SAT BLTN "},{"t":"Plot the book's radar report","s":"Met 27 · Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","h":"chapters/met-27.html#rarep","k":"RAREP gives echo-area points as bearing/range (km), the echo character (SLD, BRKN, SCT…), its tendency, movement (direction in tens of degrees, speed in km/h) and echo-top heights in km AMSL (only within 200 km).  "},{"t":"Echo character words","s":"Met 27 · Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","h":"chapters/met-27.html#echo-terms","k":"Radar reports describe echoes in standard words: EYE and SPRL BAND for cyclones, SQL LN for a squall line, SLD, BRKN, SCT and WDLY SCT for coverage, and INCG/DEC/NO CHG for the trend.  Words for shape, words for cover, words for change."},{"t":"Tap through a SIGMET","s":"Met 27 · Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet","k":"A SIGMET names the MWO, its sequence number and validity (no more than 4 h, 6 h for TC and VA), the phenomenon, whether observed or forecast, where and how high, its movement and intensity change, and an outlook for cyclones and ash.  Who, number, when, what, where, which way, worse or better."},{"t":"In what order are SIGMET items written?","s":"Met 27 · Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-order","k":"Write a SIGMET in fixed order: MWO, SIGMET number, VALID, phenomenon, OBS/FCST with time, location and levels, movement, intensity change, then the outlook.  "},{"t":"SIGMET distribution facts","s":"Met 27 · Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":"  "},{"t":"Supersonic aircraft: SIGMET SST","s":"Met 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":"separate numbering"},{"t":"Sent on AFTN priority: DD","s":"Met 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":""},{"t":"To neighbouring MWOs within: 1100 NM","s":"Met 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":""},{"t":"Other MWOs' SIGMETs go to: local FIC","s":"Met 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":""},{"t":"RAREP heights only within: 200 km","s":"Met 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":""},{"t":"Squall-line echo: ≥ 60 km, 10:1","s":"Met 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-27.html#sigmet-facts","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 27 · Radar Report, SIGMET and Satellite Bulletin","h":"chapters/met-27.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"RAREP — radar report (p.255–257)","s":"Met 27 · notes","h":"chapters/met-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SIGMET message format (p.257–258)","s":"Met 27 · notes","h":"chapters/met-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Satellite bulletin (p.259)","s":"Met 27 · notes","h":"chapters/met-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 27 · notes","h":"chapters/met-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 28. Met Documentation and Briefing","s":"What met information crews and operators get, how and when briefing is given, what goes into flight documentation (T-3, T-4, charts), and how national SIGWX charts are made.","h":"chapters/met-28.html","k":"Briefing · flight documents · SIGWX "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 28 · Met Documentation and Briefing","h":"chapters/met-28.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How does a met briefing work?","s":"Met 28 · Met Documentation and Briefing","h":"chapters/met-28.html#brief","k":"Crews get weather for departure, en route, destination and alternates, in person 3 hours before departure. Low-level flights are briefed up to FL100 (FL150 in hills). Without enough notice the briefing is oral and recorded in the briefing register.  Three hours, in person, everything on the route."},{"t":"T-3 or T-4?","s":"Met 28 · Met Documentation and Briefing","h":"chapters/met-28.html#forms","k":"Met T-3 is a table for flights up to 500 NM (heights AMSL); Met T-4 is a cross-section for longer flights (flight levels). En-route heights are in flight levels; aerodrome cloud heights are above aerodrome elevation.  Three is less: T-3 for short flights."},{"t":"Significant for high-level or low-level flights?","s":"Met 28 · Met Documentation and Briefing","h":"chapters/met-28.html#hilo","k":"Thunderstorms, CB, squall lines, hail, turbulence, icing, freezing precipitation, widespread dust/sand storms and marked mountain waves matter to every flight. Low-level flights also get fog, precipitation and anything below 5000 m visibility.  "},{"t":"National SIGWX numbers","s":"Met 28 · Met Documentation and Briefing","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":"All four MWOs prepare SIGWX for their FIRs and Chennai compiles the national medium-level (FL100–250, with the 0 °C isotherm) and high-level (FL250–630) charts: four a day, issued 9 h after data time, valid 24 h.  Chennai compiles; zero degrees on the medium chart."},{"t":"Prepared by: all 4 MWOs","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":"compiled at Chennai"},{"t":"Medium level: FL100–250","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":"with 0 °C isotherm"},{"t":"High level: FL250–630","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":""},{"t":"Issued: 4 × a day","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":"00, 06, 12, 18 UTC data"},{"t":"Issued after data time: 9 h","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":""},{"t":"Valid: 24 h","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":""},{"t":"TC shown if: ≥ 34 kt","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":""},{"t":"Briefing: 3 h before departure","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":""},{"t":"Notice for documentation: 3 h AMO · 12 h AMS","s":"Met 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-28.html#sigwx","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 28 · Met Documentation and Briefing","h":"chapters/met-28.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"What is supplied (p.260)","s":"Met 28 · notes","h":"chapters/met-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Briefing (p.260–261)","s":"Met 28 · notes","h":"chapters/met-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flight documentation","s":"Met 28 · notes","h":"chapters/met-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"National SIGWX charts (p.262)","s":"Met 28 · notes","h":"chapters/met-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Heights in documentation","s":"Met 28 · notes","h":"chapters/met-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 29. Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","s":"How to read the two route-forecast forms handed to crews: the tabular Met T-3 and the pictorial cross-section Met T-4.","h":"chapters/met-29.html","k":"Met T-3 and Met T-4 samples "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Met 29 · Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","h":"chapters/met-29.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Read the sample Met T-3 (Kolkata → Delhi)","s":"Met 29 · Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","h":"chapters/met-29.html#t3","k":"Read a T-3 column by column: upper winds and temperatures at each level, then cloud, visibility, significant weather and the 0 °C isotherm at each station along the route.  Columns are places, rows are levels."},{"t":"Find it on the T-3","s":"Met 29 · Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","h":"chapters/met-29.html#t3game","k":"On the sample T-3 (Kolkata → Delhi): 0 °C isotherm 15 000 ft at Kolkata and 14 000 ft at Delhi, QNH 1006 and 1008, CB embedded at Lucknow (base 2000, tops 35 000 ft), visibility 1500 m in TS at Delhi.  "},{"t":"What a Met T-4 cross-section looks like","s":"Met 29 · Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","h":"chapters/met-29.html#t4","k":"Met T-4 draws the route as a vertical slice: cloud layers by flight level (CI high, AS middle, SC/CB/ST low), with a table of winds and temperatures at FL100–400 at each end and words like veering, backing, weakening.  T-4 is a picture; T-3 is a table."},{"t":"Abbreviations on the forms","s":"Met 29 · Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":"  "},{"t":"ISOL: < 50 %","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"OCNL: 50–75 %","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"FRQ: > 75 %","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"FEW / SCT: 1–2 / 3–4 octas","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"BKN / OVC: 5–7 / 8 octas","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"M: minus","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"EMBD: embedded","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"LOC: locally","s":"Met 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/met-29.html#abbr","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 29 · Flight Forecast: Tabular and Cross-Section","h":"chapters/met-29.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Met T-3 — tabular flight forecast (sample, Table 29.1)","s":"Met 29 · notes","h":"chapters/met-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met T-4 — cross-section route forecast (sample, Table 29.2)","s":"Met 29 · notes","h":"chapters/met-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Met 29 · notes","h":"chapters/met-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Met 30. Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","s":"Everything the book keeps at the back: synoptic systems, scales of motion, tornado and cyclone scales, weather at fronts, iso-lines, rainfall terms, climates, chart conventions, and the other drivers of the SW monsoon (Tibetan High, Somali jet, IOD, MJO).","h":"chapters/met-30.html","k":"Book appendices A–J "},{"t":"How big and how long-lived is each kind of weather?","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#scales","k":"Micro (≤ 1 km, minutes): gusts. Meso (5–500 km, under a day): TS, squall lines, sea breezes, mountain waves. Synoptic (thousands of km, days): lows, fronts, jets. Planetary (≥ 5 days): ENSO, ITCZ, Rossby waves.  "},{"t":"Which iso-line joins what?","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#iso","k":"Tach = speed (tachometer), gon = angle/direction, hyet = rain, allo = change, pycn = density, hel = sun (Helios), hypse = height.  Tach-speed, Gon-angle, Hyet-wet, Hel-sun."},{"t":"Name the line","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#iso-game","k":"Iso-lines: isotach equal wind speed, isogon equal wind direction, isohyet equal rainfall, isallobar equal pressure change, isohel equal sunshine.  Tach is speed, hyet is rain, hel is sun."},{"t":"T-numbers of cyclonic disturbances","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#tscale","k":"Satellite T-numbers grade a disturbance: T1.5 low, T2.0 depression, T2.5 deep depression, T3.0 cyclonic storm, T3.5 severe, T4.0–6.0 very severe, T6.5 and above super cyclonic storm.  T3 is where a storm becomes a cyclone."},{"t":"Saffir–Simpson and Fujita scales","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#saffir","k":"The Saffir–Simpson scale grades hurricanes 1–5 by wind (64 kt up to over 135 kt), central pressure and surge; the Fujita scale grades tornadoes F0–F5 by wind speed.  "},{"t":"Before, at and after each front (NH)","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#fronts","k":"Ahead of either front pressure falls and the wind backs. A warm front brings lowering layer cloud and worsening visibility, then a veer and little else. A cold front brings CB and squalls, a sharp veer, a pressure rise, a temperature drop and rapid clearing.  Falls and backs before, rises and veers after."},{"t":"How forecasts describe rain","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#rain-terms","k":"Forecast words for spread: widespread ≥ 75% of stations, fairly widespread 51–74%, scattered 26–50%, isolated ≤ 25%. For amount: a rainy day is 2.5 mm or more; heavy rain 64.5–124.4 mm.  Most, many, a few, isolated."},{"t":"Parts of the day in IST forecasts","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#day-parts","k":"IST forecasts split the day into six four-hour blocks: early hours 00–04, morning 04–08, forenoon 08–12, afternoon 12–16, evening 16–20, night 20–24.  Six blocks of four hours."},{"t":"Other monsoon drivers","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#drivers","k":"Besides ENSO, the monsoon is pushed by the Tibetan High (aloft, feeding the TEJ), the Somali jet (low-level, cross-equatorial), the Indian Ocean Dipole (positive = more rain) and the eastward-moving MJO (30–60 days).  Tibet above, Somali below, IOD and MJO alongside."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Met 30 · Appendices: Scales, Terms and Monsoon Drivers","h":"chapters/met-30.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"A. Synoptic systems","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"B. Meteorological scales","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"C. Weather with fronts","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"D. Miscellaneous terms","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"E. Major climates","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"F. Met charts","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"G. Other systems influencing the SW monsoon","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"H–J. Pictures and normals","s":"Met 30 · notes","h":"chapters/met-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 1. The Solar System","s":"The earth's elliptical orbit, why we have seasons, sunrise, sunset and the three twilights, the moon, leap years, and the difference between apparent, mean and sidereal days.","h":"chapters/nav-01.html","k":"Orbit · seasons · twilight · kinds of day "},{"t":"Why does a pilot care about the sun?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#start","k":"The sun sets the clock (time and longitude), the calendar (the year and leap years) and the light you may fly in (sunrise, sunset and twilight). Start here "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where is the earth in each season?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#orbit","k":"The orbit is an ellipse: closest (perihelion, fastest) in early January, farthest (aphelion, slowest) in early July. Seasons come from the 23.5° tilt, not from distance.  Perihelion in January: the NH is nearest the sun in its winter."},{"t":"What happens when in the year?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#year","k":"Perihelion ~3 Jan, equinox ~21 Mar, solstice ~21 Jun, aphelion ~4 Jul, equinox ~23 Sep, solstice ~22 Dec.  Equinox = equal night. Solstice = sun stands still at a tropic."},{"t":"What causes what?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#effects","k":"Rotation → day and night. Revolution → the year. Tilt → seasons and unequal day and night. Elliptical orbit → the apparent solar day varies.  Spin = day, lap = year, lean = season."},{"t":"Sort the effects by cause","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#sort","k":"Only the tilt explains seasons and unequal day lengths. Only the ellipse explains why the true sun is an irregular clock.  "},{"t":"Civil, nautical, astronomical twilight","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#twilight","k":"Twilight is measured by how far the sun's centre is below the sensible horizon: civil 0–6° (the one pilots use), nautical 6–12°, astronomical 12–18°. Almanac times are in LMT.  Six, twelve, eighteen: civil, nautical, astronomical."},{"t":"How deep is the sun below the horizon?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#tw-layers","k":"At sunset the sun's centre is already about 50′ below the sensible horizon (refraction lifts the image). Civil twilight then lasts until 6°.  Refraction: sunrise early, sunset late."},{"t":"Sensible or visible horizon?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#horizon","k":"The sensible horizon is the true level through your eye; the visible horizon is where the earth seems to meet the sky, pushed down by refraction and height.  "},{"t":"Apparent, mean and sidereal day","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#days","k":"Apparent day (true sun) varies; mean day (mean sun) is a steady 24 h; sidereal day (a star) is 23 h 56 min. Equation of time = apparent − mean.  Sundial minus clock = equation of time."},{"t":"Is it a leap year?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#leap","k":"Every year divisible by 4 is a leap year, except century years, which must be divisible by 400 (2000 yes; 1900 no).  Centuries need 400."},{"t":"What does the moon do?","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#moon","k":"The moon shines by reflected sunlight, goes round once in about 29½ days (synodic month), and rises about 50 minutes later each day.  Moon late by fifty."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Earth's year: 365 d 5 h 48 m 45 s","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Axis to orbit plane: 66.5°","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Obliquity of ecliptic: 23.5°","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Sidereal day: 23 h 56 min","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Synodic month: 29½ days","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Moonrise later each day: ~50 min","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Sun centre at sunset: ~50′ below","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Midnight sun / polar night: beyond ~66.5°","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Leap century: ÷ 400","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Civil twilight ends: 6° below","s":"Nav 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 1 · The Solar System","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"The earth's orbit","s":"Nav 1 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tilt and seasons","s":"Nav 1 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Horizon and twilight","s":"Nav 1 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Moon","s":"Nav 1 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Year and day","s":"Nav 1 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 1 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 2. The Earth","s":"The earth's real shape and size, the nautical mile, latitude (geocentric vs geodetic) and longitude, great circles, small circles and rhumb lines, and how to work out change of latitude and longitude.","h":"chapters/nav-02.html","k":"Shape · latitude · longitude · great circles · d.lat and d.long "},{"t":"How do we say where anything is on a round earth?","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#start","k":"A slightly squashed ball, a grid of latitude and longitude on it, and two kinds of line between places: the great circle (shortest) and the rhumb line (constant track). Start here "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"The earth in numbers","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":"Equatorial radius 3443 NM, polar 3432 NM: compression 1/297. The standard NM is 1852 m (6076 ft), shortest at the equator and longest at the poles.  Flattened at the poles, so a minute of arc is longer there."},{"t":"Polar radius: 3432 NM","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":"6 356 752 m"},{"t":"Equatorial radius: 3443 NM","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":"6 378 137 m"},{"t":"Compression: 1/297","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":""},{"t":"Polar diameter shorter by: ~23½ NM","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":""},{"t":"1 NM (standard): 1852 m = 6076 ft","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":""},{"t":"NM at equator / pole: 6046 / 6108 ft","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":""},{"t":"Geodetic–geocentric max: 11.6′ at 45°","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#size","k":""},{"t":"Geocentric or geodetic latitude?","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#geodetic","k":"Charts use geodetic latitude (from the normal to the spheroid). Geocentric latitude uses the line to the centre. They differ most, about 11.6′, at 45°.  Geodetic = the line the ground stands on; geocentric = the line to the centre."},{"t":"Latitude or longitude?","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#latlong","k":"Latitude is measured from the equator along a meridian (0–90° N/S); longitude from Greenwich along the equator (0–180° E/W). Parallels are small circles; meridians are half great circles.  Lat is flat (the rungs of a ladder); long runs long, pole to pole."},{"t":"Great circle or rhumb line?","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#lines","k":"A great circle's plane passes through the earth's centre and its shorter arc is the shortest distance. A rhumb line crosses every meridian at the same angle (constant track) and is longer. The equator is both.  Great = through the centre. Rhumb = same angle."},{"t":"Great circle, small circle or rhumb line?","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#linesort","k":"Meridians and the equator are great circles; every other parallel is a small circle and a rhumb line. The equator is both a GC and a rhumb line.  "},{"t":"Working out d.lat and d.long","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#dlong","k":"d.lat: subtract in the same hemisphere, add across the equator. d.long: same, but if the sum passes 180° use 360° minus it (the short way across the date line).  Same names subtract, different names add."},{"t":"d.lat","s":"Nav 2 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#dlong","k":""},{"t":"d.long","s":"Nav 2 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#dlong","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":"  "},{"t":"1° of latitude: 60 NM","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"1′ of latitude: 1 NM","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"1 NM: 1852 m","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"Max geodetic − geocentric: 11.6′ at 45°","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"Compression: 1/297","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"Longitude range: 0–180° E/W","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"Latitude range: 0–90° N/S","s":"Nav 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#nums2","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 2 · The Earth","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Shape and size","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nautical mile","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Latitude","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Longitude and meridians","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Great circle, small circle, rhumb line","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Change of latitude and longitude","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 2 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 3. Projections","s":"How a round earth is put on a flat chart, what \"conformal\" means, and how Mercator, Lambert's conformal conic, polar stereographic, transverse and oblique Mercator show scale, convergence, great circles and rhumb lines. Plus chart scale sums.","h":"chapters/nav-03.html","k":"Mercator · Lambert · polar stereographic · scale "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How do GC and RL look on each chart?","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#three","k":"Mercator: rhumb line straight, great circle bows towards the pole. Lambert and polar stereographic: great circle (nearly) straight, rhumb line bows towards the pole.  Great circles always bow towards the pole; rhumb lines towards the equator."},{"t":"Compare the projections","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#compare","k":"Scale is correct where the surface touches the earth: Mercator at the equator, Lambert on its two standard parallels, polar stereographic at the pole.  Where it touches, it's true."},{"t":"Which projection is it?","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#which","k":"Straight rhumb line → Mercator. Two standard parallels → Lambert. Convergence factor 1, perspective and conformal → polar stereographic. Datum meridian → transverse Mercator.  "},{"t":"What makes a chart conformal?","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#conformal","k":"Conformal (orthomorphic) means bearings are correct: meridians and parallels cross at 90° and the scale is the same in every direction at a point.  Ortho-morphic = right shape (locally)."},{"t":"How fast does Mercator scale grow?","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#merc-dial","k":"Mercator scale is practically constant within about 8° of the equator, doubles at 60° (sec 60 = 2) and becomes useless beyond about 70°: the poles cannot be shown.  Sixty degrees, double the scale."},{"t":"Chart scale calculators","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#scale","k":"Mercator: denominators scale with cos lat (equator denominator = denominator at lat ÷ cos lat). Any chart: scale = chart distance ÷ earth distance in the same units.  Same units on both sides of the colon."},{"t":"Mercator scale at another latitude","s":"Nav 3 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#scale","k":""},{"t":"Scale from a measured length","s":"Nav 3 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#scale","k":""},{"t":"Large scale or small scale?","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#bigsmall","k":"A large-scale chart has a small denominator (1 : 250 000) and shows more detail over less area. The worse the weather, the larger the scale you want.  Large scale = large detail, small area."},{"t":"Units to know","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":"  "},{"t":"1 NM: 1852 m ≈ 6080 ft","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"1 km: 3280 ft","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"1 SM: 5280 ft","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"1 inch: 2.54 cm","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"1000 m: 0.54 NM","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"1° of latitude: 60 NM","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"Larger scale: smaller denominator","s":"Nav 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#units","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 3 · Projections","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Why projections distort","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Orthomorphism (conformality)","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Simple cylindrical (tangent at the equator, light at centre)","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mercator (direct)","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Simple conic and Lambert's conformal conic (LCC)","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Polar stereographic","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transverse and oblique Mercator","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Quick rules","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Scale","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Polar stereographic straight-line tracks (worked method)","s":"Nav 3 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 4. Convergency","s":"Why great-circle tracks change as you fly, how to work out convergency and the conversion angle, how to turn a GC track into a rhumb-line track and back, and how to tell the hemisphere from two GC bearings.","h":"chapters/nav-04.html","k":"Convergency · conversion angle · GC vs RL tracks "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Why does a great-circle track keep changing?","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#gcfly","k":"Meridians are not parallel, so a straight (great-circle) route crosses each one at a different angle: here 070° at A and 110° at B. That 40° change is the convergency. The rhumb line keeps 090° but is longer. GC vs RL, animated Track change between two meridians = convergency."},{"t":"Play with it: watch the great circle bend","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#sim","k":"Move the points towards the pole and the great circle bows further from the rhumb line: convergency (d.long × sin lat) grows, and the conversion angle is half of it.  The great circle always sits on the polar side of the rhumb line."},{"t":"The two formulas","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#formula","k":"Convergency = d.long × sin mean latitude (0 at the equator, = d.long at the pole). Conversion angle = half of it.  Long × sine, then halve it."},{"t":"Convergency","s":"Nav 4 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#formula","k":""},{"t":"Conversion angle","s":"Nav 4 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#formula","k":""},{"t":"Which hemisphere are the points in?","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#hemisphere","k":"Subtract the two great-circle bearings (A→B and B→A): exactly 180° → equator; more than 180° → northern hemisphere; less → southern.  More than 180, north is your fate."},{"t":"Add or subtract the conversion angle?","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#apply","k":"Draw it: the GC sits on the polar side. NH eastbound at departure, RL = GC + CA; NH westbound, RL = GC − CA. The southern hemisphere is the mirror image.  Sketch first, sign second."},{"t":"Convergence on each chart","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#charts","k":"Mercator shows no convergence (correct only at the equator); Lambert shows d.long × sin (parallel of origin); polar stereographic shows d.long (correct at the pole).  "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Convergency at the equator: 0","s":"Nav 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Convergency at the pole: = d.long","s":"Nav 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"sin 30°: 0.5","s":"Nav 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"sin 45°: 0.707","s":"Nav 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"sin 60°: 0.866","s":"Nav 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Conversion angle: ½ conv","s":"Nav 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 4 · Convergency","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Definitions","s":"Nav 4 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"On charts","s":"Nav 4 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Working rules","s":"Nav 4 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 4 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 5. Time","s":"How longitude turns into time, converting between LMT, UTC, zone time and IST, what happens at the international date line, and how to use almanac sunrise times.","h":"chapters/nav-05.html","k":"LMT · UTC · zone time · IST · date line "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Play with it: what time is it along the world?","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#sim","k":"Every degree of longitude is 4 minutes. East is ahead, west is behind. Slide UTC and watch day and night sweep westwards; cross 180° and the date changes. Play: time around the world Longitude east, UTC least; longitude west, UTC best."},{"t":"Arc to time","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#arc","k":"360° = 24 h, so 15° = 1 h, 1° = 4 min, 15′ = 1 min and 1′ = 4 s.  One degree, four minutes."},{"t":"How far ahead or behind is a longitude?","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#offset","k":"LMT − UTC = longitude ÷ 15 h: +5 h 30 min at 82°30′E (IST), +12 h just short of 180°E, −12 h just short of 180°W.  Longitude east, UTC least; longitude west, UTC best."},{"t":"Converters","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#convert","k":"Always work in UTC: convert the departure time to UTC, add the flight time, then convert the arrival UTC to local time.  Convert in, fly in UTC, convert out."},{"t":"LMT from UTC","s":"Nav 5 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#convert","k":""},{"t":"Arc to time","s":"Nav 5 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#convert","k":""},{"t":"Crossing the date line","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#dateline","k":"The date line is roughly the 180° meridian. Crossing it westbound (towards Asia) the date goes forward a day; eastbound (towards the Americas) it goes back.  Go west, gain a date."},{"t":"Which date is it now?","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#dlstamp","k":"Westbound across 180°: add a day. Eastbound: take a day off. Then still convert to UTC for any time arithmetic.  "},{"t":"Kinds of time","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#kinds","k":"LMT is your own meridian's mean time; UTC is Greenwich's; zone time uses 15° zones; standard time is the legal time of a country (IST = UTC + 5:30).  Local, universal, zone, standard: from your meridian outwards."},{"t":"Sunrise at another longitude","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#sunrise","k":"On a given date sunrise happens at the same LMT all along a latitude. Find that LMT, then convert it to UTC with the new longitude.  Same latitude, same LMT."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"1 hour of arc: 15°","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"1°: 4 min","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"1′: 4 s","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"IST: UTC + 5:30","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"IST meridian: 82°30′E","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Time zones: 24 × 15°","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Zone letters: A–Z (no I, O)","s":"Nav 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 5 · Time","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Basics","s":"Nav 5 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Conversions","s":"Nav 5 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"International Date Line","s":"Nav 5 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Sunrise and sunset","s":"Nav 5 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Zone time","s":"Nav 5 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 5 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 6. Compass and Directions","s":"True, magnetic and compass north, variation and deviation, and how to convert a heading from compass to true and back without mixing up the signs.","h":"chapters/nav-06.html","k":"True · magnetic · compass · CDMVT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which north is which?","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#norths","k":"True north is the geographic pole; magnetic north is where a free magnet points; compass north is where your compass points in this aircraft. Variation separates true and magnetic, deviation separates magnetic and compass. Three norths Variation is the earth's fault; deviation is the aeroplane's."},{"t":"Play with it: three norths, one heading","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#sim","k":"Compass + deviation = magnetic; magnetic + variation = true (easterly +, westerly −). The aircraft never moves; only the north you measure from changes. Play: C D M V T Cadbury's Dairy Milk Very Tasty: C D M V T."},{"t":"East is least, west is best","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#rhyme","k":"Going from true to magnetic (or magnetic to compass): subtract east, add west. Going the other way (C → T): add east, subtract west.  East is least, west is best."},{"t":"Quick conversions","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#practice","k":"Write C D M V T across the page and fill in the gaps one step at a time; the sign comes from east/west, never from memory of the answer.  "},{"t":"Variation or dip?","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#lines","k":"Iso-GONAL / a-GONIC are about variation (the angle). Iso-CLINAL / a-CLINIC are about dip (the slope). Next chapter covers dip.  Gon = angle, clin = slope."},{"t":"Rules to know","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Order: C D M V T","s":"Nav 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"C → T: +E −W","s":"Nav 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"T → C: −E +W","s":"Nav 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Variation east: M < T","s":"Nav 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Deviation caused by: the aircraft","s":"Nav 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Zero variation: agonic","s":"Nav 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 6 · Compass and Directions","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Three norths","s":"Nav 6 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Variation and deviation","s":"Nav 6 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 6 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 7. Distances on the Earth","s":"Units of distance and how to convert them, great-circle distance along a meridian, departure along a parallel (d.long × cos lat), and the position-finding sums built on them.","h":"chapters/nav-07.html","k":"NM · km · SM · GC distance · departure "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Units to know by heart","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":"1 NM = 1852 m = one minute of latitude. 1 km = 0.54 NM; 1 SM = 0.87 NM. Longest to shortest: NM > SM > km.  Nautical beats statute beats kilo."},{"t":"1 NM: 1852 m","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":"6076–6080 ft"},{"t":"1 km: 0.54 NM","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":"3280 ft"},{"t":"1 SM: 0.87 NM","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":"5280 ft"},{"t":"1° latitude: 60 NM","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":""},{"t":"1′ latitude: 1 NM","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":""},{"t":"km definition: 1/10 000 equator→pole","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":""},{"t":"NM longest at: the poles","s":"Nav 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#units","k":""},{"t":"Where do the units come from?","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#unitpairs","k":"The NM is an angle on the earth (1′ of arc at the centre); the km was 1/10 000 of the equator-to-pole meridian; the statute mile is just 5280 ft.  "},{"t":"Why does a degree of longitude shrink?","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#departure","k":"Meridians converge, so the distance between them along a parallel (departure) is d.long in minutes × cos latitude: 60 NM per degree at the equator, 30 NM at 60°, zero at the pole.  Departure = d.long × cos lat."},{"t":"Distance calculators","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#calc","k":"Along a meridian: d.lat × 60. Along a parallel: d.long′ × cos lat. Turn km into NM (÷ 1.852) before turning NM into degrees.  "},{"t":"Departure","s":"Nav 7 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"d.long from a distance flown E/W","s":"Nav 7 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"km to degrees of latitude","s":"Nav 7 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"Fly N, E, S, W 600 NM each: where do you end up?","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#square","k":"The east leg is flown at 60°N, where 600 NM is 20° of longitude; the west leg at 50°N, where 600 NM is only 15.6°. You end 4.4° (171 NM) east of the start. The square that does not close Equal miles, unequal degrees."},{"t":"Traps in distance sums","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#traps","k":"Over the pole add the two co-latitudes. Across 180° convert the longitude name. Equal legs east and west at different latitudes never cancel.  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 7 · Distances on the Earth","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Units","s":"Nav 7 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Great circle distance along a meridian (or the equator)","s":"Nav 7 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Departure (rhumb-line distance along a parallel)","s":"Nav 7 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 7 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 8. Magnetism and Compasses","s":"The earth's magnetic field (H, Z, dip), aircraft hard and soft iron, deviation coefficients A, B and C, compass swinging, acceleration and turning errors, and the direct-reading and remote-indicating compasses.","h":"chapters/nav-08.html","k":"Dip · deviation coefficients · compass errors · slaved compass "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Dip, H and Z","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#dip","k":"The total field splits into H (horizontal, the part that points the compass) and Z (vertical). At the magnetic equator dip is 0° and H is greatest; at the magnetic poles dip is 90° and H is zero.  H for heading-help: biggest at the equator, gone at the pole."},{"t":"Hard iron or soft iron?","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#iron","k":"Hard iron is permanently magnetised (P, Q, R) and does not change with heading or latitude. Soft iron is magnetised by the earth's field (cZ, fZ), so it changes with latitude and vanishes at the magnetic equator.  Hard keeps it, soft borrows it."},{"t":"Coefficients A, B and C","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#coeff","k":"A is the same on every heading; B is biggest on east/west and is corrected on east; C is biggest on north/south and is corrected on north. Total deviation = A + B sin hdg + C cos hdg.  B for Broadside (E/W); C for Course north (N/S)."},{"t":"When must the compass be swung?","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#swing","k":"Swing the compass whenever its deviation may have changed: new compass or aircraft, big latitude change, lightning, magnetic cargo, a long time on one heading, and at the set intervals.  New, moved, struck, loaded, parked, due."},{"t":"What must a direct-reading compass do, and how is it tested?","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#drc-req","k":"A direct-reading compass must stay horizontal (magnets hung below the pivot), be dead beat (liquid, damping filaments, short strong magnets) and be sensitive (strong magnets, iridium pivot). Test it for bubbles, pivot friction (±10–15° returns within 2°) and damping (90° deflection returns through 85° in about 6.5–8.5 s). What a good compass needs Level, dead beat, sensitive."},{"t":"Which way does the compass swing? (NH)","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#apde","k":"Acceleration errors are greatest on east/west headings and nil on north/south. Accelerate → apparent turn towards the nearer pole; decelerate → towards the equator.  APDE: Accelerate Pole, Decelerate Equator."},{"t":"Turning errors","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#turning","k":"Turning errors are greatest turning onto north or south. Through the nearer pole the compass is sluggish, so roll out early; through the equator side it is lively, so roll out late.  APPS: Approaching the Pole, the compass is Sluggish."},{"t":"Remote-indicating (slaved) compass","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#slaved","k":"A slaved compass joins a flux valve (which senses the magnetic meridian) to a gyro (which gives a steady card). It works to higher latitudes and almost removes turning and acceleration errors.  Flux finds north, gyro holds it."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Dip at magnetic equator: 0°","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Dip at magnetic pole: 90°","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"DRC unreliable beyond: ~70° lat","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Flux valve spokes: 3 at 120°","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Slaving rate: ~5°/min","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Roll cut-out: ~10° bank","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Max deviation one item: 1°","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"All items together: ≤ 2°","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Swing interval (book): 3 months","s":"Nav 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 8 · Magnetism and Compasses","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Magnets","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Earth's magnetism","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft magnetism","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Coefficients","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Compass swing","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Errors due to dip (direct-reading compass)","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Direct reading compass (E-type)","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Requirements of a direct-reading compass (p.100–101)","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"DRC serviceability tests (p.101)","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Remote indicating (gyro-magnetic / slaved) compass","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 8 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 9. Dead Reckoning","s":"Navigating by calculation: headings, tracks, drift and track error, the triangle of velocities, runway wind components, airspeeds from IAS to TAS, and the time-distance sums that keep a DR plot honest.","h":"chapters/nav-09.html","k":"Triangle of velocities · drift · track error · runway winds "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Play with it: the triangle of velocities","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#triangle","k":"Air vector (heading/TAS) plus wind vector equals ground vector (track/GS). Change the wind and watch the heading swing into it and the groundspeed change. Play: the wind triangle One arrow for the aircraft, two for the ground, three for the wind."},{"t":"Which side of the triangle?","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#arrows","k":"Heading and TAS make the air vector (one arrow); W/V is the wind vector (three arrows); track and GS make the ground vector (two arrows). Know any four of the six and you can find the other two.  1 for air, 2 for ground, 3 for wind."},{"t":"Drift and track error","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#drift","k":"Drift is from heading to track; track error is from required track to track made good. Right of the reference = starboard, left = port.  Drift starts at the nose."},{"t":"From the ASI to TAS","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#speeds","k":"Each step corrects one error: instrument → IAS, position → CAS, compressibility → EAS, density → TAS. Mach = TAS ÷ local speed of sound.  I Can Eat Toast: IAS, CAS, EAS, TAS."},{"t":"What fraction of the wind is crosswind?","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#xwind","k":"Crosswind = wind × sin angle: about ½ at 30°, 0.7 at 45°, 0.9 at 60° and all of it at 90°. Headwind = wind × cos angle.  30-45-60: half, seven-tenths, nine-tenths."},{"t":"Runway components","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#rwy","k":"Runways are magnetic, so convert a true wind to magnetic first. θ = wind direction − runway direction; crosswind W sin θ, headwind W cos θ (negative = tailwind).  Make it magnetic first."},{"t":"Crosswind","s":"Nav 9 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#rwy","k":""},{"t":"Headwind","s":"Nav 9 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#rwy","k":""},{"t":"Compass heading chains","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#chain","k":"Track → heading: starboard drift is subtracted, port added. Then true → magnetic → compass with east least, west best.  "},{"t":"How do you lose time with a dog-leg?","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#dogleg","k":"Turn 60° off track, fly for the time you want to lose, turn 120° back, fly the same time again, then turn 60° onto track: an equilateral triangle that costs exactly one leg's time.  60 out, 120 back, 60 on."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"sin 30°: 0.5","s":"Nav 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"sin 45°: 0.71","s":"Nav 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"sin 60°: 0.87","s":"Nav 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Distress frequency: 121.5 MHz","s":"Nav 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Emergency squawk: 7700","s":"Nav 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Compressibility matters above: ~300 kt TAS","s":"Nav 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 9 · Dead Reckoning","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Terms","s":"Nav 9 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Triangle of velocities","s":"Nav 9 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Runway components","s":"Nav 9 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Time and DR position","s":"Nav 9 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 9 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 10. DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","s":"ISA and density, altimeter settings, pressure, true and density altitude, altimeter error problems, the 1-in-60 rule in both planes, and ILS deflection sums.","h":"chapters/nav-10.html","k":"ISA · QFE/QNH/QFF · PA · DA · 1-in-60 · ILS dots "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where are the ISA pressure levels?","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#isa","k":"ISA: 15 °C and 1013.25 hPa at MSL, cooling 1.98 °C per 1000 ft to −56.5 °C at 36 090 ft. Half the pressure (500 hPa) is gone by 18 000 ft.  Seven at ten, five at eighteen, three at thirty."},{"t":"Play with it: QFE, QNH and QNE","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#sim","k":"Each setting measures from a different datum: runway, sea level, or the 1013 hPa surface. Play: QFE, QNH, QNE QFE Field, QNH Nautical (sea), QNE Every flight level."},{"t":"Wrong setting: how much clearance is left?","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#hilo","k":"Fly into lower pressure or colder air without resetting and the altimeter over-reads: you are lower than you think. Play: wrong setting High to low (or hot to cold), look out below."},{"t":"Over-reads or under-reads?","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#overunder","k":"Flying from high to low pressure, or from warm to cold air, without changing the setting, the altimeter over-reads: the aircraft is lower than indicated.  "},{"t":"Altitude calculators","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#calc","k":"PA = elevation + (1013 − QNH) × 30. DA ≈ PA + 118 ft per °C above ISA. True altitude: 4% of the indicated altitude per 10 °C off ISA. Glide path: height ≈ distance × angle × 101.  Thirty a hectopascal, 118 a degree, 4 per 10."},{"t":"Pressure altitude","s":"Nav 10 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"Density altitude","s":"Nav 10 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"True altitude","s":"Nav 10 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"Glide-path height","s":"Nav 10 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#calc","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter settings","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#settings","k":"QFE reads zero on the field; QNH reads the field elevation (reduced to MSL with ISA); QFF uses the actual temperature (for met charts); 1013.25 gives pressure altitude and flight levels.  "},{"t":"The 1-in-60 rule, sideways and up","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#one60","k":"1° off over 60 NM is 1 NM off. Sideways: track error = off-track × 60 ÷ distance gone. Vertically: height (ft) ≈ distance (NM) × angle × 101.  One in sixty, both ways."},{"t":"ILS needle deflections","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#ils","k":"Full scale is ±0.7° on the glide slope and ±2.5° on the localiser; on a 5-dot display each dot is 0.14° and 0.5°. Beyond half-scale glide-slope deflection, go around.  Point seven up-down, two point five side to side."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"ISA MSL: 15 °C · 1013.25 hPa","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Density MSL: 1.225 kg/m³","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Lapse: 1.98 °C / 1000 ft","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tropopause: 36 090 ft, −56.5 °C","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ft per hPa at MSL: 27 (30 in sums)","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"500 hPa: 18 000 ft","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"3° GP: ~318 ft per NM","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ROD: GS × angle × 1.69","s":"Nav 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 10 · DR Elements: Pressure and Altimetry","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"ISA and density","s":"Nav 10 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter settings and altitudes","s":"Nav 10 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"1-in-60 rule","s":"Nav 10 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ILS deflection","s":"Nav 10 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 10 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 11. DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","s":"Static and total air temperature, ram rise and recovery factor, the air data computer, the chain from IAS to TAS, Mach number and the speed of sound, and what happens to CAS, TAS and Mach when you climb or descend through different layers.","h":"chapters/nav-11.html","k":"SAT · TAT · ram rise · IAS→TAS · Mach "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Play with it: CAS, TAS and Mach in a climb","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#speeds-sim","k":"Pick a layer and the speed you hold. The held speed is the vertical line; watch whether the other two lean right (increase) or left (decrease) as you climb. Play: CAS, TAS, Mach Climbing in ISA: Coffee, Tea or Me (C-T-M) from left to right."},{"t":"SAT or TAT?","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#sattat","k":"SAT (static, OAT) is the temperature of the undisturbed air. TAT is what a probe reads after the air is brought to rest and heated by compression: always warmer, more so the faster you go.  Total is Toasted."},{"t":"Temperature and Mach formulas","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#tat","k":"Work in kelvin: SAT = TAT ÷ (1 + 0.2 M²). The speed of sound depends only on temperature: 38.94 √T kt, so TAS = Mach × 38.94 √T.  Sound only cares how warm it is."},{"t":"SAT from TAT","s":"Nav 11 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#tat","k":""},{"t":"Ram rise","s":"Nav 11 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#tat","k":""},{"t":"Speed of sound","s":"Nav 11 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#tat","k":""},{"t":"TAS from Mach","s":"Nav 11 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#tat","k":""},{"t":"Correcting the airspeed","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#chain","k":"Instrument error → IAS; position error → CAS; compressibility → EAS; density → TAS; wind → GS. At the same CAS, higher or warmer means a higher TAS.  I Can Eat Toast Gladly."},{"t":"Increase, decrease or constant?","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#which","k":"In ISA below the tropopause, climbing at constant CAS raises TAS and Mach; at constant Mach, TAS and CAS fall. In an isothermal layer constant Mach means constant TAS.  "},{"t":"Order of the lines on the book's graphs","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#order","k":"Left to right on the book's climb/descent diagrams: climbing in ISA or an isothermal layer C-T-M; descending M-T-C; climbing an inversion C-M-T; descending an inversion T-M-C.  Coffee, Tea or Me."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"LSS at 15 °C: 661.5 kt","s":"Nav 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"LSS at −56.5 °C: 573 kt","s":"Nav 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"LSS formula: 38.94 √T(K)","s":"Nav 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Ram rise: (TAS/87.1)²","s":"Nav 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Compressibility matters above: 300 kt TAS","s":"Nav 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"TAS rule of thumb: +2% per 1000 ft","s":"Nav 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 11 · DR Elements: Temperature and Airspeed","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"SAT and TAT","s":"Nav 11 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airspeeds","s":"Nav 11 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mach number","s":"Nav 11 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Climbs and descents (book's graphs)","s":"Nav 11 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 11 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 12. PNR, PET and Range","s":"How far you can go and still get back (PNR), where it is equally quick to go on or turn back (critical point), still-air range, and how wind, fuel and engine failure move each of them.","h":"chapters/nav-12.html","k":"Point of no return · critical point · SAR "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where are the CP and the PNR on a windy route?","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#cpfly","k":"The CP (equal time on or back) moves into wind: with a headwind out it is beyond half way. The PNR depends on fuel: here 7 h of safe endurance takes you 840 NM out and back. CP and PNR, animated CP creeps into wind; PNR follows the fuel."},{"t":"Calculator: PET and PNR","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#calc","k":"Both use H ÷ (O + H): distance to CP = D × H/(O + H); time to PNR = E × H/(O + H), distance = that time × O. PET / PNR calculator Home over the sum."},{"t":"What moves the CP and the PNR?","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#rules","k":"Wind moves the CP (into wind); fuel moves the PNR (in proportion). Still air gives the furthest PNR; any wind, from any direction, brings it closer.  Wind for the CP, fuel for the PNR."},{"t":"Does the CP move?","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#moves","k":"Only the groundspeeds out and home move the CP. Extra fuel, a beam wind or a heavier aircraft at the same TAS leave its distance alone.  "},{"t":"Formula card","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#formulas","k":"Safe endurance = (fuel on board − reserve) ÷ consumption. Still-air range = fuel available × TAS ÷ consumption.  "},{"t":"Safe endurance","s":"Nav 12 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#formulas","k":""},{"t":"Still air range","s":"Nav 12 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#formulas","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 12 · PNR, PET and Range","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Point of no return (PNR / PSR / radius of action)","s":"Nav 12 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Critical point (CP) / equal-time point (ETP / PET)","s":"Nav 12 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 12 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 13. In-flight Navigation","s":"Planning a navigation flight, picking and using visual checkpoints, fixing position with radio aids (RBI, RMI, VOR, DME), running fixes, 1-in-60 corrections, time-to-station sums and revising ETAs.","h":"chapters/nav-13.html","k":"Planning · map reading · fixes · 1-in-60 · time to station "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"The flight-planning sequence","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#plan","k":"Information first (rules, warnings, met), then charts and route, then the arithmetic (tracks, safe altitudes, TAS, headings, GS, fuel), a re-check, and alternates last.  Read, route, reckon, re-check."},{"t":"Which features make good checkpoints?","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#checkpoints","k":"A good checkpoint is big enough to see, small enough to pinpoint and unique. Line features along track help tracking; across track they help timing. Choose them 5–10 minutes apart.  Seen, small, single."},{"t":"Which fix is most accurate?","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#fixes","k":"Two position lines crossing at 60–90° from accurate aids (twin DME) give the best fix. With three lines, aim for 120° cuts; the centre of the cocked hat is the fix. A running fix takes the time of the last line.  Cut at right angles, trust the DME."},{"t":"RBI, RMI and VOR bearings","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#bearings","k":"ADF on an RBI: QDM = relative bearing + magnetic heading, and QTE uses the variation at the aircraft. A VOR radial is already magnetic at the station, so use the station's variation.  NDB: variation at the aircraft. VOR: variation at the VOR."},{"t":"Time to station and corrections","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#time","k":"Time to a beacon (min) = elapsed minutes × 60 ÷ degrees of bearing change. To regain track: track-error angle + closing angle, both from 1-in-60. Revise ETAs with the GS actually made good.  Sixty over the change."},{"t":"Time to station","s":"Nav 13 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#time","k":""},{"t":"Total correction (1-in-60)","s":"Nav 13 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#time","k":""},{"t":"Revised ETA","s":"Nav 13 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#time","k":""},{"t":"Which way do you join the hold?","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#hold","k":"Right-hand hold: take your heading minus the inbound course. From −70° to +110°: direct (a 180° sector). From +110° to +180°: teardrop (70°). From +180° to +290°: parallel (110°). Left-hand holds are the mirror image. Holding entries Sectors 180 / 70 / 110: direct, teardrop, parallel."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Best 2-line cut: 60–90°","s":"Nav 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Best 3-line cut: 120°","s":"Nav 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Checkpoints apart: 5–10 min","s":"Nav 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"45° bearing change: distance flown = distance to go","s":"Nav 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"At 60 NM: 1° of radial ≈ 1 NM","s":"Nav 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Off-track rule: 1 in 60","s":"Nav 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 13 · In-flight Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Pre-flight planning","s":"Nav 13 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Visual navigation (map reading)","s":"Nav 13 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Climb, descent and cruise","s":"Nav 13 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fixes and position lines","s":"Nav 13 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"1-in-60 corrections","s":"Nav 13 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 13 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 14. Mass and Balance","s":"How an aeroplane's mass builds up from basic empty to ramp mass, how the CG is found and why its position matters, the structural and performance limits, the traffic-load calculation, and the Indian weighing and load-sheet rules.","h":"chapters/nav-14.html","k":"Mass terms · CG · limits · traffic-load sums · Indian rules "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How the masses build up","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#stack","k":"BEM + crew/catering = DOM; + traffic load = ZFM; + take-off fuel = TOM; + taxi fuel = ramp mass. Useful load = traffic load + usable fuel.  Basic, Dry, Zero, Take-off, Ramp: each adds one thing."},{"t":"In the DOM or not?","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#inout","k":"The DOM is the aircraft ready for this operation minus usable fuel and traffic load: crew, their bags, catering, water and removable service equipment are in; passengers, cargo and usable fuel are out.  Dry = ready to go, but no fuel and no payload."},{"t":"Finding the maximum traffic load","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#limit","k":"Allowed TOM = lowest of: regulated TOM, regulated landing mass + trip fuel, MZFM + take-off fuel. Traffic load = allowed TOM − DOM − take-off fuel.  Take the lowest of three."},{"t":"Forward or aft CG?","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#cg","k":"A forward CG is stable but costly: heavier controls, more tail download, higher stall speed, less range. An aft CG is light and twitchy, with poorer stall and spin recovery.  Nose-heavy is safe but slow; tail-heavy is quick but dangerous."},{"t":"CG calculators","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#cgcalc","k":"CG = total moment ÷ total mass (arms aft of the datum are positive). To shift the CG: mass moved = total mass × CG change ÷ distance moved.  Moments over mass."},{"t":"CG position","s":"Nav 14 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#cgcalc","k":""},{"t":"Mass to shift","s":"Nav 14 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#cgcalc","k":""},{"t":"Indian rules and standard masses","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":"  "},{"t":"Crew: 85 kg","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":"75 + 10"},{"t":"Adult passenger: 75 kg","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Child 2–12: 35 kg","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Infant < 2: 10 kg","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Re-weigh > 2000 kg: every 5 years","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Re-weigh < 2000 kg: only after major repair/mod","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Load sheet kept: ≥ 4 months","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Weight schedule approved by: Regional Airworthiness Office","s":"Nav 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 14 · Mass and Balance","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Definitions","s":"Nav 14 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Weighing and records (India)","s":"Nav 14 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"CG position effects","s":"Nav 14 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Effects of increased mass","s":"Nav 14 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mass terms","s":"Nav 14 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 14 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 15. Performance","s":"Performance terms, the forces in a climb, the take-off flight path and its four segments, every V-speed, cruise and descent performance, glide, and the declared runway distances with stopways, clearways and displaced thresholds.","h":"chapters/nav-15.html","k":"V-speeds · take-off segments · declared distances · balanced field "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which piece of runway counts for what?","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#declared-hs","k":"TORA is the runway; ASDA adds the stopway; TODA adds the clearway; LDA starts at the (displaced) threshold. Runway: tap the parts Stop on the stopway, climb over the clearway."},{"t":"TORA, TODA, ASDA and LDA","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#declared","k":"Clearway adds to TODA only; stopway adds to ASDA only; a displaced threshold shortens LDA only (for landings towards it).  Clear-to-climb (TODA), stop-to-stop (ASDA)."},{"t":"The take-off climb segments","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#segments","k":"Segment 1 ends when the gear is up; segment 2 (to at least 400 ft at V2) is the limiting one; segment 3 is the level acceleration for flap retraction; the final segment reaches at least 1500 ft clean.  Gear, climb, clean up, climb on."},{"t":"Take-off speeds in order","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#vorder","k":"VMCG ≤ V1 ≤ VR ≤ VLOF ≤ V2. V1 is the go/stop decision speed; VR is at least 1.05 VMCA; V2 (at 35 ft) at least 1.1 VMCA and 1.2 VS.  Control, decide, rotate, lift, climb safely."},{"t":"V-speeds that get asked","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#vspeeds","k":"Learn the gear trio (VLE, VLO), the flap limit (VFE), the structural trio (VNO, VNE, VA) and the control speeds (VMCA highest with an aft CG, VMCG always below it).  "},{"t":"Better or worse take-off performance?","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#factors","k":"Dense air (high pressure, low temperature), a headwind and a lighter aircraft all shorten the take-off. Heat, height, tailwind, mass and failed antiskid lengthen it.  Hot, high, heavy: hopeless."},{"t":"Balanced field and V1","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#bfl","k":"Raising V1 shortens the take-off distance required but lengthens the accelerate-stop distance. The balanced field length is where they are equal: the shortest field for the conditions.  Balance go against stop."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 15 · Performance","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Terms","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Take-off flight path and segments","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"V-speeds","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cruise, ceiling, descent, glide","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Declared distances","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Factors","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 15 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 16. Flight Planning and Monitoring","s":"Why and how a flight is planned, when a flight plan must be filed and how early, what to do when the flight deviates from it, and the ICAO flight plan form item by item, with the book's worked Amsterdam–Lisbon example.","h":"chapters/nav-16.html","k":"ICAO flight plan · filing times · Items 7–19 "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"The flight-plan clock","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#times","k":"File no earlier than 120 h and no later than 60 min before EOBT. Delayed 30 min (controlled) or 1 h (uncontrolled): amend or refile.  120 hours early at most, one hour late at worst."},{"t":"Is a flight plan required?","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#need","k":"File a flight plan for any flight given ATC service, IFR in advisory airspace, flights across borders, and wherever ATS needs it for alerting/SAR or military co-ordination.  Controlled, advised, crossing, searched-for, intercepted."},{"t":"Look-alike terms","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#defs","k":"Filed = as sent; current = with clearances. IFR ETA is over the approach fix, VFR ETA over the aerodrome. EET is point to point; EAT is when you leave the hold.  "},{"t":"What must be reported","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":"Off track: correct at once. ATC-assigned speed not held: tell ATS immediately. Planned TAS/Mach off by 10 kt / M0.02, or an estimate off by more than 2 min: tell ATS.  "},{"t":"TAS change: ± 10 kt (19 km/h)","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Mach change: ± 0.02","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Time estimate: > 2 min","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Speed change in Item 15: 5% TAS / 0.01 M","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Points off-route: ≤ 30 min / 200 NM","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Destination alternates: max 2","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Item 7: max 7 characters","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"RPL: ≥ 10 occasions","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#deviate","k":""},{"t":"Controlled VFR flight, weather going below VMC","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#vmc","k":"Ask for an amended VMC clearance; failing that stay VMC and tell ATC (leave the airspace or land); in a control zone ask for special VFR; or ask for IFR.  "},{"t":"Changing IFR to VFR","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#rules","k":"Only the pilot can cancel IFR, with the words \"CANCELLING MY IFR FLIGHT\". ATS answers \"IFR FLIGHT CANCELLED AT (time)\" and never suggests it.  The pilot cancels; ATS only confirms."},{"t":"The form at a glance","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#form","k":"Seven items carry most questions: 7 (≤ 7 characters), 8 (rules/type), 9 (wake), 10 (equipment), 13 (EOBT), 15 (route), 16 (TEET + ≤ 2 alternates), 18 (indicators), 19 (survival, cross out what is missing).  Who (7) · how (8) · what (9, 10) · from (13) · way (15) · to (16) · extras (18, 19)."},{"t":"Items 8 and 9 codes","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#item8","k":"Item 8: I, V, Y (IFR first), Z (VFR first). Item 9 wake: Heavy ≥ 136 t, Medium 7–136 t, Light ≤ 7 t.  "},{"t":"Read an Item 15 route","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#item15","k":"Item 15 starts with speed and level, then route designators and points; a \"/\" after a point gives a new speed and level from there, and VFR/IFR after a point changes the rules.  "},{"t":"Speed and level formats","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#levels","k":"Speed: K + 4 (km/h), N + 4 (kt), M + 3 (Mach). Level: F + 3, S + 4, A + 3, M + 4, or VFR.  "},{"t":"Item 19: which box?","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#item19","k":"Cross out what you do NOT carry: R/ U (243.0), V (121.5), E (ELT); S/ P D M J; J/ L F.  "},{"t":"Item 19: what are you carrying?","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#survival","k":"R/ radios: U 243.0 MHz, V 121.5 MHz, E ELT. S/ survival kit: P polar, D desert, M maritime, J jungle. J/ life jackets: L lights, F fluorescein, U/V radio.  Radios, kit, jackets: cross out what is missing."},{"t":"Indian rules in numbers","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":"  "},{"t":"File before EOBT: ≥ 60 min","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Briefing records kept: 6 months","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Aerodrome vicinity (no ATZ): 5 NM, 3000 ft AGL","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Non-scheduled into India: YA No. / AOR No. in Item 18","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Club flights in ATZ: may file by phone","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":""},{"t":"FIR boundary times: Item 18 EET/","s":"Nav 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 16 · Flight Planning and Monitoring","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Why plan (p.277–278)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Principles of fuel planning (p.278)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"EDTO / ETOPS (Annex 6)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Definitions (Annex 2 / Doc 4444)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"When a flight plan is required","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Adherence, changes and deviations","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Weather below VMC (controlled VFR flight)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Changing flight rules","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Closing a flight plan","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"India: filing flight plans","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Repetitive flight plans (RPLs)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Submission","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Completing the form: general rules","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 7 — Aircraft identification (max 7 characters, no hyphens or symbols)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 8 — Flight rules and type of flight (1–2 characters)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 9 — Number, type, wake category","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 10 — Equipment and capabilities","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 13 — Departure aerodrome and time (8 characters)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 15 — Route","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 16 — Destination, TEET, alternates","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 18 — Other information","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Item 19 — Supplementary information","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Book example (Amsterdam → Lisbon)","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 16 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 17. Air Data Systems","s":"Where the pressure instruments get their air: the pitot tube and static ports, what the alternate static source does to each instrument, the air data computer (sensors, transducers, computer, outputs) and the errors common to all pressure instruments.","h":"chapters/nav-17.html","k":"pitot-static · alternate static · ADC · CADC errors "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which pressure feeds which instrument","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#ps","k":"Pitot (ram) pressure goes only to the ASI. Static pressure goes to the ASI, the altimeter and the VSI. The pitot opening faces the airflow; the static port lies parallel to it.  Pitot: one customer. Static: everyone."},{"t":"Pitot tube vs static port","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#ports","k":"The pitot tube faces the airflow and senses ram pressure; it ices, so it is heated. The static ports lie flush and parallel to the airflow, one each side to cancel attitude effects.  "},{"t":"Something is blocked: what does the instrument do?","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#blocked","k":"Static blocked: the altimeter freezes, the VSI sits at zero, the ASI over-reads in a descent and under-reads in a climb. Pitot blocked (drain open or closed): only the ASI is affected.  SOD and SUC: Static blocked, Over-reads in Descent, Under-reads in Climb."},{"t":"Alternate static source inside the cabin","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#alt","k":"Lower static pressure: the altimeter reads high, the ASI reads high, and the VSI briefly shows a climb before settling back.  Cabin air is thinner: everything reads high."},{"t":"Inside an air data system","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#adc","k":"Sensors (pitot, static, temperature, AoA) → transducers (to electrical signals) → computer (TAS, Mach, corrected pressure and OAT) → outputs to displays and many other systems.  Sense, convert, compute, send."},{"t":"ADC: input or output?","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#outs","k":"In: pitot and static pressure, total air temperature, power, type data. Out: displays, autopilot, autothrottle, yaw damper, GPWS, pressurisation, recorder, altitude reporting.  "},{"t":"Errors in the CADC and its instruments","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#errs","k":"Position error is built into each CADC for its aircraft type; scale error is fixed with a camshaft. The ASI under-reads in thin air (density) and over-reads at speed (compressibility, ignored below 300 kt TAS).  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 17 · Air Data Systems","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"The pitot-static system","s":"Nav 17 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Air data system","s":"Nav 17 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"CADC errors (common to all instruments)","s":"Nav 17 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Errors specific to the ASI and VSI","s":"Nav 17 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 17 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 18. Altimeter","s":"The altimeter is a barometer that shows static pressure as height above a set datum. Three builds (simple, sensitive, servo) and seven errors, remembered as BLIP PBT, with the \"high to low, look out below\" rule for pressure and temperature.","h":"chapters/nav-18.html","k":"simple · sensitive · servo · BLIP PBT errors "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What is inside the case","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#case","k":"The altimeter is a barometer: a sealed, near-vacuum capsule inside a case fed with static pressure. As you climb, static pressure falls, the capsule expands and the linkage drives the needles.  Capsule empty, case static."},{"t":"Simple, sensitive, servo","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#types","k":"Simple: one capsule, gets insensitive up high and lags. Sensitive: capsules in series plus a vibrator. Servo: an induction (electromechanical) pick-off virtually removes lag; ±60 ft at sea level.  Servo = sensor talks electrically: no lag."},{"t":"The seven errors: BLIP PBT","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#blip","k":"Blockage, Lag, Instrument, Position, Pressure, Barometric, Temperature. Position error always under-reads; temperature error is the one no altimeter setting can fix.  BLIP PBT: seven ways to be wrong about height."},{"t":"High to low, look out below","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#hilo","k":"Into lower pressure or colder air at a constant indicated altitude, you sink: the altimeter over-reads. Warm air: you are higher than indicated. Position error: under-reads.  Hot to cold, don't be bold. Cold kills."},{"t":"Try it: QNH, QNE and temperature","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#sim","k":"Change the setting and the datum moves; change the temperature and the true height under the same reading moves. Play: settings "},{"t":"One static vent blocked: over or under?","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#slip","k":"With one vent blocked, only the clear vent counts. Slip towards the blocked side and the clear vent is on the lee side (low pressure): the altimeter over-reads.  Slip towards the blockage, read too high."},{"t":"Static blocked in an unpressurised aircraft","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#block","k":"First choice: the alternate static source. Last resort: break the VSI glass so the cockpit feeds the static system. Never break the altimeter glass.  Break the VSI, never the altimeter."},{"t":"Altimeter numbers","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Per hPa near MSL: ≈ 27–30 ft","s":"Nav 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"1 hPa at 10 000 ft: ≈ 50 ft","s":"Nav 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Servo tolerance at MSL: ± 60 ft","s":"Nav 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Unserviceable if error ≥: 50 ft (set to current setting)","s":"Nav 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Zebra flag shows: below 10 000 ft","s":"Nav 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 18 · Altimeter","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Purpose and principle","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Simple (service) altimeter","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Sensitive altimeter","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Servo altimeter","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Errors — \"BLIP PBT\"","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"From the questions","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 18 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 19. Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","s":"The ASI is a differential pressure gauge: pitot pressure in the capsule, static in the case, and the difference is ½ρV². The colour arcs mark the speed limits, six errors (BLIP DC) explain why it lies, and the Machmeter adds an altitude capsule to show TAS as a fraction of the speed of sound.","h":"chapters/nav-19.html","k":"P − S · arcs and radials · BLIP DC · SOD SUC PUD POC · (P − S) ÷ S "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What goes where inside the ASI","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#case","k":"Pitot pressure (dynamic + static) goes into the capsule and static pressure into the case, so the capsule moves by the dynamic pressure alone, ½ρV². That is airspeed. Capsule and case Capsule pitot, case static: the difference is speed."},{"t":"One capsule, or two and a ratio arm","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#inside","k":"The ASI has one capsule measuring P − S. The Machmeter adds an altitude capsule and a ratio arm, so it shows (P − S) ÷ S: TAS divided by the local speed of sound. ASI and Machmeter inside Machmeter = ASI + altimeter capsule."},{"t":"Why the scale needs square-law compensation","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#square","k":"Dynamic pressure grows with the square of airspeed, so without compensation the low-speed end of the scale would be cramped. The mechanism compensates so the scale reads evenly. Square law "},{"t":"From the needle to groundspeed","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#chain","k":"IAS + position/instrument error = CAS (RAS). CAS + compressibility = EAS. EAS + density = TAS. TAS + wind = groundspeed. At MSL in ISA, CAS = TAS. IAS to groundspeed I Can Eat Toast Gladly: IAS, CAS, EAS, TAS, GS."},{"t":"What each colour on the dial means","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#dial","k":"White VS0 to VFE, green VS1 to VNO, yellow VNO to VNE, red line VNE. On twins, a red radial for VMC and a blue radial for VYSE. VA and VLO are not marked. Arcs and radials White for flaps, green for go, yellow for smooth air, red for never."},{"t":"Where is it marked on the ASI?","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#marks","k":"Bottom of white = VS0, top of white = VFE, bottom of green = VS1, top of green / bottom of yellow = VNO, top of yellow = VNE (red line), blue radial = VYSE. Which mark? White for flaps, green for normal, yellow for smooth air, red for never."},{"t":"The six errors: BLIP DC","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#blip","k":"Blockage, Lag, Instrument, Position (under-reads), Density (under-reads going to thinner air) and Compressibility (over-reads above about 300 kt TAS). BLIP DC BLIP DC: six ways the ASI lies."},{"t":"Blocked: over, under, frozen or zero?","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#block","k":"Static blocked: under-reads climbing, over-reads descending (SUC, SOD). Pitot and drain blocked: over-reads climbing, under-reads descending (POC, PUD) — it acts like an altimeter. Pitot blocked with the drain clear: falls to zero. Blockages SOD SUC PUD POC."},{"t":"How the Machmeter works","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#mach","k":"Mach number = TAS ÷ local speed of sound. The Machmeter divides dynamic by static pressure, (P − S) ÷ S, through a ratio arm. Density and temperature cancel, so its only errors are blockage, instrument and position. Machmeter Speed of sound: temperature only."},{"t":"Which limit bites in a fast descent?","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#limits","k":"High up, the Mach limit (MMO) is reached first; lower down the IAS limit (VMO, a CAS) takes over. A fast descent is limited first by MMO, then by VMO below a certain level. The barber pole shows the limit that applies. VMO or MMO Mach high, VMO low."},{"t":"Mach regimes and key numbers","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":" Speed regimes "},{"t":"Subsonic: below M 0.75","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"Transonic: M 0.75 – 1.2","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"Supersonic: M 1.2 – 5","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"Hypersonic: M 5 – 10","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"Compressibility matters: TAS ≥ 300 kt","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"ASI under-reads TAS: ≈ 2% per 1000 ft","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"LSS at ISA MSL: 661 kt","s":"Nav 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#regimes","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 19 · Air Speed Indicator and Machmeter","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Principle","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"From IAS to groundspeed","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ASI markings","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Errors — \"BLIP DC\"","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Machmeter","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"From the questions","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 19 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 20. Vertical Speed Indicator","s":"The VSI compares static pressure fed straight into a capsule with static pressure that leaks slowly into the case. The lag between them is the rate of climb or descent. Lag is its main error; the instantaneous VSI removes it with dashpots, at the price of a false climb in steep turns.","h":"chapters/nav-20.html","k":"calibrated leak · lag · BLIP T · IVSI dashpots · leaks "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Direct static against leaking static","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#works","k":"The capsule sees static pressure at once; the case sees it through a calibrated leak and lags. Climbing, the case is left at higher pressure and squeezes the capsule (contracts) → needle up. Level, they equalise → zero. How it works Climb: capsule contracts. Descent: capsule expands."},{"t":"Inside and outside the capsule","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#what","k":"A VSI measures the difference between the pressure inside and outside a capsule, both static. No pitot pressure reaches it, so a pitot blockage has no effect. What it measures Static in, static out: pitot plays no part."},{"t":"The five errors: BLIP T","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#blipt","k":"Blockage (static blocked → zero), Lag (the main error), Instrument, Position, and Transonic jump (a brief false reading as a shock passes the static vent near Mach 1). BLIP T BLIP T: lag is the big one."},{"t":"High, low or zero?","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#leaks","k":"Partly blocked orifice: over-reads both ways. Case leak in a pressurised cabin: high in a climb, low in a descent. Case leak unpressurised: zero. Static pipe partly blocked: too low both ways. Pitot: no effect. Leaks and blockages Cabin pressure in the case pushes the needle up."},{"t":"What the dashpots fix and what they break","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#ivsi","k":"Dashpot accelerometers react at once to a pitch change and pump the capsule, removing lag. But they also feel turn acceleration: above about 40° of bank the IVSI shows a climb, and it oscillates in turbulence. Instantaneous VSI IVSI: no lag, but climbs in a turn."},{"t":"What the IVSI shows through a 45° turn","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#turn","k":"Roll in: the dashpot piston is thrown out and a false climb shows. Hold the turn: it settles to zero. Roll out: a false descent shows briefly. IVSI in a steep turn "},{"t":"VSI numbers","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Ground tolerance: ± 200 fpm (−20 to +50 °C)","s":"Nav 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"IVSI turn error above: ≈ 40° bank","s":"Nav 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"100 kt on a 3° slope: ≈ 500 fpm","s":"Nav 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"ROD rule: TAS × 101 × tan slope","s":"Nav 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 20 · Vertical Speed Indicator","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Principle","s":"Nav 20 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Errors — \"BLIP T\"","s":"Nav 20 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Instantaneous VSI (IVSI)","s":"Nav 20 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Leaks and blockages (from the questions)","s":"Nav 20 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 20 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 21. Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","s":"A fast, rim-heavy rotor keeps its axis fixed in space (rigidity) and answers a push 90° later (precession). The artificial horizon and DGI use rigidity; the turn indicator uses precession. Each has its own errors: the AH climbs in turns, the DGI drifts 15 sin lat per hour, the turn needle over-reads with high RPM.","h":"chapters/nav-21.html","k":"rigidity · precession · AH · DGI · turn and slip · ROGOTU "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"The two properties every gyro has","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#props","k":"Rigidity: the spin axis holds its direction in space, more so with higher RPM, more mass and mass at the rim. Precession: a push on the rim takes effect 90° on in the direction of rotation, at a rate proportional to the force and inversely to RPM. Rigidity and precession Fast and heavy at the rim = rigid. Push it and it moves 90° later."},{"t":"Space, tied, earth or rate?","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#types","k":"Space gyro: free, fixed to space (INS). Tied gyro: held by an external force (DGI). Earth gyro: held by gravity (AH). Rate gyro: one degree of freedom, measures rate (turn indicator). Degrees of freedom = number of gimbals. Four gyro types AH vertical earth (AVE2), DGI horizontal tied (DHT2), TSI horizontal rate (THR1)."},{"t":"Inside the artificial horizon","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#ah-limits","k":"A vertical-axis earth gyro: the inner gimbal gives pitch, the outer gimbal bank; the sky plate rides on the outer gimbal and the little aircraft is fixed to the glass. Old types topple beyond 55° pitch / 90° roll; modern ones work to 85° pitch and full roll. AH build and limits "},{"t":"What a classic AH shows through 360° of turn","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#ah-turn","k":"Air-driven AH (rotor anticlockwise seen from above): always a false climb in a turn. At 90° it under-banks, at 180° pitch error is greatest with bank nearly right, at 270° it over-banks, at 360° everything cancels. AH errors in a turn Under at 90, over at 270, all square at 360."},{"t":"What does the air-driven AH show?","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#ah-acc","k":"Acceleration (including the take-off roll): false climb and bank to the right. Deceleration: false descent and bank to the left. Any turn: false climb. AH acceleration error Speed up: climb right. Slow down: dive left."},{"t":"Why the DGI drifts","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#dgi","k":"Real drift comes from imperfections and friction. Apparent drift comes from the earth turning under the gyro (15 sin lat °/h) and from flying across meridians (GS/60 × tan lat). The latitude nut cancels earth-rate drift for one latitude. Check against the compass every 15 minutes. DGI and drift SONI: South Out, North In."},{"t":"How fast does a DGI drift with latitude?","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#drift-dial","k":"Earth-rate drift of a DGI is 15 × sin(latitude) degrees per hour: nil at the equator, 7.5°/h at 30°, about 13°/h at 60° and the full 15°/h at the poles. Hence the latitude nut and a check against the compass every 15 minutes. Earth-rate drift Fifteen sine lat, every hour."},{"t":"Rate-1 numbers","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":"Rate 1 is 3° per second, a full circle in two minutes. Bank for rate 1 ≈ TAS ÷ 10 + 7: 120 kt → 19°, 250 kt → 32°. Weight does not change it. Rate 1 and bank "},{"t":"Rate 1: 3°/s (2-minute circle)","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"Rate 2 / 3 / 4: 6 / 9 / 12 °/s","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"Bank for rate 1: TAS ÷ 10 + 7","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"120 kt: ≈ 19°","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"150 kt: ≈ 22°","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"250 kt: ≈ 32°","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"90° of heading at rate 1: 30 s","s":"Nav 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rate1","k":""},{"t":"Slip or skid?","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#nb","k":"The needle shows the turn; the ball shows balance. Ball inside the turn = slip (too much bank for the rate). Ball outside = skid (too much rudder). Step on the ball. Needle and ball Step on the ball."},{"t":"What is the aircraft doing?","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#nb-cases","k":"Needle and ball on the same side: turning that way with too much bank (slipping). Needle and ball on opposite sides: turning the needle’s way with too little bank (skidding). On the ground the ball swings opposite to the turn. Read the needle and ball Ball and needle together: slipping; apart: skidding."},{"t":"Over-reads or under-reads?","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#rogotu","k":"RPM too high → over-reads; G (load factor) high → over-reads; spring tension too high → under-reads. A slow rotor under-reads. ROGOTU "},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 21 · Gyroscope and Gyro Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Gyroscopic principles","s":"Nav 21 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Types of gyro","s":"Nav 21 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Artificial horizon (attitude indicator)","s":"Nav 21 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Directional gyro indicator (DGI)","s":"Nav 21 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turn and slip indicator and turn coordinator","s":"Nav 21 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 21 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 22. Inertial Navigation","s":"An inertial system is dead reckoning without the outside world: accelerometers measure acceleration, the computer integrates it once for velocity and again for distance, and adds it to the start position. The gimballed INS keeps its accelerometers level mechanically; the strap-down IRS does it with ring laser gyros and maths.","h":"chapters/nav-22.html","k":"integrate twice · STBY ALIGN NAV ATT · Schuler 84.4 min · RLG and dither "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"From acceleration to latitude and longitude","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#chain","k":"Accelerometers sense acceleration; one integration gives velocity (groundspeed), a second gives distance, added to the start position. The platform (or the maths in an IRS) keeps the accelerometers level so only horizontal motion is measured. Integrate twice Acceleration → velocity → distance: integrate twice."},{"t":"What goes in and what comes out","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#io","k":"In: start position and waypoints, GPS, barometric altitude, and TAS (only so it can compute wind). Out: attitude, true and magnetic heading, TAS and GS, position, wind and drift, vertical speed. Inputs and outputs TAS in, wind out."},{"t":"Bringing an INS to life","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#modes","k":"STBY warms up and takes the present position. ALIGN levels and finds the meridian by gyro-compassing: the aircraft must not move. NAV computes everything. ATT, after a failure, gives attitude only. OFF to NAV Stand by, align still, then navigate."},{"t":"Still available in ATT mode?","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#att","k":"ATT mode keeps acceleration, angular rates and attitude. Position, groundspeed and wind are gone, and heading must be entered by hand. ATT mode: what is left "},{"t":"Where INS errors come from","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#errors","k":"A level platform behaves like a pendulum as long as the earth’s radius: Schuler period 84.4 minutes, and its errors stay bounded. Drift is about 1–2 nm/h for old platforms and 0.5 nm/h for RLG systems. Error rate = radial error ÷ hours in NAV. Schuler and drift Schuler swings every 84.4 minutes."},{"t":"Work out an INS error rate","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#rate","k":"Split the position error into N–S (minutes of latitude = nm) and E–W (minutes of longitude × cos lat), combine by Pythagoras, then divide by the hours flown. Error rate sum "},{"t":"Gimballed INS against strap-down IRS","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#irs","k":"The IRS has no moving platform: ring laser gyros and accelerometers are bolted to the airframe and the platform is kept in software. No precession or topple, fast alignment, insensitive to g — but laser lock needs a dither motor, and it costs more. INS or IRS Laser lock? Dither it."},{"t":"Advantage or disadvantage of inertial navigation?","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#pros","k":"For: self-contained, continuous, all latitudes and weather, great-circle. Against: drifts with time, position can be inserted only on the ground, expensive, alignment hard at high latitude. For and against "},{"t":"INS numbers","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"Schuler period: 84.4 min","s":"Nav 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Position entry: to 6 seconds of arc","s":"Nav 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Battery back-up: 15 min","s":"Nav 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"ATT heading reset: ≈ every 10 min","s":"Nav 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"RLG rate range: 0.004–400 °/s","s":"Nav 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Alignment difficult above: 75° latitude","s":"Nav 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 22 · Inertial Navigation","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Principle","s":"Nav 22 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Modes and alignment","s":"Nav 22 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Errors and Schuler","s":"Nav 22 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"IRS with ring laser gyros (strap-down)","s":"Nav 22 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Advantages and disadvantages of inertial systems","s":"Nav 22 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 22 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 23. Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","s":"Three systems watch the aircraft’s height and space: the radio altimeter measures true height above the ground with a frequency-swept beam; TCAS listens to other transponders and tells you to climb or descend; GPWS combines radio height, sink rate, glideslope and configuration into spoken warnings.","h":"chapters/nav-23.html","k":"radio altimeter FMCW · TCAS TA and RA · GPWS modes 1–7 · EGPWS "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Measuring height with a frequency sweep","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-how","k":"The transmitter sweeps its frequency continuously (FMCW, 4200–4400 MHz). By the time the echo comes back from the ground the transmitter has moved on, so the difference between the sent and received frequencies is proportional to the time delay, and so to height. How a radio altimeter works Bigger frequency gap = higher."},{"t":"Radio altimeter numbers","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":"Range 0–2500 ft (above that, modulation cycles overlap). Accuracy ±2 ft up to 500 ft, then ±3% + 5 ft. About 1 W. Tested on the ground only. Radio altimeter facts "},{"t":"Band: 4200–4400 MHz (SHF), mean 4300","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"Sweep: ±50 MHz, 300 times/s","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"Range: 0–2500 ft","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"Accuracy 0–500 ft: ± 2 ft","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"Above 500 ft: ± 3% + 5 ft","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"Power: ≈ 1 W","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"Display steps: 5 ft","s":"Nav 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-facts","k":""},{"t":"True of a radio altimeter?","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-use","k":"It is self-contained and gives true height above whatever is directly below, to the lowest wheels in the landing configuration. It is not a pressure or density altitude, and it gives no warning of terrain ahead. What it is and is not Radio altimeter: wheels above the ground below, not above sea level."},{"t":"Mask, flag, test and DH","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#ra-ind","k":"The pointer hides behind a mask above 2500 ft, when switched off or on a transmitter fault; a flag shows for noise, airframe reflections or power loss. Press-to-test swings to a preset height. The DH light comes on below the set height, with a tone that rises from 100 ft above DH and stops at DH. The indicator Masked above 2500 ft, flagged on a fault."},{"t":"Four symbols, four levels of urgency","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#tcas-sym","k":"Hollow diamond: other traffic. Solid cyan diamond: proximate (within 6 nm and ±1200 ft). Amber circle: traffic advisory — look but don’t manoeuvre. Red square: resolution advisory — follow it. TCAS symbols Diamond, filled diamond, amber circle, red square."},{"t":"What each ACAS level can do","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#tcas-gen","k":"ACAS I gives traffic advisories only. ACAS II adds vertical resolution advisories, coordinated between two mode S aircraft. ACAS III would add horizontal advisories. An RA can be given against any aircraft with an altitude-reporting (mode C or S) transponder. ACAS I, II, III ACAS I tells, ACAS II tells you what to do."},{"t":"Which kind of advisory?","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#tcas-ra","k":"A TA is information. A preventive RA (\"MONITOR VERTICAL SPEED\", maintain vertical speed) stops a conflict developing. A corrective RA (\"CLIMB, CLIMB\", \"LEVEL OFF\") tells you to change your vertical speed, normally to 1500 ft/min (2500 for \"INCREASE\"). Preventive or corrective Preventive: keep it; corrective: change it."},{"t":"The seven GPWS / EGPWS modes","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#gpws","k":"1 sink rate, 2 terrain closure, 3 height loss after take-off (\"DON’T SINK\"), 4 too low with gear or flaps up, 5 below glideslope, 6 minimums (and bank angle in EGPWS), 7 windshear (EGPWS). Active from 50 to 2450 ft radio height. GPWS modes Sink, Terrain, Don’t sink, Too low, Glideslope, Minimums, Windshear."},{"t":"What to do when it speaks","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#gpws-act","k":"A caution (alert): stop or reduce the descent, work out why, act, inform if necessary. A warning: maximum power and climb at the pitch for the stick shaker (maximum gradient), then tell ATC. EGPWS adds terrain ahead from a database and the aircraft’s position. Alert or warning Caution: think and correct. Warning: full power and climb."},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 23 · Altitude Alert and Warning Systems","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Radio (radar) altimeter — RADALT","s":"Nav 23 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"TCAS / ACAS","s":"Nav 23 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"GPWS","s":"Nav 23 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 23 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 24. Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","s":"Engine instruments measure pressure (capsules and Bourdon tubes), temperature (thermocouples, resistance and vapour pressure), RPM (drag cups, generators, probes), fuel flow and quantity (vanes, impellers, capacitance probes) and torque. Colour arcs mark the limits.","h":"chapters/nav-24.html","k":"Bourdon tube · thermocouple · drag-cup tacho · fuel flow · capacitance gauge · torque "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Capsule or Bourdon tube?","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#press","k":"Diaphragms, capsules and bellows measure low pressures; the Bourdon tube, an oval-section curved tube that straightens under pressure, measures higher ones. The two principal direct-reading types are Bourdon tube and capsule. Measuring pressure Bourdon for the big pressures."},{"t":"What the colours mean on engine gauges","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#arcs","k":"Green normal, amber caution, red limits everywhere. Carburettor air temperature: yellow −10 to +15 °C is the icing band, red above +40 °C is detonation. MAP: blue and green are lean and rich ranges, the red radial is maximum take-off MAP. Colour arcs Yellow on the carb gauge = ice."},{"t":"Which method does each instrument use?","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#temp","k":"Thermocouples (two dissimilar metals, no power needed) read EGT and CHT. Oil and carburettor air temperature use a resistance bulb with a ratio meter, or a vapour-pressure Bourdon tube. Turbojets may use a radiation pyrometer. Reactance is not a temperature method. Six ways to measure temperature Hot things get thermocouples."},{"t":"Reading the outside air temperature","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#coat","k":"An air-temperature probe always picks up some heating from compression (ram rise). Correcting the indicated reading for known errors gives the corrected outside air temperature (COAT), the static air temperature used for navigation. Ram rise and COAT Probe reads warm; correct it to COAT."},{"t":"Three ways to count revolutions","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#rpm","k":"A mechanical tacho drags an aluminium cup with a spinning magnet. Generator tachos send a voltage (DC) or a frequency (AC, immune to wire length). Tacho probes count pulses from a toothed wheel — the turbofan choice. Tachometers Drag cup, generator or toothed-wheel probe."},{"t":"Flowmeters and contents gauges","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#fuel","k":"Independent flowmeters use a spring-loaded vane; integrated ones use a turbine impeller and an integrator to show fuel used too. Float gauges are only right in steady level flight; capacitance gauges average many probes and, compensated, read mass. Fuel flow and quantity Capacitance counts kilograms."},{"t":"Why turboprops need a torquemeter","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#torque","k":"Power = torque × RPM, and over-torquing damages the engine and reduction gear, so turboprop pilots need a torquemeter. Hydro-mechanical types turn torque into oil pressure; electronic types use strain gauges. Torquemeters Power = torque × RPM."},{"t":"Engine instrument numbers","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#nums","k":"  "},{"t":"MAP scale: 10–30 in Hg (50+ turbocharged)","s":"Nav 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"CHT thermocouple: up to 400 °C","s":"Nav 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"CAT gauge range: −50 to +150 °C","s":"Nav 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"CAT icing band: −10 to +15 °C (yellow)","s":"Nav 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"CAT detonation: above +40 °C (red)","s":"Nav 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 24 · Power Plant and System Monitoring Instruments","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Purpose","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Temperature","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"RPM (tachometers)","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fuel flow","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fuel quantity","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Torque","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 24 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 25. Basics of Electronic Displays","s":"The glass cockpit: the FMS computes the route and performance from a 28-day database; EFIS shows flying data on the PFD (EADI) and navigation on the ND (EHSI) in a strict colour code; EICAS (Boeing) and ECAM (Airbus) show engines and systems with their warnings.","h":"chapters/nav-25.html","k":"FMS · PFD/ND · EADI/EHSI · colour code · MAP/PLAN/VOR/ILS · EICAS/ECAM "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What the FMS is made of","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#fms","k":"Two units do the work: the FMC (computer and database) and the CDU (keyboard and screen). The database of airports, waypoints, navaids, airways, SIDs and STARs is updated every 28 days; a performance database holds the aircraft’s drag, thrust and fuel data. The FMS FMC thinks, CDU talks, database lasts 28 days."},{"t":"Which screen shows what","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#pfdnd","k":"The PFD (Boeing EADI) is the main flying instrument: attitude, speed tape on the left, altitude, flight director, ILS deviations, mode annunciators, radio altitude and DH, altimeter setting. The ND (EHSI) shows navigation: track, route, waypoints, weather radar, wind. PFD and ND PFD = piloting; ND = navigating."},{"t":"What each colour means on EFIS","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#colours","k":"White: scales and selected data. Cyan: inactive route and database items. Magenta: the active route, flight director and computed data. Green: engaged and valid. Amber: cautions. Red: warnings. Colour code Magenta is where you are going."},{"t":"How decision height shows on the EADI","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#dh","k":"The pilot sets DH on the ADI section of the EFIS control panel. Radio altitude shows digitally, then below 1000 ft becomes a circular scale that erases anticlockwise as you descend. At DH the DH display flashes yellow. DH and radio altitude Set DH on the ADI panel; it flashes yellow when you reach it."},{"t":"What each EHSI mode can show","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#modes","k":"MAP is track-up with the aircraft at the bottom and can show everything. PLAN is the FMC route, true north up, with no weather radar and no wind. Weather radar appears only in MAP and the expanded VOR/ILS (and expanded NAV) modes. MAP, PLAN, VOR, ILS PLAN: no weather, no wind."},{"t":"Which line is which?","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#routes","k":"Active route: solid magenta. Modified active route: white short dashes. Inactive route: cyan long dashes. Offset path: magenta dash-dot. Route lines Magenta is active, white is modified, cyan is inactive."},{"t":"Heading reference and other numbers","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#north","k":"EFIS heading is magnetic between 73°N and 65°S and true beyond. The trend vector predicts 30, 60 and 90 s ahead; the vertical deviation scale is ±400 ft. Magnetic or true "},{"t":"Magnetic heading between: 73°N and 65°S","s":"Nav 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#north","k":""},{"t":"Database update: every 28 days","s":"Nav 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#north","k":""},{"t":"Trend vector: 30, 60, 90 s","s":"Nav 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#north","k":""},{"t":"Vertical deviation scale: ± 400 ft","s":"Nav 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#north","k":""},{"t":"Expanded compass arc: ≈ 70°","s":"Nav 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#north","k":""},{"t":"Boeing EICAS against Airbus ECAM","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#eicas","k":"EICAS shows primary engine data and alerts on the upper screen and secondary data on the lower; maintenance mode is ground-only. ECAM shows systems as a checklist on the left and a diagram on the right, with normal, advisory, failure and manual modes. EICAS or ECAM EICAS: engines up top; ECAM: checklist left, diagram right."},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#tricks","k":"  "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 25 · Basics of Electronic Displays","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Flight management system (FMS)","s":"Nav 25 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"EFIS","s":"Nav 25 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"EHSI / ND modes","s":"Nav 25 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"EICAS (Boeing) and ECAM (Airbus)","s":"Nav 25 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 25 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nav 26. Basic Radio Theory","s":"Every radio aid is an electromagnetic wave doing something useful. Work out wavelength from frequency, place each aid in its band, and know how each band travels: as a surface wave hugging the ground, a line-of-sight wave, or a sky wave bounced off the ionosphere.","h":"chapters/nav-26.html","k":"λ = c/f · bands · AM/FM · ground and sky waves · skip and dead space "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Frequency and wavelength","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#wave","k":"Wavelength = speed of light ÷ frequency, with c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s. Double the frequency, halve the wavelength. Keep metres with hertz. λ = c ÷ f Three hundred over megahertz gives metres."},{"t":"Wavelength from frequency","s":"Nav 26 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#wave","k":""},{"t":"Frequency from wavelength","s":"Nav 26 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#wave","k":""},{"t":"Frequency, wavelength, phase, polarisation","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#terms","k":"Frequency counts waves per second, wavelength measures one cycle in metres. Polarisation is the plane of the electric field; around a transmitting aerial there are both an electric and a magnetic field. Wave terms "},{"t":"Amplitude, frequency and phase modulation","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#mod","k":"AM varies the carrier amplitude: simple receiver, but static and noise get in; VHF comms are AM. FM varies the carrier frequency: free of static but needs more power for the same range. Phase modulation is used by GPS. AM vs FM AM: Affected by Mush. FM: Free of Mush."},{"t":"Which band is each aid in?","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#bands","k":"NDB/ADF are LF/MF; VOR, localiser, markers and VHF comms are VHF (metric); glide path, DME, SSR and GPS are UHF; radio altimeter, MLS and weather radar are SHF (centimetric). Band ladder Each band is ten times the last: 3–30–300."},{"t":"LF/MF, VHF, UHF or SHF?","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#whichband","k":"Learn the frequency of each aid and its band follows. Edge cases: 295 kHz is still LF, the glide path is UHF even though its localiser is VHF. Sort the aids "},{"t":"Kilometric, metric, centimetric…","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#metric","k":"The name is the wavelength: VHF is metric (10–1 m), UHF decimetric, SHF centimetric, EHF millimetric. LF is kilometric, MF hectometric. Wavelength names "},{"t":"Surface, direct, reflected and ducted waves","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#ground","k":"Surface waves bend round the earth by diffraction, only below about 2 MHz, and fade faster over land and at higher frequency. VHF and up travel only in straight lines, so range is line-of-sight: 1.25 (√H₁ + √H₂). Ground waves Low frequency hugs the ground; high frequency needs to see you."},{"t":"Line-of-sight range","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#los","k":"Range (NM) = 1.25 × (√ station height + √ aircraft height), heights in feet. With the station at sea level that is 12.5 × √FL. LOS range Root, root, times one and a quarter."},{"t":"Maximum LOS range","s":"Nav 26 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#los","k":""},{"t":"Height needed for a range","s":"Nav 26 · formula","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#los","k":""},{"t":"Skip distance and dead space","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#sky","k":"Skip distance runs from the transmitter to the first returning sky wave. Dead space is the gap between the end of the surface wave and that first sky wave: no signal at all. Sky waves Skip starts at the Tx; dead space starts where the ground wave dies."},{"t":"The ionospheric layers","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#iono","k":"UV makes the ionosphere. D (≈75 km) and E (≈125 km) weaken at night, D vanishing, so waves refract from higher up: the skip distance grows at night. Ionosphere "},{"t":"What happens at night (same frequency)?","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#night","k":"At night the reflecting layer is higher, so the skip distance and dead space grow and a receiver can fall into the dead space. Cure: halve the HF frequency. Sun up, frequency up; sun down, frequency down. Day or night? Sun up, frequency up. Sun down, frequency down."},{"t":"Frequencies to know","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":" Aid frequencies "},{"t":"ADF / NDB: 190–1750 / 190–450 kHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"HF comms: 2–25 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"Markers: 75 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"Localiser: 108.1–111.95 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"VOR: 108.0–117.95 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"VHF comms: 118–137 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"Glide path: 329.15–335 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"DME: 960–1213 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"SSR: 1030 / 1090 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"GPS: L1 1575.42, L2 1227.6 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"Radio altimeter: 4200–4400 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"MLS: 5031–5091 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"Weather radar: 9375 MHz","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":""},{"t":"D / E layer: ≈ 75 / 125 km","s":"Nav 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#freqs","k":"D vanishes at night"},{"t":"Memory tricks","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#tricks","k":" Memory tricks "},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Nav 26 · Basic Radio Theory","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Electromagnetic waves","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transmission and modulation","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Frequency bands","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Properties and propagation","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Ionosphere","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Operating frequencies","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Nav 26 · notes","h":"chapters/nav-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 1. Definitions and Abbreviations","s":"The words the whole syllabus is written in: what counts as an aircraft, the parts of an aerodrome, declared distances, height versus altitude, the minimum altitudes and the emergency phases.","h":"chapters/reg-01.html","k":"Aircraft · aerodrome areas · declared distances · heights · minima "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which machines are aircraft?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#aircraft","k":"An aircraft is supported by reactions of the air, but not by air reacting against the ground (so a hovercraft is not one). Aerodynes get lift dynamically from moving surfaces; balloons and airships float. What is an aircraft Air holds it up, not air squashed against the ground."},{"t":"Small or large aeroplane?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#mass","k":"Small aeroplane: MCTOM 5,700 kg or less; large: over 5,700 kg. A seaplane uses water only; an amphibian uses land or water. Small vs large 5,700 kg splits small from large."},{"t":"Movement area or manoeuvring area?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#areas","k":"The manoeuvring area is for take-off, landing and taxiing and excludes aprons. The movement area is the manoeuvring area plus the aprons. Aerodrome elevation is the highest point of the landing area. Aerodrome areas MOVEment area: everywhere aircraft MOVE, aprons included."},{"t":"Name that part of the aerodrome","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#area-stamp","k":"Clearway is for climbing over, stopway is for stopping on, RESA catches overruns, the apron is for parking and loading, and the runway-holding position protects the runway. Name the area "},{"t":"What are the four declared distances?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#declared","k":"TORA is the runway for the ground run. TODA = TORA + clearway. ASDA = TORA + stopway. LDA is the runway available for the landing ground run (often shortened by a displaced threshold). Declared distances Clear to climb (TODA), Stop to stop (ASDA)."},{"t":"Other aerodrome words","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#aero-words","k":"A runway is wet with any visible water up to 3 mm; near-parallel runways converge by 15° or less; a temporary aerodrome is used for no more than 6 months. Aerodrome words "},{"t":"Altitude, height, elevation or flight level?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#vertical","k":"Altitude is measured from MSL (QNH), height from a chosen datum (QFE), elevation is a point on the ground above MSL, and a flight level is a pressure surface referenced to 1013.2 hPa. Below the transition altitude fly altitudes; at and above the transition level fly flight levels; the transition layer lies in between. Heights and levels Altitude: from the sea. Height: from the spot. Elevation: the ground itself."},{"t":"The minimum altitudes","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#minalt","k":"MSA gives 1,000 ft clearance within 25 NM of a radio aid. MEA adds nav-aid reception and comms to obstacle clearance; MOCA gives obstacle clearance only. MDA belongs to 2D approaches, DA to 3D approaches. Minimum altitudes MSA: 1,000 ft within 25 NM."},{"t":"DA/H versus MDA/H","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#datum","k":"DA/DH is the go-around decision point on a 3D approach, measured from the threshold. MDA/MDH is the floor of a 2D or circling approach, measured from aerodrome elevation. DA vs MDA DA decides; MDA is a floor."},{"t":"Instrument runway categories","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#cats","k":"Non-precision: visibility 1,000 m. Cat I: DH 200 ft, RVR 550 m. Cat II: DH 100–200 ft, RVR 300 m. Cat III: DH below 100 ft or none, RVR below 300 m. Runway categories Two hundred and 550 for Cat I; one hundred and 300 for Cat II."},{"t":"Which approach manoeuvre is it?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#proc","k":"A base turn joins non-reciprocal tracks; a procedure turn reverses onto the reciprocal; a racetrack loses height or sets up the inbound; CDFA flies the NPA final without levelling off. Approach manoeuvres "},{"t":"CTR, CTA, FIR and the rest","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#airspace","k":"A control zone starts at the surface, a control area above it. An FIR gives information and alerting; controlled airspace (A–E) gives ATC. Airspace words Zone from the ground, area from above it."},{"t":"Prohibited, restricted or danger area?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#pdr","k":"Prohibited: no flight at all. Restricted: flight only on conditions. Danger: hazardous activity at notified times, and only danger areas can lie over the high seas. P, R or D area "},{"t":"Flight-plan words and times","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#times","k":"The filed plan is as filed; the current plan includes later clearances. EOBT is when you start moving, EAT when you leave the hold. Ceiling counts cloud below 20,000 ft covering more than half the sky. Plans and times "},{"t":"The three emergency phases","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#phases","k":"INCERFA: uncertainty about an aircraft's safety. ALERFA: apprehension about its safety. DETRESFA: reasonable certainty it is in grave and imminent danger. Emergency phases Uncertain → Alarmed → Distressed."},{"t":"The four kinds of ELT","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#elt","k":"ELT(AF) is fixed, ELT(AP) is attached but removable, ELT(AD) deploys on impact, and ELT(S) is carried out and switched on by survivors. ELT types "},{"t":"Which State is it?","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#states","k":"Registry: whose register. Operator: where the operator is based. Design: who designed the type. Manufacture: where it was assembled. Occurrence: where the accident happened. Which State "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Small aeroplane: ≤ 5,700 kg","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Microlight 1 / 2 seat: 330 / 450 kg","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":"wing ≥ 10 m²"},{"t":"MSA: 1,000 ft in 25 NM","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ceiling counts cloud below: 20,000 ft","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":"more than half the sky"},{"t":"Wet runway: ≤ 3 mm","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Near-parallel runways: ≤ 15°","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Air-taxiing: < 20 kt","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cat I: DH 200 ft · RVR 550 m","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cat II: DH 100 ft · RVR 300 m","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"NPA runway visibility: ≥ 1,000 m","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Type A / B split: 250 ft","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Night: sun below horizon: 6°","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Temporary aerodrome: ≤ 6 months","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Petroleum in bulk: > 900 L","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"MDH threshold rule: 2 m (7 ft)","s":"Reg 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 1 · Definitions and Abbreviations","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Aircraft and their kinds (p.3–38)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aerodrome areas (p.4–36)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Declared distances (p.14)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vertical references (p.8–36)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Minimum altitudes (p.18–21)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Approaches and runways (p.16–19)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airspace and ATS (p.5–35)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flight plans and times (p.13–35)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Emergency and SAR words (p.8–31)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Operations and people (p.4–34)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Navigation words (p.12–38)","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 1 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 2. International Organisations and Conventions","s":"Where the law of the air comes from: the Chicago Convention and its key articles, how ICAO is built and what it publishes, the freedoms of the air, the treaties on crime and on compensation, and the Indian bodies that apply them.","h":"chapters/reg-02.html","k":"Chicago 1944 · ICAO · Annexes · freedoms · Tokyo, Hague, Montreal, Warsaw "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Start here: from Paris to Chicago","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#story","k":"Paris 1919 set the first rules and created ICAN; Chicago 1944 replaced it with 96 articles and created ICAO, a UN agency since 1947; each State then turns SARPs into national law. Paris to Chicago "},{"t":"Which article says what?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#articles","k":"Art 1 sovereignty over your own airspace; Art 5 non-scheduled flights may cross and make technical stops; Art 6 scheduled services need permission; Art 7 cabotage; Art 12 rules of the air (Convention rules over the high seas); Art 17 nationality = State of registry; Art 29 documents on board; Art 38 notify differences. Key articles One is Own sky; Twelve is the Rule-book; Twenty-nine is the Paperwork."},{"t":"The documents every international flight carries (Art 29)","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#docs","k":"Every international flight carries the C of R, C of A, crew licences, journey log book, radio licence (if radio fitted), passenger list and cargo manifest (if carried). Documents on board Register, airworthy, licences, log, radio, people, cargo."},{"t":"Whose certificate counts?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#licences","k":"Certificates and licences come from the State of Registry and other States must recognise them if they meet ICAO minimums, except a State may refuse licences given to its own nationals by another State. Whose certificate "},{"t":"How is ICAO organised?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#icao","k":"The Assembly (all States, one vote each) meets at least once every three years and elects a 36-State Council. The Council adopts SARPs, appoints the 19-member Air Navigation Commission and the Secretary-General. HQ is Montreal; 7 regional offices serve 9 regions. ICAO structure Three years, thirty-six seats, nineteen navigators."},{"t":"Standard, Recommended Practice, PANS or SUPPS?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#pubs","k":"Standards are necessary and differences must be notified; Recommended Practices are desirable. PANS apply worldwide, SUPPS to one region. SARPs, PANS, SUPPS Standard = shall, Recommended = should."},{"t":"The 19 Annexes","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#annexes","k":"Annexes 2, 5, 7 and 8 contain Standards only; the other 15 contain both Standards and Recommended Practices. The 19 Annexes Standards only: 2-5-7-8, \"two five seven eight, no room to debate\"."},{"t":"ICAO Docs worth knowing","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#docs-icao","k":"Doc 4444 is PANS-ATM, Doc 8168 PANS-OPS, Doc 7030 the regional SUPPS, Doc 8400 abbreviations and codes, Doc 7910 location indicators. ICAO Docs "},{"t":"What are the five freedoms of the air?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#freedoms","k":"Technical freedoms: 1st fly over without landing, 2nd land for non-traffic purposes (fuel, repairs). Commercial freedoms: 3rd put down traffic from home, 4th pick up traffic for home, 5th carry traffic between two other States. Cabotage (inside one foreign State) is not a freedom and may be refused (Art 7). Five freedoms 1 fly over · 2 fuel stop · 3 drop off · 4 pick up · 5 between strangers."},{"t":"Which freedom is it?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#freedom-stamp","k":"Overflight is the 1st freedom, a fuel stop the 2nd, dropping home traffic abroad the 3rd, collecting traffic for home the 4th, and traffic between two foreign States the 5th. Domestic carriage by a foreign airline is cabotage. Which freedom "},{"t":"Transit Agreement or Transport Agreement?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#agreements","k":"India ratified the Transit Agreement (freedoms 1–2) but not the Transport Agreement (freedoms 3–5), which it trades in bilateral agreements. Transit vs Transport "},{"t":"Which convention fights which crime?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#crime","k":"Tokyo 1963: offences on board and the commander's powers. Hague 1970: hijacking (unlawful seizure). Montreal 1971: sabotage and violence against civil aviation. Montreal Protocol 1988: violence at airports. Montreal 1991: marking plastic explosives. Beijing 2010: aircraft used as weapons, BCN and cyber attacks (not ratified by India). Crime conventions Tokyo Talks about on-board crime, Hague Hijacks, Montreal Mends the rest."},{"t":"Tokyo Convention: when is an aircraft \"in flight\"?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#tokyo","k":"For jurisdiction an aircraft is in flight from take-off power to the end of the landing run; for the commander's powers, from doors closed to doors opened. The commander may require crew help but only request passenger help. Tokyo: in flight Power to stop for jurisdiction; doors to doors for the commander."},{"t":"When may another State interfere with an aircraft in flight?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#tokyo-juris","k":"The State of registration has jurisdiction; another State may interfere with an aircraft in flight only if the offence affects its territory, nationals, security or flight rules, or a treaty requires it. Tokyo jurisdiction "},{"t":"Liability and finance conventions","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#liability","k":"Warsaw 1929 and Montreal 1999 cover the carrier's liability to passengers and cargo; Rome 1952 covers damage on the ground (not ratified by India); Cape Town 2001 covers aircraft finance. Liability treaties Warsaw and Montreal carry passengers; Rome the ground; Cape Town the money."},{"t":"Name the convention","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#conv-stamp","k":"Lost baggage: Warsaw. Damage on the ground: Rome. Unruly passenger: Tokyo. Hijacking: Hague. Bomb on board: Montreal. Name the convention "},{"t":"Withdrawing from a convention","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#denounce","k":"Withdrawal is by written notice to ICAO: 6 months for the security conventions, 90 days for Montreal 1999. Withdrawing "},{"t":"DGCA or AAI?","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#india","k":"The Ministry of Civil Aviation makes policy. DGCA is the safety regulator: it registers aircraft, licenses people, certifies aerodromes and operators. AAI (formed 1 April 1995) builds and runs airports and provides ATS, CNS aids and AIS. DGCA or AAI DGCA regulates, AAI operates."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Chicago Convention signed: 7 Dec 1944","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Articles: 96","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Paris Convention: 13 Oct 1919","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ICAO a UN agency: 1947","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ICAO members: 193","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Council: 36 States","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Air Navigation Commission: 19 members","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Assembly meets: ≥ once in 3 years","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Regional offices / regions: 7 / 9","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Annexes: 19","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":"Standards only: 2, 5, 7, 8"},{"t":"Notice after an amended Standard: 60 days","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AAI formed: 1 Apr 1995","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Montreal 1999 no-contest limit: 113,100 SDR","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Denounce security conventions: 6 months","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Denounce Montreal 1999: 90 days","s":"Reg 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 2 · International Organisations and Conventions","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Paris Convention, 1919 (p.39)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Chicago Convention, 1944: key articles (p.39–45)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ICAO (p.45–47)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ICAO publications (p.47–49)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Freedoms of the air (p.49)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Unlawful acts conventions (p.50–53)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Liability conventions (p.54–56)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Indian organisations (p.53–54)","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 2 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 3. Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","s":"How every aircraft carries its identity: the marks painted on it and how big they must be, the fireproof plate by the door, India's VT and VU marks, when DGCA cancels a registration, and what a lease means.","h":"chapters/reg-03.html","k":"Annex 7 · VT and VU · mark sizes · ID plate · cancellation · leasing "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where do the marks go, and how big?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#marks","k":"On a heavier-than-air aircraft the marks are at least 50 cm high on the wings and at least 30 cm on the fuselage and vertical tail, with every character in a group the same height. Lighter-than-air aircraft need at least 50 cm. Mark sizes Wings are wide: fifty. Body is slim: thirty."},{"t":"Is this mark tall enough, and where may it go?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#heights","k":"On heavier-than-air aircraft the marks on the wings must be at least 50 cm high; on the fuselage and vertical tail at least 30 cm. Lighter-than-air aircraft also need at least 50 cm. How tall? Fifty on the wings, thirty on the body."},{"t":"Who assigns what?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#who","k":"The ITU gives each State its call-sign letters; the State of Registry assigns the 1–5 character registration mark; ICAO assigns common marks (4YA–4YZ) to international operating agencies. Who assigns marks ITU gives the letters, the State the mark, ICAO the 4Y."},{"t":"Could this be a registration mark?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#banned","k":"Letters must not be confusable with the 5-letter International Code of Signals groups, 3-letter Q-code groups, SOS, or urgent signals such as XXX, PAN and TTT. Banned letters "},{"t":"The identification plate","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#plate","k":"A fireproof identification plate with at least the nationality and registration marks is fixed near the main entrance. ID plate "},{"t":"VT, VU or U?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#india","k":"India keeps its pre-independence marks: VT for civil aircraft and VU for the Air Force, then a hyphen and three letters (VT-EPC). Unmanned aircraft use U plus six alphanumerics. India's ITU block AT–AW is not used. VT, VU, U VT = Viceroy's Territory, a colonial leftover."},{"t":"What is on the certificate of registration?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#cofr","k":"The certificate of registration lists the aircraft type, constructor's number, year, marks, owner details, usual station and validity, plus lease details for a leased aircraft. C of R contents "},{"t":"Will DGCA cancel this registration?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#cancel","k":"DGCA may cancel a registration for false information, rule breaches, destruction or permanent withdrawal, an ended lease, a C of A lapsed 5 years or more, or the public interest. Cancellation "},{"t":"May the aircraft show it?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#display","k":"Owner names and logos are allowed if they cannot be confused with the marks; advertisements need DGCA permission; civil aircraft may not carry State aircraft markings. What may be shown "},{"t":"Lessor or lessee?","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#lease","k":"The lessor provides the aircraft; the lessee operates it, holds the authority and exercises operational control. Leasing firms registered in GIFT City IFSC are regulated by the IFSCA. Leasing "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Wing marks: ≥ 50 cm","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fuselage and tail marks: ≥ 30 cm","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Lighter-than-air marks: ≥ 50 cm","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Registration mark length: 1–5 characters","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Common mark symbols: 4YA–4YZ","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Indian civil mark: VT + 3 letters","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Air Force mark: VU","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Unmanned aircraft: U + 6 characters","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"India's unused ITU block: AT–AW","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"C of A lapse → cancel: ≥ 5 years","s":"Reg 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 3 · Aircraft Nationality and Registration Marks","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Annex 7 basics (p.69)","s":"Reg 3 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Size of marks (p.70)","s":"Reg 3 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Indian provisions (p.70–73)","s":"Reg 3 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Leasing (p.73)","s":"Reg 3 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 3 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 4. Rules of the Air","s":"The everyday rules of flying: who gives way, which lights you show, when and how to file, what to do when the radio dies, how much weather VFR needs, every signal from the ground or an interceptor, and which level to cruise at.","h":"chapters/reg-04.html","k":"Annex 2 · right of way · lights · flight plans · RCF · VMC · signals · cruising levels "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Start here: whose rules, and who is in charge?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#story","k":"The rules apply over India and to Indian aircraft everywhere. The PIC answers for them whether flying or not, has final authority, and may break them only when safety demands it. Whose rules "},{"t":"What may you not do with an aircraft in India?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#aero","k":"No negligent or reckless flying. Aerobatics only by a licensed pilot, with passengers' written consent, finishing by 2,000 ft AGL and never within 2 NM of an aerodrome below 6,000 ft. No formation flying and no towing of aircraft by civil aircraft in India. Drops, sprays and parachute descents only as permitted. Prohibited flying Aerobatics: 2 NM, 2,000 ft, written consent."},{"t":"Who gives way?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#row","k":"Head-on: both turn right. Converging: the aircraft that has the other on its right gives way. Overtaking: the one behind (within 70° of the other's tail) keeps clear by turning right. The aircraft with right of way keeps its heading and speed. Who gives way If it's on your right, it's in the right."},{"t":"Who gives way to whom when converging?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#row-cats","k":"Least manoeuvrable wins: power-driven heavier-than-air aircraft give way to airships, gliders and balloons; airships give way to gliders and balloons; gliders give way to balloons. Any power-driven aircraft gives way to one seen to be towing. Give-way ladder Balloons beat gliders beat airships beat powered planes."},{"t":"You or them: who gives way?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#row-stamp","k":"If the other aircraft is on your right, you give way; head-on, both turn right; the overtaker keeps clear; the lower landing aircraft and anything already landing have priority. You or them "},{"t":"Landing, taking off and taxiing","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#row-land","k":"Of two landing aircraft the lower goes first, but powered aircraft always give way to gliders. Taxiing aircraft give way to those taking off. On the ground, head-on means stop or turn right. Landing and taxiing Lower lands first; everyone gives way to gliders."},{"t":"What do the navigation lights tell you?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#navlights","k":"Red on the left wing and green on the right each shine 110° from dead ahead; the white tail light covers 140° (70° each side of the tail). A light holding a steady bearing means collision risk: see green and you have right of way; see red and you give way by turning right. Navigation lights Red to red or green to green: safe. Red and green together: head-on."},{"t":"At night you see… where is it going?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#lights-stamp","k":"Red and green together means head-on, white alone means it is going away, red and white means it crosses right to left, green and white left to right. Read the lights "},{"t":"Which lights, and when?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#lights-when","k":"From sunset to sunrise show navigation and anti-collision lights in flight; aircraft fitted with anti-collision lights show them by day too. On the ground also show lights for extremities and running engines. Which lights when "},{"t":"Flying at or near an aerodrome","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#aerodrome","k":"Near an aerodrome watch the traffic, make left turns unless told otherwise, and take off and land into wind. With a tower, keep a listening watch and get permission before moving. Near an aerodrome "},{"t":"When must a flight plan be filed?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#fpl","k":"File at least 3 hours (180 min) and no more than 120 hours before EOBT; RPA plans at least 24 hours before. If departure slips by 15 minutes or more, tell ATC. Close the plan with an arrival report as soon as possible after landing. Filing times Three hours before, five days at most, fifteen minutes late = call."},{"t":"Is a flight plan required?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#fpl-when","k":"A flight plan is needed for any flight receiving ATC, IFR in advisory airspace, flights across borders, and where the ATS authority requires it for SAR or air defence. Plan required? Controlled, advised, crossing borders, wanted for SAR."},{"t":"Changes you must report","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#fpl-changes","k":"Regain track at once if off it; report any change to assigned Mach or IAS immediately, a TAS change of 10 kt or M 0.02, and an estimate change of more than 2 minutes. Changes to report Ten knots, point oh two, two minutes."},{"t":"Controlled VFR flight: the weather is going below VMC","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vmc-lost","k":"A controlled VFR flight losing VMC asks for a clearance that keeps it VMC, or stays VMC and tells ATC it is leaving or landing, or asks for special VFR in a CTR, or asks for IFR. Losing VMC "},{"t":"Radio failure in IMC: what do you fly?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#rcf","k":"Squawk 7600. Hold the last assigned speed and level (or MFA if higher) for 20 minutes after a missed compulsory report (non-radar) or 7 minutes (radar), then follow the filed flight plan. Go to the destination aid, start descent at the last EAT (or ETA), fly the approach and land within 30 minutes. In VMC: stay VMC and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome. Radio failure 76 = radio nix. 20 without radar, 7 with radar, land within 30."},{"t":"How much weather does VFR need?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vmc","k":"At and above 10,000 ft: 8 km visibility. Below that: 5 km. Everywhere stay 1,500 m horizontally and 1,000 ft vertically from cloud, except at or below 3,000 ft AMSL (or 1,000 ft above terrain) in Class F and G, where it is clear of cloud with the surface in sight. VMC minima 8 up high, 5 down low; one-five-hundred metres and one thousand feet from the cloud."},{"t":"The VFR rules in numbers","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":" VFR numbers "},{"t":"CTR: no VFR take-off/landing without clearance if ceiling below: 1,500 ft","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":"450 m"},{"t":"…or ground visibility below: 5 km","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"VFR hours (India): SR −20 to SS +20 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"VFR ceiling unless authorised: FL 150","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"Never VFR above (RVSM): FL 290","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"Max seaward in controlled airspace: 100 NM","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"Over congested area: 1,000 ft above highest obstacle within 600 m","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"Elsewhere: 500 ft","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":"above ground or water"},{"t":"Semicircular levels apply above: 3,000 ft AGL","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":""},{"t":"Reduced visibility allowed (ATS): 3,000 m","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#vfr","k":"helicopters 1,500 m (India 1,000 m)"},{"t":"Special VFR: who, where, how much?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#svfr","k":"Special VFR is a VFR flight cleared inside a control zone below VMC, with ground visibility of at least 1,500 m (1,000 m for PC 1/2 helicopters). In India only pilots with an instrument or instructor rating may fly it. Special VFR Special VFR needs 1,500 m."},{"t":"IFR minimum levels and changing rules","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#ifr","k":"Without a published minimum, IFR flies 2,000 ft above the highest obstacle within 8 km over mountains, 1,000 ft elsewhere. Cancel IFR only for a stretch of uninterrupted VMC. IFR minimum levels Two thousand over mountains, one thousand elsewhere, within 8 km."},{"t":"Distress or urgency?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#distress","k":"Distress (grave and imminent danger): SOS, MAYDAY, red rockets fired one at a time, red parachute flare. Urgency (must land, no immediate help): landing lights or nav lights switched on and off repeatedly. Very urgent message about safety: XXX or PAN PAN. Distress or urgency Red means distress; flashing lights mean urgency."},{"t":"Interception: what does this signal mean?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#intercept","k":"Rocking wings and flashing lights from above, ahead and to the left, then a slow turn: you have been intercepted, follow me. Abrupt climbing break-away: you may proceed. Gear down, steady landing lights over the runway: land here. Squawk 7700, call on 121.5 MHz, and follow visual signals over any other radio instruction. Interception signals Rock = follow, Break = go, Gear = land."},{"t":"What the intercepted pilot does","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#intercept-do","k":"When intercepted: follow the interceptor, call on 121.5 MHz, squawk 7700, and if instructions conflict, obey the interceptor's visual signals or radio over anyone else's. If intercepted "},{"t":"Light signals from the tower","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#tower-lights","k":"Steady green: land / take off. Steady red: keep circling / stop. Green flashes: come back to land / taxi. Red flashes: aerodrome unsafe / clear the landing area. White flashes: land here and go to the apron / back to the start. Red pyrotechnic: do not land for now. Tower light signals Steady = do it now; flashing = get ready."},{"t":"What is the tower's light gun telling you?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#tower-cards","k":"The same colour means different things in the air and on the ground: steady green clears you (to land / to take off), steady red holds you (keep circling / stop), flashing green brings you back or lets you taxi, flashing red keeps you away, flashing white sends you to the apron or back to the start. Light cards Steady = do it now; flashing = get ready."},{"t":"What does the tower want?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#tower-stamp","k":"Green flashes on the ground mean taxi; steady green means land or take off; red flashes in the air mean the aerodrome is unsafe; steady red on the ground means stop; white flashes on the ground mean go back. Read the light "},{"t":"How do you acknowledge a light signal?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#ack","k":"Acknowledge a light signal in flight by rocking the wings by day or flashing landing lights twice by night; on the ground by moving ailerons or rudder by day. Acknowledging "},{"t":"Signals laid out in the signal area","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#ground","k":"Red square with two yellow diagonals: landing prohibited. One diagonal: take special care. White dumbbell: use only runways and taxiways. Landing T: land along the shaft towards the cross-arm. Double white cross: gliders flying. Black C on yellow: ATS reporting office. Ground signals Two stripes = no; one stripe = slow."},{"t":"Marshalling signals","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#marshal","k":"Learn the core marshalling signals: straight ahead, turn, normal and emergency stop, brakes, chocks, start and cut engines, slow down, hold, all clear. Marshalling Arms crossed overhead: stop."},{"t":"Rules for the signalman","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#marshal-rules","k":"Signalmen are DGCA-approved, wear fluorescent vests, use wands, bats or gloves (lit wands at night), and stand on the aircraft's left. Engines are numbered from the signalman's right. Signalman rules "},{"t":"Air defence clearance (ADC) numbers","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":" Air defence clearance "},{"t":"ADIZs in India: N, S, E, W, Central","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":"+ sub-ADIZ South East"},{"t":"Exempt local flights: 5 NM & 1,000 ft AGL","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":"around the ARP"},{"t":"Validity, scheduled: EOBT −15 / +45 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":""},{"t":"Validity, non-scheduled domestic: EOBT −15 min / +3 h","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":""},{"t":"International inbound: ADC from FIC: 10 min before","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":"entering Indian airspace"},{"t":"Same-day return ADC void if delayed: > 30 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#adiz","k":""},{"t":"Which level for my track?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#levels","k":"Magnetic track 000–179°: IFR at odd thousands (FL 50, 70 … 290, 310 … 410), VFR at odd + 500 (FL 55, 75). Track 180–359°: IFR even (FL 60, 80 … 280, 300 … 400), VFR even + 500. RVSM (1,000 ft) applies FL 290–410; above FL 410 levels go in 4,000 ft steps (east 450, 490; west 430, 470). No VFR in or above RVSM airspace. Cruising levels East is odd, west is even (Odd East, Even West: OE-EW)."},{"t":"Pick the correct cruising level","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#levels-stamp","k":"Eastbound (000–179°) IFR flies odd levels and VFR odd + 500; westbound (180–359°) IFR flies even levels and VFR even + 500. Pick the level "},{"t":"Hijacked: what level do you fly?","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#unlawful","k":"A hijacked aircraft keeps its track and level if it can; if it must deviate without contact, it flies 500 ft off the normal IFR levels where 1,000 ft separation applies. Hijack levels "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Overtaking zone: < 70°","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":"from the other's tail"},{"t":"Red / green light arc: 110°","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"White tail light arc: 140°","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aerobatics complete by: 2,000 ft AGL","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"No aerobatics within: 2 NM below 6,000 ft","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Formation (ICAO): 0.5 NM / 100 ft","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"File flight plan: ≥ 3 h, ≤ 120 h","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":"before EOBT"},{"t":"Delay to report: 15 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Time estimate error to report: > 2 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mach / TAS error to report: M 0.02 / 10 kt","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RCF non-radar: 20 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RCF radar: 7 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RCF land within: 30 min","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMC at/above 10,000 ft: 8 km","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMC below 10,000 ft: 5 km","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Distance from cloud: 1,500 m / 1,000 ft","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Special VFR: 1,500 m","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":"PC1/2 helicopters 1,000 m"},{"t":"IFR min level, mountains: 2,000 ft in 8 km","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"IFR min level, elsewhere: 1,000 ft in 8 km","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ATS clocks: ± 30 s","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":"data link ± 1 s"},{"t":"RVSM band: FL 290–410","s":"Reg 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 4 · Rules of the Air","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Applicability and responsibility (p.77–78)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"General rules (p.78–81)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Right of way (p.80–81)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lights to be displayed (p.81–82, 113–115)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Simulated instrument flight (p.82)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"At or near an aerodrome (p.82)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flight plans (p.83–84)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Time (p.84)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ATC clearances and deviations (p.85–86)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Radio communication failure (p.86–87)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"VMC minima (p.87–88)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Visual flight rules (p.88–89)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Special VFR (p.89)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Instrument flight rules (p.89–91)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Distress and urgency signals (p.91–92)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ADIZ and air defence clearance (p.92)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Interception (p.92–96)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Light signals from the tower (p.96–97)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Visual ground signals (p.97–98)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Marshalling (p.98–112)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cruising levels (p.112–113)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Unlawful interference (p.115–116)","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 4 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 5. Air Traffic Services","s":"Who looks after you in the air: the three air traffic services, how much protection each class of airspace gives, how Indian airspace and its codes are organised, what ATIS says, and how ATS raises the alarm when an aircraft goes silent.","h":"chapters/reg-05.html","k":"Annex 11 · airspace classes · FIR · ATC, FIS, alerting · ATIS · route designators "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What are the air traffic services?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#services","k":"ATS has five objectives: prevent collisions in the air (a) and on the manoeuvring area (b), keep traffic flowing (c), give useful information (d), and alert SAR (e). ATC meets a, b and c; FIS meets d; alerting meets e. Of the three ATC parts, only aerodrome control also meets objective b. Three services Control, Inform, Alert."},{"t":"Which objective is it?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#objectives","k":"ATS objectives: (a) prevent collisions in the air, (b) prevent collisions with obstructions on the manoeuvring area, (c) keep traffic flowing, (d) give useful information, (e) alert SAR. ATS objectives "},{"t":"How is airspace designated?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#designate","k":"FIRs give information and alerting; control areas and zones give ATC to IFR flights. A control area starts at least 700 ft up; a control zone extends at least 5 NM from the aerodrome. Designating airspace "},{"t":"What does each airspace class give you?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#classes","k":"A: IFR only, all separated. B: IFR and VFR, all separated. C: IFR separated from everyone; VFR separated from IFR only. D: IFR from IFR; VFR gets traffic information only. E: IFR controlled; VFR needs no clearance or radio. F: advisory for IFR. G: information only. 250 kt below 10,000 ft everywhere except A, B and IFR in C. Airspace classes A All IFR, B Both separated, C Cross-separated, D Don't separate VFR, E Easy for VFR, F Friendly advice, G Go as you please."},{"t":"Which class am I in?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#class-stamp","k":"No VFR: Class A. VFR separated from everyone: B. VFR separated only from IFR: C. VFR with clearance but no separation: D. VFR with no clearance or radio needed: E. Advisory: F. Information only: G. Which class "},{"t":"How is airspace around a busy Indian airport arranged?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#india-classes","k":"Around airports handling more than 20 movements an hour with primary and secondary radar, Class C runs from 4,000 ft to FL 285, Class D covers out to 30 NM, and airspace above FL 285 is proposed as Class A. VFR flights need a pre-departure clearance to enter Class C and stay responsible for terrain clearance. India around airports 20 an hour → C from 4,000 to 285, D to 30 NM."},{"t":"Class rules worth remembering","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#class-rules","k":"Classes A–E are controlled; E is never a control zone; where classes stack, the less restrictive one applies at the shared level. Class rules "},{"t":"Reading a location indicator: VIDP","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#location","k":"Four letters: the first is the AFSRA (V for India and neighbours), the second the State or FIR (A Mumbai, E Kolkata, I Delhi, O Chennai), the last two the place. ICAO divides the world into 22 AFSRAs. Location indicators Delhi is I (Indraprastha), Mumbai A (Arabian Sea), Kolkata E (East), Chennai O (Ocean)."},{"t":"How are things named on charts?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#idents","k":"NDBs use 2–3 letters, VORs 3, ILSs 4 starting with I, aerodromes 4-letter location indicators, and significant points 5 letters with two vowels. Chart identifiers "},{"t":"Reading an area designator: VA(P)-2","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#pdr","k":"Nationality letters of the FIR, then P (prohibited: never), R (restricted: conditions) or D (danger: active times by NOTAM), then a number not repeated in the State. P/R/D designators "},{"t":"TSA or TRA?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#tsa","k":"A TSA is sealed for one user; a TRA may be crossed with ATC clearance. Both are numbered 501–999 by FIR. TSA vs TRA "},{"t":"Does this flight get ATC service?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atc","k":"ATC is given to IFR flights in Classes A to E, VFR flights in B, C and D, all special VFR flights, and all aerodrome traffic at controlled aerodromes. Who gets ATC IFR in A–E, VFR in B–D, all special VFR."},{"t":"Whom does an ATC clearance separate?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#sep","k":"ATC separates all flights in A and B, IFR from IFR in C, D and E, IFR from VFR in C, and IFR from special VFR. Who is separated A and B separate everyone; C keeps IFR off VFR."},{"t":"Must you read it back?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#readback","k":"Always read back route clearances; anything about entering, landing on, taking off from, holding short of, crossing or backtracking on a runway; and runway in use, altimeter settings, SSR codes, level, heading and speed instructions, and transition levels. CPDLC messages need no voice read-back. Read-back items Numbers you fly and runways you touch."},{"t":"Air traffic flow management in India","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atfm","k":"Air traffic flow management balances demand against capacity. AAI runs a Central Command Centre in Delhi with flow positions at major units. Flow management "},{"t":"What does flight information service tell you?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#fis","k":"Flight information service gives SIGMETs, volcanic ash and radioactive release warnings, nav-aid and aerodrome changes, weather, and collision hazards in Classes C to G. Flight information "},{"t":"What order is an ATIS broadcast in?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis","k":"An ATIS covers one aerodrome, carries a letter designator in alphabetical sequence, is updated at once on a significant change, lasts ideally no more than 30 seconds, and is never sent on an ILS voice channel. You acknowledge it on first contact, and ATS still gives you the altimeter setting. ATIS order Name, Letter, Runway, Wind, Weather, Pressure."},{"t":"ATIS rules in numbers","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":" ATIS rules "},{"t":"Ideal length: ≤ 30 s","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":""},{"t":"Aerodromes per ATIS: 1","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":""},{"t":"Never broadcast on: ILS voice channel","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":""},{"t":"Preferred channel: discrete VHF","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":"or VOR voice"},{"t":"Cloud reported below: 5,000 ft","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":"or highest MSA if higher"},{"t":"Always repeated by ATS: altimeter setting","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#atis-rules","k":""},{"t":"Advisory service versus control","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#advisory","k":"Advisory service on Class F routes gives advice only, so the flight plan and its changes are not subject to clearance. Advisory service "},{"t":"When does ATS raise each emergency phase?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#alert","k":"INCERFA: nothing heard 30 minutes after a due report, or no arrival within 30 minutes of ETA. ALERFA: inquiries fail, or cleared to land but not landed within 5 minutes with no contact, or performance impaired, or unlawful interference. DETRESFA: distress probable, fuel exhausted, or forced landing likely or made. Alert phases Thirty minutes worried, five minutes alarmed, fuel gone = distress."},{"t":"Which phase would ATS declare?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#alert-stamp","k":"Thirty minutes without a report or 30 minutes overdue is INCERFA; not landing 5 minutes after landing clearance, or a hijack, is ALERFA; fuel exhausted or a forced landing likely is DETRESFA. Which phase "},{"t":"Who gets alerting service, and how?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#alert-rules","k":"Alerting service goes to all ATC flights, other known flights, and hijacked aircraft. The FIC or ACC collects the information and tells the RCC. Alerting service "},{"t":"Reading a route designator: UL301F","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes","k":"One letter plus 1–999 forms the basic designator. A, B, G, R are regional non-RNAV; L, M, N, P regional RNAV; H, J, V, W non-regional non-RNAV; Q, T, Y, Z non-regional RNAV. Prefix K (helicopter low-level), U (upper), S (supersonic). Suffix F (advisory only) or G (FIS only). Maximum six characters. Route designators ABGR old regional; LMNP new regional; HJVW old local; QTYZ new local."},{"t":"Flying the Indian route network","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":" Indian route rules "},{"t":"Clearance before entering controlled airspace: ≥ 10 min","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":""},{"t":"Join/cross a route: on direct VHF: 10 min before","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":""},{"t":"Join/cross a route: via en-route radio: 20 min before","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":""},{"t":"Foreign → Indian FIR boundary estimate: 10 min before","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":""},{"t":"Cross a route at: 90°","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":"at a reporting point, level for track"},{"t":"Reports on routes without reporting points: 30 min, then hourly","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":""},{"t":"MFA on ATS routes: 1,000 ft in 10 km","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":"each side of centre line"},{"t":"Border buffer: 15 NM","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":"unless on an ATS route or to/from an aerodrome"},{"t":"Designator length: ≤ 6 characters","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#routes-india","k":"preferably 5"},{"t":"Strayed or unidentified?","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#stray","k":"A strayed aircraft is known but off track or lost; an unidentified aircraft is seen but not known. Flying off ATS routes is prohibited in Indian FIRs unless ATC allows it. Strayed aircraft "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Speed limit (most classes): 250 kt IAS","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":"below 10,000 ft AMSL"},{"t":"CTA base: ≥ 700 ft","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":"200 m"},{"t":"CTR radius: ≥ 5 NM","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":"9.3 km"},{"t":"Class C (India): 4,000 ft – FL 285","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Class D (India): to 30 NM","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Class C airport traffic: > 20 / hour","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AFSRAs: 22","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"INCERFA: 30 min","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ALERFA after landing clearance: 5 min","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hijack squawk: 7500","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ATIS length: ≤ 30 s","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TSA/TRA numbers: 501–999","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aerodrome vicinity (no ATZ): 5 NM, 3,000 ft AGL","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Border buffer: 15 NM","s":"Reg 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 5 · Air Traffic Services","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Objectives and divisions (p.135–136)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Designating airspace (p.136–137)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airspace classes (p.137–139)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ATS units and FIRs (p.139–140)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Local and training flights (p.141)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Control areas and zones (p.141–142)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Emergencies (p.142)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ATC service (p.142–144)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flight information service (p.144–145)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ATIS (p.145–147)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Advisory service (p.147)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Alerting service and emergency phases (p.147–148)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ATS route designators (p.148–150)","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 5 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 6. Separation Methods and Minima","s":"How controllers keep aircraft apart without radar and with it: by height, by track, by time or distance along the route, by Mach number, and the exact minimum for each case.","h":"chapters/reg-06.html","k":"Doc 4444 · vertical · lateral · longitudinal · DME · radar · Mach number technique "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Who is separated, and what is composite separation?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#who","k":"Separation is given to all flights in A and B, IFR in C to E, IFR against VFR in C and IFR against special VFR. Composite separation may halve each element, by regional agreement only. Who is separated "},{"t":"How much vertical separation?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#vsm","k":"A nominal 1,000 ft below FL 290 and 2,000 ft at or above FL 290, except 1,000 ft between RVSM-approved aircraft in RVSM airspace (FL 290–410). Cruise climb is not allowed in Indian FIRs. Vertical minima Below 29: one. Above 29: two (unless RVSM)."},{"t":"Can I be cleared to the level another aircraft just left?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#vacate","k":"You may be cleared to a level another aircraft has reported vacating, unless there is severe turbulence, you share a holding pattern, or performance differences could close the gap. Vacated levels "},{"t":"Climbing in VMC keeping your own separation","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#vmc-climb","k":"By day in VMC, in Classes D and E and at or below 10,000 ft, a pilot may climb or descend keeping own separation from one other aircraft, if both pilots agree. Own separation "},{"t":"When are two aircraft laterally separated?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#lateral","k":"VOR: radials at least 15° apart and one aircraft at least 15 NM from the VOR. NDB: tracks at least 30° apart and one aircraft at least 15 NM out. GNSS (or VOR + GNSS): tracks 15–135° apart, one aircraft at least 15 NM (FL 010–190) or 23 NM (FL 200 and above) from the common point. VOR and NDB VOR 15/15, NDB 30/15."},{"t":"GNSS lateral separation table","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#gnss","k":"GNSS lateral separation needs tracks 15–135° apart and one aircraft at least 15 NM (FL 010–190) or 23 NM (FL 200+) from the common point. GNSS table "},{"t":"Same, reciprocal or crossing track?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#tracks","k":"Same track: angular difference under 45° (or over 315°). Reciprocal: between 135° and 225°. Crossing: everything else, 45–135° and 225–315°. Track types Within 45 = same; within 45 of opposite = reciprocal."},{"t":"Classify the two tracks","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#track-stamp","k":"Under 45° apart is the same track, 135–225° is reciprocal, everything else is crossing. Classify tracks "},{"t":"The time ladder: same level, same track","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#time-ladder","k":"15 minutes is the basic minimum. 10 minutes when nav-aids allow frequent position and speed checks. 5 minutes if the leader is at least 20 kt faster and both started from the same aerodrome or reported over the same point. 3 minutes if the leader is at least 40 kt faster. Time ladder 15 basic, 10 with fixes, 5 at +20, 3 at +40."},{"t":"Every time-based minimum","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#time-matrix","k":"The basic time minimum is 15 minutes, 10 with frequent fixes, 5 or 3 with a 20 or 40 kt faster leader. Reciprocal tracks need vertical separation 10 minutes either side of passing. All time minima Fifteen, ten, five, three."},{"t":"What is the minimum here?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#time-stamp","k":"15 minutes basic, 10 with good nav-aids, 5 with a 20 kt faster leader from a common point, 3 with 40 kt; 10 minutes either side on reciprocal tracks. Minimum here "},{"t":"DME / GNSS distance minima","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#dme","k":"Same level, same or crossing track (under 90°): 20 NM, or 10 NM if the leader is at least 20 kt faster. Climbing or descending on the same track: 10 NM with one aircraft holding level. Reciprocal: cleared through each other's levels only once positively passed and at least 10 NM apart. Needs direct VHF voice and the same DME station. DME minima DME doubles the radar number: 20, or 10 with a 20-knot lead."},{"t":"Conditions for DME/GNSS separation","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#dme-rules","k":"DME/GNSS separation needs direct VHF voice contact, distances from the same DME station, and simultaneous readings. DME conditions "},{"t":"Radar minima in India","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#radar","k":"In India radar separation is 5 NM, reduced to 3 NM at Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, Kolkata and Shamshabad. Radar in India Five miles, three at the big five."},{"t":"Mach number technique","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#mnt","k":"Turbojets on the same track keep an assigned Mach number (±0.01) so their spacing stays predictable. Basic MNT minimum is 10 minutes (leader faster by at least M 0.02), 9 minutes at M 0.03, 8 at M 0.04. With RNAV, 80 NM can replace the 10 minutes. Mach technique Point-oh-two buys ten minutes."},{"t":"Performance-based longitudinal minima","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#pbn-sep","k":"With RNP 10 or 4, RCP 240 and RSP 180, aircraft may be 50 NM apart; RNP 2 or 4 allows 30 NM; ADS-C reports set the interval. Performance-based "},{"t":"What is essential traffic?","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#essential","k":"Essential traffic is controlled traffic that is not separated by the minimum from a given flight; both flights must be told about each other. Essential traffic "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Vertical below FL 290: 1,000 ft","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Vertical at/above FL 290: 2,000 ft","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":"RVSM 1,000 ft to FL 410"},{"t":"VOR lateral: 15° · 15 NM","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"NDB lateral: 30° · 15 NM","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"GNSS lateral: 15 / 23 NM","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":"below / above FL 200"},{"t":"Same track: < 45°","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Reciprocal: 135°–225°","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Basic time: 15 min","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"With frequent fixes: 10 min","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"5 min needs: +20 kt","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"3 min needs: +40 kt","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DME same track: 20 NM","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":"10 NM if +20 kt"},{"t":"Radar (India): 5 NM","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":"3 NM at 5 radars"},{"t":"MNT: 10 min at M 0.02","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RNAV + MNT: 80 NM","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Composite: ≥ ½ each","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMC own separation: ≤ 10,000 ft, day, D & E","s":"Reg 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 6 · Separation Methods and Minima","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"General (p.169)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vertical separation (p.169–170)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Horizontal separation: three kinds (p.170)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lateral separation (p.170–172)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Track definitions (p.172)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Longitudinal separation by time (p.172–176)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Longitudinal separation by DME/GNSS distance (p.176–179)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Surveillance separation in India (p.179)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Summary (p.179–180)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mach number technique (p.180–181)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Performance-based separation (p.181–182)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Essential traffic (p.182)","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 6 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 7. Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","s":"How ATC spaces aircraft in the busy air around an airport: one departure after another, departures against an arrival, visual approaches, and the times you are told when you must wait.","h":"chapters/reg-07.html","k":"Doc 4444 · departures 1-2-5 min · departures vs arrivals · visual approach · EAT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How close can two departures be?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#deps","k":"One minute if their tracks diverge by at least 45° straight after take-off. Two minutes if the first aircraft is 40 kt or more faster on the same track. Five minutes if the second will climb through the first one's level on the same track. Departure gaps 1-2-5: one for a fork, two for a faster leader, five for climbing through."},{"t":"Minimum between these departures?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#dep-stamp","k":"Departures diverging by 45° or more need 1 minute; a leader 40 kt faster on the same track needs 2 minutes; climbing through a leader's level needs 5 minutes. Departure minima "},{"t":"When may an aircraft take off ahead of an arrival?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#dep-arr","k":"Complete instrument approach: departures in any direction until the arrival starts its procedure or base turn; then only in a direction at least 45° off the reciprocal of the approach, and at least 3 minutes before the arrival reaches the runway. Straight-in: any direction until 5 minutes out; 45° off the reciprocal until 3 minutes out (or until it passes a designated fix). Departure vs arrival Turn starts = 45° rule. Straight-in: 5 then 3."},{"t":"Can this departure go?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#dep-arr-flow","k":"Before the arrival turns inbound (or more than 5 minutes out on a straight-in), departures may go any way; after that only 45° off the reciprocal of the approach and 3 minutes before the arrival. Can it go "},{"t":"When can separation be reduced near an aerodrome?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#reduce","k":"Separation near an aerodrome may be reduced if the tower can see both aircraft, or the pilots can see each other and keep their own separation. Reduced separation "},{"t":"Must ATC pass this to a departing aircraft without delay?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#dep-info","k":"Significant changes to wind, visibility, RVR, temperature (turbine aircraft), and hazards such as CB, turbulence, wind shear, hail, icing, squall lines, freezing precipitation, mountain waves, dust storms or tornadoes; also changes in the aids needed for take-off and climb. Departure info "},{"t":"Visual approach: who asks, who is responsible?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#visual","k":"A visual approach is an IFR flight finishing all or part of the approach by sight of the terrain. Pilot or controller may start it (not the controller if the crew may not know the area). ATC still separates it from other traffic, but the pilot keeps terrain clearance. Visual approach ATC keeps the traffic apart; you keep the hills away."},{"t":"Visual approach: how much visibility?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#visual-vis","k":"A visual approach needs at least 2,800 m (Cat A/B), 3,200 m (Cat C) or 3,600 m (Cat D), or 5 km for a circling-only runway, and never below NPA minima. Visual app. visibility 2,800, 3,200, 3,600 by category."},{"t":"Expected approach time and holding","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#eat","k":"An EAT is given to any arrival delayed 10 minutes or more, preferably before it starts descent. It is revised if it changes by 5 minutes or more, and sent by the quickest means if holding will last 30 minutes or more. If you are held somewhere other than the IAF you get an onward clearance time instead. EAT and holding 10 to get one, 5 to change it, 30 to rush it."},{"t":"Delay that triggers an EAT: ≥ 10 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#eat","k":""},{"t":"Revise EAT if it changes by: ≥ 5 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#eat","k":""},{"t":"Quickest means if holding: ≥ 30 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#eat","k":""},{"t":"Pass EAT by: start of initial descent","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#eat","k":""},{"t":"Onward clearance time: time to leave an en-route hold","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#eat","k":""},{"t":"Maintaining own separation in VMC","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#own-sep","k":"Own separation in VMC is allowed by day in Classes D and E at or below 10,000 ft, from one aircraft, with fallback instructions in case VMC is lost. Own separation "},{"t":"What does approach tell an arriving aircraft, and when?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#arr-info","k":"On first contact: approach type and runway, met information, runway surface, aid changes. At the start of final: significant wind changes, wind shear, visibility/RVR. During final, at once: sudden hazards like a vehicle on the runway, wind variations, surface or aid changes, RVR changes. Arrival info First contact, start of final, at once."},{"t":"What counts as a significant wind change?","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#wind","k":" Wind change "},{"t":"Mean headwind component: 10 kt","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#wind","k":""},{"t":"Mean tailwind component: 2 kt","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#wind","k":""},{"t":"Mean crosswind component: 5 kt","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#wind","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Diverging departures: 1 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":"≥ 45° apart"},{"t":"Faster leader: 2 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":"≥ 40 kt, same track"},{"t":"Climbing through: 5 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Off reciprocal of approach: ≥ 45°","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Before arrival at runway: 3 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Straight-in: any direction until: 5 min out","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Visual app. Cat A/B: 2,800 m","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Visual app. Cat C: 3,200 m","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Visual app. Cat D: 3,600 m","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Circling-only runway: 5 km","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"EAT given if delay: ≥ 10 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"EAT revised if change: ≥ 5 min","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Own separation in VMC: ≤ 10,000 ft","s":"Reg 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 7 · Separation in the Vicinity of Aerodromes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Departing aircraft (p.195–196)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Departing vs arriving aircraft (p.196–197)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reduced separation near aerodromes (p.197)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Information for departing aircraft (p.197–198)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Arriving aircraft (p.198–199)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Own separation in VMC (p.199–200)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Expected approach time (p.200)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Information for arriving aircraft (p.200–201)","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 7 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 8. Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","s":"What the tower does for you between the gate and the sky: who in the tower talks to you, what it tells you and in which order, who it lets go first, how it keeps the runway clear, and what to do if you get lost on the ground.","h":"chapters/reg-08.html","k":"Doc 4444 · tower positions · taxi · runway incursion · priorities · lights · friction "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Who in the tower is responsible for what?","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#positions","k":"The aerodrome controller has the runway and the air around the aerodrome. The ground controller has the manoeuvring area except the runways. Clearance delivery gives start-up and ATC clearances to departing IFR flights. Tower positions Runway = Tower; Taxiway = Ground; Paperwork = Delivery."},{"t":"The tower prevents collisions between…","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#collide","k":"The tower prevents collisions in its airspace and circuit, on the manoeuvring area, between landing and departing aircraft, and with vehicles and obstructions. Collisions prevented "},{"t":"From the gate to the runway","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#startup","k":"Close the doors before calling, give persons on board and security status, take the taxi information, get an explicit cross or hold-short, finish checks, then take off on a clearance that names the runway. Gate to runway Doors closed before you call."},{"t":"Information before taxiing, in order","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#taxi-info","k":"Runway, surface wind, QNH (QFE on request), air temperature (turbine aircraft), visibility if under 10 km or RVR, correct time. Before joining the circuit or starting an approach: runway, wind, QNH. Pre-taxi info Really Windy Queens Take Very little Time."},{"t":"Rules for using the runway","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#runway-rules","k":"Report \"vacated\" only when the whole aircraft is past the holding position; tell ATC before taxi if you need to back-track; only one aircraft is cleared to land at a time. Runway rules Vacated means the tail is past the line."},{"t":"How close to the runway can you be held?","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#holding","k":"Never closer than the runway-holding position. Where there is none, hold 50 m from the runway edge if the runway is 900 m or longer, 30 m if shorter. No line-up and hold at the approach end while another aircraft is landing, until it has passed the point where you would hold. Holding distances Nine hundred metres: fifty back; under that: thirty."},{"t":"Something on the runway after a clearance was given","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#incursion","k":"Always tell the aircraft what and where the obstruction is. Cancel the take-off clearance if it has not started rolling. Tell a landing aircraft to go around. Runway incursion Tell, Cancel, Go around."},{"t":"Lost on the manoeuvring area: what first?","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lost","k":"An aircraft unsure of its position stops and at the same time tells ATS. If it knows it is on a runway, it tells ATS, gets off the runway via a nearby taxiway, then stops. A vehicle tells ATS, clears the area to a safe distance, then stops. Lost on ground Taxiway: stop & tell. Runway: tell, leave, stop."},{"t":"Must the tower tell you about it?","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#surface","k":"The tower reports works, rough surfaces, contamination, de-icing fluid, snow banks, temporary hazards, lighting failures and power failures on the movement area. Surface condition "},{"t":"Vehicles and emergencies on the ground","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lvp","k":"In low visibility procedures vehicles and people on the manoeuvring area are kept to a minimum and the ILS sensitive area is protected; emergency vehicles have priority over all other surface traffic. Low visibility "},{"t":"The order of priority","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#priority","k":"First an aircraft in an emergency that must land (engine failure, acute fuel shortage, total radio failure); then military fighters and live scrambles; medical flights; VVIP; SAR; large military formations; urgent military/RPA tasks; aircraft on big weather deviations; and aircraft diverting to an alternate. Priority order Emergency, Fighters, Scramble, Medical, VVIP, SAR."},{"t":"When may the next aircraft roll or cross the threshold?","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#land-dep","k":"A departure may not start its roll, and an arrival may not cross the threshold, until the preceding departure has crossed the runway end or started a turn, and every preceding landing aircraft is clear of the runway. A landing clearance is never given before the preceding landing aircraft has crossed the threshold. Runway clear Clear the strip, cross the end, or turn away."},{"t":"Take-off and landing clearances","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#clearances","k":"A take-off or landing clearance names the runway. Landing clearance waits until the preceding landing aircraft has crossed the threshold. An immediate take-off means taxi on and go in one movement. T/O and landing "},{"t":"Suspending VFR operations","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#vfr-susp","k":"Approach, the ACC, the tower or the ATS authority may suspend VFR; the tower then holds VFR departures and recalls local VFR flights. VFR suspended "},{"t":"When are the aerodrome lights on?","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lights","k":"Sunset to sunrise; by day when visibility is 5,000 m or less (India); on request; or whenever useful for safety. Lights may be switched off if they can be back on 1 hour before an arrival. PAPI/VASI runs day and night whenever its runway is in use. After a take-off, keep a runway lit: as long as needed at ATC aerodromes, at least 15 minutes elsewhere. Aerodrome lights Five km by day, one hour to relight, fifteen minutes after you go."},{"t":"Daytime use if visibility: ≤ 5,000 m","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lights","k":""},{"t":"Relight before an arrival: ≥ 1 h","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lights","k":""},{"t":"PAPI / VASI: day & night","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lights","k":"whenever the runway is in use"},{"t":"Runway lit after T/O (no ATC): ≥ 15 min","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lights","k":""},{"t":"Low-level wind shear: surface to ~1,500 ft","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#lights","k":""},{"t":"Wind shear reports and hot spots","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#shear","k":"A pilot's wind shear report is passed to later aircraft until it is in the ATIS, and to the met office. Hot spots are risky places on the movement area designated by the aerodrome operator. Wind shear, hot spots "},{"t":"Runway friction codes","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#friction","k":"Measured coefficient 0.40 or more is good (5); 0.25 or less is poor (1). Conditions are given for each third of the runway, the first third being the one you touch down on. Friction codes Forty is fine, twenty-five is terrible."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Hold from edge, runway ≥ 900 m: 50 m","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hold from edge, runway < 900 m: 30 m","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Visibility given if below: 10 km","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Circuit area: ≈ 4 NM","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":"joining traffic to 8 NM"},{"t":"Lights by day: vis ≤ 5,000 m","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Relight lead time: 1 h","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Runway lights after T/O: ≥ 15 min","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Good friction: ≥ 0.40","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Poor friction: ≤ 0.25","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft cleared to land at once: 1","s":"Reg 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 8 · Procedures for Aerodrome Control Service","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Functions of the tower (p.205–206)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Start-up, take-off and landing (p.206–207)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Traffic and taxi circuit (p.207–208)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Information from the tower (p.208–211)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other traffic and priorities (p.211–213)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Suspension of VFR (p.213)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Circuit, departures and landings (p.213–215)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aeronautical ground lights (p.215–216)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wind shear, hot spots, friction (p.216–217)","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 8 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 9. Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","s":"How radar and its cousins find you, prove which blip is you, how close they then let aircraft fly, how fast you may go near the airport, and how to work your transponder in Indian airspace.","h":"chapters/reg-09.html","k":"Doc 4444 · PSR, SSR, ADS-B, ADS-C, MLAT · identification · radar minima · transponder rules "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"PSR, SSR, ADS-B, ADS-C or MLAT?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#systems","k":"PSR sees the aircraft's echo with no help from it. SSR interrogates the transponder. ADS-B has the aircraft broadcast its own GNSS position to everyone. ADS-C sends the same data only to specific units under a contract. MLAT locates transponder replies by time difference of arrival and needs no extra avionics. Surveillance systems B for Broadcast to everyone, C for Contract with someone."},{"t":"Why ADS-B?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#adsb","k":"ADS-B shows pilots the same traffic as controllers, cuts runway incursions, covers remote areas cheaply, and allows more direct routes. Why ADS-B "},{"t":"Which radar service where?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#services","k":"Radar control is given in controlled airspace, radar advisory in Class F, radar flight information anywhere in the FIR. Vectors are magnetic headings. Radar services Control in controlled airspace, advice in F, information anywhere."},{"t":"What ATC uses surveillance for","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#uses","k":"Surveillance is used to vector departures and arrivals, resolve conflicts, help with navigation, handle radio failures, and catch route or level deviations. Uses of radar "},{"t":"How does ATC know which blip is you?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#ident","k":"With primary radar only: match the blip to your position report; identify you within 1 NM of the runway end just after departure; or ask for a turn of 30° or more and watch which single blip turns. With SSR: a verified discrete code, your Mode S identity, a code change, or SQUAWK IDENT. With ADS-B: your identity in the label or TRANSMIT ADS-B IDENT. PSR identification PSR: Report, Runway (1 NM), Rotate (30°)."},{"t":"Which identification method is it?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#ident-stamp","k":"A heading change of 30° or more is the turn method; a blip just past the runway end is the departing-aircraft method; codes and IDENT are SSR; TRANSMIT ADS-B IDENT is ADS-B. Which method "},{"t":"SSR alone, and transponder failures","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#transp-fail","k":"If a mandatory transponder fails in flight you may continue to the next landing; if it fails before departure you need ATC authorisation requested in the flight plan. Transponder failure "},{"t":"How close may radar let aircraft fly?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#minima","k":"ICAO standard 5 NM; may be reduced to 3 NM where capability allows; 2.5 NM between aircraft on the same final within 10 NM of the threshold. In India: 5 NM generally, 3 NM at Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, Kolkata and Shamshabad. Radar minima 5 normal, 3 good radar, 2.5 on short final."},{"t":"Speed control in India","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#speed","k":"Below 10,000 ft no more than 250 kt IAS. Arrivals below 10,000 ft within 15 NM of the aerodrome VOR/DME no more than 220 kt. ATC may add limits or lift them with \"No speed restriction\". Speed limits 250 below ten, 220 within fifteen."},{"t":"Below 10,000 ft: ≤ 250 kt IAS","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#speed","k":""},{"t":"Arrivals within 15 NM, below 10,000 ft: ≤ 220 kt IAS","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#speed","k":""},{"t":"To cancel: \"No speed restriction\"","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#speed","k":""},{"t":"When does surveillance service end?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#end","k":"Surveillance service ends automatically when an arrival is told to contact the tower; you are given your distance from touchdown first. End of service "},{"t":"Which code do I squawk in Indian airspace?","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#transponder","k":"Transponder on at all times in flight in all Indian FIRs. Departures use the code given on departure; international arrivals keep the code from the adjacent FIR; domestic flights keep the last assigned code; with no code assigned, squawk A 2000 before entering. On the ground, only from entering the runway for take-off until vacating after landing. Which code Squawk always on in Indian skies."},{"t":"Checking your Mode C / ADS-B level","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#modec","k":"Report your level on first contact with a radar unit. The controller compares it with the displayed level: tolerance ±200 ft in RVSM airspace and ±300 ft elsewhere. If within tolerance you hear nothing; if not, check your setting and confirm level, and if it still disagrees you will be told to stop Mode C. Mode C check RVSM 200, the rest 300."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Departing-aircraft ident: within 1 NM","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":"of the runway end"},{"t":"Turn method: ≥ 30°","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Standard radar minimum: 5 NM","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Reduced: 3 NM","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Same final, within 10 NM: 2.5 NM","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Below 10,000 ft: 250 kt","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Arrivals within 15 NM: 220 kt","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"No code assigned: 2000","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mode C tolerance RVSM: ± 200 ft","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mode C tolerance elsewhere: ± 300 ft","s":"Reg 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 9 · Use of ATS Surveillance Systems","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Surveillance systems (p.221–223)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Radar services (p.223–224)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SSR without primary radar; transponder failure (p.224)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Identification (p.224–226)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Separation and speed control (p.226–227)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Termination of service (p.227)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transponder procedures in India (p.227–228)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mode C / ADS-B level verification (p.228)","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 9 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 10. Aeronautical Information Services","s":"Where a pilot finds the official information about airspace, routes and aerodromes, how each kind of change reaches you (amendment, supplement, NOTAM or circular), and how the 28-day AIRAC cycle keeps everyone in step.","h":"chapters/reg-10.html","k":"Annex 15 · AIP, AMDT, SUP · NOTAM · AIRAC · AIC · PIB · Indian NOTAM series "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What is in the Integrated Aeronautical Information Package?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#package","k":"The package is the AIP with its Amendments and Supplements, NOTAM with PIBs, AICs, and checklists and summaries. AAI provides all of it in India except AICs, which DGCA publishes. Information package AAI publishes, except Circulars come from DGCA."},{"t":"Which document carries this change?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#which","k":"Permanent change: AIP Amendment. Temporary change lasting 3 months or more, or bulky text/graphics: AIP Supplement. Temporary short change or anything at short notice: NOTAM. Advance notice or explanation that fits neither: AIC. Which document Forever = AMDT, for months = SUP, for now = NOTAM, for your information = AIC."},{"t":"The three parts of the AIP","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#aip","k":"The AIP has three parts: GEN (regulations and services), ENR (en-route rules, airspace, routes, warnings) and AD (aerodromes). AIP India has two volumes. AIP parts GEN, ENR, AD."},{"t":"AIP Supplement rules","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#sup","k":"AIP Supplements carry temporary changes of 3 months or more on yellow pages at the front of each part, numbered per year, with a checklist at least monthly. AIP Supplements "},{"t":"Reading a NOTAM number","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#notam","k":"Each NOTAM has a series letter, a four-digit number that restarts each calendar year, a stroke, and the two-digit year. It deals with one subject and one condition only. NOTAMN is new, NOTAMR replaces, NOTAMC cancels. NOTAM number "},{"t":"NOTAM or not?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#notam-not","k":"Things that do not affect safe operations are not NOTAMed: routine apron or taxiway work, marking work when another runway is usable, harmless obstructions, partial lighting or comms failures with alternatives, no marshalling, broken signs, and parachuting in the listed cases. NOTAM or not No safety effect, no NOTAM."},{"t":"Special NOTAMs and trigger NOTAMs","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#special","k":"SNOWTAM reports snow, ice and water with a runway condition code per third; ASHTAM reports volcanic ash; a trigger NOTAM flags an AIRAC supplement in the PIB. Danger area activation needs 7 days' notice. Special NOTAMs "},{"t":"India's NOTAM series","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#series","k":"A: international locations, more than 2 hours, general international distribution. B: international locations, 30 minutes to 2 hours, adjacent stations only. C: domestic-only locations. D: military airfields used by domestic flights. G: general and lasting information, Delhi NOF only. India NOTAM series A All-world, B Brief, C Civil-domestic, D Defence, G General."},{"t":"How often are the checklists issued?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#checklists","k":" Checklists "},{"t":"NOTAM checklist: ≤ 1 month","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#checklists","k":"India: current on the 1st of each month"},{"t":"AIP Supplement checklist: ≤ 1 month","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#checklists","k":"India: each January"},{"t":"AIC checklist: ≥ once a year","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#checklists","k":""},{"t":"Full NOTAM summary (India): 1 January","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#checklists","k":""},{"t":"AIP amendments (India): once a year, April","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#checklists","k":""},{"t":"How far ahead does an AIRAC change travel?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#airac","k":"AIRAC changes take effect on common dates every 28 days and are not changed again for 28 days. Paper copies leave the AIS at least 42 days before the effective date so they arrive 28 days before; major changes go out 56 days ahead where practicable. AIRAC timing 56 major, 42 send, 28 arrive, 28 cycle."},{"t":"Must this change go through AIRAC?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#airac-what","k":"AIRAC covers planned significant changes to airspace limits (FIR, CTA, CTR, advisory areas, routes, permanent P/R/D, ADIZ), nav-aid and comms details, holding/approach/departure/noise procedures, met facilities, runways and stopways (Part 1), and obstacles, taxiways, aprons, service hours, customs and temporary hazards (Part 2). AIRAC items "},{"t":"When is an AIC issued?","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#aic","k":"An AIC carries information that fits neither the AIP nor a NOTAM: long-term notice of changes and explanatory or advisory material. In India DGCA publishes it, with a yearly checklist. When an AIC "},{"t":"Pre-flight and post-flight information","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#brief","k":"The PIB summarises current NOTAMs before flight. After flight, report unserviceable aids and bird or wildlife hazards at once. Briefing "},{"t":"AIS in India","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":" AIS in India "},{"t":"Provider: AAI","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":"AIC by DGCA"},{"t":"AIS HQ: Safdarjung, New Delhi","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":""},{"t":"International NOTAM offices: 4","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":"Mumbai, Kolkata, Delhi, Chennai"},{"t":"AIP volumes: 2","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":"I: GEN + ENR · II: AD"},{"t":"NOTAM via: AFS / AFTN","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Other documents via: mail","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#india","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"AIP Supplement threshold: ≥ 3 months","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AIRAC cycle: 28 days","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AIRAC distribution: 42 days ahead","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Major changes: 56 days ahead","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Reach users: 28 days ahead","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"P/R/D activation notice: ≥ 7 days","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Desirable minimum notice: 24 h","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"NOTAM serial: 4 digits / year","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Series A: > 2 h","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Series B: 30 min – 2 h","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AIRAC reference date: 29 Jan 1998","s":"Reg 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 10 · Aeronautical Information Services","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Purpose and who does it (p.233)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"AIP (p.233–235)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"NOTAM (p.235–237)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"AIRAC (p.237–238)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"AIC (p.238–239)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Charts, pre- and post-flight (p.239–240)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"India (p.240–245)","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 10 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 11. Search and Rescue","s":"Who comes looking when an aircraft is in trouble, what a pilot must do on seeing or hearing a distress, and how aircraft, ships and survivors talk to each other without a radio.","h":"chapters/reg-11.html","k":"Annex 12 · RCC · India's SRR · at the scene · ground-air codes · COSPAS-SARSAT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Who provides search and rescue over India?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#org","k":"Over land, AAI coordinates SAR through four RCCs (Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai) under a committee chaired by the Secretary, Ministry of Civil Aviation. Over the sea, the Indian Coast Guard runs three MRCCs (Mumbai, Chennai, Port Blair) under a board chaired by its Director General. India's SRR matches its FIRs minus the part over Bhutan. SAR in India Land = AAI × 4; Sea = Coast Guard × 3 (Port Blair is the odd one)."},{"t":"What must a SAR aircraft carry?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#equip","k":"SAR aircraft talk on distress and on-scene frequencies, carry a homing device, can talk to ships over the sea and carry the International Code of Signals. Dropped food and water containers have blue streamers. SAR equipment Blue streamers: water is blue."},{"t":"You see an aircraft or ship in distress. What do you do?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#scene","k":"Keep it in sight until you have to leave or the RCC says it is no longer needed, fix its position, and report everything you can to the RCC or ATS. The first aircraft on scene, if it is not a SAR aircraft, takes charge until the first SAR aircraft arrives. At the scene Watch it, Fix it, Report it, Run the scene."},{"t":"Who controls the scene?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#charge","k":"The first aircraft to reach the scene takes charge on scene until the first SAR aircraft arrives; then the SAR aircraft takes over. Being first counts, not radio contact. Who is in charge First there runs it, till SAR arrives."},{"t":"You hear a MAYDAY. What do you do?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#distress-heard","k":"Acknowledge it, write down the position, take a bearing on the transmission, tell the RCC or ATS everything, and if you choose, head for the position while waiting for instructions. Distress call heard Acknowledge, Record, Bearing, Inform, Go."},{"t":"How does an aircraft lead a ship to a craft in distress?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#ship","k":"Circle the ship at least once, cross its projected course close ahead at low altitude rocking the wings (or opening and closing the throttle, or changing propeller pitch), then fly off in the direction it should go. To say help is no longer needed, cross its wake close astern doing the same. Directing a ship Ahead = follow me. Astern = thanks, not needed."},{"t":"How does the ship reply?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#ship-reply","k":"A ship that understands hoists the code pennant, flashes T T T or turns to follow; one that cannot comply hoists flag N or flashes N N N. Ship replies "},{"t":"What do the symbols laid on the ground mean?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#codes","k":"Survivors: V need assistance, X need medical help, N no, Y yes, an arrow means we are going this way. Rescue teams: LLL operation completed, LL all found, ++ only some found, XX returning to base, NN nothing found, still searching. Symbols must be at least 2.5 m long. Ground-air codes V = Very much need help; X = eXtra (medical) help."},{"t":"Making and answering ground symbols","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#code-rules","k":"Lay symbols at least 2.5 m (8 ft) long in strong contrast, using anything to hand; attract attention with radio, flares, smoke or mirrors. An aircraft that understands rocks its wings by day or flashes its landing lights twice at night; no response means not understood. Making the symbols "},{"t":"What does this ground symbol say?","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#code-stamp","k":"Survivors ask with V (need help) and X (need medical help). Rescue teams report with LLL (operation complete), XX (returning to base) and NN (nothing found). Read the symbol V for help, X for a doctor."},{"t":"Satellite-aided SAR in India","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":"India is part of COSPAS-SARSAT, which detects 406 MHz beacons to within about 5 km. Ground stations (LUTs) are at Bengaluru and Lucknow; the Indian Mission Control Centre at Bengaluru passes alerts to the RCCs over the AFS. COSPAS-SARSAT 406 MHz, 5 km, Bengaluru."},{"t":"Beacon frequency: 406 MHz","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":""},{"t":"Location accuracy: ≈ 5 km","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":""},{"t":"Local user terminals: Bengaluru, Lucknow","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":""},{"t":"Mission control (INMCC): Bengaluru","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":""},{"t":"Linked to RCCs by: AFS","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":""},{"t":"Also covers: 14 nearby SRRs","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#sat","k":"e.g. Sri Lanka, Nepal, Maldives, Singapore"},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"SAR service: 24 h","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AAI RCCs: 4","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":"Delhi, Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai"},{"t":"Coast Guard MRCCs: 3","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":"Mumbai, Chennai, Port Blair"},{"t":"Symbol length: ≥ 2.5 m","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":"8 ft"},{"t":"Circle a ship: ≥ once","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ship radio: 2182 / 4125 kHz","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":"121.5 MHz"},{"t":"Beacon: 406 MHz","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Satellite accuracy: 5 km","s":"Reg 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 11 · Search and Rescue","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Organisation (Annex 12) (p.253)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"India (p.253–254)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SAR equipment (p.254–255)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pilot at the scene (p.255)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pilot intercepting a distress call (p.255–256)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Signals with surface craft (p.256)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Ground–air visual codes (p.257)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Satellite-aided SAR (p.258)","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 11 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 12. Visual Aids for Navigation","s":"How an aerodrome talks to you without a radio: paint on the ground, coloured lights, PAPI, signs and markers; how obstacles are marked; and the fire cover each aerodrome must give.","h":"chapters/reg-12.html","k":"Annex 14 · markings · approach lights · PAPI · runway & taxiway lights · signs · obstacles · RFF "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Indicators every aerodrome needs","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#indicators","k":"Every aerodrome has at least one wind direction indicator (one lit at night). A landing direction indicator is a T. The tower of a controlled aerodrome has a signalling lamp showing red, green and white. The signal area is at least 9 m square with a white border. Wind sock and signals "},{"t":"What colour is this marking?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#colours","k":"Runway markings are white. Taxiway, turn-pad and stand markings are yellow. Apron safety lines are red in India. Mandatory instruction markings are white on red. Marking colours Runway white, taxiway yellow, danger red."},{"t":"Name every marking on a precision runway","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rwy-marks","k":"From the threshold in: threshold stripes (starting 6 m in), the designation number, the centre line, touchdown zone pairs and the aiming point; side stripes along the edges. Runway markings Stripes, Number, Line, Pairs, Big pair."},{"t":"Reading a runway designator: 27L","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#designation","k":"Two digits are the magnetic bearing of the runway, from the approach, to the nearest ten degrees (a single digit gets a leading zero). Parallel runways add a letter, left to right as seen on approach: L R, L C R, L R L R… Runway designation Magnetic heading over ten, seen from the approach."},{"t":"How many threshold stripes?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#thr-stripes","k":"Threshold stripes start 6 m from the threshold and their number depends on runway width: 18 m 4, 23 m 6, 30 m 8, 45 m 12, 60 m 16. Threshold stripes Width ÷ 4 roughly: 18→4, 30→8, 45→12, 60→16."},{"t":"Aiming point distance and TDZ pairs by runway length","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#aim-tdz","k":"The aiming point starts 150, 250, 300 or 400 m from the threshold as LDA grows past 800, 1,200 and 2,400 m. Touchdown zone pairs are 1, 2, 3, 4 or 6 as LDA passes 900, 1,200, 1,500 and 2,400 m. Aiming point and TDZ Longer runway, further aiming point."},{"t":"Marking a displaced threshold","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#displaced","k":"A displaced threshold gets a transverse stripe at least 1.8 m wide. If permanently displaced, arrows on the runway before it; if temporarily displaced, all earlier markings are blanked except the centre line, which becomes arrows. Runway edge lights before a displaced threshold show red to the approach. Displaced thresholds "},{"t":"Pattern A or pattern B?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#holding-marks","k":"Pattern A (two solid and two dashed lines, solid on the holding side) marks the holding position at non-precision and take-off runways and the one closest to a precision runway. Pattern B (the ladder) marks further-out Cat II/III holding positions. From 26 November 2026 only the larger A2/B2 sizes are valid. Holding markings A = At the runway; B = Back for Cat II/III."},{"t":"Apron and taxiway marking numbers","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":"Isolated parking at least 100 m from other stands and buildings; VOR checkpoint circle 6 m with 15 cm lines; stand lines at least 15 cm and apron safety lines 10 cm wide; mandatory instruction marking characters 4 m high. Apron numbers "},{"t":"Isolated aircraft parking: ≥ 100 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":"from stands, buildings, public"},{"t":"VOR checkpoint circle: 6 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":"line 15 cm"},{"t":"Lead-in / turning lines: ≥ 15 cm","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":""},{"t":"Apron safety line: ≥ 10 cm","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":""},{"t":"Mandatory marking characters: 4 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":""},{"t":"Mandatory marking from lines: ≥ 1 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":""},{"t":"Enhanced centre line: up to 47 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#apron","k":""},{"t":"Simple, Cat I or Cat II/III approach lights?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#als","k":"Simple approach lights: a centre row of at least 420 m with an 18 or 30 m crossbar at 300 m. Cat I: centre row of 900 m, lights every 30 m, a 30 m crossbar at 300 m. Cat II/III: the 900 m centre row plus red side-row barrettes to 270 m and crossbars at 150 m and 300 m. Approach lights 420 simple, 900 precision; every crossbar at 300."},{"t":"Approach lighting systems compared","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#als-matrix","k":"A simple approach light system is at least 420 m long; Cat I systems are 900 m with a crossbar at 300 m; Cat II/III add a second crossbar at 150 m and red side rows over the inner 270 m. Approach light table Simple 420, precision 900."},{"t":"Approach light rules","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#als-rules","k":"Simple approach lights serve non-precision runways (and code 3–4 night non-instrument runways); Cat I and Cat II/III systems serve their categories. Only an ILS/MLS azimuth antenna may stick up through the light plane within 60 m of the centre line. Sequenced flashers fire twice a second towards the threshold. Approach light rules "},{"t":"What is the PAPI telling you?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#papi","k":"PAPI has four units on the left of the runway. Two red and two white means on slope; more white means high, more red means low; all white is too high, all red too low. APAPI has two units: one red and one white on slope. PAPI Two and two, you're true; all white, too high; all red, too low."},{"t":"When must a runway have a PAPI?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#vasi-when","k":"A visual approach slope indicator is required for turbojets, where the approach is hard to judge (water, featureless terrain, no lights, deceptive slopes), where obstacles lie under the approach, where undershoot or overrun is dangerous, or where turbulence is common. When a PAPI is required "},{"t":"What colour is each runway light?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rwy-lights","k":"Threshold and wing bars green towards the approach; runway end red; edge lights white, turning yellow on the last 600 m or third; centre line white, then alternate red and white from 900 m to go, then all red for the last 300 m; touchdown zone white barrettes; stopway red. Runway light colours Green to land on, red to stop at; centre line counts down 900 then 300."},{"t":"When is each runway light required?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rwy-light-rules","k":"Edge lights for night or precision runways; centre line lights on Cat II/III and for take-off below 400 m RVR; touchdown zone lights on Cat II/III; threshold identification lights flash white 60–120 times a minute; stopway lights are red. Runway light rules Cat II/III gets centre line and touchdown zone lights."},{"t":"What colour is each taxiway and apron light?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#taxi-lights","k":"Taxiway centre line lights are green; on an exit taxiway they alternate green and yellow until clear of the ILS sensitive area. Taxiway edge lights are blue. Stand guidance lights are yellow with a red stop light. Stop bars and runway guard lights are red and yellow warnings at the runway. Taxiway lights Green to follow, blue at the edges, yellow means \"still near the runway\"."},{"t":"At what RVR does each protection kick in?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#stopbar","k":"Below 350 m RVR: stop bars at every runway-holding position, taxiway centre line lights, intermediate holding position lights. Below 550 m (or up to 1,200 m with heavy traffic) without stop bars: runway guard lights. RVR thresholds 350 = stop bars; 550 = guard lights."},{"t":"Read the sign","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#signs","k":"Mandatory instruction signs are white on red: you must not pass without tower clearance (runway designation, holding position, NO ENTRY). The location sign is yellow on black: you are here. Direction, destination, runway exit, runway vacated and intersection take-off signs are black on yellow. Signs Red = stop. Black square, you are there. Yellow = which way."},{"t":"Rules for signs and designators","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#sign-rules","k":"Signs are frangible and low. Only mandatory signs use red. Numbers alone are reserved for runways; taxiways use letters (not I, O or X) with optional numbers. Aerodrome identification letters are at least 3 m high; road-holding signs are white on red 1.5 m from the road edge. Sign rules "},{"t":"Aerodrome beacon or identification beacon?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#beacons","k":"An aerodrome beacon flashes 20–30 times a minute, white only or white alternating with green (land) or yellow (water). An identification beacon flashes its identity in Morse, green at land aerodromes, yellow at water aerodromes. Beacons Green for ground, yellow for water."},{"t":"Which obstacle light is which?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#obstacles","k":"Low-intensity obstacle lights are fixed red (Types A, B) or flashing red (E) on fixed objects, flashing blue on emergency vehicles and flashing yellow on other vehicles and follow-me cars. Medium-intensity: A flashing white, B flashing red, C fixed red. High-intensity lights flash white. Obstacle lights Blue = police/fire; yellow = follow me; white flashes = big tall things."},{"t":"How is an obstacle painted?","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#marking-obs","k":"Big unbroken surfaces (4.5 m or more both ways) get a chequered pattern of 1.5–3 m rectangles, dark at the corners, in orange and white or red and white. Small objects (under 1.5 m both ways) are one colour, orange or red. Emergency vehicles are red, service vehicles yellow. Marking obstacles Big things get chequers, small things one colour."},{"t":"Closed runways and unserviceable areas","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#closed","k":"A permanently closed runway or taxiway gets a closed marking (crosses), its normal markings removed and lights off. Unserviceable parts that can be bypassed get flags, cones or boards by day and fixed red lights at night. Closed and unusable areas "},{"t":"Aerodrome RFF category","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff","k":"The RFF category is set by the longest aeroplane normally using the aerodrome (and its fuselage width), from category 1 (under 9 m) to 10 (76–90 m). If those biggest aeroplanes make fewer than 700 movements in the busiest three months, one category lower is allowed. RFF categories Under 700 movements in 3 months: drop one."},{"t":"Fire cover in numbers","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":"Fire services must reach any point of an operational runway within 3 minutes (aim: 2 minutes). Foam is the principal agent and dry chemical powder the complementary one. Access roads should reach 1,000 m into the approach area. RFF numbers Three minutes, aim for two; foam first, powder after."},{"t":"Response time (required): ≤ 3 min","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":"any runway point, movement area"},{"t":"Response objective: ≤ 2 min","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":"optimum conditions"},{"t":"Principal agent: foam","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":""},{"t":"Complementary agent: dry chemical powder","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":""},{"t":"One category lower if: < 700 movements","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":"busiest 3 consecutive months"},{"t":"Access roads into approach: 1,000 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#rff-rules","k":""},{"t":"Clearway and stopway dimensions","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#cwy-swy","k":"A clearway extends at least 75 m each side of the extended centre line with ground no higher than a 1.25% upward slope. A stopway is as wide as the runway and strong enough to stop the aeroplane without damage. Clearway and stopway "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Signal area: ≥ 9 m square","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Threshold stripes start: 6 m in","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Centre line stripe + gap: 50–75 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Transverse stripe: ≥ 1.8 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Simple ALS: ≥ 420 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cat I ALS: 900 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ALS crossbar: at 300 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cat II/III side rows: 270 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"PAPI units: 4","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":"APAPI 2"},{"t":"Edge light spacing: 60 / 100 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Yellow edge section: 600 m or ⅓","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Centre line coding: 900 m / 300 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TDZ lights: 900 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Stop bars: RVR < 350 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Guard lights: RVR < 550 m","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Beacon: 20–30 / min","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Threshold ident. lights: 60–120 / min","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RFF response: 3 min","s":"Reg 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 12 · Visual Aids for Navigation","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Indicators and signalling devices (p.263)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Markings: general (p.263–264)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Runway markings (p.264–268)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Taxiway and apron markings (p.268–272)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lights: general (p.272–273)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Approach lighting (p.273–276)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Visual approach slope indicators (p.276–277)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Runway lights (p.277–283)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Taxiway and apron lights (p.283–286)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Signs (p.286–289)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Markers (p.289–290)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Obstacles (p.290–293)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Restricted-use areas (p.293–294)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Rescue and fire-fighting (p.294–296)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Declared distances, clearway, stopway (p.296)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wildlife and FOD (p.296–297)","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 12 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 13. PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","s":"How instrument departures, approaches and holds are built to keep you clear of obstacles, and exactly how to fly them: gradients, segments, aircraft categories, circling, holding entries and speeds, altimeter setting, transponder codes and ACAS.","h":"chapters/reg-13.html","k":"Doc 8168 · departures · approach segments · categories · circling · holding · altimeter setting · SSR · ACAS "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How is obstacle clearance built into a procedure?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#areas","k":"Each segment has a primary area (half the total width) with full minimum obstacle clearance, and a secondary area each side (a quarter of the width each) where the clearance tapers to zero at the outer edge. With no track guidance in a turn, the whole width is primary. Protected areas Half in the middle at full MOC, a quarter each side fading to zero."},{"t":"Why 3.3% on departure?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#pdg","k":"The obstacle identification surface rises at 2.5% from 5 m above the departure end of the runway; adding a 0.8% margin gives the standard procedure design gradient of 3.3% (helicopters 5%). Obstacle clearance is zero at the DER and grows by 0.8% of distance; at least 90 m (295 ft) in turn areas. Departure gradient 2.5 + 0.8 = 3.3."},{"t":"Departure numbers","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":"A departure is straight if within 15° of the runway, otherwise turning. No turn below 394 ft (120 m) above the aerodrome, and none assumed before 600 m from the start of the runway. Gradients needed only to 200 ft or less are not published. Departure numbers "},{"t":"Standard PDG: 3.3%","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":"helicopters 5%"},{"t":"OIS: 2.5%","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":""},{"t":"MOC growth from DER: 0.8% of distance","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":""},{"t":"MOC in turns: 90 m (295 ft)","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":""},{"t":"Straight departure: ≤ 15°","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":""},{"t":"Earliest turn height: 120 m (394 ft)","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":"helicopters 90 m"},{"t":"Earliest turn point: 600 m","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":"from start of runway"},{"t":"Close-in gradient not published: ≤ 60 m (200 ft)","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":""},{"t":"Calculation basis: 2,000 ft, ISA +15","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-rules","k":""},{"t":"Should you accept a radar vector on departure?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#dep-flow","k":"Accept a radar vector after take-off only once engine-out obstacle clearance is assured: above the minimum altitude for that, or on a departure that is not obstacle-critical. Otherwise keep flying the published departure. Accept a vector? Clear the obstacles first, then take the vector."},{"t":"Omnidirectional and SID departures","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#omni","k":"With no track guidance, an omnidirectional departure starts at the DER: climb 3.3% straight ahead to 394 ft with 295 ft clearance before turning more than 15°. If obstacles prevent it, a turn altitude, a higher gradient or sector restrictions are published, or a SID or visual avoidance is required. A SID ends at the first en-route fix. Omnidirectional "},{"t":"GBAS, SBAS, ABAS: who augments GNSS?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#rnav","k":"GBAS sends corrections from a ground transmitter (and supports GLS approaches); SBAS relays them via geostationary satellites from a network of ground stations; ABAS uses on-board information. An RNAV computer cannot detect a wrong data entry. GNSS augmentation "},{"t":"Which approach category is my aircraft?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#cats","k":"Category is set by Vat, the threshold speed (1.3 Vso or 1.23 Vs1g at maximum landing mass): A below 91 kt, B 91–120, C 121–140, D 141–165, E 166–210. Maximum circling speeds are 100, 135, 180, 205 and 240 kt. Aircraft categories Ninety-one, one-twenty-one, one-forty-one, one-sixty-six."},{"t":"Which category?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#cat-stamp","k":"Approach categories go by Vat (1.3 VSO): A under 91 kt, B 91–120, C 121–140, D 141–165, E 166–210. Boundaries belong to the higher category (121 kt is C). Pick the category Every 20–25 kt is a new letter."},{"t":"How steep can the final approach be?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#descent","k":"The optimum final descent with a FAF is 5.2% (3°, 318 ft per NM). Maximum 6.5% (3.7°) for Cat A and B, 6.1% (3.5°) for Cat C, D, E, and 10% for helicopters. A straight-in NPA final may be up to 30° off the runway; beyond that it becomes a circling approach. Descent gradients Three degrees = 318 ft per mile ≈ 5.2%."},{"t":"What are the segments of an instrument approach?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#segments","k":"Arrival (STAR) to the IAF; initial IAF to IF with 1,000 ft clearance; intermediate IF to FAF/FAP with clearance reducing to 500 ft while you configure; final to the MAPt (246 ft with FAF, 295 ft without, for an NPA); then the missed approach at 2.5%. Approach segments Arrive, Initiate, Intermediate, Finish, Miss: 1,000 → 500 → 246/295 → 164 ft."},{"t":"Each segment in detail","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#seg-matrix","k":"Obstacle clearance shrinks as the approach goes on: 300 m (1000 ft) in the initial segment, 300 → 150 m in the intermediate, 75 m (246 ft) on a non-precision final with a FAF, 50 m (164 ft) in the final phase of the missed approach. Segment table Three hundred, one fifty, seventy-five, fifty."},{"t":"Procedure turn, base turn or racetrack?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#reversal","k":"A 45°/180° procedure turn and an 80°/260° procedure turn reverse onto the reciprocal; a base turn joins a non-reciprocal inbound track; a racetrack flies outbound for 1, 2 or 3 minutes then turns 180° back. Entry is restricted and the timing must be flown exactly, because the airspace allows nothing else. Reversal procedures 45/180, 80/260, base, racetrack."},{"t":"How accurate is a fix?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#fix-tol","k":"A fix is really a tolerance area. Terminal radar within 20 NM is good to ±0.8 NM, en-route radar within 40 NM to ±1.7 NM, and DME to ±(0.25 NM + 1.25% of distance). Fix tolerances "},{"t":"Terminal radar (≤ 20 NM): ± 0.8 NM","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#fix-tol","k":""},{"t":"En-route radar (≤ 40 NM): ± 1.7 NM","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#fix-tol","k":""},{"t":"DME: ± 0.25 NM + 1.25%","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#fix-tol","k":""},{"t":"MSA / TAA radius: 25 NM","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#fix-tol","k":"1,000 ft clearance"},{"t":"STAR convergence starts: 25 NM before IAF","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#fix-tol","k":""},{"t":"Final approach fix or final approach point?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#npa-pa","k":"The FAF starts the final segment of a non-precision approach; the FAP, where the intermediate altitude meets the glide path, starts a precision approach. Do not descend below the outer marker/DME check altitude before crossing it. A step-down fix gives a lower OCA/H only if identified. FAF vs FAP FAF for non-precision, FAP for precision."},{"t":"How are OCA/H, MDA/H and DA/H related?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#oca","k":"OCA/H is the highest obstacle plus the minimum obstacle clearance. The operator adds a margin to get MDA/H (2D and circling) or DA/H (3D). Precision and APV heights are measured from the threshold; non-precision and circling from the aerodrome elevation (or the threshold if it is more than 7 ft lower, except circling). OCA, MDA, DA Obstacle + MOC = OCA; OCA + margin = minimum."},{"t":"NPA, APV or precision?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#classes","k":"Type A approaches have minima of 250 ft or more; Type B below 250 ft (Cat I, II, III). NPAs are 2D (VOR, NDB, LOC, RNP APCH/LNAV); APVs are PBN 3D Type A (Baro-VNAV, LPV, LNAV/VNAV); precision approaches are ILS, MLS, PAR, GLS and SBAS Cat I. Approach types Type A from 250 ft; Type B below."},{"t":"The three phases of a missed approach","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#missed","k":"Start the missed approach no lower than the DA/H, or at the MAPt no lower than the MDA/H. Initial phase: MAPt to start of climb, no turns. Intermediate: climb to where 164 ft (50 m) clearance is reached, track change up to 15°. Final: turns allowed, on to a hold, new approach or en route. Standard gradient 2.5%. Missed approach Initial: pitch and power, no turns. Intermediate: 15°. Final: turn."},{"t":"Circling to land","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#circling","k":"The circling area is made of arcs around each threshold joined by tangents, sized for the category's speed, a 25 kt wind and 20° bank (or 3°/s). Stay at or above MDA/H until visual reference is established, the threshold is in sight and you can land. If you lose sight of the runway, make a climbing turn towards the landing runway and fly the missed approach. Circling Circle at or above MDA until you can see and land."},{"t":"Which entry do I fly?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#hold","k":"Draw the entry sectors from the fix: sector 1 (parallel) on the non-holding side within 110° of the inbound track, sector 2 (teardrop/offset, 30° from the reciprocal) in the 70° wedge on the holding side, sector 3 (direct) for everything else. There is ±5° of flexibility at the boundaries. Holding entry trainer Direct for most, teardrop on the holding side, parallel on the other."},{"t":"Holding speeds and timing","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#hold-speeds","k":"Up to 14,000 ft hold at 230 kt (170 kt in Cat A/B-only holds); to 20,000 ft 240 kt; to 34,000 ft 265 kt; above that Mach 0.83. Turbulence speed (280 kt or M 0.8) only with ATC approval. Outbound legs are 1 minute up to 14,000 ft and 1½ minutes above. Holding speeds 230-240-265-.83; one minute low, ninety seconds high."},{"t":"Flying the hold","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#hold-rules","k":"Turn at 25° bank or 3°/s, whichever is less. Start outbound timing over or abeam the fix, whichever is later. Correct both heading and timing for wind. A DME limit ends the outbound leg; a limiting radial reached first is followed. Minimum holding level is 984 ft above obstacles, up to 1,969 ft in mountains. Holding rules "},{"t":"Parallel runway operating modes","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#parallel","k":"Mode 1 independent parallel approaches need centre lines at least 1,035 m apart; Mode 2 dependent approaches 915 m; Mode 3 independent departures 760 m; Mode 4 segregated operations 760 m, adjusted for stagger. Parallel runways Independent arrivals need the most room: 1,035."},{"t":"RNAV (VOR/DME) approaches","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#rnav-app","k":"An RNAV approach based on one VOR/DME is a non-precision approach. The FAF is a fly-by waypoint, the threshold and MAPt are flyover waypoints, final MOC is 246 ft, and the missed approach area splays 15° to a missed approach holding fix. RNAV approaches "},{"t":"When do you change between QNH and 1013?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#altimeter","k":"At or below the transition altitude fly altitudes on QNH; above it, flight levels on 1013.2 hPa. Change to 1013 at the transition altitude when climbing, and back to QNH at the transition level when descending. In India the lowest transition altitude is 4,000 ft. Altimeter setting Climb: Altitude first (TA). Descend: Level first (TL)."},{"t":"Altimeter numbers","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":"Flight level zero is at 1013.2 hPa and levels are at least 500 ft apart. The transition altitude is normally at least 3,000 ft above the aerodrome, rounded up to the next 1,000 ft, and never below 4,000 ft in India. A serviceable altimeter reads within 60 ft of the known elevation. Altimeter numbers "},{"t":"FL 0: 1013.2 hPa","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":""},{"t":"Levels apart: ≥ 500 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":""},{"t":"TA above aerodrome: ≥ 3,000 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":"rounded up to 1,000 ft"},{"t":"Lowest TA in India: 4,000 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":""},{"t":"TL above TA (option): 1,000 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter check (to 30,000 ft): ± 60 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter check (to 50,000 ft): ± 80 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#alt-rules","k":""},{"t":"Keeping terrain clearance with the altimeter","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#terrain","k":"In order of preference: current QNH from a good network; available QNH plus the forecast lowest sea-level pressure; climatological lowest levels. On approach use QNH (or QFE where specified). With no transition altitude, fly flight levels en route. Terrain clearance "},{"t":"Which code?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#codes","k":"Codes are four octal digits (0–7). Squawk what ATC gives you, else 2000. 7700 emergency, 7600 radio failure, 7500 unlawful interference. If asked to confirm 7500 you may confirm or stay silent; silence means it is genuine. Never IDENT unless asked. Squawk codes 75 taken alive, 76 radio nix, 77 going to heaven."},{"t":"TCAS calls out. What do you do?","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#acas","k":"Never manoeuvre on a traffic advisory alone; use it to look and prepare. On a resolution advisory, follow it at once, even against an ATC instruction, never opposite to it, unless a stall, wind shear or GPWS warning takes priority. Change your path only as much as needed, tell ATC, and return to the clearance when clear. ACAS RA TA: Look. RA: Obey (then tell ATC)."},{"t":"How ACAS works","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#acas-facts","k":"ACAS interrogates transponders out to about 14 NM (at least 4.5 NM in busy areas). It gives TAs against any Mode C transponder, but RAs only against altitude-reporting aircraft and only in the vertical plane. Two ACAS aircraft coordinate their RAs, so ignoring yours weakens both. ACAS facts "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"PDG: 3.3%","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Earliest turn: 394 ft / 600 m","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Straight-in NPA: ≤ 30°","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Optimum final: 5.2% / 3°","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Initial MOC: 1,000 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Final MOC with/without FAF: 246 / 295 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Missed approach climb: 2.5%","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Missed final-phase MOC: 164 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"FAF from threshold: 5 NM (≤ 10)","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Holding speeds: 230/240/265/M.83","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Outbound leg: 1 / 1½ min","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Holding MOC: 984 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mode 1 / 2 spacing: 1,035 / 915 m","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mode 3 / 4 spacing: 760 m","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"India lowest TA: 4,000 ft","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ACAS range: 14 NM","s":"Reg 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 13 · PANS-OPS: Aircraft Operations","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Doc 8168 (p.325)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"General departure criteria (p.325–327)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"RNAV / GNSS (p.330–331)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fixes (p.331–332)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Approach types and aircraft categories (p.332–333)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Descent gradient (p.333)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Approach segments (p.333–338)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Classification (p.338–341)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Circling (p.342–344)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Holding (p.344–347)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Parallel runways (p.347)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"RNAV approaches (VOR/DME) (p.348)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter setting (p.348–350)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SSR and transponders (p.350–352)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ACAS (p.352–354)","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 13 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 14. National Law","s":"The Indian laws behind your licence: who regulates you, where you may never fly, what you must not do before or during a flight, how fuelling and logbooks work, and what it costs if you break the rules.","h":"chapters/reg-14.html","k":"Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam 2024 · Aircraft Rules 1937 · fuelling · age · logbooks · public health "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Who and what does the Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam cover?","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#bva","k":"The 2024 Act covers all of India, Indian citizens anywhere, Indian aircraft anywhere, foreign aircraft in Indian airspace, and aircraft run by non-citizens based in India. It sets up three bodies under the Central Government: DGCA for safety, BCAS for security and AAIB for accident investigation. Military aircraft are exempt. BVA 2024 Safety, Security, Investigation: DGCA, BCAS, AAIB."},{"t":"Penalties and protected zones","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#penalties","k":"Breaking the Act or its rules can mean up to 2 years in prison and/or a ₹1 crore fine. Slaughtering animals or dumping waste within 10 km of an aerodrome: up to 3 years and/or ₹1 crore. Buildings and trees can be controlled up to 20 km away. Penalties and zones 2 years, 1 crore; 10 km dirt, 20 km trees."},{"t":"General penalty: 2 years","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#penalties","k":"and/or ₹1 crore"},{"t":"Slaughter / waste near airport: 3 years","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#penalties","k":"and/or ₹1 crore"},{"t":"Slaughter / rubbish zone: 10 km","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#penalties","k":""},{"t":"Buildings / trees zone: 20 km","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#penalties","k":""},{"t":"Public-health rules penalty: 3 months","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#penalties","k":"and/or ₹1,000"},{"t":"Is this allowed under the Aircraft Rules?","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#rules-flight","k":"Arms and explosives need Central Government written permission; photographs at licensed aerodromes or from aircraft need DGCA permission; drops are limited to ballast, emergency jettison, message bags, nav aids, towing ropes and (with DM/police permission) paper or flower petals; phones only after landing and clear of the runway. Allowed or not? Guns need the Centre, cameras need DGCA."},{"t":"Key Aircraft Rules by number","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#docs-rules","k":"Know the Aircraft Rules by number: 5 registration and marks, 7 documents on board, 8 arms and explosives (Central Government), 10 mail, 12 prohibited areas, 13 photography (DGCA written permission), 15 a valid C of A. Rules by number Thirteen for the camera, eight for the guns."},{"t":"Straying into a prohibited area","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#prohibited","k":"If you stray into a prohibited area, signal distress, leave it, and land at the nearest aerodrome; do not go deeper or start descending over it. Examples include Rashtrapati Bhavan and Baroda (ground to unlimited), Mathura refinery (10 km, unlimited), Bhubaneswar (to 50,000 ft), Kalpakkam (10 km, to 10,000 ft) and the Towers of Silence (1 mile). Prohibited areas Strayed in? Signal, get out, land."},{"t":"Safety distances when refuelling","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#fuel","k":"No smoking or flames within 30 m. Only essential people within 15 m. Ground equipment out of the 3 m danger zone around the wings and fuel vehicle, and flame-proof or 15 m (gasoline) / 6 m (kerosene) away. Stop fuelling if a jet's exhaust comes within 43 m. Bond and earth the aircraft and truck. Fuelling distances 3 – 6 – 15 – 30 – 43."},{"t":"Other fuelling rules (Rule 25A/25B)","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#fuel-rules","k":"Petroleum in bulk (over 900 L) only from approved trucks, hydrants or barges. Engines and ignition off, radio and radar off. Passengers may stay aboard only with a crew attendant. Stop at once for a spill and never wash fuel into drains. Fuelled aircraft are hangared only in non-flammable, ventilated buildings. Fuelling rules "},{"t":"Age limits for flying","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#age","k":"You can be in sole control of an aircraft at 16. Commercial pilots may fly to 65, but between 60 and 65 only in multi-crew operations with the other pilot under 60 (except single-pilot aircraft up to 5,700 kg in India). Age limits 16 to solo, 60 needs a younger mate, 65 to retire."},{"t":"Alcohol, drugs and special passengers","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#alcohol","k":"Crew may not take alcohol, sedatives or narcotics within 12 hours of flight or be intoxicated at all. Passengers with mental disorders or epilepsy fly only if certified fit, alcohol-free for 12 hours and accompanied or sedated as needed. Prisoners and animals need a DGCA permit. Alcohol and passengers Twelve hours, bottle to throttle."},{"t":"Which licence for which flight?","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-types","k":"Private aircraft need a valid PPL (not a student licence); public transport and aerial work need a CPL or ATPL. Only Category A-registered aircraft may be used for public transport. Instructors need an instructor rating; dispatchers need DGCA approval. Who may fly what Pay needs a CPL; public transport needs Category A."},{"t":"How many cabin crew?","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#cabin","k":"Aeroplanes with 10–50 seats need 1 cabin crew; more than 50 seats need 2, plus 1 more for every 50 seats (or part) above 99. Cabin crew count 1 to 50; 2 to 99; then +1 per 50."},{"t":"My licence has expired. What do I need?","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#renewal","k":"Expired under 2 years: normal renewal. 2 to 3 years: a skill test plus the Air Regulations exam. Over 3 years: every test for a new licence. Separately, an illness or injury keeping you off duty 15 days or more invalidates the licence until you get a new medical; tell DGCA at once. Licence renewal Under 2: renew. 2 to 3: skill + Regs. Over 3: start again."},{"t":"How do you log each kind of flight time?","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#logbook","k":"Logbooks are in ink, kept 5 years, self-certified monthly and certified quarterly (Ops Manager/Chief Pilot, CFI, or a DGCA officer). A student logs PIC only when alone; an instructor logs PIC marked \"instructional\"; simulator time is instrument ground time. Logging time Ink, 5 years, monthly self, quarterly boss."},{"t":"Numbers for licences and documents","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":"Documents not carried must be produced within 7 days. Illness of 15 days or more needs a new medical. Logbooks are kept 5 years. Draft CARs stay on the DGCA website 30 days. Route changes need 7 days' notice to DGCA. Licence numbers "},{"t":"Produce documents not carried: 7 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"Sick leave needing new medical: ≥ 15 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"Logbook retention: 5 years","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"Logbook certification: quarterly","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":"Mar, Jun, Sep, Dec"},{"t":"Draft CAR comment period: 30 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"New route / timetable notice: 7 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"Drone UIN needed above: 250 g","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"False lights zone: 5 NM","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#licence-misc","k":""},{"t":"Rules for operators","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#ops-manual","k":"Scheduled services need Central Government permission (foreign airlines via an air services agreement). New, changed or discontinued routes and timetables need DGCA concurrence with 7 days' notice. Operators keep a DGCA-approved Operations Manual and carry a copy on every flight. Operators and routes "},{"t":"Public health rules in numbers","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":"The commander sends a health report at least 2 hours before landing in India. Incubation periods: yellow fever and plague 6 days, cholera 5 days, smallpox and typhus 14 days. Pilgrims need cholera and yellow fever certificates. Bodies of those who died of yellow fever, plague or anthrax come in only as ashes. Public health Plague and yellow fever 6, cholera 5, smallpox and typhus 14."},{"t":"Health report before landing: ≥ 2 h","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Yellow fever, plague: 6 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Cholera: 5 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Smallpox, typhus: 14 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Penalty: 3 months / ₹1,000","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Bodies: YF, plague, anthrax: ashes only","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Other remains: triple sealed","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Customs rules and the airport health office","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#customs","k":"International flights must arrive at and leave from designated customs aerodromes, except in emergencies, and the PIC must let customs board and examine the aircraft. The Airport Health Organisation (APHO) under the Health Ministry handles yellow fever checks, disinsection, sanitation and dead body clearance. Customs and APHO "},{"t":"Other aviation rules in India","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#other-rules","k":"Besides the Aircraft Act and Rules, India has separate rules for obstructions near aerodromes (1994), dangerous goods (2003), security (2011), accident investigation (2017), drones (2021) and restricted radiotelephony (2025). Other rules "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"General penalty: 2 yr / ₹1 cr","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Alcohol before flight: 12 h","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Solo age: 16","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Max age: 65","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":"60–65 multi-crew"},{"t":"Renewal thresholds: 2 / 3 years","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sick → new medical: 15 days","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"No smoking zone: 30 m","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Danger zone: 3 m","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet exhaust limit: 43 m","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Bulk petroleum: > 900 L","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cabin crew 10–50 seats: 1","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Logbooks kept: 5 years","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Health report: 2 h before","s":"Reg 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 14 · National Law","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam, 2024 (p.387–389)","s":"Reg 14 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft Rules, 1937: key rules (p.389–399)","s":"Reg 14 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Indian Aircraft Rules, 1920 (customs) (p.396)","s":"Reg 14 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Indian Aircraft (Public Health) Rules, 1954 (p.396–398)","s":"Reg 14 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other rules (p.398–399)","s":"Reg 14 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 14 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 15. Personnel Licensing","s":"What you need to hold each pilot licence in India, and what keeps it valid: schooling, English level, medical class and validity, minimum ages, flying hours, skill tests, ratings and privileges.","h":"chapters/reg-15.html","k":"Annex 1 · CAR Section 7 · medicals · ELP · SPL, PPL, CPL, ATPL · instrument rating "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Education, cross-country and authority","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#basics","k":"The Central Government (DGCA) grants and renews licences. SPL and PPL need Class 10; CPL and ATPL need Class 10+2 with Physics and Mathematics. A cross-country flight goes beyond 100 NM from the departure aerodrome, or 50 NM for PPL and microlight purposes. Education and XC PPL 50, everyone else 100."},{"t":"English language proficiency levels","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#elp","k":"Level 4 (Operational) is the minimum and is re-tested every 3 years; Level 5 (Extended) every 6 years; Level 6 (Expert) never. Levels 1–3 are not acceptable. English proficiency Four for three, five for six, six for ever."},{"t":"Which medical class?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#med-class","k":"Class 1 for CPL and ATPL (and PPL holders using instrument rating privileges). Class 2 for PPL, student pilot licences, microlight and FRTOL(R). Class 3 for air traffic controllers. Which medical class One for pros, two for private, three for controllers."},{"t":"How long is a medical valid?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#med-valid","k":"Class 1: 12 months up to age 60, then 6 months (6 months above 40 for single-crew operations). Class 2: 24 months up to 50, then 12. Class 3: 4 years up to 40, 2 years to 50, then 1 year. Medical validity Class 1 halves at 60; Class 2 halves at 50."},{"t":"When is your licence suddenly invalid?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#invalid","k":"Sickness or injury keeping you from your licensed work for 15 days or more, or any injury likely to impair your efficiency, invalidates the licence until you pass a fresh medical; you or your employer tell DGCA at once. Never fly when you know your fitness is below standard. Licence invalid Sick 15 days or more: fresh medical."},{"t":"Minimum ages for each licence","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#ages","k":"Sole control and SPL at 16, PPL at 17, CPL at 18, ATPL at 21. Commercial flying stops at 65 (60–65 multi-crew with a younger pilot). Licences are now valid for 10 years from issue. Licence ages 16, 17, 18, 21: sweet sixteen to legal twenty-one."},{"t":"How long do licences, ratings and checks last?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":"Licences are valid 10 years (since October 2023). An instrument rating lasts 12 months from its test, and a proficiency check 6 months; renewing within 2 months before expiry keeps the old expiry date as the start of the new period. Apply for licence renewal within the month before expiry. Validity periods "},{"t":"Pilot licence: 10 years","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":"since 10 Oct 2023"},{"t":"Renewal application window: 1 month before expiry","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":""},{"t":"Instrument rating: 12 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":""},{"t":"Proficiency check (> 5,700 kg): 6 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":""},{"t":"Keep expiry date if renewed within: 2 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":""},{"t":"SPL renewal: 12 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#validity","k":"max 24 months from issue"},{"t":"What may a student pilot do?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#spl","k":"A student pilot (oral exams passed, police clearance obtained) may fly within India as PIC of rated types, always under an instructor's supervision, VFR only, with no passengers, goods or pay, and no cross-country until 10 hours solo and the Navigation and Met exams are passed. Student Pilot's Licence Student: solo, supervised, VFR, no passengers."},{"t":"PPL, CPL and ATPL side by side","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#compare","k":"PPL: 40 hours (20 solo, a 150 NM cross-country). CPL: 200 hours (100 PIC, a 300 NM cross-country, 10 h instrument, 5 h night). ATPL: 1,500 hours (500 cross-country, 75 night, 100 instrument). CPL holders command aeroplanes up to 5,700 kg; ATPL holders above it. PPL vs CPL vs ATPL 40 → 200 → 1,500."},{"t":"CPL skill test and renewals","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#skill","k":"The CPL skill test (within 6 months of applying) covers general flying by day and night, a day cross-country of at least 250 NM with a landing elsewhere, and a night cross-country of at least 120 NM back to base without landing. Renew a CPL with 10 hours PIC in the last 6 months (half of co-pilot time counts) or the day and night tests; a PPL with 5 hours PIC in the last 12 months. Skill tests Day 250 out and land; night 120 round trip."},{"t":"Does the licence allow this flight?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#privileges","k":"A PPL flies for no pay, with passengers at night only with a night rating and 5 night take-offs and landings in 6 months. A CPL may command aeroplanes up to 5,700 kg (10 night take-offs and landings for night passengers) and be co-pilot of anything rated. An ATPL commands above 5,700 kg after 100 hours as co-pilot on type and 10 route checks. Can I fly it? PPL flies for free; CPL commands up to 5,700 kg."},{"t":"Night rating, open rating and instructor rating","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#ratings","k":"A PPL night rating needs 50 h PIC with 5 h night (5 take-offs and landings in 6 months), a 100 NM dual night cross-country, and 5 h dual instrument. Open ratings: PPL 250 h PIC + 4 types for single piston ≤ 1,500 kg; CPL 1,000 h (500 PIC) + 4 types; ATPL 1,000 h PIC on 14,000 kg+ aeroplanes for anything ≤ 5,700 kg. Ratings "},{"t":"The instrument rating","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#ir","k":"An IR needs 100 h PIC (50 cross-country) and 40 h instrument time (no more than 20 on the ground trainer, 5 in the last 6 months), plus a test within 6 months. It lasts 12 months. A test on a light single or multi covers all aeroplanes of that kind up to 5,700 kg; above 5,700 kg it covers only the type tested. Instrument rating 100 PIC, 40 IF, 12 months."},{"t":"From ATPL to captain of a jet","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#atpl-pic","k":"To command an aeroplane above 5,700 kg, an ATPL holder needs 100 hours as co-pilot on the type, then 10 consecutive satisfactory route checks (5 by night) under a check pilot (only 6, 3 at night, with 100 h previous heavy PIC), a proficiency check in the last 6 months, and a current IR for IFR. ATPL command 100 hours, 10 checks, 5 at night."},{"t":"CPL with instrument rating: what's different?","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#cpl-ir","k":"A CPL with IR needs 200 h within the last 5 years, 50 h cross-country PIC and 50 h instrument (max 20 simulator), and up to 30 h of the 100 h PIC may be student PIC time, logged by the student only with the instructor's name. CPL with IR "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Cross-country: 100 NM","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":"PPL 50 NM"},{"t":"ELP level 4: 3 years","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ELP level 5: 6 years","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Class 1 medical: 12 → 6 months at 60","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Class 2 medical: 24 → 12 months at 50","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Licence validity: 10 years","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sick → fresh medical: 15 days","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"PPL hours: 40 (20 solo)","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CPL hours: 200 (100 PIC)","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ATPL hours: 1,500","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CPL PIC limit: 5,700 kg","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Night passengers: PPL / CPL: 5 / 10 ldg in 6 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"IR validity: 12 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Proficiency check: 6 months","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ATPL route checks: 10 (5 night)","s":"Reg 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 15 · Personnel Licensing","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"General (p.415–418)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Medicals (p.416–417)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Validity and ages (p.417)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Student Pilot's Licence (p.418–419)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Private Pilot's Licence (Aeroplanes) (p.419–421)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Commercial Pilot's Licence (Aeroplanes) (p.421–424)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"CPL with Instrument Rating (p.424–427)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airline Transport Pilot's Licence (Aeroplanes) (p.427–430)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Instrument Rating (Aeroplanes) (p.430–431)","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 15 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 16. Airworthiness of Aircraft","s":"Who says an aircraft is fit to fly, which country is responsible for that, and when the certificate stops being valid.","h":"chapters/reg-16.html","k":"Annex 8 · Type Certificate · C of A · suspension · special flight permit "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which State does this?","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#who","k":"The State of Design issues the Type Certificate approving the type design. The State of Registry issues (or renders valid) the Certificate of Airworthiness for each individual aircraft, and must recognise foreign certificates issued to at least ICAO standards. Which State? Design approves the Type; Registry certifies the Tail."},{"t":"What does Annex 8 cover?","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#annex8","k":"Annex 8 sets broad airworthiness standards from the engineering side; Annex 6 Part I covers performance limits from the operational side. Annex 8's detailed aeroplane standards apply to multi-engined aeroplanes over 5,700 kg, and it has four parts: definitions, certification and continuing airworthiness, large aeroplanes, helicopters. Annex 8 vs Annex 6 Annex 8 builds it, Annex 6 flies it."},{"t":"Security features built into aircraft","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#security","k":"After hijackings and terrorist attacks, aircraft design now includes protected systems, an identified least-risk bomb location, and a strengthened cockpit door, ceiling and floor. Security by design "},{"t":"Can this aircraft fly on its C of A?","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#india","k":"A C of A is deemed suspended if the aircraft stops conforming to the rules, is modified or repaired outside them, suffers major damage or develops a major defect. DGCA may also suspend or cancel it if it doubts the aircraft's or type's safety. No flight while suspended, except on a special flight permit. Is the C of A valid? Damage, Defect, Deviation = Deemed suspended."},{"t":"Getting and keeping a C of A in India","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#india-facts","k":"The owner or operator applies to DGCA for a C of A, special C of A or validation of a foreign one. Safety-affecting modifications and repairs need DGCA's prior approval. DGCA endorses the C of A where a flight manual is required. DGCA rules "},{"t":"From design to a grounded aircraft","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#life","k":"The State of Design approves the type; the State of Registry issues or validates each aircraft's C of A; it stays valid while the aircraft is maintained to the rules; damage, defects or unapproved changes suspend it until put right, or a special flight permit is given. Life of a C of A Type, tail, keep, suspend, permit."},{"t":"Engineering (Annex 8) or operations (Annex 6)?","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#sort","k":"Annex 8 is about how the aircraft is designed, built and kept airworthy; Annex 6 Part I is about how it is operated within its performance limits. Annex 8 or 6? "},{"t":"Numbers and names to know","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Annex 8 large-aeroplane scope: > 5,700 kg","s":"Reg 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":"multi-engined"},{"t":"Parts of Annex 8: 4","s":"Reg 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Guidance manual: Doc 9760","s":"Reg 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Type Certificate: State of Design","s":"Reg 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"C of A: State of Registry","s":"Reg 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Flying while suspended: special flight permit","s":"Reg 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 16 · Airworthiness of Aircraft","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Annex 8 (p.443–444)","s":"Reg 16 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Indian provisions (p.444–445)","s":"Reg 16 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 16 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 17. Operational Procedures","s":"The day-to-day rules of airline flying: what papers you carry, when the weather is too poor to take off or land, what equipment the aircraft needs, how much fuel to load and how long the crew may work.","h":"chapters/reg-17.html","k":"Annex 6 · documents · AOC · low visibility · approach ban · equipment · oxygen · fuel · FDTL "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Annex 6 parts and who is allowed on the flight deck","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#annex6","k":"Annex 6 Part I covers international commercial aeroplanes, Part II international general aviation (1969) and Part III helicopters (1986). Only assigned crew, an inspector on duty, or someone the Operations Manual allows may be on the flight deck, and the commander has the final say. Annex 6 and the flight deck Part I airlines, Part II general aviation, Part III helicopters."},{"t":"Must it be carried on board?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#docs","k":"Carry the certificates (registration, airworthiness, review, noise, AOC), crew licences, radio licence, journey log, Operations Manual, MEL, Flight Manual, cabin crew manual, checklists, the search checklist with the least-risk bomb location, maintenance release, LOPA, charts, weight schedule, load and trim sheet, passenger list, cargo manifest, dangerous goods list, insurance and a safety card at every seat. Documents on board Certificates, licences, manuals, checklists."},{"t":"How documents must be carried","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#docs-rules","k":"Documents are carried as physical copies unless DGCA approves them on an EFB; DGCA certificates go in original or DGCA-attested copies. Flight information is kept on the ground until duplicated, or carried in a fireproof container. Document rules "},{"t":"The Air Operator Certificate","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#aoc","k":"The AOC is issued by the State of the Operator (where the operator's principal place of business is) once the operator shows its organisation, training, flight operations, ground handling and maintenance are adequate. It stays valid only while those standards are kept under continuous surveillance. AOC The AOC comes from the State of the Operator."},{"t":"Passengers, baggage and smoking","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#cabin","k":"Persons with reduced mobility may not be refused if they tell the airline when booking or checking in. Deportees and persons in custody need procedures and the commander must be told. No smoking in aisles, toilets, unprotected cargo areas or where oxygen is in use. Cabin rules "},{"t":"EDTO and RVSM approval","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#edto","k":"EDTO (ETOPS) applies when the diversion time to an en-route alternate, in still air at one-engine-out cruise speed for twins (all-engine speed for three or four engines), exceeds the threshold time set by DGCA. RVSM aircraft must show and hold the level automatically, alert at deviations of no more than 300 ft and report pressure altitude. EDTO and RVSM Twins count one-engine-out time; three and four engines all-engines."},{"t":"How low can you take off?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#lvto","k":"A low-visibility take-off is one with RVR below 400 m and needs DGCA approval. Scheduled operators may go down to 125 m with a 90 m visual segment at the start of the roll; non-scheduled and general aviation never below 500 m. Below Cat I conditions (550 m RVR / 800 m visibility), LVPs must be in force. Take-off minima 400 = LVTO; 125 airlines; 500 GA."},{"t":"Before a Cat II/III approach or LVTO","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#cat2","k":"Cat II/III needs an aeroplane certificated for DH below 200 ft, a system recording autoland successes and failures, authority approval, at least two pilots and a radio-altimeter DH. Before starting, the commander checks the facilities, that LVPs are in force and that the crew is qualified. Anti-skid, reversers, wipers and window heat must work for LVTO. Cat II/III conditions Two pilots and a radio altimeter for Cat II/III."},{"t":"Normal or restricted aerodrome minima?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#aom","k":"Normal aerodrome operating minima come from AAI-published facilities and OCAs. Restricted minima add 100 ft to the DA/MDA and 400 m to the visibility/RVR. Aerodrome minima Restricted = +100 ft, +400 m."},{"t":"The RVR has dropped. Can I continue?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#ban","k":"Don't start an approach if the RVR or visibility is below minimum. If it drops after you start, you may not continue below 1,000 ft above the aerodrome or into the final approach segment. Once past that point you may continue to DA/MDA and land if you have the visual reference there. Approach ban Below minimum before 1,000 ft or the FAF: stop. After: carry on to minimums."},{"t":"Which RVR controls the landing?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#rvr","k":"Touchdown-zone RVR always controls. Mid-point and stop-end RVRs also control if reported and relevant (down to about 60 kt): mid-point at least 125 m (50 m with roll-out guidance), stop-end at least 50 m. Controlling RVR "},{"t":"Touchdown zone: always controlling","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#rvr","k":""},{"t":"Mid-point minimum: 125 m","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#rvr","k":"50 m with roll-out guidance"},{"t":"Stop-end minimum: 50 m","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#rvr","k":""},{"t":"\"Relevant\" = high-speed part: down to ~60 kt","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#rvr","k":""},{"t":"Which aeroplanes need which equipment?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#thresholds","k":"Forward-looking wind shear and GPWS for turbojets over 5,700 kg or more than 9 passengers; ACAS II for turbine aeroplanes over 5,700 kg or more than 19 passengers; a reinforced cockpit door over 45,500 kg or more than 60 seats; wipers over 5,700 kg; two combination recorders over 15,000 kg. Equipment thresholds GPWS 9, ACAS 19, door 60."},{"t":"Flight data and cockpit voice recorders","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#recorders","k":"FDRs keep at least the last 25 hours; CVRs and data-link recorders the last 2 hours (25 hours for new aeroplanes over 27,000 kg from 2022). Aeroplanes over 15,000 kg needing both get two combination recorders, one forward and one aft. The CVR runs from before the aircraft moves under its own power until it can no longer move, and may not use magnetic tape or wire. Flight recorders Data 25, voice 2 (new heavies 25)."},{"t":"First-aid kits, medical kits and megaphones","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#medkits","k":"One first-aid kit per 100 passengers (up to 4 for 301 or more). One universal precaution kit where cabin crew are required, two above 250 passengers. A doctor's medical kit above 100 passengers on sectors over 2 hours. Megaphones: 1 for 61–99 seats, 2 for 100 or more. Kits and megaphones One first-aid kit per hundred."},{"t":"How much oxygen must be carried?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#oxygen","k":"Unpressurised: above 10,000 ft (700 hPa) oxygen for all crew and 10% of passengers for any time beyond 30 minutes up to 13,000 ft, and for everyone above 13,000 ft (620 hPa). Pressurised: enough for everyone after a decompression for any time above 10,000 ft, and at least 10 minutes for all cabin occupants when flying above 25,000 ft (or unable to get down to 13,000 ft within 4 minutes). Oxygen 10 thousand: 10% after 30 min; 13 thousand: everyone; 25 thousand: 10-minute supply."},{"t":"Protective breathing and over-water equipment","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#pbe","k":"Pressurised aeroplanes, and unpressurised ones over 5,700 kg or 19 seats, carry protective breathing equipment for every flight crew member on duty and every required cabin crew member, with 15 minutes of gas. Over water, life rafts hold everyone even if the biggest is lost, each with a locator light, survival kit and two survival ELTs. Crew PBE and life rafts "},{"t":"What makes up the minimum fuel?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#fuel","k":"Taxi + trip + contingency (5% of trip, at least 5 minutes holding at 1,500 ft) + destination alternate (or 15 min holding if no alternate) + final reserve (turbine 30 minutes holding at 1,500 ft; piston 45 minutes) + any additional fuel + discretionary fuel at the commander's choice. Fuel stack Taxi, Trip, Contingency, Alternate, Reserve, Additional, Discretionary: TTCARAD."},{"t":"Pick the fuel figure","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#fuel-stamp","k":"Contingency fuel is normally 5% of trip fuel (at least 5 minutes' holding at 1500 ft). Final reserve: 30 min for turbines, 45 min for pistons. No destination alternate: 15 min alternate fuel. Turbine to an isolated aerodrome: 2 h including final reserve. Fuel numbers Thirty for jets, forty-five for pistons."},{"t":"How long may a two-pilot crew fly?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#fdtl","k":"In any 24 hours a two-pilot crew may fly 8 hours with an FDP of 11 hours for up to 6 landings, shrinking the landings as the FDP grows (12:30 → 3). 9 hours flight time allows a 13-hour FDP with 2 landings; 10 hours, 13 hours with 1 landing. Duty touching the night: 8 h flight time, 10 h FDP, 2 landings. FDTL two-pilot 8 hours, 11 hours, 6 landings."},{"t":"Augmented crews and ULR","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#fdtl-aug","k":"A 3-pilot crew may fly 12 hours with an FDP of 14–16 hours depending on the rest facility; 4 pilots 14 hours with 16–18 hours FDP; ultra long range (4 crew) 17 hours flight time with a 21-hour FDP. Augmented crew "},{"t":"Cumulative flight time and duty","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#cumulative","k":"Flight time: 35 h in 7 days, 65 in 14, 100 in 28, 300 in 90, 1,000 in 365. Duty: 60, 100, 190, 600 and 1,800 hours. Cumulative limits 35-65-100-300-1,000."},{"t":"Duty words you must know","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#fdtl-defs","k":"An FDP runs from reporting to engines off after the last flight; a duty period from reporting until free of all duties. Local night is 8 hours between 2200 and 0800; night duty touches 0000–0600; the window of circadian low is 0200–0600. Crew stay acclimatised to the departure time zone for 48 hours. FDTL definitions FDP: report to engines off."},{"t":"Unforeseen circumstances: how far can limits stretch?","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":"Flight time may be extended by up to 1 hour and the FDP by up to 2 hours, with one extra landing for a diversion; no more than 4 hours of flight time and 8 hours of FDP extension in 28 days. Rest grows by 2 hours for small extensions and 4 hours for larger ones. Extensions "},{"t":"Flight time extension: ≤ 1 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"FDP extension: ≤ 2 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Extra landing (diversion): 1","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Cumulative in 28 days: 4 h FT / 8 h FDP","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Extra rest (FDP ≤ 1 h / FT ≤ 30 min): + 2 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Extra rest (beyond): + 4 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Reported but not flown: 2 × time, min 12 h rest","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Records kept: 18 months","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#extension","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"LVTO: RVR < 400 m","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Scheduled LVTO minimum: 125 m","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"GA / non-scheduled: 500 m","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Restricted AOM: +100 ft, +400 m","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Approach ban point: 1,000 ft / FAF","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"FDR / CVR: 25 h / 2 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"GPWS / ACAS / door: 9 / 19 / 60 pax","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Oxygen 10% pax after: 30 min","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Contingency fuel: 5%","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Final reserve (turbine): 30 min","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Two-pilot FT / FDP: 8 h / 11 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flight time 7 / 28 / 365 days: 35 / 100 / 1,000 h","s":"Reg 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 17 · Operational Procedures","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Annex 6 (p.449)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"General requirements (p.449–451)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Documents carried (CAR Section 2 Series X Part VII; Rule 7) (p.450)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Operator certification (p.451)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Operational requirements (p.451–454)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"All-weather operations (p.454–456)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Instruments and equipment (p.456–461)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fuel policy (p.459–461)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Emergency equipment (p.461–463)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flight and duty time limitations (CAR Section 7 Series J) (p.463–468)","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 17 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 18. Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","s":"The things that can bite you in real operations, from ice and birds to fire, decompression, wind shear and the wake of a bigger aircraft, and the rules that keep each one in check.","h":"chapters/reg-18.html","k":"MEL · icing · birds · noise abatement · fire · decompression · wind shear · wake turbulence · fuel jettison "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"MEL or MMEL?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#mel","k":"The manufacturer draws up the MMEL for the type, approved by the authority. The operator's MEL, approved by DGCA and part of the Operations Manual, tells the PIC whether a flight may start or continue with an item inoperative; it may be stricter but never more lenient than the MMEL. MEL vs MMEL Master by the Maker; MEL by the operator."},{"t":"Why there is no such thing as a little ice","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#ice","k":"Frost or ice as thin as 0.4 mm ruins lift, raises drag and spoils handling, especially at take-off. Cold-soaked fuel can chill the wing so that ice forms in rain or humid air even when the air is above freezing; this clear ice may be invisible and is found by touch. Never take off with contamination: de-ice, then anti-ice. Icing Clean wing or no wing."},{"t":"De-icing or anti-icing?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#deice","k":"De-icing removes frost, ice, snow or slush with heated de-icing fluid; anti-icing coats a clean surface with anti-icing fluid to keep it clean for a limited (holdover) time. Fluids only matter until lift-off; then the aircraft's own systems take over. De-icing vs anti-icing "},{"t":"Bird strike facts","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#birds","k":"Ninety per cent of bird strikes happen below 500 m. Away from the aerodrome the only defence is avoiding known bird concentrations; on the aerodrome, shell crackers are the most effective scarer. Birds on the runway leave about 2 seconds before an aircraft at about 135 kt arrives. Bird strikes Nine in ten below five hundred."},{"t":"Strikes below 500 m: 90%","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#birds","k":""},{"t":"Best scarer: shell crackers","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#birds","k":""},{"t":"Birds take off before a 135 kt aircraft: ≈ 2 s","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#birds","k":""},{"t":"Defence en route: avoid concentrations","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#birds","k":"migration, daily cycles"},{"t":"Noise abatement rules","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#noise","k":"Noise abatement departure procedures never start below 800 ft and are not flown when wind shear is reported or suspected. On approach, be in landing configuration by the outer marker or 5 NM, whichever comes first. Noise must not choose the runway if the crosswind exceeds 15 kt, the tailwind exceeds 5 kt, or the runway is not clear and dry. Noise abatement 800 ft up; 15 across, 5 behind."},{"t":"What kind of fire, and what puts it out?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#fire","k":"Class A (wood, paper, cloth) is cooled with water; Class B (fuel, oil, hydraulic fluid) needs foam or halon, never water; Class C (electrical) needs a non-conducting agent such as halon; Class D (burning metals) needs special dry powder and never halon. A and C are the commonest on board. Fire classes A Ash, B Barrel, C Current, D Dense metal."},{"t":"Which extinguisher is which?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#extinguishers","k":"Halon (BCF) is green and fights A, B and C fires; CO₂ is black for B and C; water is red for A; dry powder is blue for D, and dry chemical (also blue) for A, B and C. A hand extinguisher lasts only 8–25 seconds, so pick the right one first time. Extinguishers Green Halon, Black CO₂, Red water, Blue powder."},{"t":"The fire triangle and halon rules","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#fire-facts","k":"A fire needs oxygen, heat and fuel; remove any one to put it out. Hand extinguishers empty in 8–25 seconds. Halon is being phased out because it damages the ozone layer; the book notes it remained permitted only in cargo compartments until 28 November 2024. Fire facts "},{"t":"The cabin has lost pressure. What happens and what do you do?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#decomp","k":"A decompression faster than 0.5 seconds is explosive: fog, dust, debris and a dazed crew. The cabin altitude warning sounds above 10,000 ft and passenger masks drop at 13,200–14,000 ft (never above 15,000 ft). Put your own mask on first, then make an emergency descent to about 10,000 ft. The main danger is hypoxia. Decompression Mask, then descend to ten."},{"t":"What does a microburst do to a departing or arriving aircraft?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#shear","k":"First a headwind (you go high and fast), then a downdraught, then a tailwind (you sink and slow): the most dangerous below 1,000 ft. A microburst is about 1 NM across at 2,000 ft and 2–2½ NM at the surface, with downdraughts up to 6,000 ft/min and surface winds of 45 kt (a 90 kt shear). It lasts up to 15 minutes, its strongest winds 2–4 minutes. Microburst Headwind lies, tailwind kills."},{"t":"When is wind shear significant?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#shear-numbers","k":"Significant wind shear is a vector change of more than 25 kt within 500 ft, 40 kt within 1,000 ft or 50 kt within 1,500 ft above the ground, or a pilot report of a 20 kt IAS gain or loss within 1,500 ft. Report it as wind and height above and below the layer, or as the airspeed change and height. Wind shear criteria 25-40-50 at 500-1,000-1,500."},{"t":"Dealing with wind shear","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#shear-do","k":"The best defence is to avoid it. If you meet it, apply full power and pitch to the maximum usable angle of attack, and warn others as soon as possible. Expect more than one microburst in the same area. Wind shear actions "},{"t":"How do wingtip vortices behave?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#wake","k":"Vortices exist only while a wing makes lift and are strongest behind an aircraft that is heavy, clean and slow. Seen from behind, the left vortex turns clockwise and the right anticlockwise. They sink 500–1,000 ft below the flight path and drift with the wind; a light crosswind can park the upwind vortex on the runway, and a light quartering tailwind is the worst case. Wake vortices Heavy, clean, slow = strongest wake."},{"t":"Which wake category?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#wake-cats","k":"Super is the A380-800. Heavy is 136,000 kg or more. Medium is over 7,000 kg and under 136,000 kg. Light is under 7,000 kg. Wake categories 136 tonnes and 7 tonnes."},{"t":"Time-based wake separation","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#wake-time","k":"Arrivals: A380 → Medium 3 min, → Light 4 min; Heavy → Medium 2 min; Heavy or Medium → Light 3 min. Departures: Heavy → Medium/Light 2 min, Medium → Light 2 min (A380 → Medium/Light 3 min); from an intermediate part of the runway add a minute (3 min, A380 4 min). Wake time minima Arrive 2-3 (A380 3-4); depart 2 (intersection 3)."},{"t":"Distance-based wake separation (radar)","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#wake-dist","k":"Behind an A380: Heavy 6 NM, Medium 7 NM, Light 8 NM. Behind a Heavy: Heavy 4 NM, Medium 5 NM, Light 6 NM. Behind a Medium: Light 5 NM. Everything else uses the normal radar minimum. Wake distance minima A380 6-7-8; Heavy 4-5-6; Medium-Light 5."},{"t":"Behind a heavy jet: what should you do?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#wake-avoid","k":"Stay above and upwind of the bigger aircraft's path. Taking off behind it, lift off before its rotation point and climb above its path. Landing behind it, touch down beyond its touchdown point. On an ILS behind it, stay on or above the glide slope. Avoiding wake Above and upwind; early off, late down."},{"t":"Forced, precautionary or ditching?","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#landings","k":"A forced landing is immediate because flight cannot continue (engine failure). A precautionary landing is planned because continuing is possible but unwise (weather, lost, fuel shortage, developing engine trouble). Ditching is a forced or precautionary landing on water. After unlawful interference the commander reports to the local authority and the State of the Operator. Forced vs precautionary Forced is now; precautionary is planned; ditching is on water."},{"t":"Dumping fuel","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#dump","k":"Tell ATC; dump clear of towns, preferably over water and away from storms, no lower than 6,000 ft. Other traffic stays 10 NM away horizontally (not behind), or if behind within 15 minutes or 50 NM, at least 1,000 ft above or 3,000 ft below. Fuel jettison 6,000 up; 10 NM aside; 1,000 above, 3,000 below."},{"t":"Minimum level: 6,000 ft","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#dump","k":"1,800 m"},{"t":"Lateral separation: 10 NM","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#dump","k":"not behind"},{"t":"Zone behind: 15 min or 50 NM","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#dump","k":""},{"t":"Others above: ≥ 1,000 ft","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#dump","k":""},{"t":"Others below: ≥ 3,000 ft","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#dump","k":""},{"t":"Wet runways and hydroplaning","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#runway","k":"If the runway may be wet at arrival, the landing distance available must be at least 115% of the required landing distance. Dynamic hydroplaning happens when standing water is deeper than the tyre tread: the tyre rides on the water, braking and steering are almost lost, and anti-skid helps. Wet runways Wet = 115%."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Ice roughness that matters: 0.4 mm","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Bird strikes below 500 m: 90%","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Noise abatement not below: 800 ft","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Noise runway limits: X-wind 15, tail 5 kt","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Extinguisher discharge: 8–25 s","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Explosive decompression: < 0.5 s","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Masks drop: 13,200–14,000 ft","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Microburst life: ≤ 15 min","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Heavy / Light: ≥ 136 t / < 7 t","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Heavy → Light arrival: 3 min / 6 NM","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fuel dump level: ≥ 6,000 ft","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Wet runway factor: 115%","s":"Reg 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 18 · Special Operational Procedures and Hazards","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"MEL and flight manual (p.481)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Icing (p.481–483)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Bird strikes (p.483)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Noise abatement (p.484)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fire and smoke (p.484–486)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hot brakes (p.486)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Decompression (p.486–487)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wind shear and microbursts (p.487–488)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wake turbulence (p.488–493)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other hazards (p.493–495)","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 18 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 19. Communications","s":"How to talk on the radio so nothing is misunderstood: the code words, the order of priority, how to say numbers, what you must read back, how call signs shorten, and which frequencies and bands carry what.","h":"chapters/reg-19.html","k":"Annex 10 · Q-codes · priorities · phraseology · call signs · readability · weather · frequencies · CPDLC "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What does each Q-code ask for?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#qcodes","k":"QDM is the magnetic heading to steer to reach the station (no wind); QDR your magnetic bearing from it; QTE your true bearing from it; QUJ the true track to it. QFE gives height above the aerodrome, QNH elevation (altitude), QNE the reading on landing with 1013.2 set, QFU the runway in use. Q-codes QDM: Magnetic heading to Me. QDR: Radial (from). QTE: True Edition of QDR."},{"t":"Which Q-code do you need?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#q-stamp","k":"QDM is the magnetic heading to steer to the station (nil wind); QDR the magnetic bearing from it; QTE the true bearing from it (for plotting); QFE gives zero on landing, QNH the elevation; QFU is the runway direction. Pick the Q-code QDM to, QDR from, QTE true from."},{"t":"Which message goes first?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#priority","k":"Distress (MAYDAY) first, then urgency (PAN PAN, including PAN PAN MEDICAL), then direction-finding, flight safety, meteorological, and finally flight regularity messages. Message priority Drowning Urgently, Directions Safely, Weather Regularly."},{"t":"How is each kind of number spoken?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers-rt","k":"Flight levels, headings, wind, runways, QNH and frequencies are spoken digit by digit. Altitudes, heights, cloud heights, visibility and RVR in whole hundreds or thousands use HUNDRED and TOUSAND. Times use just the minutes unless confusing; time checks are to the nearest half minute in UTC. Saying numbers Levels and headings digit by digit; heights in hundreds and thousands."},{"t":"Which standard word fits?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#phrases","k":"ROGER only means \"received all of your last transmission\" and never answers a read-back or a yes/no question. AFFIRM is yes, NEGATIVE is no, WILCO is \"understood and will comply\", STANDBY is \"wait, I will call you\" (neither yes nor no). GO AHEAD is no longer used; OVER and OUT are not normally used on VHF. Standard words ROGER heard it, WILCO will do it, AFFIRM yes to it."},{"t":"Phrases that are easy to mix up","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#phrase-pairs","k":"BREAK separates parts of one message; BREAK BREAK separates messages to different aircraft. CHECK means examine a system and needs no answer; CONFIRM asks for verification. NEGATIVE I SAY AGAIN corrects a wrong read-back. MONITOR means listen out on a frequency; CONTACT means establish communication. Easily confused words "},{"t":"Full and abbreviated aircraft call signs","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#callsigns","k":"A registration call sign VTEJP may shorten to VJP (first character and at least the last two). An operator plus registration (AIRINDIA TEJP) shortens to the operator plus at least the last two (AIRINDIA JP). An operator plus flight number (AIRINDIA 809) is never abbreviated. Abbreviate only after the ground station has done so and no confusion is possible. Call signs Flight numbers never shrink."},{"t":"Which call-sign suffix for which station?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#stations","k":"CONTROL is an ACC, APPROACH is approach, TOWER is aerodrome control, GROUND is surface movement, DELIVERY is clearance delivery, INFORMATION is a flight information service, RADIO an aeronautical station, HOMER a DF station and PRECISION a precision approach radar. Station suffixes Control, Approach, Tower, Ground, Delivery: from the airways down to the gate."},{"t":"The readability scale","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#readability","k":"Readability 1 is unreadable, 2 readable now and then, 3 readable but with difficulty, 4 readable, 5 perfectly readable. Test transmissions must not last more than 10 seconds. Readability scale Three = with difficulty; five = perfect."},{"t":"Read-backs, frequency changes and tests","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#readback","k":"Read back route clearances, runway clearances (enter, land, take off, hold short, cross, taxi, backtrack) and runway in use, altimeter settings, SSR codes, level, heading and speed instructions and transition levels; QNH always. In controlled airspace get permission before changing frequency. Keep test transmissions under 10 seconds. Read-back and frequency change If it changes your path, read it back."},{"t":"Reading traffic information","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#radar-rt","k":"Traffic is given as a clock bearing (or cardinal direction if you are turning), distance, its direction of flight, and its speed, type or level. ATC tells you when the conflict is over; avoiding action is given only when collision risk is imminent. Traffic information Clock, distance, direction, then type or level."},{"t":"Weather terms on the radio","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":"FEW is 1–2 oktas, SCT 3–4, BKN 5–7, OVC 8. CAVOK needs visibility of 10 km or more, no cloud below 5,000 ft or the highest MSA, no CB and no significant weather. A TREND covers the next 2 hours. VOLMET is broadcast on HF by Mumbai and Kolkata. Weather on the radio "},{"t":"FEW: 1–2 oktas","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"SCT: 3–4 oktas","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"BKN: 5–7 oktas","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"OVC: 8 oktas","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"CAVOK visibility: ≥ 10 km","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"CAVOK cloud: none < 5,000 ft","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":"or highest MSA"},{"t":"TREND covers: 2 hours","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"VOLMET (India): HF: Mumbai, Kolkata","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"ADS-CPDLC (India): Mumbai, Delhi, Kolkata, Chennai","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#wx-rt","k":""},{"t":"Emergency frequencies and codes","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":"121.5 MHz (VHF) and 243 MHz (UHF) are the aeronautical distress frequencies, 2182 kHz the international MF distress frequency and 500 kHz the maritime one. ELTs transmit on 121.5, 243 and 406 MHz. Stay on the frequency where the distress began unless moving helps. Distress frequencies 121.5 is half of 243."},{"t":"VHF distress: 121.5 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":""},{"t":"UHF distress: 243 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":""},{"t":"MF distress: 2182 kHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":""},{"t":"Maritime distress: 500 kHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":""},{"t":"ELT: 121.5 / 243 / 406 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":""},{"t":"SSR: 7700 / 7600 / 7500","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#distress-f","k":""},{"t":"Position reports and special air-reports","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#reports","k":"A position report gives identification, position, time, level, next position and time, and the ensuing significant point. A special air-report (AIRREP SPECIAL) reports moderate or severe turbulence or icing, severe mountain waves, thunderstorms (with or without hail), heavy dust or sand storms, volcanic ash or pre-eruption activity. Heavy aircraft add HEAVY (A380: SUPER) on first contact. Position reports Who, where, when, how high, what next."},{"t":"Which band does each system use?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#bands","k":"ADF uses LF/MF (190–1750 kHz); HF comms 2–25 MHz; markers 75 MHz; localiser 108.1–111.95 and VOR 108–117.95 MHz; VHF comms 118–137 MHz; glidepath 329–335 MHz (UHF); DME 960–1213 MHz; SSR 1030/1090 MHz; GPS L1 1575.42 MHz; radio altimeter 4.2–4.4 GHz and weather radar 9375 MHz (SHF). Frequency bands Low for the ADF, VHF for talking, UHF for the glide path."},{"t":"The frequency bands","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#band-names","k":"Each band is ten times the last: VLF 3–30 kHz, LF 30–300 kHz, MF 300 kHz–3 MHz, HF 3–30 MHz, VHF 30–300 MHz, UHF 300 MHz–3 GHz, SHF 3–30 GHz, EHF 30–300 GHz. Wavelength = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ frequency. Band ranges "},{"t":"How far does a radio signal reach?","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#propagation","k":"VLF and LF travel far as ground waves; MF and HF use sky waves bent back by the ionosphere; VHF and above are line of sight. Line-of-sight range in NM is 1.25 × (√ transmitter height + √ receiver height) in feet. Range grows with the square root of power, so doubling range needs four times the power. Range formulas 1.25 √h; four times the power for twice the range."},{"t":"Line-of-sight range","s":"Reg 19 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#propagation","k":"Heights in feet. An aircraft at 10,000 ft and a station at sea level: about 125 NM."},{"t":"Wavelength","s":"Reg 19 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#propagation","k":"c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s. 300 MHz → 1 m."},{"t":"Range and power","s":"Reg 19 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#propagation","k":"Twice the range needs four times the power."},{"t":"CPDLC, ADS-C and SATCOM","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#datalink","k":"CPDLC is a two-way data link for non-time-critical strategic messages that supplements voice and is chosen beyond VHF range. ADS-C/CPDLC (FANS-1A) works at Chennai, Mumbai, Delhi and Kolkata; log on 10 minutes before entering the FIR. SATVOICE is accepted alongside HF as a long-range system; RCP n is the round-trip time in seconds. CPDLC and SATCOM "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Test transmission: ≤ 10 s","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Readability scale: 1–5","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Time checks: nearest ½ min","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VHF distress: 121.5 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"MF distress: 2182 kHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VHF comms: 118–137 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"SSR: 1030 / 1090 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Glidepath: 329–335 MHz","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"LOS range: 1.25(√h₁+√h₂)","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CPDLC log-on: 10 min before FIR","s":"Reg 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 19 · Communications","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Abbreviations and Q-codes (p.521–523)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Message categories, in priority order (p.523)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transmitting numbers (p.523–525)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Standard words (p.525–528)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Station call signs (p.528)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Read-back, frequency change, tests (p.528–529)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft call signs (p.529)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Radar phraseology (p.529–530)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Weather (p.530–531)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"India: channels and broadcasts (p.531–532)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Distress (p.532)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"IFR communications and reports (p.532–535)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Radio waves (p.535–538)","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 19 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 20. Aircraft Accident and Incident","s":"When something goes wrong, what kind of event it legally is, who must be told within 24 hours, who investigates, and why you must not touch the wreckage.","h":"chapters/reg-20.html","k":"Annex 13 · accident · serious incident · serious injury · AAIB · 24-hour notification "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Is this an accident?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#classify","k":"Between boarding and disembarkation, it is an accident if someone is killed (within 30 days) or seriously injured by the aircraft, its parts or jet blast; if the aircraft suffers damage affecting its strength, performance or handling (but not damage limited to one engine, tyres, brakes, small dents, or from bird strike or hail); or if the aircraft is missing. Accident or not? Dead, Damaged or Disappeared."},{"t":"Serious incident or ordinary incident?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#serious-inc","k":"A serious incident is one where an accident very nearly happened: a near collision needing avoidance, CFIT barely avoided, take-off or landing on a closed or wrong runway, fire or smoke anywhere, crew needing emergency oxygen, crew incapacitation, fuel emergencies, severity-A runway incursions, runway excursions, uncontained engine failures. Serious incident? A serious incident is an accident that nearly happened."},{"t":"What counts as a serious injury?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#injury","k":"A serious injury needs hospital for more than 48 hours starting within 7 days, a fracture (not simple fingers, toes or nose), severe lacerations, internal organ injury, second or third degree burns or burns over 5% of the body, or exposure to infectious substances or harmful radiation. A fatal injury causes death within 30 days. Serious injury 48 hours, 7 days, 5%, 30 days."},{"t":"Who investigates, and who joins in?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#states","k":"The State of Occurrence starts the investigation (in India, the DG of the Aircraft Accident Investigation Bureau, whatever the registration). The States of Registry, Operator, Design and Manufacture may take part; any State that provides help on request may send an accredited representative. The final report goes to the States concerned and ICAO. Who investigates Where it happened investigates; the rest join in."},{"t":"Who must be told, and how fast?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#notify","k":"The PIC (or the owner, operator or hirer if the PIC is killed or incapacitated) notifies AAIB and DGCA by the quickest means within 24 hours, and for events in India also the District Magistrate and the nearest police station. The aerodrome operator and ATS in-charge also report to AAIB. Notification Within a day: AAIB, DGCA, DM, police."},{"t":"May the wreckage be moved?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#wreckage","k":"The Investigator-in-Charge controls the wreckage, recorders and ATS records. Nothing may be moved except to rescue people or animals, prevent fire or other destruction, clear an obstruction to the public or transport, or bring wreckage on water to safety; goods, baggage and mail only under supervision. CVR and image recordings may not be used for disciplinary or legal action. Touch the wreckage? Touch nothing except to save life or prevent more harm."},{"t":"Does this damage make it an accident?","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#damage","k":"Damage is an accident only if it affects the aircraft's strength, performance or handling. Damage limited to one engine (cowlings, accessories), propellers, wing tips, antennas, tyres, brakes, small dents, or caused by hail or a bird strike, is not. Damage: accident or not? Birds, hail, one engine, small bits: not an accident."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Fatal injury: death within: 30 days","s":"Reg 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hospitalisation: > 48 h","s":"Reg 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":"starting within 7 days"},{"t":"Burns: > 5% of body","s":"Reg 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Notify AAIB / DGCA: 24 h","s":"Reg 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ATS incident report: 24 h","s":"Reg 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Design/manufacture rep. for aircraft over: 100,000 kg","s":"Reg 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 20 · Aircraft Accident and Incident","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Annex 13 (p.555)","s":"Reg 20 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Definitions (p.555–558)","s":"Reg 20 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reporting and investigation in India (p.558–560)","s":"Reg 20 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 20 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 21. Facilitation","s":"How an international flight, its crew, passengers and cargo get through the airport and the border quickly, with the least paperwork.","h":"chapters/reg-21.html","k":"Annex 9 · aircraft documents · General Declaration · Crew Member Certificate · customs in India "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Facilitation words","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#defs","k":"Cargo excludes mail, stores and baggage. A deportee was in a State lawfully or not and is ordered to leave. A direct transit area lets passengers transit without entering the State. Embarkation and disembarkation exclude people continuing on the same through-flight. Definitions "},{"t":"May a State demand it?","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#docs","k":"A State may ask for a General Declaration and a cargo manifest in the Annex 9 formats, in electronic or paper form, and no more than three copies. It should not normally require a passenger manifest and shall not require declarations of stores remaining on board, baggage lists, extra mail forms, or any visa or fee for aircraft documents. Documents demanded GD and cargo yes; stores, bags and visas no; three copies max."},{"t":"Crew Member Certificates and crew visas","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#crew","k":"A Crew Member Certificate is an identity card, separate from the licence. States waive the visa for crew presenting a CMC who arrive on duty to join their next flight (and should also when they arrive by other means). Technical staff needed urgently to repair a stranded aircraft get temporary entry without delay. Crew Member Certificate A CMC is an ID card, not a licence."},{"t":"Which Indian authority wants which document?","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#india","k":"Customs wants the General Declaration and cargo manifest; immigration the General Declaration and passenger manifest; health the General Declaration only. No flight leaves India without immigration and customs clearance on the General Declaration. Documents in India General Declaration for everyone; cargo manifest to customs, passenger manifest to immigration."},{"t":"Flying into and out of India","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#india-rules","k":"International flights make their first landing and final departure at an international (or notified customs) aerodrome, and may not carry traffic between two Indian points. A foreign aircraft visiting for a flight and leaving within 6 months pays no customs duty with a written declaration. Export cargo is presented 4 working hours before departure. Indian entry rules First and last at an international airport; 6 months duty-free."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#nums","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Copies of any document: max 3","s":"Reg 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Duty-free stay of a foreign aircraft: 6 months","s":"Reg 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#nums","k":"written declaration on arrival"},{"t":"Export cargo presented: 4 working hours","s":"Reg 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#nums","k":"before departure"},{"t":"Visa for aircraft documents: none","s":"Reg 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Crew visa with a CMC on duty: waived","s":"Reg 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Green or red channel?","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#channels","k":"Nothing dutiable: walk through the green channel. Anything dutiable: the red channel. Crew declare currency, gold and electronics on arrival and departure; transit passengers stay in the customs area. Green or red channel Green to go, red to declare."},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 21 · Facilitation","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Definitions (p.565–566)","s":"Reg 21 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Annex 9 (p.566–568)","s":"Reg 21 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"India (p.568–572)","s":"Reg 21 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 21 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 22. Security","s":"What a State must do to keep civil aviation safe from hijacking and sabotage, what happens when a seized aircraft lands, and who handles security in India.","h":"chapters/reg-22.html","k":"Annex 17 · unlawful interference · seized aircraft · screening · BCAS "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How Annex 17 came about","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#history","k":"A wave of violent crime against aviation in the late 1960s led to an Extraordinary ICAO Assembly in June 1970 and then Annex 17. Until 1985 the main threat was hijacking; later amendments covered sabotage, baggage reconciliation, cargo and mail, couriers, cockpit protection and response management. History "},{"t":"A hijacked aircraft lands in your State. What must you do?","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#seized","k":"Help it with navigation aids, ATS and permission to land; once landed, keep it on the ground unless letting it go is needed to protect lives; and notify the State of Registry, the State of the Operator, every State whose citizens were killed, injured, held hostage or are on board, and ICAO. Seized aircraft Help it land, hold it there, tell everyone."},{"t":"Screening and special passengers","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#pax","k":"Potentially disruptive passengers board before everyone else. If screened passengers mix with unscreened people after screening, they and their cabin bags are screened again. Armed foreign officers may fly only after the State of embarkation notifies the destination State and the PIC is told. Passenger security Disruptive passengers board first."},{"t":"Who runs aviation security in India?","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#india","k":"BCAS, headed by a Director General, regulates aviation security under the Aircraft (Security) Rules 2011. The Commissioner of Security (Civil Aviation) is the Annex 17 authority and runs the National Civil Aviation Security Programme. Security incidents are reported to the Commissioner immediately. Security in India BCAS regulates; the Commissioner commands."},{"t":"After a hijacked aircraft lands: told or not?","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#notify","k":"The landing State keeps the seized aircraft unless letting it go protects lives, and informs the State of Registry, the State of the Operator, every State whose citizens were killed, injured, held or on board, and ICAO. Who is told Registry, Operator, the people's States, and ICAO."},{"t":"Dates and names to know","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Extraordinary ICAO Assembly: June 1970","s":"Reg 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Focus only on hijacking until: 1985","s":"Reg 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft (Security) Rules: 2011","s":"Reg 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Indian regulator: BCAS","s":"Reg 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":"headed by a DG"},{"t":"Annex 17 authority in India: Commissioner of Security (CA)","s":"Reg 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Inquiry officer at least: Asst Commissioner of Security","s":"Reg 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#nums","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 22 · Security","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Annex 17 (p.575–577)","s":"Reg 22 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"India (p.577–578)","s":"Reg 22 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 22 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 23. Human Performance and Limitations","s":"Why the human is both the strongest and the weakest link in aviation, which sciences Human Factors draws on, and what accident statistics say about where and why things go wrong.","h":"chapters/reg-23.html","k":"human error · disciplines · accident causes · the body in flight "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Why is the human both the best and the worst part of the system?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#human","k":"The human is the most flexible, adaptable and valuable part of aviation, and also the most vulnerable. About three accidents in four come from less-than-optimum human performance. Human error is now seen as part of how the mind works and is generally inescapable. Strong and weak Three in four accidents start with a human."},{"t":"Which science answers which question?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#disciplines","k":"Human Factors is multidisciplinary and problem-oriented. Psychology covers the mind and errors, engineering the machine, physiology and medicine the body and health, sociology groups and crews, anthropometry body size and reach. Disciplines Mind, machine, body, health, group, size."},{"t":"Which discipline would you ask?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#disc-stamp","k":"Reach and seat size: anthropometry. How the brain handles information: psychology. Effects of hypoxia: physiology. Crew make-up: sociology. Smoke toxicity: medicine. Jet lag: chronobiology. Which discipline "},{"t":"What makes a professional pilot?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#pilot","k":"Beyond handling skill and theory, a professional pilot needs situation awareness, communication, threat and error management, decision making, teamwork, stress management, fitness, reliability and a balanced personality. Professional pilot "},{"t":"How is a typical flight's time split?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#phase-time","k":"The book splits flight time into about 2% (departure end), 83% (climb, cruise and descent) and 15% (approach and landing). Approach and landing take little time but account for the largest share of accidents. Flight time Short phases, big risk."},{"t":"What do accident statistics say?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#causes","k":"About 1 accident per million airport movements. Human factors are a major cause in over 73% of accidents, a figure unchanged since the 1950s. CFIT is the most common type of accident, and most happen during approach and landing. Why accidents happen Seventy-three percent, since the fifties."},{"t":"What does flight demand of the body?","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#body","k":"Flight brings large changes in barometric pressure and temperature and fast movement in three dimensions. The body compensates for low oxygen by breathing faster, changing blood chemistry and making more red cells, but with no oxygen at all death follows in about 5–8 minutes. Body in flight Pressure, temperature, three-D speed."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Accidents from human performance: ≈ 3 in 4","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Human factors cited: > 73%","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Unchanged since: 1950s","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Accident rate: ≈ 1 per million movements","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Most common accident: CFIT","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flight time split: 2% · 83% · 15%","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"No oxygen → death: 5–8 min","s":"Reg 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 23 · Human Performance and Limitations","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Why it matters (p.583)","s":"Reg 23 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The disciplines (p.583–585)","s":"Reg 23 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The professional pilot (p.585)","s":"Reg 23 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Accidents (p.585–586)","s":"Reg 23 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The body in flight (p.586)","s":"Reg 23 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 23 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 24. CRM, TEM and LOFT","s":"How crews use every resource they have, how the SHEL model explains where errors come from, how threats and errors are managed, and why LOFT is training rather than a test.","h":"chapters/reg-24.html","k":"crew resource management · SHEL model · threats and errors · LOFT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What is Crew Resource Management?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#crm","k":"CRM is the effective use of all available resources (people, procedures, equipment and oneself) for safe and efficient operation. It is about cognitive and interpersonal skills, not stick-and-rudder skill, and applies to single-pilot flying too. Tenerife 1977 made it a full programme. What CRM is Use everything and everyone."},{"t":"What is the SHEL model?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#shel","k":"SHEL (Edwards, 1972; ICAO Doc 9859) stands for Software, Hardware, Environment and Liveware. The human (Liveware) sits at the hub and everything else must be matched to it. Errors come from mismatches at the interfaces as much as from the blocks themselves. SHEL model The human in the middle; trouble at the edges."},{"t":"What is in each block?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#shel-parts","k":"Software: procedures, manuals, checklists, symbology, maps. Hardware: flight deck, instruments, controls. Environment: inside and outside the workplace. Liveware: the human, the most critical and flexible part; the second L is other people. SHEL blocks "},{"t":"Which SHEL interface does it mismatch?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#interface","k":"Controls, displays and cramped seats are Liveware–Hardware; checklists, manuals and charts Liveware–Software; noise, heat and body-clock disruption Liveware–Environment; communication and crew pairing Liveware–Liveware. Name the interface SHEL: Software, Hardware, Environment, Liveware around the person."},{"t":"What affects the individual (the central L)?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#factors","k":"Factors in the individual are physical, physiological, psychological and psychosocial. The effect of the physiological and psychosocial factors is the most pronounced. Individual factors "},{"t":"What did technology change?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#automation","k":"GPWS, introduced in the 1980s, brought a substantial drop in hull-loss rates. Automation does not remove error: it relocates the potential for error, can increase workload and gives rise to new forms of error. GPWS and automation "},{"t":"What makes CRM effective?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#foundations","k":"Nobody has the full picture, so everyone contributes. Errors are treated as normal and responded to without punishment. Good programmes are operationally relevant, use the airline's own incidents, and examine threats and both good and bad error management. Foundations No one has the whole picture."},{"t":"The four aims of CRM training","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#aims","k":"CRM training aims at error avoidance, threat management, error management and undesired aircraft state management. Four aims Avoid, manage threats, manage errors, manage the state."},{"t":"The CRM skills and what they look like","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#skills","k":"The CRM skills include leadership, decision making, communication, situation awareness, team building, workload and automation management, vigilance, briefings, setting bottom lines, contingency management, evaluating plans and assertiveness. CRM skills "},{"t":"Steep, shallow or right?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#gradient","k":"The trans-cockpit authority gradient is the authority relationship between captain and first officer. A domineering captain with an unassertive F/O makes it steep; two captains together make it shallow. The aim is recognised authority with good working relationships. Authority gradient Steep silences; flat stalls; balanced works."},{"t":"The six kinds of threat","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#threats","k":"Threats come from outside the crew's influence but must be managed: environmental, ATC, aircraft, crew support, operational and cabin. TEM accepts that threats and errors will happen and manages them rather than trying to eliminate them. Threats CRM manages resources; TEM manages threats."},{"t":"Which TEM error is it?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#errors","k":"TEM has five error types: intentional non-compliance (wilful), procedural (right intention, wrong execution, or forgetting), communication, proficiency (knowledge or handling skill) and operational decision (a needlessly risky choice). Error types Wilful, slip, said, skill, choice."},{"t":"When is a choice an operational decision error?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#decision","k":"A decision counts as an operational decision error if at least one of three things was true: a more conservative option was available and not taken; the decision was not shared aloud; or there was time to evaluate it that was not used. Unmanaged errors can lead to an undesired aircraft state. Decision error test "},{"t":"What is Line-Oriented Flight Training?","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#loft","k":"LOFT is non-jeopardy, facilitated, full-mission simulator training that mirrors line flying and stresses communication, management and leadership. Scenarios often come from accident and incident reports. It validates training and procedures and is never a check of individuals. LOFT Real-time, real-mission, no-fail."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"SHEL model: Edwards, 1972","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ICAO manual: Doc 9859 (SMM)","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CRM catalyst: Tenerife 1977","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"GPWS cut hull losses: 1980s","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aims of CRM: 4","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Threat types: 6","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TEM error types: 5","s":"Reg 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 24 · CRM, TEM and LOFT","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"CRM (p.589–590)","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SHEL model (p.590–596)","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Making CRM work (p.597–598)","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"CRM skills (p.598–600)","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"TEM (p.600–602)","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"LOFT (p.602–603)","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 24 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 25. Aviation Psychology","s":"How a pilot's mind takes in, stores and acts on information, where it breaks down under too much or too little load, and how stress, fatigue, body clock and sleep shape performance.","h":"chapters/reg-25.html","k":"workload · memory · attention · vigilance · stress · fatigue · circadian rhythm · sleep "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What drives workload, and what does overload look like?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#workload","k":"Workload depends on task difficulty, tasks in parallel, tasks in series and the time available. About 60% of crew resources is an acceptable load. Overload shows as a sharp drop in performance, funnelled attention, mental blocking, mood swings and panic. Thorough flight planning is the key to reducing cockpit workload. Workload Hard, many, switching, rushed."},{"t":"How does the brain turn a stimulus into an action?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#model","k":"Information is detected by the senses, perceived (given meaning), decided on by the central decision maker using short- and long-term memory, acted on, and the result fed back. A failure at any stage affects the outcome. Processing model Sense, make sense, choose, do, check."},{"t":"Sensory, short-term and long-term memory","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#memory","k":"Sensory memory is separate for each sense and decays fast (iconic memory 0.5–1 s). Short-term memory holds 7 ± 2 items for 10–20 s unless rehearsed. Long-term memory stores indefinitely but retrieval can fail; it holds semantic, episodic and procedural memory. Three memories One second, twenty seconds, forever."},{"t":"Which kind of long-term memory?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#ltm","k":"Semantic memory is general knowledge, episodic memory is events in your own life, and procedural memory is skills such as flying a coordinated turn. Which memory "},{"t":"How does attention work, and why is it the weak point?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#attention","k":"Attention is limited and deals with one item at a time, so pilots must prioritise; poor management of attention is the major danger. Selective attention samples inputs for relevance; divided attention time-shares several activities. The cocktail party effect is picking up relevant information without meaning to. Attention One thing at a time, very fast."},{"t":"Hypo, optimum and hyper vigilance","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#vigilance","k":"Hypo-vigilance comes from monotony, tiredness, need for sleep and lack of stimulation, showing as reduced perception and muscular heaviness, with micro-sleeps. Hyper-vigilance is an agitated, panicky state that leads to poor decisions. The optimum lies between them. Complacency is a related danger with automation. Vigilance Too bored, just right, too wound up."},{"t":"The Anderson model: how does a skill develop?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#skills","k":"A motor programme (skill) develops through the cognitive, associative and automatic (autonomous) phases. Once learnt it is stored in long-term memory and runs without conscious thought. Learning a skill Think it, link it, forget you're doing it."},{"t":"Reflexes, reaction time and startle","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#reflex","k":"Reflexes are unconditioned (blinking), conditioned (learned) or trained (improved by practice). A startle reflex jerks the body and interrupts the task, but a skilled task returns to normal within 5–10 seconds. Fear makes startle stronger. Reflexes and startle "},{"t":"The steps of a good decision","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#decide","k":"Recognise the problem, understand its nature, gather information, list options, pick the best, act, follow up and communicate, then get feedback. A good decision a minute ago may not be good two minutes later. Once you have an explanation, beware ignoring information that does not fit. Making a decision "},{"t":"Six things that spoil situational awareness","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#sa","k":"Stress, boredom, fatigue, emotional disturbance, poor communication and interruptions all interfere with situational awareness. Gather information, don't rush, consider every option, and don't see what you want to see. SA spoilers "},{"t":"What does automation do to the pilot?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#auto","k":"Automation makes the pilot a manager of interfaces. It relocates error rather than removing it and often makes the role more demanding. Manual workload falls but mental workload may not, especially at busy terminals. Reliable automation breeds complacency and skill loss. Pilots and machines are complementary. Automation Automation moves error; it doesn't delete it."},{"t":"How is a cockpit designed around people?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#design","k":"Murphy's law: if it can be operated wrongly, it will be, so standardisation is the most important design requirement. Cockpits fit the central 90% of people, are built around the design eye position, and use digital displays for quantities and analogue ones for trends and orientation. Cockpit design If it can go wrong, design it out."},{"t":"How does arousal affect performance?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#arousal","k":"Performance follows an inverted U. Too little arousal (cruise) lets attention wander; too much narrows attention to a funnel. The optimum is lower for difficult thinking tasks and higher for endurance tasks. Moderate stress can improve performance. Arousal curve Bored and panicked both fly badly."},{"t":"Where does stress come from, and how do you manage it?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#stress","k":"Stress comes when demands exceed capability, when no solution seems possible, or from hunger, thirst, pain and lack of sleep; stressors add up. It boosts physical strength rather than thinking. Signs include confusion, poor coordination and fast high-pitched speech. Manage it by recognising it, accepting it and having a coping strategy: know your limits and fly well inside them. Stress Recognise, accept, cope."},{"t":"How much stress do life events add?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#life","k":"Life events are scored and added: death of a spouse 100, divorce 73, separation 65, job loss 47, moving 20. A total under 60 means free of life stress, 60–80 normal, 80–100 high, over 100 serious. Treat with caution: people vary widely. Life stress Under 60 free, over 100 serious."},{"t":"Tiredness or fatigue?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#fatigue","k":"Tiredness follows a normal day and a good night's sleep cures it. Fatigue is a very deep tiredness from the cumulative effects of a stressful life or environment; it creeps up and sufferers often deny it. Symptoms: memory lapses, mistakes, mood swings, reduced awareness, poor coordination. Tiredness or fatigue Sleep cures tiredness, not fatigue."},{"t":"What happens when, over a typical day?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#clock","k":"The body clock in the hypothalamus runs a daily cycle. Body temperature is lowest about 04:30 and highest about 19:00. Melatonin starts about 21:00, peaks 02:00–04:00 and stops about 07:30. Alertness peaks mid-morning, coordination and reaction time in the afternoon. Circadian day Coldest at half past four, hottest at seven."},{"t":"How long to adapt after crossing time zones?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#jetlag","k":"Rhythms re-synchronise at about 1–1.5 h a day. Allow about 1.5 days per zone eastbound and 1 day per zone westbound: going east is harder. Over 3–4 zones with a layover over 24 h, the book advises keeping to the departure country's rhythm as long as possible. The free-running clock is about 25 h. Jet lag East is a beast, west is best."},{"t":"Eastbound","s":"Reg 25 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#jetlag","k":""},{"t":"Westbound","s":"Reg 25 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#jetlag","k":""},{"t":"Daily shift","s":"Reg 25 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#jetlag","k":""},{"t":"The stages of sleep, from lightest to deepest","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#sleep","k":"NREM restores the body and REM restores the brain. Stage 1 is the transition, stage 2 light sleep (about half the night), stages 3–4 deep slow-wave sleep. REM first comes 70–90 minutes in, with rapid eye movements, waking-like brain waves and dreams; later cycles have more REM. Sleep stages NREM fixes the body, REM fixes the mind."},{"t":"What does losing sleep do?","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#sleep-facts","k":"Sleep loss brings fatigue, poor concentration and attention, sensory illusions and mood disorders, and it is worse at altitude and under high workload. Sufferers rarely notice their own decline. Sleep inertia can last up to 20 minutes after a nap. Sleep length depends mainly on where in the body clock you go to sleep. Sleep loss "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Iconic memory: 0.5–1 s","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Short-term memory: 10–20 s, 7 ± 2 items","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Information by sight: 70–80%","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Acceptable workload: ≈ 60%","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Error rate (practised): 1 in 100 → 1 in 1,000","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Startle recovery: 5–10 s","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Design population: central 90%","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Life stress free: < 60 points","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Serious life stress: > 100 points","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Lowest body temperature: ≈ 04:30","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Free-running clock: ≈ 25 h","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Resync rate: 1–1.5 h / day","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"East / west adaptation: 1.5 / 1 day per zone","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"First REM: 70–90 min","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Stage 2 share: ≈ 50%","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Nap inertia: up to 20 min","s":"Reg 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 25 · Aviation Psychology","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Aim and human error (p.609)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Workload (p.610)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Information processing (p.610–612)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Attention and vigilance (p.613–614)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Skills and reflexes (p.614–615)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reliability and memory (p.616–617)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Response and cognition (p.618)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Decisions, SA and communication (p.619–621)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Automation (p.621–623)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"People and design (p.624–626)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Stress (p.627–629)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fatigue and arousal (p.629–630)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Circadian rhythm and jet lag (p.631–633)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Sleep (p.633–634)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mental health (p.634–635)","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 25 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Reg 26. Aviation Physiology","s":"What altitude, pressure, acceleration and the cockpit environment do to the body, how the eyes and ears can mislead you, and the health rules that keep a pilot fit to fly.","h":"chapters/reg-26.html","k":"gas laws · hypoxia · decompression · eye · ear · illusions · G · health "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which gas law explains what?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#gaslaws","k":"Boyle: gas expands as pressure falls, so trapped gas hurts. Henry: less gas stays dissolved at low pressure, giving decompression sickness. Dalton: oxygen's partial pressure falls with altitude, giving hypoxia. Graham: gases diffuse from high to low, which is gas exchange. Charles: pressure rises with temperature. Gas laws Boyle bloats, Henry fizzes, Dalton starves, Graham swaps."},{"t":"Which law is at work?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#law-stamp","k":"Ear pain on descent and gut expansion: Boyle. Bends: Henry. Hypoxia although air is still 21% oxygen: Dalton. Oxygen moving into the blood: Graham. Name the law "},{"t":"How much oxygen is left at each height?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#o2","k":"Oxygen is always 21% of the air, but its partial pressure falls with pressure: about 76% of the sea-level amount at 8,000 ft, half at 18,000 ft and a quarter at 36,000 ft. Pressure falls fastest low down. Oxygen with height Eighteen is half, thirty-six is a quarter."},{"t":"What do you breathe at each altitude?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#zones","k":"Air alone to 10,000 ft; an air-oxygen mix to about 33,700 ft; 100% oxygen to 40,000 ft; 100% oxygen under pressure above that. The physiological deficient zone is 12,000–50,000 ft; above 63,000 ft body fluids boil. Oxygen zones Ten, thirty-four, forty, sixty-three."},{"t":"The body systems a pilot should know","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#body","k":"Breathing (14–18 a minute) is driven by the CO₂ level in the blood. The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood through the aorta; cardiac output is about 5 litres a minute and the resting pulse 60–80. Red cells carry haemoglobin; platelets clot; white cells fight infection. Blood pressure 120/80 is normal and hypertension is a major cause of unfitness. Lungs, heart, blood "},{"t":"The four kinds of hypoxia","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#hyp-types","k":"Hypoxic hypoxia: too little oxygen pressure (altitude). Anaemic: blood cannot carry it (anaemia, bleeding, carbon monoxide). Stagnant: blood not flowing (cold, low blood pressure, G). Histotoxic: cells cannot use it (cyanide, drugs, alcohol). Hypoxia types Air, blood, flow, cells."},{"t":"Which type of hypoxia?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#hyp-stamp","k":"CO poisoning is anaemic, high G is stagnant, alcohol is histotoxic and climbing without oxygen is hypoxic hypoxia. Which hypoxia "},{"t":"Hypoxia or hyperventilation?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#hyp-hyper","k":"Both cause dizziness and tingling. Hypoxia is lack of oxygen, insidious, often with euphoria; act with oxygen and descend below 10,000 ft. Hyperventilation is over-breathing that drops CO₂; below 10,000 ft assume it is hyperventilation and slow the breathing to 10–12 a minute. 100% oxygen fixes hypoxia within 3–4 breaths. Hypoxia or hyperventilation Can't tell? Oxygen first; below ten thousand, slow down."},{"t":"Carbon monoxide in the cockpit","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#co","k":"CO is colourless, odourless and tasteless. Haemoglobin takes it up over 200 times more readily than oxygen, and piston exhaust can be 9% CO. Suspect it with headache, poor concentration, nausea and cherry-red lips: heater off, vents open, oxygen, land. Carbon monoxide Heater off, vents open, land."},{"t":"How long before you can fly after…?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#waits","k":"Alcohol 24 h. Blood donation 48 h. Scuba diving 24 h. Rapid decompression 12 h. Local anaesthetic 12 h, general 48 h. Major dental work 48–72 h. No medication unless an aviation medicine specialist clears it. Waiting times Drink and dive 24; donate and general 48."},{"t":"Alcohol and flying","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#alcohol","k":"Alcohol is a depressant and cuts hypoxia tolerance: at 6,000 ft one drink acts like two. It leaves at 0.015% BAC an hour (about a drink an hour) and coffee does not speed it up. 40 mg/100 mL already causes many more errors, and drinkers often feel they are doing better. Alcohol One drink at 6,000 ft acts like two."},{"t":"How long do you have after losing oxygen?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#tuc","k":"Time of useful consciousness shrinks fast with height: 20–30 min at FL180, 3–5 min at FL250, 1–2 min at FL300, 30 s–1 min at FL350 and 15–20 s at FL400. Rapid decompression roughly halves it, so oxygen must be instantly at hand. Time of useful consciousness FL350: half a minute."},{"t":"Where do the nitrogen bubbles go?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#dcs","k":"Nitrogen bubbles (Henry's law) cause the bends in joints, the creeps in skin, the chokes in the lungs and the staggers in the brain. DCS is possible from 18,000 ft and likely unpressurised above 25,000 ft. Treat with 100% oxygen, warmth, descent and landing; no flying for 12 h after a rapid decompression. Decompression sickness Bends, creeps, chokes, staggers."},{"t":"Humidity, temperature, vibration","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#env","k":"Cabin humidity at altitude can be only 3–8% (40–60% is normal), so drink water and avoid coffee and tea. 20 °C is comfortable; 30 °C raises heart rate and sweating; cold numbs the hands. Vibration at 1–4 Hz affects breathing, at 30–40 Hz vision; 0.1–2 Hz causes airsickness. Cabin environment "},{"t":"Obvious or subtle incapacitation?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#incap","k":"Obvious incapacitation is a total loss of function, such as cardiac arrest or a fit. Subtle incapacitation is partial and gradual, from low blood sugar, fatigue or pain, and is often more dangerous because colleagues miss it. Any epileptic fit permanently bars a licence; a faint with a clear cause does not. Incapacitation Subtle is sneakier than obvious."},{"t":"Tap each part of the eye","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#eye","k":"The cornea does most of the focusing, the iris sets the light, the lens fine-tunes the focus onto the retina. Cones packed in the fovea give sharp colour day vision; rods in the periphery see dim light and movement. The blind spot is where the optic nerve leaves. The eye Cones for colour and centre; rods for dark and edges."},{"t":"Seeing at night and in the glare","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#night","k":"Rods take about 30 minutes to adapt fully, cones about 7; dim red light gives moderate adaptation in 20 minutes but distorts chart colours. A bright light ruins adaptation in seconds, so close one eye. Adaptation suffers above 5,000 ft cabin altitude, from smoking and CO, and from lack of vitamin A. An empty sky lets the eye relax to 1–2 m. Night vision "},{"t":"Which defect, which lens?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#defects","k":"Myopia (short sight): eyeball too long, corrected with a concave lens. Hypermetropia (long sight) and presbyopia (lens stiffens at 40–50) use convex lenses. Astigmatism is a misshapen cornea. Glaucoma raises the pressure and narrows the field. Pilots with glasses carry a spare pair. Eye defects "},{"t":"How does the ear hear and balance?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#ear","k":"The Eustachian tube equalises the middle ear; trouble clearing comes on descent. The otoliths sense linear acceleration and the semicircular canals angular acceleration, but neither senses a steady state, so only the eyes and instruments show a steady turn. Hearing spans 16–20,000 Hz; over 90 dB for long damages it. Ear and balance Otoliths: straight. Canals: turning. Steady: nothing."},{"t":"Why do sloping and odd-width runways fool you?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#slope","k":"An upsloping or narrow runway looks steep, so you think you are high and come in low and short. A downsloping or wide runway looks flat, so you think you are low and come in high and long. Runway slope Up and narrow → low. Down and wide → high."},{"t":"Will this make you fly the approach too low or too high?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#approach","k":"Feeling high leads to a low, shallow approach: upslope, narrow runway, featureless terrain, rain on the windscreen, haze and the black hole. Feeling low leads to a high approach: downslope, wide runway, bright approach lights. High or low Feel high, fly low."},{"t":"Which illusion is it?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#vestib","k":"Acceleration feels like pitching up (somatogravic), deceleration like pitching down. A slow roll unnoticed then corrected gives the leans. A head movement in a long turn gives Coriolis, the worst of all. A stared-at light that drifts is autokinesis. Sloping cloud gives a false horizon. Light flickering at 4–20 Hz gives flicker vertigo. Name the illusion When senses disagree, believe the instruments."},{"t":"How much G in a level turn?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#g","k":"In a level turn the load factor is 1 ÷ cos(bank): 1.41 g at 45°, 2 g at 60°, about 3 g at 70°. Positive G brings tunnel vision, grey-out, then black-out; negative G brings red-out. Tense the legs and abdomen and ease the pull. Alcohol, smoking, fatigue, heat, obesity and illness cut G tolerance. G forces Sixty degrees, double weight."},{"t":"Load factor","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#g","k":""},{"t":"+G sequence","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#g","k":""},{"t":"−G","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#g","k":""},{"t":"Weight, exercise, diet and drugs","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#health","k":"BMI is weight in kg divided by height in metres squared: normal 20–25 for men, 19–24 for women. Exercise to double the resting heart rate for 20 minutes three times a week. Never fly on an empty stomach or with gastroenteritis. Caffeine up to 250–300 mg a day. No medication unless cleared by an aviation medicine specialist. Health and fitness "},{"t":"BMI","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Exercise","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Caffeine","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Hypothermia","s":"Reg 26 · formula","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#health","k":""},{"t":"Fly today, or stay on the ground?","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#fit","k":"Do not fly with gastroenteritis (even on medicine), within 12 h of a local or 48 h of a general anaesthetic, within 48–72 h of major dental work, with a blocked sinus, or on any medicine an aviation medicine specialist has not cleared. Any doubt: stay on the ground. Fit to fly? Any doubt, stay on the ground."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":" Numbers to know "},{"t":"Oxygen in air: 21% at all heights","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"O₂ available at 8,000 / 18,000 / 36,000 ft: 76% / ½ / ¼","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Supplementary O₂ from: 10,000 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"100% O₂ from: 33,700 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fluids boil: > 63,000 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Critical threshold (no O₂): ≈ 22,000 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Smoker's hypoxia: ≈ 7,000 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Night vision impaired from: 5,000 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CO affinity: > 200 × O₂","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Breathing rate: 14–18 / min","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Resting pulse: 60–80","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Normal BP: 120/80","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TUC at FL350: 30 s – 1 min","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DCS possible from: 18,000 ft","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Rods / cones: 100 M / 6 M","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Dark adaptation: rods 30 min, cones 7 min","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hearing range: 16–20,000 Hz","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flicker vertigo: 4–20 Hz","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"G at 60° bank: 2 g","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"BMI normal (men / women): 20–25 / 19–24","s":"Reg 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Reg 26 · Aviation Physiology","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Atmosphere and gas laws (p.647–650)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure, oxygen and zones (p.650–652)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Body systems (p.652–657)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hypoxia (p.659–660)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"CO and hyperventilation (p.661–662)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Alcohol, BP, blood donation (p.663–665)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"TUC and decompression (p.665–668)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Incapacitation and fits (p.669–670)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The eye (p.671–676)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The ear (p.676–679)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Illusions (p.679–685)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"G (p.685–686)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Health and fitness (p.687–691)","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Reg 26 · notes","h":"chapters/reg-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 1. Aerodynamics Basics","s":"The physics vocabulary of flight, the chain from indicated to true airspeed and Mach number, the three ways of explaining lift, why viscosity and Reynolds number matter, and where the lift force actually acts.","h":"chapters/hb-01.html","k":"Terms · airspeeds · Bernoulli · Reynolds number · CP and AC "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Name every part of an aerofoil","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#anatomy","k":"Chord line joins leading and trailing edge; the mean camber line runs midway between the surfaces. Angle of attack is chord to relative airflow; angle of incidence is chord to the aircraft datum. Lift is at 90° to the airflow, drag along it, and their resultant (total reaction) acts through the centre of pressure.  Attack is to the Air; Incidence is In the aircraft."},{"t":"Wing definitions","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#defs","k":"Aspect ratio = span² ÷ area; taper = tip chord ÷ root chord; wash-out = less incidence towards the tip; load factor = lift ÷ weight; the MAC is the chord through the centroid of the wing area.  Wash-out washes the tip angle out."},{"t":"From IAS to Mach number","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#speeds","k":"IAS is the dynamic pressure the ASI reads. Correct it for position error to get CAS, for compressibility to get EAS, and for density to get TAS (TAS = EAS ÷ √(ρ/ρ₀)). Mach number is TAS divided by the local speed of sound, 39√T kt.  I Can Easily Tell: IAS → CAS → EAS → TAS."},{"t":"Pressure and airspeed formulas","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#press","k":"Dynamic pressure q = ½ρV², so doubling the speed gives four times the q. TAS = EAS ÷ √(ρ/ρ₀) (about twice EAS at 40 000 ft); LSS = 39 √T kt; Mach = TAS ÷ LSS.  Double the speed, four times the push."},{"t":"Dynamic pressure","s":"Handbook 1 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#press","k":"Double the speed → 4× q."},{"t":"TAS from EAS","s":"Handbook 1 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#press","k":"At 40,000 ft ρ/ρ₀ ≈ ¼ → TAS ≈ 2 × EAS."},{"t":"Speed of sound","s":"Handbook 1 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#press","k":"661 kt at ISA MSL (288 K)."},{"t":"Mach number","s":"Handbook 1 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#press","k":""},{"t":"Weight","s":"Handbook 1 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#press","k":"60,000 kg → 588,600 N of lift needed in level flight."},{"t":"Why does faster air have lower pressure?","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#venturi","k":"Continuity: mass flow is constant, so where the stream narrows (A×V = constant) the air speeds up. Bernoulli: total pressure is constant (P + ½ρV² = constant), so where it speeds up its static pressure falls. Faster flow over the wing's upper surface means lower pressure on top: lift.  Squeeze, speed, suck."},{"t":"Lift without a wing: the Magnus effect","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#magnus","k":"A spinning cylinder or ball drags air round with it (circulation): the flow speeds up on one side and slows on the other, so pressure differs and there is a net force. That is the circulation theory of lift, and why golf balls carry and cricket balls swing.  "},{"t":"Three explanations of lift","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#theories","k":"Lift can be explained three ways: lower pressure over the top (continuity + Bernoulli), air deflected downwards (momentum), or circulation round the aerofoil. They describe the same flow.  Pressure, push-down, spin: one lift."},{"t":"Viscosity and Reynolds number","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#rn","k":"Air (a gas) gets more viscous when warmer; liquids get less. A higher Reynolds number (ρVl/μ) gives a more energetic boundary layer that separates later, raising CLmax and the stalling angle.  High Reynolds, late separation."},{"t":"Which way does the centre of pressure move?","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#cp","k":"On a cambered aerofoil the CP moves forward as angle of attack increases (furthest forward at CLmax, then jumps back at the stall). On a symmetric aerofoil it stays near 25% chord. On a flat plate it moves back. The aerodynamic centre, where pitching moment does not change with AoA, is fixed at 25% chord below about 0.4 Mach.  CP wanders, AC stays at a quarter."},{"t":"CP or AC?","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#cp-stamp","k":"The centre of pressure is where the total reaction acts; on a cambered wing it moves forward as AoA rises and jumps back at the stall. The aerodynamic centre (about 25% chord, subsonic) is where the pitching moment stays constant.  CP wanders, AC stays put."},{"t":"Gas laws and laws of motion","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#gas","k":"Boyle: PV constant at constant temperature. Charles: V ∝ T at constant pressure. Gay-Lussac: P ∝ T at constant volume. Combined: PV = nRT. Newton 2: force = rate of change of momentum.  Boyle squeezes, Charles heats."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":"  "},{"t":"LSS at ISA MSL: 661 kt","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":"340 m/s"},{"t":"LSS formula: 39 √T kt","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Incompressible below: ≈ 0.4 M","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AC position (subsonic): 25% chord","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CP range, cambered: 20–30% chord","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Density at 40,000 ft: ≈ ¼ of MSL","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":"TAS ≈ 2 × EAS"},{"t":"μ air, MSL: 1.79 × 10⁻⁵ kg/m/s","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Laminar near LE: RN < 0.5 million","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fully turbulent: RN > 10 million","s":"Handbook 1 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 1 · Aerodynamics Basics","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#quiz","k":"  "},{"t":"Physical terms (2-3)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Laws (2-4)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Density and pressures (2-5)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airspeeds (2-5)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Streamline flow and lift theories (2-6 to 2-7)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Viscosity and Reynolds number (2-8)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Definitions (2-9)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Centre of pressure and aerodynamic centre (2-10)","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 1 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-01.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 2. Airflow, Lift and Drag","s":"What the air actually does round a wing, from stagnation point to separation; how lift builds with angle of attack; the two families of drag, the tip vortex behind induced drag, the speeds the drag curve defines, and ground effect.","h":"chapters/hb-02.html","k":"Boundary layer · lift curve · parasite and induced drag · VMD · ground effect "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What happens to the air between leading and trailing edge?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#flow","k":"Air stops dead at the leading-edge stagnation point (maximum pressure), divides, speeds up to its fastest and lowest pressure near maximum camber, then slows against a rising (adverse) pressure. The boundary layer starts laminar, turns turbulent at the transition point and finally separates where it runs out of energy. Air round the wing Stop, split, speed up, slow down, split off."},{"t":"Laminar or turbulent: which boundary layer do we want?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#bl","k":"The laminar layer is thin with little friction but separates easily; the turbulent layer has more friction but more energy, so it clings on against the adverse pressure gradient. Roughness and speed move the transition point forward. Laminar vs turbulent Laminar is slick but quits; turbulent rubs but clings."},{"t":"How does lift build as the nose comes up?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#aoa-steps","k":"From zero lift at about −4°, lift grows as the suction peak strengthens and moves forward — best L/D near 4° — until near 16° the separation point races forward and the wing stalls. Lift vs angle of attack "},{"t":"The lift equation and what it tells you","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#lift-eq","k":"L = CL × ½ρV² × S, with V as TAS. The same IAS gives the same lift at any height; doubling the speed lets CL fall to a quarter. Lift equation Same IAS, same lift, any height."},{"t":"Lift","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#lift-eq","k":"V is TAS. ½ρV² is dynamic pressure, so the same IAS gives the same lift at any height."},{"t":"CL needed for level flight","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#lift-eq","k":"Double the speed → CL (and roughly AoA) to ¼."},{"t":"Speed for a new CL","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#lift-eq","k":"Lift constant: V² × CL is constant."},{"t":"How does camber change the lift curve?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#lift-curve","k":"A symmetric section gives no lift at 0° and its curve passes through the origin. Camber moves the whole curve up and to the left: lift at 0°, a negative zero-lift angle, a higher CLmax and a slightly lower stalling angle. Camber and the lift curve Camber climbs: up and left."},{"t":"What does each design change do to the lift curve?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#cl-factors","k":"More camber raises CLmax; higher aspect ratio steepens the curve and lowers the stalling angle; sweepback lowers CLmax but raises the stalling angle; a rough or sharp leading edge brings an earlier, more abrupt stall; higher Reynolds number helps. What shapes the lift curve Camber lifts the max; sweep raises the angle, lowers the max."},{"t":"Lift facts examiners love","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#lift-facts","k":"V in the lift equation is TAS; most lift comes from the upper surface; best L/D is near 4°; and sharp-edged wings can use a leading-edge vortex for extra lift. Lift facts "},{"t":"Parasite or induced?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-sort","k":"Parasite drag is the price of moving through air; induced drag is the price of making lift. Parasite or induced "},{"t":"Why does making lift create drag?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#vortex","k":"High pressure under the wing spills round the tip to the low pressure on top: outward underneath, inward on top. The vortices shed at the tips push the air behind the wing down (induced downwash), which tilts the lift vector rearward. That rearward component is induced drag. Tip vortices Out below, in above, round the tip: the lift leans back."},{"t":"Wake vortices: what makes the worst one?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#wake","k":"Wake vortices are strongest behind heavy, clean, slow aircraft; they sink about 1000 ft, trail up to 9 nm and drift along the ground in light winds. Wake turbulence "},{"t":"What changes each kind of drag?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-factors","k":"Parasite drag grows with the square of speed and with gear and flaps but ignores weight; induced drag falls with the square of speed, rises with weight and falls with higher aspect ratio. Drag factor table Parasite grows with speed squared; induced falls with it."},{"t":"Which speed gives what?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-curve","k":"Total drag is least where parasite drag equals induced drag: VMD, about 4° AoA and best L/D. VMP (0.76 VMD) gives least power: prop endurance and minimum sink. 1.32 VMD, where a line from the origin touches the curve, gives jet range. The drag curve VMD is RAGE: Range (props), Angle of climb, Glide range, Endurance (jets)."},{"t":"Does VMD go up or down?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#curve-shift","k":"Anything that adds parasite drag (flaps, gear, damage) lowers VMD; more weight raises it. Does VMD move? "},{"t":"Drag formulas","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-eq","k":"Parasite drag ∝ V², CDi = CL² ÷ πAe, and induced drag ∝ 1/V². VMP is 0.76 VMD; jet best range is at 1.32 VMD. Drag formulas "},{"t":"Parasite drag","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-eq","k":"Grows with the square of EAS."},{"t":"Induced drag coefficient","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-eq","k":"e = 1 for an elliptical wing."},{"t":"Induced drag with speed","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-eq","k":""},{"t":"VMP and jet range speed","s":"Handbook 2 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#drag-eq","k":""},{"t":"What changes inside ground effect?","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#ground","k":"Within about half a wingspan of the ground the vortices are squashed, downwash weakens and effective AoA rises. Induced drag falls, so the aircraft floats; the tail loses downwash, so the nose pitches down; and the static ports see higher pressure, so ASI and altimeter under-read. Ground effect Half a span: float, nose drops, gauges low."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"BL edge: 99%","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":"of free-stream speed"},{"t":"BL thickness: 2 mm LE · 20 mm TE","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Zero-lift AoA (cambered): ≈ −4°","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Best L/D AoA: ≈ 4°","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":"CL 12–25 × CD"},{"t":"Stalling AoA: ≈ 16°","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Least drag AoA: ≈ −2°","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"LE roughness ignored aft of: 20% chord","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Gear/flaps parasite rise: up to 80%","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Induced share at take-off: up to ¾","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Vortex sink: ≈ 1000 ft","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Vortex trail: up to 9 nm","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMP: 0.76 VMD","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet range speed: 1.32 VMD","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ground effect within: ½ wingspan","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Low-drag max thickness: 40–50% chord","s":"Handbook 2 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 2 · Airflow, Lift and Drag","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Subsonic airflow over an aerofoil (2-11)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transition, separation and the creation of lift (2-12)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lift (2-13)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lift curves and factors affecting CL (2-13 to 2-14)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Parasite drag (2-15)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Induced drag and tip vortices (2-16)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Factors affecting induced drag (2-17)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Factors affecting CD (2-17)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Total drag and the speeds it defines (2-18)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"L/D ratio and ground effect (2-19)","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 2 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-02.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 3. Stalls, Spins and Upsets","s":"Why a wing stalls and how designers make it stall politely; everything that raises or lowers the stalling speed; deep, shock and super stalls; how a wing drop turns into autorotation and a spin; and the recovery sequences for spins and upsets.","h":"chapters/hb-03.html","k":"Critical AoA · stall speed factors · deep stall · autorotation · pitch-up · spin moments · UPRT "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What exactly is a stall?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#what","k":"A stall is boundary-layer separation: the separation point creeps forward as AoA rises until, past the critical angle, the flow breaks down. It always happens at the same critical AoA whatever the speed, weight or attitude; lift does not vanish, it just becomes too little. What a stall is Speed doesn't stall a wing; angle does."},{"t":"Gentle or abrupt stall?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#tame","k":"Large leading-edge radius, washout, stall strips, vortex generators and rectangular planforms make the root stall first and gently; sharp edges, thin sections, sweep and high taper bring abrupt or tip-first stalls. Gentle or abrupt stall "},{"t":"How much does the stall speed rise?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-eq","k":"Stall speed varies with the square root of weight and of load factor: 20% heavier is 10% faster, 4 g doubles it, and a 60° bank raises it by 41%. Stall-speed sums Stall speed goes with the square root."},{"t":"Basic stall speed","s":"Handbook 3 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-eq","k":"Clean, stated weight, power off, straight and level."},{"t":"New weight","s":"Handbook 3 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-eq","k":"20% heavier → about 10% faster."},{"t":"Load factor","s":"Handbook 3 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-eq","k":"4 g → 2 × Vs."},{"t":"Bank angle","s":"Handbook 3 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-eq","k":"30° ×1.07 · 45° ×1.2 · 60° ×1.4 · 75° ×2."},{"t":"Why are steep turns near the ground dangerous?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#bank-chart","k":"In a level turn the wing must carry 1/cos φ times the weight, so the stall speed rises by √(1/cos φ): only 7% at 30°, 20% at 45°, 40% at 60° and doubles at 75°. Bank and stall speed 30, 45, 60, 75 → 1.07, 1.2, 1.4, 2."},{"t":"Does the stalling IAS go up or down?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-factors","k":"Flaps, power and slipstream lower the stalling IAS; forward CG, ice, frost, high Mach and load factor raise it; climbing alone leaves the stalling IAS unchanged. Up or down? "},{"t":"Stall-speed factors on one card","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#vs-table","k":"Weight and load factor act through a square root; flaps, power and slipstream lower Vs; forward CG, contamination and compressibility raise it. Stall-speed table Heavy, loaded or forward CG: stall faster. Flaps and power: slower."},{"t":"Why can a T-tail jet get stuck in a stall?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#deep","k":"A swept wing keeps its CP forward at the stall, so the nose pitches up. A high T-tail then sits in the wing's turbulent wake and the elevator loses its bite; the rising descent rate increases the AoA further. A stick pusher prevents the aircraft ever getting there. Deep stall Swept + T = stuck; push before you're stuck."},{"t":"Deep, shock or super stall?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#types","k":"Deep stall traps swept-wing T-tail aircraft; shock stall comes from separation behind a shock near M 0.82; super stall afflicts low-aspect-ratio deltas at very high angles. Deep, shock, super stall "},{"t":"Why does a dropped wing keep dropping?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#autorot","k":"Rolling adds AoA to the down-going wing and removes it from the rising wing. Past the stall, more AoA means less lift and more drag, so the low wing loses lift (keeps rolling) and gains drag (yaws the same way). Roll and yaw together are the start of a spin, which is why aileron is the wrong way to lift a wing at the stall. Autorotation Past the peak, down means weaker and draggier."},{"t":"Swept wings: tip stall and pitch-up","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#pitchup","k":"Swept tips stall first because the boundary layer drifts outboard; losing their lift moves the CP forward and increases tail downwash, so the nose pitches up. Fences, vortex generators, saw-tooth leading edges and vortilons help. Tip stall and pitch-up Tips stall first, nose pitches up."},{"t":"What keeps a spinning aircraft going round?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#spin","k":"A spin needs a stall plus yaw: both wings stalled, one more than the other. The aircraft corkscrews down a helix (tan θ = p ÷ c) with lift supplying the centripetal force and drag roughly balancing weight. After about two turns the aerodynamic and inertial moments balance and it settles. The spin No stall, no spin."},{"t":"Which moments help the spin?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#moments","k":"Aerodynamic roll is pro-spin while aerodynamic pitch and yaw are anti-spin; inertial pitch is pro-spin. Out-spin rudder and forward stick provide the recovery moments. Spin moments "},{"t":"Erect vs inverted; nose-up vs nose-down","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#attitude","k":"In an erect spin roll and yaw go the same way; in an inverted spin they oppose. A flatter, nose-high spin descends more slowly but rotates faster and is harder to recover. Erect vs inverted "},{"t":"Spin recovery, step by step","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#recover","k":"Throttle closed and ailerons neutral, full opposite rudder, a pause for the rudder to bite, then stick progressively forward to unstall the wings. The spin may briefly tighten as the stick goes forward; keep going. Spin recovery Power off, ailerons neutral, Rudder, Pause, Push."},{"t":"UPRT: what is an upset and how do you recover?","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#uprt","k":"An upset is pitch more than 25° up or 10° down, bank beyond 45°, or a speed inappropriate for the conditions. Recognise it on the ADI, confirm with ASI, VSI and altimeter, then: energy state, autopilot and autothrottle off, speed brakes in, recover as the manufacturer says. Upset recovery 25 up, 10 down, 45 over."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Stall warning margin: 5 kt or 5%","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":"CAS"},{"t":"Max roll at the stall: 20°","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Weight +20%: Vs +10%","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"4 g: 2 × Vs","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Bank 30° / 45°: ×1.07 / ×1.2","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Bank 60° / 75°: ×1.4 / ×2","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ice or snow: Vs +30%","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":"lift −30%, drag +40%"},{"t":"Frost: Vs +15%","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Compressibility felt above: ≈ 20,000 ft","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Shock stall: ≈ M 0.82","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Delta super-stall AoA: up to 40°","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Spin settles after: ≈ 2 turns","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Upset pitch: > 25° up · > 10° down","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Upset bank: > 45°","s":"Handbook 3 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 3 · Stalls, Spins and Upsets","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"What a stall is (2-20)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Stall behaviour and how designers tame it (2-20)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Basic stalling speed and what changes it (2-21)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Types of stall (2-22)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Autorotation (2-22)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tip stall and pitch-up (2-23)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Spin (2-24)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Moments in a spin (2-25 to 2-26)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Spin recovery (2-26)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"UPRT — upset prevention and recovery (2-26)","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 3 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-03.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 4. Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","s":"What makes a good aerofoil, how each wing planform shares out its lift and where it stalls first, why designers sweep, taper, twist and add canards, and how slats and flaps raise CLmax for take-off and landing.","h":"chapters/hb-04.html","k":"Aerofoil types · planforms · sweep · delta and canard · aspect ratio · slats and flaps "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What would the perfect aerofoil have?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#ideal","k":"An ideal aerofoil has a high CLmax, low drag across a range of angles, a good lift/drag ratio and a small, stable movement of the centre of pressure. The ideal aerofoil "},{"t":"High-lift, general-purpose or high-speed section?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#types","k":"High-lift sections are thick and deeply cambered; high-speed sections are thin, sharp and nearly symmetric with maximum thickness near mid-chord, like laminar-flow sections. Aerofoil types Fat and curved for lift, thin and flat for speed."},{"t":"Where does each planform stall first?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#planforms","k":"Elliptical wings give the least induced drag but stall all at once with no warning. Rectangular wings stall at the root, keeping the ailerons working. High taper, pointed tips and sweepback load up the tips and tend to tip-stall. Planforms and stall Square stalls at the root; sharp and swept stall at the tip."},{"t":"Elliptical vs rectangular; moderate vs high taper","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#planform-pairs","k":"Elliptical wings have the least induced drag but stall all at once; rectangular wings stall at the root; the more taper, the more the tip works and the earlier it stalls. Planform pairs "},{"t":"What does sweepback do to the airflow?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#sweep","k":"Only the component of the airflow at right angles to the leading edge (V cos Λ) makes lift and sets the local Mach number; the spanwise component (V sin Λ) just slides along the wing, dragging the boundary layer out to the tips. Hence: higher critical Mach, but lower CLmax, higher stall speed and tip stall. Sweepback airflow Cos does the work, sine slides to the tip."},{"t":"Sweepback: the good and the bad","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#sweep-list","k":"Sweep delays compressibility and smooths the ride in turbulence, but costs CLmax, raises stall speed and brings tip stall, pitch-up, Mach tuck and high descent rates on approach. Sweep: pros and cons Sweep buys speed, pays in stall manners."},{"t":"Delta, forward sweep, crescent or canard?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#special","k":"Crescent wings keep the boundary layer inboard, tail-less deltas stall at high angles, slender deltas gain vortex lift, forward-swept wings stall at the root and canards trim with less drag. Special wings "},{"t":"Canard: who stalls first?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#canard","k":"On a canard aircraft, a wing stalling first costs stability and a canard stalling first costs control; conventional aircraft avoid both by stalling the wing before the tail. Canard stall order "},{"t":"High or low aspect ratio?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#ar","k":"High aspect ratio cuts induced drag for transports and gliders; low aspect ratio suits fast combat aircraft with high roll rates and stalling angles but much induced drag at low speed. Aspect ratio Long wings glide; short wings roll."},{"t":"Flying a low-AR wing slowly: the traps","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#lowar","k":"Flying a low-aspect-ratio wing slowly invites huge induced drag: over-rotation, steep slow turns or pulling up below the glide path quickly bleed speed and raise sink. Low-AR traps "},{"t":"How does each flap and slat work?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#devices","k":"Trailing-edge flaps add camber (Fowler also adds area) and so raise CLmax but lower the stalling angle. Slats open a slot that energises the boundary layer: they raise CLmax by up to 70% and raise the stalling angle without adding camber. A Krueger flap hinges from under the leading edge and adds camber but no slot. Flaps and slats Flaps bend, slats blow, Fowler grows."},{"t":"Which device gives the most CLmax?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#clmax-rank","k":"Fowler flaps give the most CLmax, then slotted, plain flap with slat, split and plain flaps; a slat alone mainly lets the wing reach a higher angle. CLmax ranking "},{"t":"Flaps, slats or both?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#flap-slat","k":"Both raise CLmax and lower stall speed; only flaps add camber, lower the stalling angle and pitch the nose down, while slats energise the boundary layer and raise the stalling angle. Flaps or slats? "},{"t":"Lowering TE flaps: what changes?","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#flap-effects","k":"Lowering trailing-edge flaps raises CLmax, lowers the stalling angle and speeds, worsens L/D, pitches the nose down, increases tail downwash and leaves the aerodynamic centre unchanged. Lowering flaps Flaps: more lift, lower angle, nose down."},{"t":"Memory tricks for this chapter","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#tricks","k":"Short phrases for the chapter’s key facts. Memory tricks "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Conventional max thickness: 25–30% chord","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Laminar / high-speed max thickness: ≈ 50% chord","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Elliptical wing CL: ≈ 1.0 root to tip","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Moderate taper: 0.5","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":"near-elliptical"},{"t":"High taper: 0.25","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":"tip stall"},{"t":"Pointed tip: taper 0","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Slender delta vortex lift: up to +30% CL","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Slender delta CP: ≈ 50% chord","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Slats raise CLmax: up to 70%","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fowler flap CL: ≈ 3","s":"Handbook 4 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 4 · Aerofoils, Wings and High-Lift Devices","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Requirements of an ideal aerofoil (2-27)","s":"Handbook 4 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Wing planforms (2-28)","s":"Handbook 4 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other wing designs (2-29)","s":"Handbook 4 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Effect of aspect ratio (2-30)","s":"Handbook 4 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lift augmentation (2-31)","s":"Handbook 4 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 4 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-04.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 5. Stability","s":"Why an aircraft returns to trimmed flight after a gust: static and dynamic stability, the tailplane and CG in pitch, the fin in yaw, the wings in roll, and what happens when yaw and roll stability are out of balance.","h":"chapters/hb-05.html","k":"Static and dynamic · neutral point and static margin · phugoid · dihedral effect · spiral and Dutch roll "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Positive, neutral or negative?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#static","k":"Static stability is only the first tendency after a disturbance: back towards equilibrium (positive), stay put (neutral) or keep going (negative). Most aircraft are statically stable in pitch and yaw and roughly neutral in roll. Static stability Bowl, table, hilltop."},{"t":"Which stability is about which axis?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#axes","k":"Longitudinal stability is about the lateral axis via the tailplane, directional stability about the vertical axis via the fin, and lateral stability about the longitudinal axis via the wings. Axes and surfaces Tail for pitch, fin for yaw, dihedral for roll."},{"t":"What does dynamic stability look like?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#dynamic","k":"All three curves start with a statically stable aircraft swinging back. Positive dynamic stability: the swings die out. Neutral: they carry on. Negative: they grow. A statically unstable aircraft is always dynamically unstable too. Dynamic stability Static is the first swing; dynamic is the story after."},{"t":"How does the tailplane keep the nose steady?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#long","k":"A gust raises the AoA of wing and tail together. The wing's lift acts ahead of the CG (nose-up), the tail's behind it on a much longer arm (nose-down). With the CG far enough forward the tail wins and the nose goes back down. Move the CG aft and the arms equalise: that CG position is the neutral point. Longitudinal balance Long tail arm wins the tug-of-war."},{"t":"Forward CG vs aft CG","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#cg","k":"A forward CG means more stability and heavier controls; the aft limit leaves a static margin before the neutral point, where the aircraft would be neutrally stable. CG, neutral point, margin Forward is stable and heavy; the neutral point is the edge."},{"t":"More or less longitudinal stability?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#long-factors","k":"Forward CG, a bigger tail or longer tail arm and longitudinal dihedral add stability; aft CG, flaps, high power with a low thrust line, wing downwash and the fuselage reduce it. Longitudinal factors "},{"t":"Phugoid or short period?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#modes","k":"The phugoid is a slow swap of height for speed at nearly constant AoA, easy to fly out of. The short-period mode is a rapid AoA and g oscillation at nearly constant speed, too fast for the pilot: control inputs can reinforce it (PIO), so pitch dampers handle it. Phugoid vs short period Phugoid = Potential ↔ kinetic, Patient. Short = Sharp."},{"t":"What keeps the nose pointing into wind?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#dir","k":"Directional stability is weathercocking: a sideslip puts the relative airflow on the fin, behind the CG, and yaws the nose back into it. The fuselage works against it (its side force acts ahead of the CG); dorsal fins help at large sideslip angles. Directional stability The fin is the weathercock's tail."},{"t":"How does dihedral level the wings?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#lat","k":"When a wing drops the aircraft slips towards it. With dihedral, the relative airflow from the slip side meets the low wing at a larger AoA, so it gains lift and rolls the aircraft level. Damping in roll alone only resists the rate of roll; it cannot bring the wings level. Dihedral effect Slip towards the low wing, the low wing lifts."},{"t":"Does it add or remove lateral stability?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#lat-factors","k":"A swept high wing can be too stable in roll; that is why big high-wing swept transports use anhedral. Lateral factors "},{"t":"Spiral divergence or Dutch roll?","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#coupling","k":"If directional stability beats lateral stability the nose keeps turning into the slip while the wings lag: a slowly tightening spiral. If lateral stability beats directional, the rising wing's extra induced drag yaws the aircraft back and forth: Dutch roll, the more dangerous of the two. Spiral vs Dutch roll Big fin → spiral; big dihedral → Dutch roll."},{"t":"Memory tricks for this chapter","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#tricks","k":"Short phrases for the chapter’s key facts. Memory tricks "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Sweepback equivalent: 10° ≈ 1° dihedral","s":"Handbook 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Usual static stability: + pitch, + yaw","s":"Handbook 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#numbers","k":"neutral in roll"},{"t":"Sideslip positive: airflow from the right","s":"Handbook 5 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 5 · Stability","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Stability and static stability (2-32)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Longitudinal static stability (2-32 to 2-33)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Manoeuvre stability (2-34)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Dynamic stability (2-34)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Directional stability (2-35)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lateral stability (2-36)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Coupled directional and lateral stability (2-37)","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 5 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-05.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 6. Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","s":"How ailerons, elevator, rudder and spoilers move the aircraft, the side-effects of rolling and yawing, and the tabs, hinges, horns and weights that make big controls light enough to fly and free of flutter.","h":"chapters/hb-06.html","k":"Rate vs displacement controls · adverse yaw · Frise and differential ailerons · tabs · horn and mass balance "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What does each control do and how?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#primary","k":"Ailerons give a rate of roll; elevator and rudder give a displacement in pitch and yaw; spoilers dump lift and add drag, rolling the aircraft when used differentially. Primary controls Ailerons give a rate; elevator and rudder a position."},{"t":"Aileron details examiners ask","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#aileron-facts","k":"Roll rate settles where aileron moment equals roll damping, so it is faster with shorter span and at altitude; a lowered aileron raises that wing’s angle of attack towards the stall. Aileron details "},{"t":"Why does the nose swing the wrong way when you roll?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#adverse","k":"Rolling right lowers the left aileron: the left wing gets more lift but also more induced drag, so the nose yaws left, away from the turn. Differential ailerons (up aileron moves further), Frise ailerons (up aileron's nose drops into the flow), an aileron–rudder interconnect, or roll spoilers on the down-going wing all add drag on the inside to cancel it. Adverse yaw Down aileron, drag: the nose goes the wrong way."},{"t":"Roll due to yaw vs yaw due to roll","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#roll-yaw","k":"Rudder produces roll in the same direction as the yaw; aileron produces yaw in the opposite direction to the roll. Roll and yaw coupling "},{"t":"Which adverse-yaw cure is it?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#cures","k":"Differential and Frise ailerons, aileron–rudder interconnects and roll spoilers all add drag on the rising-aileron side to cancel adverse yaw. Adverse-yaw cures "},{"t":"Taileron, elevon, ruddervator or flaperon?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#mixed","k":"Tailerons and elevons combine elevator and aileron, ruddervators combine rudder and elevator, and flaperons combine flap and aileron. Combined surfaces "},{"t":"Which way does each tab move?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#tabs","k":"A balance tab is geared to move opposite to its control surface, helping it along: lighter stick, slightly less effect. An anti-balance tab moves the same way: heavier, more effective. A servo tab is the only part the pilot moves; it flies the surface. A trim tab is set by the trim wheel opposite to the surface until stick force is zero. Tabs Balance opposes and eases; anti agrees and stiffens."},{"t":"The tab table","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#tab-table","k":"Balance, servo and trim tabs move opposite to the surface and lighten the stick; an anti-balance tab moves the same way and makes it heavier and more effective. Tab table Tabs move against the surface; anti-balance moves with it."},{"t":"Which tab is it?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#tab-sort","k":"Match each behaviour to the tab: feel (anti-balance), linkage-driven help (balance), pilot-driven tab (servo/spring) or cockpit-set zero force (trim). Which tab? "},{"t":"How do hinges, horns and weights lighten and steady a control?","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#balance","k":"Put some of the surface ahead of the hinge (inset hinge, horn or internal balance) and the air pushes it round with you: lighter controls without losing effectiveness, as long as the CP never gets ahead of the hinge (overbalance). A mass-balance weight ahead of the hinge puts the surface CG on the hinge line to stop flutter. Hinges, horns, weights Horns and insets lighten; lead stops the shake."},{"t":"Easy to confuse","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#balance-pairs","k":"Inset hinges, horns and internal balance lighten the stick but must not move the centre of pressure ahead of the hinge; mass balance puts the surface CG on the hinge to prevent flutter. Balance pairs "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Inset hinge limit: 20–25%","s":"Handbook 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#numbers","k":"of control chord"},{"t":"Servo tab useless beyond: ≈ 20° deflection","s":"Handbook 6 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#numbers","k":"at low IAS"},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 6 · Flight Controls, Tabs and Balancing","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Primary controls (2-38)","s":"Handbook 6 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Secondary effects (2-39)","s":"Handbook 6 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aerodynamic balance (2-40)","s":"Handbook 6 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hinges, horns and mass balance (2-41)","s":"Handbook 6 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 6 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-06.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 7. Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","s":"The four forces in level flight, climbing, gliding and turning; which speed gives the best angle, rate, range and endurance for props and jets; how wind, weight, altitude and flaps change each; and the cruise techniques that stretch range.","h":"chapters/hb-07.html","k":"Four forces · Vx and Vy · cruise and step climb · glide for range and endurance · turns · SAR and little m "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What balances in level flight?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#level","k":"In level flight lift equals weight and thrust equals drag; the tailplane balances the CP–CG moment at the cost of trim drag. Level flight "},{"t":"Why is lift less than weight in a climb?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#climb","k":"In a steady climb the weight splits into W cos θ, which lift must balance, and W sin θ, which acts backwards along the path and must be overcome by thrust in addition to drag. So lift is less than weight and thrust is more than drag; in a vertical climb lift is zero. Climb forces Climbing: the wing does less, the engine does more."},{"t":"Angle of climb or rate of climb?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#vxvy","k":"Angle comes from excess thrust: sin θ = (T − D) ÷ W, best at Vx. Rate comes from excess power: ROC = (T − D) × V ÷ W, best at Vy. For a jet Vx is VMD; a propeller aircraft's Vx is near the safe unstick speed. Vx and Vy X marks the angle (thrust); Y is why we go up fast (power)."},{"t":"Where is the best climb speed on the power curves?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#power","k":"Power required (drag × TAS) is a bowl. A piston/prop's power available falls away at low speed, so its best climb speed sits close to VMP; a jet's power available rises with speed, so its biggest gap — and best climb speed — is much faster, with a wide band of usable speeds. Where the curves cross are the minimum and maximum level speeds at that power. Power curves "},{"t":"What changes the climb?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#climb-factors","k":"Weight, altitude, temperature and flaps all reduce climb angle and rate; headwind only steepens the gradient over the ground. What changes the climb Heavy, high, hot or flapped: climb suffers."},{"t":"Climb formulas","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#roc-eq","k":"Climb angle comes from excess thrust over weight, rate of climb from excess power over weight, and gradient from rate over groundspeed. Climb formulas Angle from spare thrust, rate from spare power."},{"t":"Climb angle","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#roc-eq","k":""},{"t":"Rate of climb","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#roc-eq","k":"Excess power ÷ weight. 1 kt ≈ 101.3 ft/min."},{"t":"Climb gradient","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#roc-eq","k":""},{"t":"Power to climb","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#roc-eq","k":"Level-flight power + climbing + accelerating as TAS rises with height."},{"t":"Cruise climb or step climb?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#cruise-climb","k":"Both follow the optimum altitude upwards as fuel burns off: cruise climb continuously, step climb in 4000 ft steps to within about 99% of its efficiency. Cruise vs step climb "},{"t":"Glide for range or for endurance?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#glide","k":"Glide angle depends only on L/D (tan θ = D/L), so the flattest glide is at VMD for any aircraft and any weight: a heavier glider flies faster down the same slope. Time in the air depends on rate of descent (D × V ÷ W), least at VMP. Wind changes the distance over the ground but never the rate of descent. Gliding Distance: VMD. Duration: VMP. Wind moves the ground, not the sink."},{"t":"Glide numbers","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#glide-eq","k":"Glide distance is height times L/D; glide angle is the inverse tangent of D/L; rate of descent is drag times speed over weight. Glide formulas "},{"t":"Glide distance","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#glide-eq","k":"Still air. Same at any weight if VMD is flown."},{"t":"Glide angle","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#glide-eq","k":""},{"t":"Rate of descent","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#glide-eq","k":"Least at VMP; higher at altitude (higher TAS)."},{"t":"Where does the turning force come from?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn","k":"Banking tilts the lift: its horizontal part pulls the aircraft round (centripetal force), its vertical part must still equal the weight, so total lift must rise to W ÷ cos φ. The load factor depends on bank alone: 2 g at 60°. Turn forces Bank tilts the lift; pull to keep the vertical bit."},{"t":"Turn formulas","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn-eq","k":"Load factor is 1/cos(bank); radius is V² ÷ (g tan bank); rate is g tan bank ÷ V — double the speed, four times the radius, half the rate. Turn formulas Double the speed, four times the radius."},{"t":"Load factor","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn-eq","k":"30° 1.15 g · 45° 1.41 g · 60° 2 g."},{"t":"Radius","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn-eq","k":"Double TAS → 4× radius."},{"t":"Rate of turn","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn-eq","k":"Double TAS → ½ the rate."},{"t":"Rate-1 bank (rule of thumb)","s":"Handbook 7 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn-eq","k":""},{"t":"What does the bank angle do to load factor and stall speed?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#bank-dial","k":"In a level turn the load factor is 1 ÷ cos(bank): 2 g at 60°. Stall speed rises with the square root of it: ×1.07 at 30°, ×1.2 at 45°, ×1.4 at 60° and ×2 at 75°. Bank and g Sixty degrees: two g, stall speed up 40%."},{"t":"Tightest and quickest turn: what do you need?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#turn-best","k":"Minimum radius and maximum rate both need CLmax, low wing loading and dense air; at the same TAS and bank, radius does not depend on weight. Tightest, quickest turn Tight turns need CLmax and dense air."},{"t":"Which speed for range, which for endurance?","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#range","k":"Range is distance per fuel; endurance is time per fuel. Propeller aircraft: VMD for range, VMP for endurance. Jets, whose fuel flow follows thrust rather than power: 1.32 VMD for range (1.19 VMD in a cruise climb) and VMD for endurance. Range and endurance speeds Props sit one step slower than jets: VMP/VMD vs VMD/1.32 VMD."},{"t":"SAR, GFC, SFC and little m","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#sar","k":"Specific air range is distance per unit of fuel, best at high TAS/drag with low weight and SFC; little m is cruise IAS over VMD. SAR and little m "},{"t":"Summary: wind, weight, altitude, flaps","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#effects","k":"Wind changes only ground-referenced gradients and ranges; weight lowers climb and endurance but not glide range; altitude and flaps reduce climb. Summary table "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Load factor at 60°: 2 g","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Load factor at 45°: 1.41 g","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"2 × TAS in a turn: 4 × radius, ½ rate","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMP: 0.76 VMD","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet range, constant alt: 1.32 VMD","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet range, cruise climb: 1.19 VMD","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"LRC: +4% speed, 99% range","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Step climb: ±2000 ft, 4000 ft steps","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Step vs cruise climb: ≈ 99% efficiency","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cruise climb until: ≈ M 0.8","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Weight +5%: fuel burn +4.5%","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Rate-1 bank: IAS/10 + 7","s":"Handbook 7 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 7 · Flight Mechanics: Climb, Glide, Turn, Range","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Level flight (2-42)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Steady climb (2-43)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cruise climb and step climb (2-44)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Descent and glide (2-45)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Steady turn (2-46)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cruise, range and endurance (2-48)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Summary of effects (2-47)","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 7 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-07.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 8. High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","s":"What changes as an aircraft approaches and passes the speed of sound: where shockwaves form and move, what they do to lift, drag, pitch and controls, the designs that delay or tame them, how stability changes, and the structural limits of the manoeuvre envelope and aeroelastic failures.","h":"chapters/hb-08.html","k":"Mcrit · shock formation · normal and oblique shocks · Mach tuck · area rule · supercritical wing · V-n envelope · flutter "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Where does each speed regime start?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#regimes","k":"Low subsonic to M 0.4, high subsonic to Mcrit (~0.75), transonic to M 1.2, supersonic to M 5 and hypersonic beyond. Speed regimes 0.4, Mcrit, 1.2, 5."},{"t":"MFS, ML, MMO, Mcrit, Mdet","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#mach-defs","k":"Mcrit is the free-stream Mach number at which the air somewhere on the aircraft (usually over maximum camber) first reaches M 1. Shocks follow, with falling CL and rising drag. More AoA (more weight) means faster local flow, so a lower Mcrit. Mcrit and friends Critical = the first sonic spot."},{"t":"Where are the shockwaves at each Mach number?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#shocks","k":"At Mcrit a normal shock appears on the upper surface; past it the shock moves back, another forms underneath, and separation behind the top shock causes the shock stall. By about M 0.98 both shocks sit at the trailing edge and lift recovers. Above M 1 a bow wave forms ahead, attaching to the leading edge by about M 1.4, when CP settles at 50% chord. Shock formation Top first, then bottom, both back, bow in front."},{"t":"CL, CD and CP through the transonic range","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#cl-cd","k":"CL rises to Mcrit, drops at the shock stall and recovers when the shocks reach the trailing edge; CD climbs throughout the transonic range; CP settles at about 50% supersonic. CL, CD and CP with Mach "},{"t":"Normal, oblique or expansion wave?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#shock-types","k":"Behind a normal shock the flow is always subsonic, unchanged in direction, with the biggest jump in pressure, density and temperature and the biggest loss of energy. An oblique shock turns the flow and may leave it supersonic, with less loss. An expansion wave turns the flow the other way and speeds it up with no energy loss. Shock types Normal stops it, oblique bends it, expansion frees it."},{"t":"What changes across each wave?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#shock-table","k":"Normal shocks slow flow to subsonic with a large energy loss; oblique shocks turn it with less loss; expansion waves speed it up with none. Across the shocks "},{"t":"Convergent or divergent: which speeds the flow up?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#duct","k":"Subsonic flow speeds up in a convergent duct, supersonic flow in a divergent one. Ducts "},{"t":"What do shocks do to lift, drag, pitch and controls?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#effects","k":"Shocks cut CLmax, add wave and interference drag, shift the CP aft to cause Mach tuck, and spoil control effectiveness or cause control buzz. Effects of shocks Shocks: less lift, more drag, nose tucks."},{"t":"Which design fixes what?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#designs","k":"Mach trimmers, area ruling, thin and swept wings, supercritical sections, canards and deltas each tackle a particular high-speed problem. High-speed designs Thin, swept, area-ruled, supercritical."},{"t":"Supercritical vs conventional aerofoil","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#supercrit","k":"A flat-topped, aft-cambered supercritical section delays and weakens the upper-surface shock while staying thick enough for strength and fuel. Supercritical aerofoil "},{"t":"Up or down as Mach number rises?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#stability","k":"As Mach rises longitudinal stability and trim drag increase, while lateral and directional stability decrease. Stability at high Mach "},{"t":"Yaw divergence vs pitch divergence","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#coupling","k":"Yaw divergence affects aircraft strong in pitch but weak in yaw; pitch divergence the reverse. Limiting roll rates and adding directional stability prevent both. Inertia coupling "},{"t":"How do you read a manoeuvre envelope?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#envelope","k":"The curved CLmax lines show the most load factor the wing can make at each EAS: beyond them it stalls, it does not break. Where the curve meets 1 g is the basic stall speed; where it meets the limit load factor is the manoeuvring speed. The g limits carry a 50% margin to failure, the VD line a 5–10% margin. V-n envelope Curve = stall; flat top = strength; right wall = speed."},{"t":"Envelope details","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#env-list","k":"The CLmax boundary means stall rather than damage; the g limits carry a 50% margin to failure; the right-hand VD wall carries a 5–10% caution margin. Envelope details "},{"t":"Aileron reversal, divergence or flutter?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#aeroelastic","k":"Aileron reversal: at high IAS a down-going aileron twists a flexible wing nose-down so the roll goes the wrong way. Divergence: with the CP ahead of the flexural axis, lift twists the wing nose-up, more lift, more twist, until it breaks. Flutter: an oscillation coupled to the structure's own vibration, usually because a control surface's CG is behind its hinge. Aeroelastic problems Reversal twists back, divergence twists up, flutter shakes apart."},{"t":"Which aeroelastic problem?","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#ae-sort","k":"Aileron reversal comes from wing twist at high speed, divergence from the CP ahead of the flexural axis, and flutter from structural vibration coupling — cured by mass balance. Reversal, divergence, flutter "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Incompressible below: M 0.4","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Transonic: M 0.75–1.2","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Supersonic: M 1.2–5","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hypersonic: M 5–10","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Mcrit (book example): M 0.81","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Shock stall: ≈ M 0.89","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Shocks at TE: ≈ M 0.98","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Bow wave attached: ≈ M 1.4","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Subsonic CP: ≈ 30% chord","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Supersonic CP: ≈ 50% chord","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cruise climb ends: ≈ M 0.8","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tip droop: up to 65°","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Compression lift: beyond M 3","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"g-limit margin: 50%","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VD margin: 5–10%","s":"Handbook 8 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 8 · High-Speed Flight, Envelope and Aeroelasticity","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Mach number and speed regimes (2-49)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"What happens as Mach number rises (2-50)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Shockwaves (2-51)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Effects of shockwaves (2-52)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Designs for high-speed flight (2-53 to 2-54)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transonic and supersonic stability (2-55)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Manoeuvre envelope (2-56)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aeroelastic problems (2-57)","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 8 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-08.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 9. Propellers","s":"A propeller is a rotating wing: the angles that describe it, why it is twisted, how fixed, variable and constant-speed propellers manage their angle of attack, the forces trying to bend and twist each blade, and the four ways a propeller pushes the aircraft off line.","h":"chapters/hb-09.html","k":"Blade angle, helix angle, AoA · slip, GMP, EMP · constant-speed unit · CTM and ATM · torque, precession, slipstream, P-factor "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Blade angle, helix angle and angle of attack","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#angles","k":"Blade angle is measured from the plane of rotation to the chord. The blade section actually moves along a helix set by rotational and forward speed; the helix angle is that path's angle from the plane of rotation. Angle of attack is what is left: blade angle minus helix angle. More forward speed steepens the helix and cuts the AoA. Blade angles Blade = Helix + Attack."},{"t":"Every pitch definition","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#pitch-defs","k":"Geometric pitch is the ideal screw advance, effective pitch the real one; slip is the difference. EMP exceeds GMP, and blades are twisted to keep the pitch constant. Pitch definitions Slip = geometric minus effective pitch."},{"t":"Blade angles from feather to reverse","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#ranges","k":"Blade angles run from feathered (85–90°) through cruise and flight idle down to ground idle and negative reverse angles. Blade-angle ranges "},{"t":"Fine or coarse?","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#types","k":"A fixed-pitch blade is right for one speed only: at low speed and high RPM its AoA is too big, at high speed too small. A variable-pitch propeller uses fine pitch for take-off and coarse for cruise; a constant-speed unit does it automatically, increasing the blade angle as TAS rises so the AoA and RPM stay put. Fine and coarse Fine to go, coarse to cruise."},{"t":"Which forces bend and twist a blade?","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#forces","k":"Centrifugal force stretches the blade; thrust bends it forward; torque bends it back against rotation. Two twisting moments fight over the pitch: centrifugal twisting moment turns it fine, aerodynamic twisting moment (CP ahead of the pitch axis) turns it coarse. Normally ATM wins; windmilling, ATM reverses and joins CTM. Forces on a blade CTM fines, ATM coarsens."},{"t":"CTM or ATM?","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#ctm-atm","k":"Centrifugal twisting moment turns blades towards fine pitch; aerodynamic twisting moment, from the CP ahead of the pitch axis, turns them coarse and normally wins. CTM or ATM? "},{"t":"Thrust, windmilling and power absorption","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#thrust","k":"A blade’s total reaction splits into thrust and rotational drag; a windmilling propeller makes drag, more than a stopped one; absorbing more power needs more blades, chord or RPM within tip-speed limits. Thrust and windmilling "},{"t":"Propeller formulas","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#solidity","k":"Solidity is total blade chord over circumference; efficiency is thrust power over shaft power; tip speed must stay below Mach 1. Propeller formulas "},{"t":"Solidity","s":"Handbook 9 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#solidity","k":""},{"t":"Efficiency","s":"Handbook 9 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#solidity","k":""},{"t":"Tip speed","s":"Handbook 9 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#solidity","k":"Keep it below M 1."},{"t":"Why does a single-engine aircraft swing on take-off?","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#effects","k":"With a clockwise propeller (seen from behind): torque reaction rolls the aircraft left and loads the left wheel; the spiral slipstream hits the left of the fin and yaws it left; at high AoA the down-going right blade bites harder (P-factor) and yaws it left; raising the tail of a tail-dragger precesses the nose left. All are worst at low speed and high power. Effects on the aircraft Clockwise prop: everything goes left."},{"t":"Which propeller effect?","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#effect-sort","k":"Contra-rotating propellers cancel torque, precession and slipstream effects. Which effect? "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Feathered: 85–90°","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"In-flight coarse: 20–35°","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flight idle: 10–12°","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Start locks: 1–2°","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ground idle: 0°","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Reverse: −2° to −8°","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Max useful blades: ≈ 6","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tip speed limit: < M 1","s":"Handbook 9 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 9 · Propellers","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Pitch and angles (2-58)","s":"Handbook 9 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fixed, variable and constant-speed propellers (2-59)","s":"Handbook 9 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Forces on a blade (2-59)","s":"Handbook 9 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Power absorption and solidity (2-60)","s":"Handbook 9 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Propeller effects on the aircraft (2-60) — clockwise propeller seen from behind","s":"Handbook 9 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 9 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-09.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 10. Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","s":"How an airframe carries its loads and why it eventually tires: load limits, safe-life and fail-safe design, fatigue, wing and fuselage construction; then the hydraulic and pneumatic systems that move the heavy parts.","h":"chapters/hb-10.html","k":"Stress and strain · proof and ultimate load · fatigue and Miner's rule · monocoque · Pascal · accumulators · bleed-air systems "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Stress, strain and the load types","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#terms","k":"Stress is force per area, strain is fractional deformation (no unit) and stiffness is their ratio; bending combines compression and tension, torsion is twisting. Load terms Stress is the push, strain is the stretch."},{"t":"Limit, proof and ultimate load","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#limits","k":"The design limit load is the most the aircraft should ever meet. At proof load (1.125 × DLL) nothing may be permanently bent and systems must still work. At ultimate load (1.5 × DLL) it may deform but must not collapse. Load limits One, one-eighth more, one-and-a-half: limit, proof, ultimate."},{"t":"Safe-life or fail-safe?","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#philosophy","k":"Safe-life parts are retired after a set life; fail-safe structures rely on redundant load paths and inspections to catch damage in time. Safe-life vs fail-safe Safe-life retires; fail-safe has a spare path."},{"t":"How many cycles until it cracks?","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#fatigue","k":"Loads well below the ultimate stress still add up. The S–N curve shows how many cycles each stress level can be applied before failure: about a hundred cycles at 80% of ultimate, but ten million at 20%. Miner's rule adds the fractions used up: Σ n/N = 1 at failure. Fatigue curve Small loads, many times, still break things."},{"t":"Where fatigue starts and how to slow it","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#fatigue-list","k":"Fatigue cracks start at holes and corners, are counted in pressurisation cycles for airliners, and grow through initiation, moderation, growth and fracture. Fatigue facts "},{"t":"Miner's rule","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#miner","k":"Damage fractions add up: when the sum of cycles applied over cycles to failure reaches one, the part fails. Miner’s rule "},{"t":"Cumulative damage","s":"Handbook 10 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#miner","k":"n = cycles applied at a stress level; N = cycles to failure at that level."},{"t":"What does each part of a wing carry?","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#wing","k":"The spar is the main spanwise beam taking bending and torsion. Ribs run chordwise and hold the aerofoil shape. Stringers stiffen the skin against buckling. Front spar, rear spar, skins and ribs together make the torsion box, which resists bending and twisting and usually holds the fuel. Wing structure Spar spans, ribs shape, stringers stiffen."},{"t":"Truss, monocoque or semi-monocoque?","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#fuselage","k":"A monocoque skin carries everything; semi-monocoque adds longerons, stringers and frames; old light aircraft used welded trusses. Fuselage construction Mono: skin only. Semi: skin plus skeleton."},{"t":"Pascal's law and force multiplication","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#pascal","k":"Pressure in a confined fluid acts equally everywhere. Push a small piston and the same pressure on a big piston gives a big force: F = P × A. Fluid barely compresses (about 1%), so the response is immediate. Pascal’s law Small push, big piston, big force."},{"t":"Pressure","s":"Handbook 10 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#pascal","k":""},{"t":"Output force","s":"Handbook 10 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#pascal","k":"1 N on 1 cm² → 50 N on 50 cm²."},{"t":"Open, constant-delivery or constant-pressure?","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#hyd-types","k":"Open systems run one service at a time; closed constant-delivery systems use cut-outs and accumulators; constant-pressure systems vary pump output and need no accumulator. Hydraulic systems Open does one job; closed keeps pressure ready."},{"t":"Trace a basic hydraulic circuit","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#hyd-system","k":"Fluid leaves the pressurised reservoir, is pumped through a filter, held at system pressure by the accumulator and relief valve, and sent by the selector valve to one side of the actuator; the other side returns to the reservoir. Hydraulic circuit Reservoir → pump → filter → accumulator → selector → jack → return."},{"t":"Which component does that?","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#parts","k":"Accumulators store and smooth pressure, reservoirs are pressurised against cavitation, valves control direction, rate and pressure, and fuses and locks guard against leaks and creep. Hydraulic parts "},{"t":"The ideal hydraulic fluid","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#hyd-fluid","k":"An ideal fluid is incompressible, lubricating, non-flammable and stable; mineral fluid is red, synthetic blue, and milky fluid means air in it. Hydraulic fluid Red mineral, blue synthetic, milky means air."},{"t":"Pneumatic vs hydraulic","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#pneumatic","k":"Pneumatic systems use engine bleed air: light, non-flammable, leak-tolerant and needing no return lines, but air compresses, so they work at lower pressure and cannot give the fast, precise, high-power response of hydraulics. Pneumatic vs hydraulic Air is light but spongy."},{"t":"What runs on bleed air, and the parts","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#pn-uses","k":"Bleed air drives air conditioning, pressurisation, anti-icing, engine starting and reversers; water traps sit at the lowest point and methanol stops freezing. Pneumatic uses and parts "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Proof load: 1.125 × DLL","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ultimate load: 1.5 × DLL","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"80% ultimate: ≈ 100 cycles","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"20% ultimate: ≈ 10 million cycles","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Crack growth stage: > 1 mm","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Composite fatigue-free below: 80% ultimate","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hydraulic fluid compression: ≈ 1%","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Biplane speed: < 200 kt","s":"Handbook 10 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 10 · Structures, Hydraulics and Pneumatics","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Terms and loads (3-3)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Load limits and design philosophies (3-3)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Metal fatigue (3-4)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Airframe construction (3-5)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hydraulics — principles (3-6)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hydraulic components (3-7)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pneumatic system (3-8)","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 10 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-10.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 11. Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","s":"Everything between the aircraft and the runway, and between the pilot and the control surfaces: gear layouts and shock struts, drum, disc, anti-skid and autobrakes, tyre care and aquaplaning, then manual, power-assisted and fly-by-wire controls with their artificial feel.","h":"chapters/hb-11.html","k":"Gear layouts · oleo struts · anti-skid and autobrake · tyre wear, creep and fusible plugs · aquaplaning · Q-feel and fly-by-wire "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which gear layout is which?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#layouts","k":"The tricycle is the modern norm, with about 90% of the weight on the mains behind the CG, so it is directionally stable on the ground. A tail-dragger has its mains ahead of the CG: low drag and a nose-high take-off roll, but prone to swing and poor forward view. Bicycle gear needs wing-tip outriggers; quadricycle gear is crosswind-sensitive. Gear layouts Mains behind the CG keep you straight."},{"t":"Gear layouts on one card","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#layout-table","k":"Tricycle gear is the stable standard; tail-draggers swing; bicycle gear needs outriggers; quadricycle gear is crosswind-sensitive; multi-bogie gear spreads load and survives a burst tyre. Layout table "},{"t":"How does an oleo-pneumatic strut work?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#oleo","k":"On landing the piston rises, forcing oil through a restricting orifice (clack valve) and compressing the gas: the gas absorbs the shock like a spring. The gas then pushes back, but oil must return through the restriction, so the extension is slowed and the rebound damped. Oleo strut Gas is the spring, oil is the brake."},{"t":"Gear facts","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#gear-facts","k":"Struts must never bottom or top out; nose gear struts are often two-stage; fixed gear can be split-axle, tripod, spring-leaf or a single strut. Gear facts "},{"t":"Drum or disc brakes?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#brakes","k":"Disc brakes dissipate heat better than drum brakes, which suffer in water and distort; hot brakes fade and badly adjusted ones drag. Drum vs disc brakes "},{"t":"What does anti-skid do?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#antiskid","k":"Anti-skid holds each wheel just short of skidding by sensing rapid wheel deceleration and briefly releasing pressure (brake modulation). It gives touchdown protection, skid control and locked-wheel protection, and usually switches itself off below about 20 kt. Anti-skid Release, regrip, repeat."},{"t":"What does the wear pattern tell you?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#tyres","k":"Over-inflation bulges the tread and wears the centre; under-inflation flattens it and wears the shoulders, overheats the tyre and is a leading cause of tyre failure. Tyres are filled with nitrogen and checked cold: they can read 10% high after taxi. Tyre wear Over = middle, under = edges."},{"t":"Tyre facts: marking, creep or protection?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#tyre-facts","k":"Markings show balance, strength and venting; creep shows as misaligned paint marks; fusible plugs and nitrogen filling are safety measures. Tyre facts "},{"t":"Tyre pressures and tubeless advantages","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#tyre-table","k":"Tyre classes run from low (25–35 psi) to extra high (over 90 psi); tubeless tyres are lighter and immune to creep damage. Tyre pressures "},{"t":"Shimmy and nose-wheel control","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#shimmy","k":"Nose-wheel shimmy is a rapid oscillation often caused by a broken torque link; twin wheels, Marstrand tyres and dampers prevent it. Shimmy "},{"t":"At what speed will it aquaplane?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#aqua","k":"Water pressure under the tyre grows with the square of speed; when it equals tyre pressure the tyre lifts off. Minimum aquaplaning speed ≈ 9 √P (P in psi) for a rotating tyre, about 7.7 √P for a non-rotating one at touchdown. Once started it continues to a much lower speed. Aquaplaning speed Nine root P."},{"t":"Rotating tyre","s":"Handbook 11 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#aqua","k":"P in psi. (34 √P with kg/cm².)"},{"t":"Non-rotating tyre (landing)","s":"Handbook 11 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#aqua","k":""},{"t":"Types of aquaplaning and braking action","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#aqua-types","k":"Dynamic aquaplaning rides on standing water, viscous on a thin film over a smooth surface, reverted-rubber on steam from a locked wheel; braking coefficients grade the surface. Aquaplaning types "},{"t":"Reversible or irreversible?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#controls","k":"With power-assisted controls hydraulics provide part of the force and the pilot still feels the air loads (reversible). With fully powered or fly-by-wire controls hydraulics do all the work, air loads cannot move the stick (irreversible), and feel has to be manufactured, usually proportional to dynamic pressure (Q-feel). Powered controls Part power: real feel. Full power: fake feel."},{"t":"Artificial feel units","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#feel","k":"Spring feel is simple but speed-blind; Q-feel scales with dynamic pressure; g-feel scales with load factor. Artificial feel "},{"t":"Take-off configuration warning: what triggers it?","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#config-warn","k":"Advancing thrust with trim, flaps, speed brake, doors or control locks wrongly set triggers the take-off configuration warning. Take-off config warning Push the throttles in the wrong set-up: the horn tells you."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Tricycle main-gear share: ≈ 90%","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Anti-skid cut-off: ≈ 20 kt","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Electrical anti-skid gain: ≈ 15%","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Autobrake disengage: < 20 kt","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Taxi to save tyres: < 22 kt (40 km/h)","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hot tyre pressure rise: up to 10%","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Tubeless weight saving: ≈ 7.5%","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aquaplaning (rotating): 9 √P kt","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aquaplaning (non-rotating): 7.7 √P kt","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Good braking coefficient: > 0.40","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Poor braking coefficient: ≤ 0.25","s":"Handbook 11 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 11 · Landing Gear, Brakes, Tyres and Powered Controls","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Landing gear design and layout (3-9)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Shock absorbers (3-10)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Brakes (3-11)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Tyres (3-12)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Nose-wheel control and shimmy (3-13)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aquaplaning (3-13)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Flight-control systems (3-14)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Powered-control components and requirements (3-15)","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 11 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-11.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 12. Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","s":"How bleed air is cooled and fed to the cabin, how the cabin pressure is scheduled and protected by its valves, and how ice is detected, prevented and removed from wings, propellers, intakes and windscreens.","h":"chapters/hb-12.html","k":"Cold air unit · bootstrap and turbo-fan · isobaric cabin · outflow and relief valves · anti-ice vs de-ice · ice detectors "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What must the cabin air be?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":" Cabin air standards "},{"t":"Temperature: 18–24 °C","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":""},{"t":"Humidity: ≈ 30%","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":""},{"t":"Flow per crew member: 10 cu ft/min","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":""},{"t":"CO maximum: 1 : 20,000","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":""},{"t":"Airliner flow: 1.5 kg/min/pax","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":"50% fresh, 50% recirculated"},{"t":"Fighter cockpit: 5 kg/min","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#standards","k":"80% pilot, 20% demist"},{"t":"How can hot bleed air come out cold?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#acm","k":"Bleed air is first cooled in a ram-air heat exchanger, then compressed (getting hotter) and cooled again, then expanded through a turbine. Making the turbine do work takes energy out of the air, so it leaves much colder; a water separator removes the condensed moisture before it is mixed with hot air to the selected temperature. Air-cycle machine Squeeze, cool, then make it work: it comes out cold."},{"t":"Turbo-compressor, bootstrap or turbo-fan?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#systems","k":"Turbo-compressor systems use LP bleed, bootstrap systems HP bleed (useless on the ground without ram air), and turbo-fan systems HP bleed with a fan so they cool on the ground too. Cooling systems Bootstrap needs ram air; turbo-fan cools on the ground."},{"t":"Air-conditioning parts","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#ac-parts","k":"Bleed and ram air are cooled in heat exchangers and the cold air unit, dried by the water separator and mixed to temperature before distribution. Air-con parts "},{"t":"How does the cabin altitude follow the aircraft?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#schedule","k":"An isobaric (airliner) cabin climbs gently with the aircraft until it reaches about 8000 ft, then stays there while the aircraft goes higher: the gap is the differential pressure the hull carries. A hypobaric fighter cabin settles near 25,000 ft so a hole causes a smaller, safer decompression. Pressure schedule Airliner 8, fighter 25, warning 10."},{"t":"What does each pressurisation valve do?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#valves","k":"Air comes in at a constant mass flow; the outflow valve sets the cabin pressure by letting it out (closing it raises the differential). The safety valve stops too much positive differential, the inward relief valve stops the outside pressure exceeding the cabin's, the dump valve empties the cabin on command, and blow-out panels protect the floor between cabin and cargo. Pressurisation valves Constant in, controlled out."},{"t":"Which valve?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#valve-stamp","k":"The outflow valve controls pressure; safety and inward relief valves guard against too much positive or negative differential; the dump valve empties the cabin and blow-out panels protect the floor. Which valve? "},{"t":"A pressurised flight from taxi to shutdown","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#press-ops","k":"Pre-pressurise on the taxi, let the cabin climb and descend gently in step with the aircraft, hold a constant differential in cruise, and open the outflow valve on the ground. A pressurised flight "},{"t":"Pressurised and unpressurised zones; cockpit gauges","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#zones","k":"Cockpit, cabin and holds are pressurised; radome, wheel bays and tail cone are not; cabin altitude, rate and differential are displayed. Pressurised zones "},{"t":"Anti-icing or de-icing?","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#ice","k":"Anti-icing stops ice forming at all (hot bleed air on intakes and leading edges, heated windscreens). De-icing lets a little build up and then removes it (pneumatic boots, cyclic propeller heating). Frost alone can stop a wing lifting, so ground de-icing fluid is used before flight. Anti-ice vs de-ice Anti = never lets it stick; De = knocks it off."},{"t":"Ice detectors","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#detectors","k":"Accretion probes, blocked-hole tubes, torque-sensing blades and vibrating rods all detect ice; the vibrating rod’s frequency falls as ice builds. Ice detectors "},{"t":"Ice-protection devices","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#devices","k":"Pneumatic boots and cyclic propeller heating remove ice; hot bleed air and heated windscreens prevent it; freezing-point fluid works best at low speed. Ice-protection devices Boots break ice; heat stops it forming."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Isobaric cabin: 8000 ft","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hypobaric cabin: 25,000 ft","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cabin altitude warning: > 10,000 ft","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Max cabin rate: 1500 fpm","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Normal cabin rate: 300–500 fpm","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Pre-pressurisation: 0.1 psi","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Inward relief opens: −0.5 to −1 psi","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ignored altitude change: ±500 ft","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Prop de-ice coverage: inner ⅓","s":"Handbook 12 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 12 · Air Conditioning, Pressurisation and Ice Protection","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Air conditioning (3-16)","s":"Handbook 12 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressurisation (3-17)","s":"Handbook 12 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Ice and rain protection (3-18)","s":"Handbook 12 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 12 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-12.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 13. Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","s":"What composites are and why they suit airframes; the fuels aircraft burn, how they are graded, coloured and contaminated; the tank-to-engine fuel system; and the emergency, oxygen, smoke and fire equipment an aircraft must carry.","h":"chapters/hb-13.html","k":"Fibre and matrix · aero-elastic tailoring · octane · AVGAS and Jet fuel · booster pumps · oxygen, ELT, extinguishers, fire detection "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What is a composite and how is it laid up?","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#composite","k":"A composite is strong fibre held in a resin matrix; the matrix carries the load into the fibres. By choosing the fibre direction in each ply, the designer decides how the part bends and twists — aero-elastic tailoring, the cure for forward-swept-wing divergence. Fibre and matrix Fibre is the muscle, resin is the glue."},{"t":"Composites: for and against","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#comp-pairs","k":"Composites are light, corrosion-proof, dent-resistant and can be tailored by direction, but they soak up moisture, handle lightning and impact poorly and cost more than metal. Pros and cons Light and rust-free, but hates lightning and knocks."},{"t":"Which fuel is which colour?","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#fuels","k":"AVGAS is dyed by grade: 80 red, 100 green, 100LL blue. Jet fuel (Jet A-1 kerosene) is clear to pale straw. Mixing up piston and turbine fuel is dangerous, so the colour is the first check. Fuel colours Red 80, Green 100, Blue Low-Lead, Straw for jets."},{"t":"Fuel data card","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#fuel-table","k":"Jet A-1 has SG 0.8, flash point about 38 °C and a freezing (wax) point near −50 °C; wide-cut Jet B is lighter (0.77) with a flash point of −20 °C, so it is far more flammable. Fuel data Jet A-1 flashes at 38 °C; Jet B at −20 °C."},{"t":"What does an octane number mean?","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#octane","k":"The octane number measures resistance to detonation, by comparison with two reference fuels: iso-octane (100, never knocks) and normal heptane (0, knocks readily). A 95 fuel knocks like a 95/5 mix of the two. Octane rating Iso is the hero (100), heptane the zero."},{"t":"Waxing, vapour lock or water?","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#fuel-problems","k":"Waxing is fuel solidifying in the cold, vapour lock is fuel boiling at altitude, and a foggy sample means water. Fuel problems "},{"t":"The ideal fuel and its additives","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#additives","k":"A good fuel flows and burns completely in every condition, carries plenty of energy per kilogram and is safe to handle; additives fight icing, static, poor lubricity, corrosion and microbes. Ideal fuel and additives "},{"t":"From the tank to the engine pump","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#system","k":"Fuel is pushed by submerged centrifugal booster pumps from vented, baffled tanks, through cross-feed and the firewall LP cock, to the engine-driven HP pump. The booster pumps prevent vapour lock and HP-pump cavitation. Tank to engine Boost low, cock at the wall, HP at the engine."},{"t":"Fuel-system numbers","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#jettison","k":" Fuel jettison "},{"t":"Tank vent space: ≥ 2%","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#jettison","k":"of tank volume"},{"t":"After jettison (book): climb to 10,000 ft","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#jettison","k":"then 45 min flight"},{"t":"What must be carried, and when?","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#equipment","k":"Life jackets beyond 50 nm from land, rafts beyond 120 minutes or 400 nm, emergency lighting for at least 30 minutes when there are more than 9 seats, and two ELTs (one automatic) above 19 passengers. Equipment numbers Jackets 50, rafts 400."},{"t":"Emergency oxygen","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#oxygen","k":"Crew diluter-demand regulators mix more oxygen as altitude rises, reaching 100% by 34,000 ft; emergency mode gives 100% under pressure. Passenger masks drop automatically above 14,000 ft cabin altitude and chemical generators last about 15 minutes. Keep oil and grease away from oxygen. Oxygen system Masks drop at 14, pure at 34."},{"t":"Smoke detector or fire detector?","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#detect","k":"Smoke detectors (toilets, cargo, avionics bays) look for particles by light or ionisation; fire detectors sense heat — old melting links, differential expansion, or a continuous loop whose resistance falls as it heats. Smoke and fire detection "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Iso-octane / heptane: 100 / 0","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet A-1 SG: 0.8","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":"at 15 °C"},{"t":"Jet A-1 flash point: 38 °C","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet B flash point: −20 °C","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Jet A wax point: −40 °C","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Composite fatigue threshold: 80% ultimate","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Life jackets: 50 nm","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Life rafts: 120 min / 400 nm","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Emergency lighting: 30 min","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ELT endurance: 48 h","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Masks drop: 14,000 ft cabin","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"100% O₂ (diluter demand): 34,000 ft","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Chemical O₂: ≈ 15 min","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Portable O₂: 1800 psi","s":"Handbook 13 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 13 · Composites, Fuel, Fuel Systems and Emergency Equipment","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Composite materials (3-19)","s":"Handbook 13 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Fuel (3-20)","s":"Handbook 13 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft fuel system (3-21)","s":"Handbook 13 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Emergency equipment (3-22)","s":"Handbook 13 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 13 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-13.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 14. Piston Engines","s":"How a reciprocating engine turns fuel into crankshaft power: its parts and volumes, the Otto and diesel cycles, the four strokes and valve timing, cooling, supercharging and turbocharging, mixture and carburettor, magneto ignition, and the twin enemies detonation and pre-ignition.","h":"chapters/hb-14.html","k":"Four-stroke Otto cycle · compression ratio · valve timing · super- and turbochargers · mixture · carburettor icing · magnetos · detonation "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Compression ratio and horsepower","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#volumes","k":"Compression ratio is total volume over clearance volume: (swept + clearance) ÷ clearance. Indicated horsepower is made inside the cylinders; friction takes some away to leave brake horsepower; the propeller turns part of that into thrust horsepower. Cylinder volumes Indicated in the cylinder, brake at the shaft, thrust at the prop."},{"t":"Compression ratio","s":"Handbook 14 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#volumes","k":""},{"t":"Indicated HP","s":"Handbook 14 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#volumes","k":"P mean effective pressure (psi), L stroke (ft), A piston area (in²), N power strokes/min, n cylinders."},{"t":"Brake and thrust HP","s":"Handbook 14 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#volumes","k":"Mechanical efficiency = BHP ÷ IHP."},{"t":"Piston-engine definitions","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#defs","k":"Swept volume lies between TDC and BDC; clearance volume is what is left above the piston at TDC. Rated altitude is the highest altitude at which sea-level power can be kept. Definitions "},{"t":"What happens in each stroke?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#strokes","k":"Intake (piston down, charge in), compression (both valves shut), power (spark just before TDC, gas drives the piston down) and exhaust (piston up, gases out): four strokes over two crankshaft revolutions, only one of which delivers power. Four strokes Suck, squeeze, bang, blow."},{"t":"Otto or diesel; two-stroke or four-stroke?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#cycles","k":"Petrol engines burn at constant volume and are limited to about 10:1 compression by detonation; diesels compress only air to about 22:1 and burn at constant pressure — more efficient but heavy and rough, so rare in aviation. Otto vs diesel Petrol sparks at 10:1; diesel squeezes to 22:1."},{"t":"Why do the valves open early and close late?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#valves","k":"Valves take time to open, and gases have momentum, so the inlet opens before TDC (lead) and closes after BDC (lag), and the exhaust opens before BDC and closes after TDC. Around TDC both are briefly open — valve overlap — and the departing exhaust helps pull the fresh charge in, raising volumetric efficiency. Valve timing Lead in, lag out, overlap at the top."},{"t":"Too hot or too cold?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#cooling","k":"A hot engine pre-ignites, knocks and fails structurally; a cold one has poor lubrication (more friction) and lower thermal efficiency. Air-cooled engines rely on fin area and ram air; liquid-cooled engines pump coolant to a radiator. Cooling "},{"t":"Supercharger or turbocharger?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#boost","k":"Both compress the intake air so the engine keeps its power at height (density is ⅓ at 30,000 ft). A supercharger is geared to the crankshaft and compresses the mixture; a turbocharger is spun by exhaust gas, compresses air before the carburettor and is controlled by a waste gate that is fully open at sea level and closes as the aircraft climbs. Super vs turbo Super = shaft; turbo = tailpipe."},{"t":"Rich or lean, and when?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#mixture","k":"Rich (≈ 8:1) uses the extra fuel to cool the cylinders and suppress detonation: take-off and climb. Lean (≈ 20:1) saves fuel for endurance but runs hotter and closer to detonation: cruise. About 15:1 is chemically correct. Rich vs lean Rich runs cool and short; lean runs hot and long."},{"t":"Why does a carburettor ice up on a warm day?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#carb","k":"The fuel float chamber feeds fuel into the venturi, where low pressure atomises it. Venturi expansion plus fuel evaporation can drop the temperature by up to 22 °C, so ice can form at the throttle valve with outside air anywhere from +30 °C to −10 °C, especially at low power. A fixed-pitch propeller shows an RPM drop; a constant-speed propeller shows a MAP drop. Carburettor icing Up to 22 °C colder: ice on a summer day."},{"t":"What would you see?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#carb-sym","k":"Carburettor ice shows as an RPM drop with a fixed-pitch propeller and a manifold-pressure drop with a constant-speed propeller; fuel injection avoids it. Icing symptoms "},{"t":"Magnetos and dual ignition","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#ignition","k":"Each cylinder has two plugs fed by two independent engine-driven magnetos, so ignition does not depend on the aircraft battery and the spark gets stronger as RPM rises. On the magneto check, switching one off should give a small RPM drop, never a stop. Magnetos Two plugs, two magnetos, no battery needed."},{"t":"Detonation or pre-ignition?","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#knock","k":"Detonation is the unburnt charge exploding after the spark (lean, hot, low-octane, high pressure); pre-ignition is the charge lighting before the spark from a hot spot or glowing carbon. Detonation vs pre-ignition Detonation after, pre-ignition before."},{"t":"Layouts and firing order","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#layout","k":" Cylinder layouts "},{"t":"Layouts: inline · opposed · V · W · radial","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#layout","k":""},{"t":"4-cylinder firing order: 1-3-4-2","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#layout","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Petrol CR: ≈ 10 : 1","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Diesel CR: ≈ 22 : 1","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Four-stroke cycle: 720°","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Efficiency: ≈ 28%","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":"40% exhaust, 32% heat"},{"t":"Rich / correct / lean: 8 / 15 / 20 : 1","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Carb temperature drop: up to 22 °C","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Carb icing OAT: +30 to −10 °C","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Density at 30,000 ft: ≈ ⅓","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Density at 40,000 ft: ≈ ¼","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Firing order (4 cyl): 1-3-4-2","s":"Handbook 14 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 14 · Piston Engines","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Parts and definitions (4-3)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Petrol vs diesel; two- vs four-stroke (4-4)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The four strokes (4-5)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cooling (4-6)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Supercharging and turbocharging (4-6)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Mixture and carburettor (4-7)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Ignition (4-7)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Detonation and pre-ignition (4-8)","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 14 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-14.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 15. Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","s":"Why a gas turbine burns at constant pressure, where its pressure and temperature peak, how a jet makes thrust and how efficiently, and how turbojets, turbofans, turboprops and turboshafts trade jet velocity for mass flow.","h":"chapters/hb-15.html","k":"Brayton cycle · pressure, temperature, velocity · thrust equation · propulsive efficiency · bypass ratio · turboprop alpha and beta · synchrophasing "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How is a gas turbine like a piston engine?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#brayton","k":"A gas turbine sucks, squeezes, burns and blows like a piston engine, but all four happen at once in different places, and fuel burns at constant pressure: the Brayton cycle. Friction and pressure losses leave the real cycle at roughly half the ideal efficiency. Brayton cycle Same four jobs, done side by side, never stopping."},{"t":"Where are pressure, temperature and velocity highest?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#ptv","k":"Pressure climbs through the compressor and peaks at the combustion-chamber inlet; temperature peaks at the combustion-chamber outlet, making the nozzle guide vanes the hottest part; the gas then gives up pressure and temperature in the turbine and is accelerated out of the nozzle. Pressure, temperature, velocity Pressure peaks going in to the burner, heat peaks coming out."},{"t":"EPR and EGT","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#indic","k":"EPR is exhaust (LP turbine outlet) pressure over intake (LP compressor inlet) pressure, a measure of thrust. EGT is measured at the LP turbine outlet. Switching bleed air on raises EGT and lowers EPR. EPR and EGT "},{"t":"How much thrust, and how efficiently?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#thrust","k":"Thrust is mass flow times the velocity added, plus a pressure term when the nozzle is choked. It is greatest at the start of the take-off run and falls as speed rises, until ram compression restores it above about 500 kt. Propulsive efficiency is 2Va ÷ (Vj + Va): best when the jet is only a little faster than the aircraft. Thrust equation Move lots of air a little, not a little air a lot."},{"t":"Total thrust","s":"Handbook 15 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#thrust","k":"Pressure thrust only when the nozzle is choked."},{"t":"Propulsive efficiency","s":"Handbook 15 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#thrust","k":""},{"t":"Overall efficiency","s":"Handbook 15 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#thrust","k":""},{"t":"What changes jet thrust?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#perf","k":"Jet thrust falls with speed (less velocity added) until ram compression helps, and falls with altitude and temperature because the air is less dense; flat rating caps thrust on cold days to protect the engine. Jet performance Hot, high air: less thrust."},{"t":"What does the fan add?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#bypass","k":"A turbofan sends most of its air round the core through the fan duct. The bypass ratio is bypass mass flow over core mass flow: below 1.5 is low bypass, above is high bypass. Moving more air more slowly wastes less kinetic energy, so the fan gives thrust for no extra fuel, with less noise. Bypass engines Bypass ratio = cold ÷ hot."},{"t":"Turbofan vs turbojet","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#fan-pros","k":"Turbofans have better take-off thrust and SFC, are 20–25% quieter and tolerate debris better, though the big fan adds drag at high speed. Turbofan advantages "},{"t":"Which engine is most efficient at which speed?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#eff-chart","k":"Turboprops have the best propulsive efficiency up to about 350 kt; then high-bypass turbofans, low-bypass fans, and finally the pure turbojet at the highest speeds. Efficiency by speed Prop, high-bypass, low-bypass, jet: as speed rises."},{"t":"Direct drive or free turbine?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#tprop","k":"A turboprop takes nearly all the gas energy out to turn a propeller: efficient below about 350 kt, with maximum thrust standing still. In a free-turbine engine the propeller sits on its own power turbine, so the starter does not have to turn the propeller and each shaft runs at its best speed. Turboprop Free turbine: starts easy, spins free."},{"t":"Alpha or beta mode?","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#modes","k":"In alpha mode the power lever sets fuel and the governor holds RPM; in beta mode, used on the ground and for reverse, the lever sets blade angle directly. Alpha and beta "},{"t":"Synchronising vs synchrophasing","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#sync","k":"Synchronising runs all propellers at exactly the same RPM to stop beat noise; synchrophasing also locks the angle between the blades of neighbouring propellers to cut noise and vibration further. Synchronising "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Actual vs ideal efficiency: ≈ 50%","s":"Handbook 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Thrust recovers above: ≈ 500 kt","s":"Handbook 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Low / high bypass: < 1.5 / > 1.5","s":"Handbook 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Turbofan noise reduction: 20–25%","s":"Handbook 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Turboprop best below: ≈ 350 kt","s":"Handbook 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Afterburner gain (book): up to 80%","s":"Handbook 15 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 15 · Gas Turbine Theory and Engine Types","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Gas turbine principles (4-9)","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Pressure, temperature and velocity through the engine (4-10)","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turbojet (4-11)","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turbofan (bypass engine) (4-12)","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turboprop and turboshaft (4-13)","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turboprop control (4-14)","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 15 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-15.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 16. Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","s":"Follow the air through the hot heart of a jet engine: how the intake slows and compresses it, how compressors add pressure and why they stall and surge, how the combustor keeps a flame alight in a gale, and how turbines extract the work and survive the heat.","h":"chapters/hb-16.html","k":"Ram recovery · critical and super-critical intakes · axial vs centrifugal · stall and surge · air split 20/20/60 · burners · NGVs · impulse and reaction · creep "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Critical, sub-critical or super-critical?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#intake","k":"A subsonic pitot intake is a divergent duct that slows the air and raises its pressure. When it delivers exactly what the engine wants (critical) that should be cruise. Sub-critical: the engine wants less and air spills round the lip (drag). Super-critical: the engine wants more, as at take-off, and the flow separates inside — auxiliary doors open to help. Intake states Sub spills, super starves."},{"t":"Ram effect and an ideal intake","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#ram","k":"Ram compression grows with forward speed, matching the compressor's own pressure rise at about M 1. On the ground the intake actually sits below ambient pressure; ram-pressure recovery is the speed at which it gets back to ambient. Ram effect "},{"t":"Supersonic intake designs","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#supersonic","k":"Supersonic intakes slow the air to about M 0.5 at the compressor through oblique shocks (small loss) and one final normal shock (big loss). The more oblique shocks, the less loss. Variable ramps keep the shock on the lip at every Mach; if it is pushed forward and oscillates you get intake buzz. Supersonic intakes Many oblique, one normal."},{"t":"Axial or centrifugal compressor?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#comp","k":"A centrifugal impeller flings air outward into a diffuser: about 4:1 in one stage, rugged and simple, but a big frontal area. An axial compressor squeezes the air in many small rotor-stator steps of about 1.2:1 each through a narrowing annulus: high overall ratio and mass flow per frontal area, but FOD-sensitive and stall-prone. Axial vs centrifugal Centrifugal is tough and fat; axial is slim and fussy."},{"t":"Axial vs centrifugal on one card","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#comp-table","k":"Axial wins on mass flow, efficiency and overall pressure ratio; centrifugal wins on simplicity, FOD tolerance and stall resistance. Comparison card Axial for flow, centrifugal for toughness."},{"t":"What happens in one stage?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#stage","k":"The rotor adds velocity; the stator diffuses it into pressure (about half the rise in each) and guides it to the next rotor. Across the stator velocity falls while pressure and temperature rise; colder air gives more pressure rise for the same work. Inside a stage "},{"t":"Why does a compressor stall?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#stall","k":"At a given RPM, less mass flow means lower axial velocity and a higher angle of attack on the blades; past the critical angle the flow separates, like a wing. A stall in one stage is a stall; all stages at once is a surge, with loud bangs, rising EGT and falling RPM. The surge margin is the gap between the operating and surge lines. Stall and surge Slow air in, steep angle on: stall."},{"t":"Cause of stall, or cure?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#stall-sort","k":"Anything that disturbs or starves the intake flow, or mismatches fuel during throttle movement, can stall a compressor; variable vanes, bleed valves, multi-spools and acceleration control keep it away. Causes and cures "},{"t":"Why is only a fifth of the air burnt?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#combustor","k":"About 20% of the air (primary) burns with the fuel near 15:1; another 20% (secondary) enters through swirl vanes to anchor and finish the flame; the remaining 60% (tertiary or dilution) cools the walls and brings the gas down to a temperature the turbine can survive. The flame travels only 2–4 m/s, so it has to be sheltered from the fast airflow. Air split 20 burn, 20 hold, 60 cool."},{"t":"Can, cannular or annular?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#chambers","k":"Multiple (can) chambers have separate casings; cannular chambers put separate flame tubes in one casing; annular chambers are a single ring — shortest, lightest and cleanest, but harder to develop and service. Chamber types "},{"t":"Which burner is which?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#burners","k":"Atomisers spray fine droplets: simplex (one orifice, narrow range), duplex (two orifices), spill (spills excess back), Lubbock (piston uncovers holes) and airspray. Vaporisers pre-mix fuel with hot primary air but need separate starting sprayers. Burners "},{"t":"What makes a good combustion chamber?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#ideal-cc","k":"It must burn completely and stably over a wide range of mixtures, release a lot of heat in a small space, give an even temperature across the turbine, make little carbon and lose little pressure. Ideal combustor "},{"t":"Impulse or reaction turbine?","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#turbine","k":"The convergent NGVs turn gas pressure into velocity and aim it at the blades. In an impulse turbine the blades only turn the jet (like a water wheel); in a reaction turbine the blade passages are convergent too, so the gas accelerates through them and pushes back. Real blades are impulse at the root, reaction at the tip. Impulse vs reaction Impulse at the root, reaction at the tip."},{"t":"Turbine facts","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#turbine-facts","k":"Turbine entry temperatures reach about 1700 °C with roughly 200 °C dropped per stage; blades are twisted, held by fir-tree roots, shrouded, and cooled by internal convection and surface films. Turbine facts "},{"t":"The three stages of creep","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#creep","k":"Creep is permanent stretching of a hot, highly loaded blade: fast but slowing at first, then steady, then accelerating to fracture. A little is allowed for in the tip clearance. Creep Creep: fast, steady, then snap."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Compressor-face Mach (supersonic intake): ≈ M 0.5","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Centrifugal stage: ≈ 4 : 1","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Axial stage: ≈ 1.2 : 1","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Single / multi-spool: 10 : 1 / 25 : 1","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Impeller tip speed: < 500 m/s","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Combustion air/fuel: ≈ 15 : 1","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flame speed: 2–4 m/s","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Combustor pressure loss: 3–8%","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Air split: 20 / 20 / 60%","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Atomiser droplets: 20–200 µm","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TET: ≈ 1700 °C","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Drop per turbine stage: ≈ 200 °C","s":"Handbook 16 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 16 · Intakes, Compressors, Combustors and Turbines","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Intakes (4-15)","s":"Handbook 16 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Compressors (4-18)","s":"Handbook 16 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Compressor stall and surge (4-20)","s":"Handbook 16 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Combustion chambers (4-22)","s":"Handbook 16 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turbines (4-25)","s":"Handbook 16 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 16 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-16.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 17. Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","s":"The back end and the support systems of a jet engine: how nozzles choke and how variable nozzles help, the reversers that stop the aircraft, reheat and water injection, the APU and starting faults, the engine fuel and oil systems, FADEC, and the rockets, ramjets and pulse-jets that do without parts of a gas turbine.","h":"chapters/hb-17.html","k":"Choked and con-di nozzles · thrust reversers · afterburning and water injection · APU · hot, hung and wet starts · OSG, BPCU, MBPU · dry sump · FADEC · ramjets and rockets "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Convergent or convergent–divergent nozzle?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#nozzles","k":"A convergent nozzle accelerates the gas only up to M 1; then it is choked and the leftover pressure leaves unused (under-expansion), though it adds some pressure thrust. A convergent–divergent nozzle lets the gas keep expanding to ambient pressure and supersonic speed — but only at its design pressure ratio, so aircraft use variable nozzles. Nozzles Converge to one; diverge to go beyond."},{"t":"Exhaust cone, jet pipe and variable nozzles","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#exhaust-parts","k":"The exhaust cone makes a divergent passage that slows the gas and shields the turbine disc; the double-walled jet pipe carries the gas to the nozzle; a variable nozzle opens with reheat to avoid surge and can also cut noise, ease taxiing and vector thrust. Exhaust parts "},{"t":"How do thrust reversers turn the flow?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#reversers","k":"Clamshell doors block the jet pipe and turn the hot gas forward through cascades; bucket (target) reversers swing behind the nozzle into the jet; on high-bypass engines a cold-stream reverser turns the fan air, which makes most of the thrust. Turboprops simply reverse the propeller pitch. Reverser types Clam inside, bucket behind, cold stream for the fan."},{"t":"What must a reverser never do?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#rev-req","k":"A reverser must give useful reverse thrust without disturbing the engine, re-ingesting hot gas or debris, heating the airframe or creating a fire risk, and it must be impossible to deploy by accident or by a failure. Reverser requirements "},{"t":"Reheat or water injection?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#reheat","k":"Reheat burns extra fuel in the jet pipe: hotter gas has a higher speed of sound, so the choked nozzle passes a faster jet. It is selected at 100% RPM, needs the nozzle opened to avoid surge, and drinks fuel (efficient only near M 2.5). Water–methanol injection instead adds mass flow to restore thrust on hot or high days. Afterburning Reheat heats the jet; water weighs the air."},{"t":"How is reheat lit?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#reheat-ign","k":"Catalytic ignition is reliable but slow; hot-streak ignition fires a flame from the main combustor into the jet pipe without overheating the turbine; spark ignition is the least reliable. Reheat ignition "},{"t":"What does the APU do?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#apu","k":"The APU is a small battery-started gas turbine in the tail that supplies air for air-conditioning and engine starting and electrical power on the ground, and emergency electrical or hydraulic power in flight. Its generator is never paralleled with another source. APU and ignition The APU: air and power when the engines sleep."},{"t":"Hot, hung or wet start?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#starts","k":"A hot start overshoots the EGT limit (too much fuel: tailwind, residual fuel, hot restart). A hung start lights but sticks below self-sustaining RPM (weak starter or battery). A wet start never lights at all (igniter fault) and needs a dry motoring run to clear the fuel. Start faults Hot = too hot, hung = too slow, wet = no light."},{"t":"OSG, BPCU or MBPU?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#fuel","k":"The overspeed governor bleeds fuel back at maximum RPM; the barometric pressure control unit trims fuel down as altitude reduces the air mass flow; the minimum burner pressure unit keeps enough fuel pressure that closing the throttle at height cannot blow the flame out — which is why idle RPM rises with altitude. Fuel-control units Governor caps RPM; barometric unit trims for height."},{"t":"What does FADEC do?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#fadec","k":"FADEC is a two-channel digital engine controller with no manual override: the healthier channel controls and hands over automatically on a fault. It meters fuel, protects against overspeed, stall and surge, controls clearances, starts and relights the engine, and runs reversers and autothrust. FADEC Two channels, no manual override."},{"t":"Open/closed loop; pressure/flow controlled","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#fuel-types","k":"A closed-loop system corrects itself against the demanded RPM; an open-loop one does not. Pressure-controlled systems set pump pressure by ambient conditions; flow-controlled systems set flow by throttle and last longer. Fuel-system types "},{"t":"Trace a dry-sump oil system","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#oil","k":"Oil is pumped from a separate tank to the bearings, then scavenge pumps (bigger than the pressure pump) return it through a fuel-cooled oil cooler, which warms the fuel at the same time. Chip detectors catch bearing debris; oil pressure is read after the pump and temperature after the cooler. Oil system Pump it in, scavenge it out, cool it with fuel."},{"t":"Oil facts","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#oil-facts","k":"Jet engines use synthetic oil that is topped up rather than changed; a high oil temperature points to a blocked cooler or low quantity, and high consumption with a sound engine points to a bad bearing seal. Oil facts "},{"t":"Ramjet, scramjet, pulse-jet or rocket?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#others","k":"A ramjet has no compressor or turbine and needs speed for its compression (working from about M 1, efficient from M 2, limited near M 5). A scramjet burns in supersonic flow, on hydrogen, to go faster. A pulse-jet breathes through valves, works from zero speed but is noisy and slow. Rockets carry their own oxidiser. Ramjet, scramjet, pulse-jet Ram needs speed; pulse needs valves; rocket needs nothing."},{"t":"End burning or internal burning?","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#grain","k":"Rocket thrust follows burning area: constant area is neutral, growing area progressive, shrinking area regressive. An end-burner gives low thrust and lets the case overheat; an internal burner gives high thrust, protects the case and keeps the CG still. Solid-rocket burning "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Exhaust temperature: 550–850 °C","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"With reheat: ≈ 1500 °C","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Choked nozzle: M 1","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Thrust vectoring: ≈ 20°","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Turbofan reheat gain: ≈ 50%","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Water injection gain: up to 30%","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"High-energy ignition: 12 J","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Low-energy ignition: 6 J","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ramjet efficient from: M 2","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ramjet limit: ≈ M 5","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"V/STOL thrust: weight + 20%","s":"Handbook 17 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 17 · Exhaust, Reverse Thrust, Reheat and Engine Systems","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Exhaust system (4-27)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Thrust reversers (4-29)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Thrust augmentation (4-30)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"APU and starting (4-32)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Engine fuel system (4-33)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Lubrication (4-34)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"FADEC (4-35)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Rockets and other engines (4-36 to 4-38)","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 17 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-17.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 18. Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","s":"The electrical vocabulary the rest of the aircraft depends on: charge, current, voltage and resistance; inductors and capacitors and why they treat AC and DC differently; the laws of induction and the hand rules; magnetism; and the cells and batteries that start it all.","h":"chapters/hb-18.html","k":"Ohm and Kirchhoff · inductors and capacitors · Faraday, Lenz, Fleming · induction · magnetism · cells in series and parallel · Ni-Cd thermal runaway "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Electrical quantities and units","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#units","k":"Charge is measured in coulombs; current is charge per second (amperes, measured in series by an ammeter); EMF is the electrical pressure (volts, measured in parallel by a voltmeter); resistance opposes current (ohms); power is volts times amps (watts). Units Ammeter in series, voltmeter in parallel."},{"t":"Ohm's law and series/parallel","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#ohm","k":"I = V ÷ R. Resistors add in series and combine as reciprocals in parallel; capacitors do the opposite (add in parallel). Kirchhoff: current into a junction equals current out; around a loop the EMFs equal the IR drops. Ohm and combinations Resistors line up to add; capacitors stand side by side to add."},{"t":"Ohm's law","s":"Handbook 18 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#ohm","k":""},{"t":"Power","s":"Handbook 18 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#ohm","k":""},{"t":"Resistors in parallel","s":"Handbook 18 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#ohm","k":"Two 20 Ω in parallel = 10 Ω; in series = 40 Ω."},{"t":"Capacitors in series","s":"Handbook 18 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#ohm","k":"In parallel they simply add."},{"t":"Six ways to make electricity","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#sources","k":"Heat at a junction of two metals (thermocouple), light (photo-electric), friction (static), pressure on a crystal (piezo-electric), chemistry (batteries) and magnetism (generators) can all produce electricity. Making electricity "},{"t":"Does current lead or lag the voltage?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#phase","k":"In a pure inductor the current lags the voltage by 90°; in a pure capacitor it leads by 90°; in a resistor they are in phase. An inductor passes DC freely but resists AC more as frequency rises; a capacitor blocks DC but passes AC more easily as frequency rises. Lead and lag CIVIL: in a C, I leads V; V leads I in an L."},{"t":"Inductor or capacitor?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#lc","k":"Inductors store energy in a magnetic field and oppose changes in current; capacitors store it in an electric field between plates and oppose changes in voltage. Reactance is the frequency-dependent opposition each gives to AC; impedance is the total. Inductor vs capacitor Inductors fight current change; capacitors fight voltage change."},{"t":"Right hand or left hand?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#hands","k":"Fleming's right-hand rule is for generators, the left-hand rule for motors. With thumb, first and second fingers at right angles: thuMb = Motion, First finger = Field, seCond finger = Current. The right-hand grip rule gives a solenoid's north pole: curl the fingers with the current and the thumb points north. Fleming's rules Generators are Right (gen-eRight); motors are Left."},{"t":"What increases an induced EMF?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#induction","k":"An EMF is induced whenever a conductor and a magnetic field move relative to each other (Faraday), and it opposes the change that caused it (Lenz). It is bigger with faster cutting, a stronger field and more turns. Electromagnetic induction Faraday makes it, Lenz opposes it."},{"t":"What does each device do?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#devices","k":"Generators and alternators make electricity from motion; motors do the reverse; rectifiers turn AC into DC and oscillators make AC signals from DC; diodes conduct one way; magnetrons generate microwaves for radar. Devices "},{"t":"How do magnetic lines of force behave?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#magnet","k":"Flux lines leave the north pole and re-enter the south, are continuous and never cross. Like poles repel, unlike poles attract. Soft iron magnetises and demagnetises easily (temporary magnets); hard steel is difficult to magnetise but keeps it (permanent magnets). Magnetic field Out of North, into South, never crossing."},{"t":"Permeability, saturation, retentivity; making and unmaking magnets","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#mag-terms","k":"Aircraft become magnetised mostly by being hammered and vibrated during construction while lying in the earth's field; magnetism is removed by shock across the field, heating above about 900 °C, or an alternating current. Magnetic terms Hammered in the factory, magnetised for life."},{"t":"What happens when you join cells?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#cells","k":"Cells in series add their voltages but keep the same capacity; cells in parallel keep the same voltage but add their ampere-hours. An 80 Ah battery can give 8 A for 10 hours or 80 A for one hour. Series and parallel Series stacks volts; parallel pools amp-hours."},{"t":"Lead-acid, Ni-Cd or lithium-ion?","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#battery-types","k":"Lead-acid cells give 2 V with sulphuric acid; nickel-cadmium cells give 1.2 V with potassium hydroxide, hold their voltage until nearly flat, and can suffer thermal runaway; lithium-ion cells give 3.6 V with the highest energy density. Battery types Ni-Cd: 1.2 V, KOH, runaway."},{"t":"Battery checks and connections","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#bat-care","k":"An aircraft battery's capacity is checked every three months and must reach at least 80% of rating; its voltage is read on load. With the master switch off it is isolated from the busbar; with the generator on line it sits in parallel and is charged. Battery checks "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Phase shift in L or C: 90°","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Standard cell: 1.08 V","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Dry cell: 1.5 V","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Lead-acid cell: 2 V","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ni-Cd cell: 1.2 V","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Li-ion cell: 3.6 V","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Battery capacity check: 3-monthly, ≥ 80%","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Demagnetising heat: ≈ 900 °C","s":"Handbook 18 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 18 · Electrical Principles, Magnetism and Batteries","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Basic terms (5-3)","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Resistance, inductance, capacitance (5-4)","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Laws (5-5)","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Devices (5-6)","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Magnetism (5-7)","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Cells and batteries (5-8)","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 18 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-18.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 19. AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","s":"How aircraft electricity is made, converted and shared out: AC theory, alternators and DC generators, AC and DC motors, transformers, inverters and TRUs, the busbar layout, bonding and static wicks, switches and circuit protection, and a little computing.","h":"chapters/hb-19.html","k":"RMS and 400 Hz · power factor · alternator vs generator · 3-phase star · synchronous and induction motors · series and shunt · transformers · busbars · CBs and fuses "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"AC formulas","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ac","k":"RMS is the DC-equivalent (heating) value of AC: 0.707 × peak. Frequency depends on poles and speed — an 8-pole alternator at 6000 RPM gives 400 Hz. Inductive reactance rises with frequency, capacitive reactance falls. Power factor is true power ÷ apparent power. AC values Point seven oh seven."},{"t":"RMS value","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ac","k":""},{"t":"Frequency","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ac","k":"8 poles at 6000 RPM → 400 Hz."},{"t":"Inductive reactance","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ac","k":""},{"t":"Capacitive reactance","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ac","k":""},{"t":"Power factor","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ac","k":"Resistive circuit: PF = 1. V and I 90° apart: true power 0."},{"t":"Why do big aircraft use AC?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#why-ac","k":"AC machines are simpler, lighter and more reliable (no commutators, which suffer at altitude), high-voltage AC needs lighter cables, and transformers and TRUs easily produce any other voltage or DC. Why AC? "},{"t":"Generator or alternator?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#gen-alt","k":"A DC generator spins its armature in a fixed field and uses a split-ring commutator to turn the AC it makes into DC — with brush arcing and ripple. An alternator spins the field inside fixed output windings, so the heavy output is taken from the stator without sliding contacts; a rectifier gives DC if needed. Aircraft alternators are typically 115/200 V, 3-phase, 400 Hz. Generator vs alternator Alternator: the field goes round, the output stays still."},{"t":"Generator vs alternator on one card","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#ga-table","k":"Generators rotate the armature and commutate; alternators rotate the field and rectify, with no arcing. Comparison card "},{"t":"Star or delta?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#three-phase","k":"A 3-phase alternator has three windings 120° apart. Aircraft use the star connection, where line current equals phase current and line voltage is √3 (1.73) × phase voltage — 115 V phase, 200 V line. Three-phase Star: line voltage is root-three times phase."},{"t":"Series, shunt or compound generator?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#dc-gen","k":"A shunt-wound generator gives nearly constant voltage and is regulated by a resistance in series with its shunt field; series-wound output varies with load, so it is not used for supply; compound winding corrects the shunt generator's droop under load. A reverse-current relay stops the battery motoring the generator. DC generator types Shunt-wound holds the voltage steady."},{"t":"Cut-out and over-voltage protection","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#cutout","k":"The reverse-current (cut-out) relay connects the generator to the bus only when its voltage exceeds the battery's (about 26 V in a 28 V system), so the battery can never drive current back into the generator. Over-voltage protection trips the generator if bus voltage climbs too high. Protection relays "},{"t":"Which motor is it?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#motors","k":"A synchronous motor runs locked to supply frequency (RPM = 120 f ÷ poles), is not self-starting and drives tachometer indicators. An induction motor's squirrel-cage rotor always slips a few per cent behind. A series DC motor gives huge starting torque but races if unloaded (starters, actuators); a shunt DC motor holds a near-constant speed (fans, pumps). Which motor? Series: strong and wild. Shunt: steady."},{"t":"Synchronous speed and back EMF","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#motor-facts","k":"Synchronous speed = 120 × frequency ÷ poles. A motor's back EMF rises with speed and limits the current it draws: a heavily loaded series motor slows, back EMF falls and torque rises. Motor facts "},{"t":"Synchronous speed","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#motor-facts","k":"4-pole at 400 Hz = 12,000 RPM."},{"t":"Induction-motor slip","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#motor-facts","k":""},{"t":"Starter-generators and actuators","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#actuators","k":"A starter-generator is one compound machine that starts the engine as a motor and then generates; it gives poor output at low RPM, so it suits turbines. Actuators are small reversible geared motors with limit switches, brakes and overload clutches. Starter-generators and actuators "},{"t":"Transformers, inverters and TRUs","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#transformer","k":"A transformer works only on AC: the turns ratio sets the voltage ratio, and since power out ≈ power in the current changes the other way. An inverter turns DC into AC (rotary or static); a transformer-rectifier unit (TRU) turns AC into DC. Transformer Inverter: DC → AC. TRU: AC → DC."},{"t":"Turns ratio","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#transformer","k":"> 1 step-up, < 1 step-down. Output 180° out of phase."},{"t":"Power balance","s":"Handbook 19 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#transformer","k":""},{"t":"How is power shared out?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#bus","k":"Generators, batteries and inverters feed busbars that distribute power. Essential loads sit on a bus that stays powered after failures; non-essential loads are shed first; a hot bus is wired straight to the battery. Paralleled AC generators need matched frequency, phase and voltage; a split-bus system avoids paralleling, ties the buses if one generator fails and uses a static inverter for essential AC if both fail. Busbars Essential stays, non-essential goes, hot is always live."},{"t":"Voltmeter or ammeter; one wire or two?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#meters","k":"A voltmeter has high resistance and is connected in parallel; an ammeter has low resistance and is connected in series (a shunt extends its range). Metal aircraft use a single-pole system with the airframe as the return; composite airframes need a two-wire system. Wiring and meters Voltmeter high and across; ammeter low and in line."},{"t":"Bonding, static wicks and screening","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#static","k":"Bonding straps keep every metal part at the same potential so no sparks jump between them; static wicks on the trailing edges bleed charge into the air in flight, and conductive tyres discharge it on landing; screening and suppressors stop electrical noise reaching the radios. Bonding and static "},{"t":"Fuse or circuit breaker?","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#protect","k":"Both are fitted in series with the load. A fuse melts before full fault current is reached and must be replaced; a circuit breaker opens at fault current and can be reset, and doubles as a switch. A trip-free breaker cannot be held in while the fault remains. Fuses and breakers Trip-free: you can't hold it in against a fault."},{"t":"Computer memory and logic","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#computers","k":"The CPU's ALU does binary arithmetic and logic, registers hold data being worked on and the control unit keeps time. ROM cannot be changed, PROM is programmed once, EPROM is erased by UV, EEPROM electrically; volatile RAM forgets when power is lost. Computer basics "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"RMS: 0.707 × peak","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft AC: 115/200 V, 3-phase, 400 Hz","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Star line voltage: 1.73 × phase","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"3-phase windings: 120° apart","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Induction-motor slip: ≈ 5%","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Shunt-motor speed drop: ≈ 10%","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":"no load to full load"},{"t":"Regulated DC: 14 V / 28 V","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cut-out closes (28 V): ≈ 26 V","s":"Handbook 19 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 19 · AC and DC Machines and Aircraft Power Systems","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Alternating current (5-9)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"AC generators and alternators (5-11)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"AC motors (5-12)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"DC generators (5-13)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"DC motors and actuators (5-15)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Transformers and inverters (5-17)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft power distribution (5-18)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Switches and protection (5-19)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Computers (5-20)","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 19 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-19.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 20. Pitot-Static Instruments","s":"The four instruments that read the air: how pitot and static pressures reach the altimeter, VSI, ASI and Machmeter, how each is built, the errors each suffers, and exactly what each one does when a pitot or static line blocks or leaks.","h":"chapters/hb-20.html","k":"Pitot and static · blockages and leaks · altimeter errors · density and true altitude · VSI and IVSI · ASI markings · Machmeter "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Which instrument takes which pressure?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#system","k":"The pitot tube faces the airflow and senses total pressure (dynamic + static); the static vents lie flush and sense static pressure only. The ASI and Machmeter use both; the altimeter and VSI use static alone. Twin vents on opposite sides cancel sideslip errors, and an alternate static source covers blockage. Pitot and static Pitot to the speed gauges; static to everyone."},{"t":"Position (pressure) error","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#position","k":"Position error is the static vent seeing local, not true, static pressure because of the airflow round it. It depends on vent location, angle of attack, speed, configuration, sideslip and shockwaves, and grows in ground effect; it is the IAS-to-CAS correction. Position error "},{"t":"What does each instrument do when a line blocks or leaks?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#blockage","k":"A blocked static line freezes the altimeter, zeroes the VSI and makes the ASI under-read in a climb. A blocked pitot (with its drain blocked too) turns the ASI into an altimeter: over-reading in a climb. A static leak into a pressurised cabin makes the altimeter show cabin altitude. Blockages and leaks Static stuck: altimeter frozen, VSI zero."},{"t":"Why is a cold day dangerous for altimeters?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt","k":"Cold air is dense, so pressure levels crowd together and a given pressure is reached at a lower true height: an altimeter in colder-than-ISA air over-reads and the aircraft is lower than indicated. Flying from high to low pressure without resetting does the same. Add about 4% per 10 °C below ISA to minimum altitudes. Altimeter errors High to low, look out below."},{"t":"Which altimeter error?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt-errors","k":"Lag makes the altimeter trail behind (under-reading in a climb); temperature and barometric errors make it over-read when the air is colder or the pressure lower than assumed; instrument and position errors come from manufacture and vent location. Error list "},{"t":"Density altitude and true altitude","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt-sums","k":"Density altitude (for performance) is pressure altitude plus 118 ft per °C above ISA. True altitude is roughly pressure altitude plus 4 ft per °C of ISA deviation per 1000 ft: at FL180 and ISA −10 the aircraft is 720 ft low. Altitude sums 118 ft per degree for density altitude."},{"t":"Density altitude","s":"Handbook 20 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt-sums","k":""},{"t":"True altitude","s":"Handbook 20 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt-sums","k":"FL180, ISA −10 → 17,280 ft."},{"t":"Cold-temperature correction","s":"Handbook 20 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt-sums","k":""},{"t":"Inside the altimeter","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#alt-build","k":"A partly evacuated capsule held open by a leaf spring expands as static pressure falls; linkages turn the pointers, a bimetallic strip compensates for case temperature, and the sub-scale sets QNH or 1013. Construction "},{"t":"How does a VSI know the climb rate?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#vsi","k":"The VSI compares direct static pressure inside a capsule with static that leaks slowly into the case through a calibrated metering unit; in a climb the case lags behind and the difference is the rate. The instantaneous VSI adds an inertial dashpot so it reacts immediately, removing lag. VSI and IVSI Leak behind, read the gap."},{"t":"What do the coloured arcs mean?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#asi","k":"White arc: flap range, from VS0 (stall, landing configuration) to VFE. Green arc: normal range, from VS1 (stall, clean) to VNO. Yellow arc: caution range, VNO to VNE, smooth air only. Red line: VNE. Blue line on a twin: VYSE, best single-engine rate of climb. ASI markings White for flaps, green to go, yellow be careful, red never."},{"t":"Density error or compressibility error?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#asi-errors","k":"The ASI is calibrated at ISA sea-level density, so at altitude it under-reads TAS (density error, corrected from EAS to TAS). At high speed the air compresses in the pitot tube and the ASI over-reads (compressibility error, corrected from CAS to EAS). ASI errors Thin air: the ASI reads low."},{"t":"How does the Machmeter work?","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#mach","k":"The Machmeter divides dynamic by static pressure using an airspeed capsule and an altitude capsule joined by a ratio arm. Because it is a ratio, density and temperature errors cancel. High up, aircraft are limited by MMO rather than VMO because the speed of sound falls with temperature. Machmeter Mach = (P − S) ÷ S: the ratio cancels the temperature."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Speed of sound, ISA MSL: 661 kt","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":"340 m/s"},{"t":"Density altitude: 118 ft / °C","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"True-altitude correction: 4 ft / °C / 1000 ft","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cold correction: 4% per 10 °C","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter error at MSL: < ±30 ft","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"IVSI turn error above: 40° bank","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Cross-hatch flag: below 10,000 ft","s":"Handbook 20 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 20 · Pitot-Static Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Pitot-static system (6-3)","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter (6-4)","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Altimeter errors (6-5)","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"VSI (6-6)","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ASI (6-7)","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Machmeter (6-8)","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 20 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-20.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 21. Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","s":"How aircraft find heading and attitude: the direct-reading compass and its errors, compass swinging, the gyro-magnetic compass and flux valve, the two properties of a gyroscope and its wander, and the three classic gyro instruments.","h":"chapters/hb-21.html","k":"Compass requirements · acceleration and turning errors · coefficients A, B, C · flux valve · rigidity and precession · wander · DG, turn and slip, artificial horizon "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What must a direct-reading compass do?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#compass-req","k":"A compass must be deadbeat (settle without swinging), sensitive to the horizontal component of the earth's field and unaffected by the vertical component (dip). Its liquid must not freeze, boil, expand much, cloud or corrode, and must be thin enough to damp without dragging. Compass requirements Deadbeat, sensitive to H, blind to dip."},{"t":"Why does the compass swing when you speed up or turn?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#compass-errors","k":"Dip pulls the magnet's north end down while its weight sits on the other side of the pivot. Accelerating on an east or west heading, inertia twists it so the card shows a turn towards the nearer pole (ANDS in the northern hemisphere). Turning through the nearer pole the compass lags (under-reads); through the equator it leads (UNOS). Both vanish at the magnetic equator. Acceleration and turning errors ANDS: Accelerate North, Decelerate South. UNOS: Undershoot North, Overshoot South."},{"t":"Error or no error?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#err-stamp","k":"Acceleration error vanishes on north–south headings, turning error when passing east or west, and both at the magnetic equator. Which error? "},{"t":"How does deviation vary with heading?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#deviation","k":"Deviation on any heading = A + B sin(heading) + C cos(heading). B (fore-and-aft magnetism) is greatest on east/west and corrected on an easterly heading; C (athwartships) is greatest on north/south and corrected on a northerly heading; A is the constant part, removed by turning the lubber line. Coefficients A, B, C B is for Bisecting East–West; C is for Cardinal North–South."},{"t":"Working out A, B and C","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#coeff","k":"B = (deviation on E − deviation on W) ÷ 2; C = (deviation on N − deviation on S) ÷ 2; A = the mean of all eight readings. Coefficient sums B is east minus west halved; C is north minus south halved."},{"t":"Coefficient B","s":"Handbook 21 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#coeff","k":""},{"t":"Coefficient C","s":"Handbook 21 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#coeff","k":""},{"t":"Deviation on any heading","s":"Handbook 21 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#coeff","k":""},{"t":"When must the compass be swung?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#swing","k":"Swing the compass after replacing compass parts, a big change of operating latitude, at periodic inspections, after a lightning strike, after long storage on one heading, and after major modifications or electrical changes. When to swing "},{"t":"How does a flux valve sense north?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#flux","k":"A three-legged soft-iron flux valve, excited with 400 Hz AC, produces an 800 Hz output in each pick-up coil proportional to the earth's field along that leg; together the three legs give the field direction with no ambiguity. Hung pendulously in the wing tip or tail on a Hooke's joint, it slaves a directional gyro at about 3° per minute. Flux valve 400 in, 800 out, three legs point north."},{"t":"Why combine a gyro and a flux valve?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#gmc","k":"The gyro gives a steady short-term heading free of turning and acceleration errors; the flux valve gives a long-term magnetic reference that stops gyro drift. The result needs no manual resetting and can drive many instruments. Gyro-magnetic compass Gyro for steadiness, flux valve for north."},{"t":"What are the two properties of a gyroscope?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#gyro","k":"Rigidity: a spinning rotor holds its plane in space, more so with higher RPM and greater moment of inertia. Precession: a force applied to the rim acts 90° further on in the direction of rotation; the rate is proportional to the torque and inversely proportional to inertia × RPM. Rigidity and precession Push here, it moves 90° round the way it spins."},{"t":"Real, apparent and transport wander","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#wander","k":"Drift is wander in the horizontal plane, topple in the vertical. Real wander comes from imperfections; apparent wander from the earth turning under the gyro (15 sin lat °/h of drift); transport wander from flying east or west across converging meridians. Wander Drift sideways, topple up and down."},{"t":"Apparent drift","s":"Handbook 21 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#wander","k":"Maximum at the poles, zero at the equator."},{"t":"Transport wander","s":"Handbook 21 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#wander","k":"Zero flying north–south along a meridian."},{"t":"Which gyro is in which instrument?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#gyro-types","k":"The directional gyro is a horizontal-axis tied gyro with two degrees of freedom (DHAT2); the turn indicator a horizontal-axis rate gyro with one (THAR1); the artificial horizon a vertical-axis earth gyro with two (AVE2). Gyro types DHAT2 · THAR1 · AVE2."},{"t":"Directional gyro","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#dg","k":"The DG holds a heading by rigidity but has no magnetic sense, so it must be reset to the compass regularly; a latitude nut offsets apparent drift. Its main error is gimbal error in combined pitch and bank, worst on inter-cardinal headings. Directional gyro Reset the DG to the compass."},{"t":"Slip or skid?","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#tsi","k":"The turn needle (a rate gyro restrained by a spring) shows rate of turn: rate 1 is 3° per second. The ball shows balance: in a skid the ball goes outward (too much rudder for the bank); in a slip it falls inward (too much bank). Step on the ball to centre it. Turn and slip Ball out = skid; ball in = slip. Step on the ball."},{"t":"Turn-indicator errors","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#tsi-err","k":"Low rotor RPM gives less precession, so the turn needle under-reads; pitching while turning adds precession and makes it over-read; the restraining spring is never perfectly linear. Turn-indicator errors "},{"t":"Artificial-horizon errors","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#ah","k":"An air-driven artificial horizon shows a false climb and left bank when the aircraft accelerates (false descent and right bank on deceleration), because its bottom-heavy rotor and erection system respond to the acceleration. In turns the erection system is fooled by centrifugal force, so erection is cut out above about 10° of bank. Artificial horizon Accelerate: it thinks you climb and turn left."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Direct compass accuracy: ±10°","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Remote compass accuracy: ±1°","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flux-valve excitation / output: 400 / 800 Hz","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flux-valve freedom: ±25° pitch and roll","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Slaving rate: ≈ 3°/min","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Earth rate: 15.04°/h","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Rate 1 turn: 3°/s","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AH erection cut-out: > 10° bank","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AH pitch cut-out: > 0.18 g","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DG / TSI / AH RPM: 12,000 / 4,500 / 22,500","s":"Handbook 21 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 21 · Compasses and Gyroscopic Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Direct-reading magnetic compass (6-9)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Aircraft magnetism and compass swing (6-10)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Gyro-magnetic (remote-indicating) compass (6-11)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Gyroscopes (6-13)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Directional gyro (6-14)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Turn and slip indicator (6-15)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Artificial horizon (attitude indicator) (6-16)","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 21 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-21.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 22. Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","s":"The instruments that measure height above the ground, compute air data, warn the crew, and watch over the engines: radio altimeter, ADC, alerting systems, and the temperature, pressure, RPM, fuel, torque and vibration gauges.","h":"chapters/hb-22.html","k":"FMCW radio altimeter · air data computer · altitude, overspeed and stall alerts · AoA sensors · thermocouples · pressure gauges · tachometers · fuel and torque gauges "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How does a radio altimeter measure height?","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#ra","k":"The radio altimeter sweeps its transmitter frequency up and down at a known rate (FMCW, around 4300 MHz). By the time the echo returns, the transmitter has moved on to a new frequency: the difference between the two is proportional to the round-trip time, and so to height above the ground below. How the RA works The older the echo, the bigger the frequency gap."},{"t":"Radio altimeter: facts, advantages, limits","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#ra-facts","k":"An FMCW radar at 4200–4400 MHz with separate transmit and receive aerials, reading 0–2500 ft to ±3% and set to show the height of the lowest wheels. It gives true height with no QNH and feeds autoland, but cannot separate aircraft or see terrain ahead. RA facts Radio altimeter: wheels height to 2500 ft."},{"t":"What goes into and out of an ADC?","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#adc","k":"The ADC takes pitot pressure, static pressure and total air temperature and computes altitude, airspeed, Mach, TAS, static temperature and vertical speed for the displays, transponder, recorder, pressurisation, autopilot and autothrottle — correcting lag, position and compressibility errors on the way. ADC Pitot, static, TAT in; everything else out."},{"t":"How does the altitude alert behave?","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#alerts","k":"Approaching the selected altitude, a white box appears around it at 900 ft to go and disappears within 300 ft. Once level, straying more than 300 ft triggers a master caution, an aural alert, the EICAS message ALTITUDE ALERT and an amber box. It is inhibited on the glideslope or with gear and flaps down. Altitude alert 900 to get ready, 300 to get worried."},{"t":"Overspeed and stall warning","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#stall-warn","k":"The overspeed warning sounds near VMO/MMO. The stall warning comes on at 5 kt or 5% above the stall (whichever is more), using AoA, flap/slat position, gear position and speed, and can shake the stick. AoA is sensed by a slotted probe or a vane. Overspeed and stall warning Stall warning 5 kt or 5% early."},{"t":"Thermocouple or resistance thermometer?","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#temp","k":"A thermocouple joins two different metals: heating the hot junction relative to the cold one makes an EMF (Seebeck effect) with no external power — used for CHT and EGT. A resistance thermometer measures the change in a wire's resistance in a Wheatstone bridge and needs power — used for oil and air temperature. A radiation pyrometer reads turbine-blade glow directly. Temperature sensing Thermocouples make their own power; bridges borrow it."},{"t":"Which sensor measures what?","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#gauges","k":"Capsules read very low pressures, bellows medium and Bourdon tubes high. Drag-cup tachometers need no electrical power; phonic-wheel probes have no moving parts. Capacitance fuel gauges use fuel as the dielectric; oil torque meters balance a helical gear's axial thrust with oil pressure. Pressure, RPM, fuel, torque Capsule low, bellows middle, Bourdon high."},{"t":"Thermocouple, resistance or pyrometer?","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#temp-sort","k":"Self-powered thermocouples read the hot places (cylinder heads, exhaust); powered resistance bulbs read oil and air; pyrometers see blade temperature without touching. Which sensor? "},{"t":"Torque and power","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#power","k":"Shaft power is torque multiplied by rotational speed. Torque and power "},{"t":"Shaft power","s":"Handbook 22 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#power","k":"N in RPM, T in N·m → watts."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"RA frequency: 4300 MHz","s":"Handbook 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":"4200–4400"},{"t":"RA range: 0–2500 ft","s":"Handbook 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RA accuracy: ±3% or ±1 ft","s":"Handbook 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Altitude alert box: 900 ft to go","s":"Handbook 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Alert deviation: ±300 ft","s":"Handbook 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Stall warning: 5 kt or 5%","s":"Handbook 22 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 22 · Radio Altimeter, Air Data and Engine Instruments","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Radio altimeter (6-18)","s":"Handbook 22 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Air data computer (6-19)","s":"Handbook 22 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Alerting systems (6-20)","s":"Handbook 22 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Engine instruments (6-21 to 6-23)","s":"Handbook 22 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 22 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-22.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 23. INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","s":"The modern flight deck: inertial navigation and how it aligns and drifts, the ring-laser gyro, the autopilot, autothrottle and autoland, the EFIS and engine/alert displays, and the flight management system that ties them together.","h":"chapters/hb-23.html","k":"IRU and alignment · Schuler loop · bounded and unbounded errors · Kalman filter · RLG · yaw damper · autoland · EFIS colours · ECAM and EICAS · FMS databases "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How does an INS know where it is?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#ins","k":"An inertial reference unit holds three accelerometers (north, east, vertical) on a gyro-stabilised or mathematically stabilised platform. Integrating acceleration once gives velocity (groundspeed); integrating again gives distance and so position from the entered start point. With TAS from the ADC it can also work out the wind. INS principle Accelerate, integrate, integrate again."},{"t":"How does an INS align?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#align","k":"With the aircraft stationary the INS warms up, levels roughly, then gyro-compasses: the accelerometers find the vertical from gravity and a gyro senses the earth's 15°/h rotation to find true north and latitude. It cannot find longitude, which must be entered. NAV must be selected before the aircraft moves. Alignment and modes Gravity gives down, earth rate gives north, you give longitude."},{"t":"Bounded or unbounded INS error?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#ins-errors","k":"Bounded errors (platform tilt, accelerometer bias, first-integrator errors) are held by Schuler tuning to an oscillation with an 84.4-minute period. Unbounded errors (initial azimuth misalignment, azimuth gyro wander, levelling-gyro topple, second-integrator errors) keep growing with time. Bounded or unbounded? Schuler: worst at 42.2, back to zero at 84.4 minutes."},{"t":"INS drift and the Kalman filter","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#ins-more","k":"INS position drifts with time (book: up to about 2 nm per hour), so modern systems blend it with GPS and radio fixes in a Kalman filter that weighs each source by how reliable it is over time, giving the best hybrid position. Drift and the Kalman filter "},{"t":"How does a ring-laser gyro sense rotation?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#rlg","k":"Two laser beams circulate in opposite directions round a sealed helium–neon cavity. When the unit rotates, one beam's path gets longer and the other's shorter, so their frequencies differ in proportion to the rotation rate (Sagnac effect). At very low rates the beams lock together, so a dither motor shakes the block. Ring-laser gyro Spin it and the twin beams fall out of tune."},{"t":"Autopilot, yaw damper and autothrottle","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#ap","k":"The autopilot stabilises the aircraft and follows selected modes; auto-trim works in pitch only; the yaw damper applies rudder opposite to any yaw rate and so prevents Dutch roll; the autothrottle sets thrust for the demanded speed or Mach. Autopilot facts Yaw damper stops Dutch roll."},{"t":"Fail-passive or fail-operational?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#fail","k":"A fail-passive autoland (two channels) causes no dangerous deviation if one fails, but will not complete the landing: the pilot takes over. A fail-operational system (three or more) carries on and lands automatically after a failure below alert height, then becomes fail-passive. Fail-passive vs fail-operational Passive: it stops safely. Operational: it carries on."},{"t":"What happens at each height in an autoland?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#autoland","k":"The radio altimeter comes alive at 2500 ft; localiser and glideslope are captured by 1500 ft with all autopilots engaged and flare armed; the stabiliser trims nose-up at about 330 ft; at 50 ft the glideslope is dropped and the flare reduces sink to about 2 ft/s while the thrust retards; roll-out follows touchdown. Autoland sequence "},{"t":"What does each EFIS colour mean?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#efis","k":"White is present data and scales; green is selected or engaged; magenta is the active route and flight-director commands; cyan is inactive or secondary data (and the sky); amber/yellow is caution; red is warning and the heaviest rain. EFIS colours Magenta is the magic line you follow."},{"t":"EADI vs EHSI","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#eadi","k":"The EADI (PFD) shows attitude with flight-director commands, autopilot modes, localiser/glideslope and radio altitude. The EHSI (ND) shows heading — magnetic between 73°N and 65°S — and the route, nav-aids, weather and traffic in VOR, ILS, MAP or PLAN modes. EADI and EHSI "},{"t":"ECAM or EICAS?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#ecam","k":"Both put primary engine data and alerts on an upper screen and system pages on a lower one. Airbus ECAM lists the corrective actions in blue that turn green when done; Boeing EICAS shows warnings in red with an aural alert and cautions in amber, and compacts its data onto one screen if the other fails. ECAM vs EICAS Blue to do, green when done."},{"t":"What does the FMS do?","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#fms","k":"The FMS combines flight planning, position fixing, LNAV/VNAV guidance and performance. Its navigation database (routes, SIDs, STARs, aids — but no RT frequencies) lasts 28 days and is loaded on the ground. It flies great circles between waypoints, prefers DME/DME among radio fixes, and cost index 0 gives minimum fuel. FMS Database every 28 days; cost index zero = fuel saver."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"IRU: 3 gyros + 3 accelerometers","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Warm-up: 3–4 min","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Earth rate: 15.04°/h","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Schuler period: 84.4 min","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":"max error 42.2 min"},{"t":"INS drift (book): ≈ 2 nm/h","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RLG align: < 10 min","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Go-around pitch: ≈ 15°","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Flare ROD: ≈ 2 ft/s","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"EHSI magnetic: 73°N to 65°S","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"FMS database: 28 days","s":"Handbook 23 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 23 · INS, Ring-Laser Gyros, Autopilot, EFIS and FMS","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Inertial navigation (6-24)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"INS errors (6-26)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Ring-laser gyro (6-27)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Autopilot and autoland (6-28)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"EFIS (6-29)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ECAM and EICAS (6-30)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"FMS (6-31)","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 23 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-23.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 24. Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","s":"The physics behind every radio aid: what a radio wave is, how frequency sets wavelength and behaviour, how waves travel along the ground, in straight lines or off the ionosphere, how information is carried by modulation, and how aircraft talk on HF, VHF and satellite links and get bearings by VDF.","h":"chapters/hb-24.html","k":"c = fλ · frequency bands · polarisation · surface, space and sky waves · skip and dead space · AM, FM, SSB · HF, SELCAL, VHF, Satcom · receivers · VDF "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Frequency, wavelength and range","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#wave","k":"Radio waves travel at the speed of light, so wavelength = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ frequency: 121.5 MHz is about 2.47 m. Range grows only with the square root of power — doubling the range needs four times the power. Frequency and wavelength c = f × λ."},{"t":"Wavelength","s":"Handbook 24 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#wave","k":""},{"t":"Power for more range","s":"Handbook 24 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#wave","k":""},{"t":"VHF line-of-sight range","s":"Handbook 24 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#wave","k":"Heights in feet."},{"t":"Decibels","s":"Handbook 24 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#wave","k":"100 × → 20 dB."},{"t":"Which aid uses which frequency?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#bands","k":"NDBs are LF/MF, HF voice 2–25 MHz, markers 75 MHz, localiser and VOR 108–118 MHz, VHF voice 118–137 MHz, glideslope about 330 MHz, DME around 1 GHz, SSR 1030/1090 MHz, GPS 1575 MHz, radio altimeter 4.3 GHz and weather radar 9.4 GHz. Who uses which band LF for NDB, VHF for VOR and voice, UHF for glide path and DME."},{"t":"Polarisation, refraction, attenuation","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#props","k":"Polarisation is the plane of the electric field. Higher frequencies refract and diffract less but are attenuated more; signals travel further over sea than land and with vertical polarisation. Skywave fading is worst at dawn and dusk. Wave properties Higher frequency: straighter, shorter."},{"t":"Why does HF need a lower frequency at night?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#sky","k":"HF (2–30 MHz) sky waves are refracted back by ionised layers created by the sun. At night the D layer disappears and the others rise, so waves come back further away and the skip distance grows; to keep the same coverage you use a lower frequency. A higher frequency always means a larger critical angle, longer skip and bigger dead space. Sky wave, skip, dead space Night: layers up, frequency down."},{"t":"Surface, space or sky wave?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#prop-types","k":"Low frequencies hug the ground as surface waves; VHF and above go straight as space waves (slightly beyond the horizon thanks to refraction, much further when ducting); HF bounces off the ionosphere as sky waves. Propagation types Low hugs the ground, VHF goes straight, HF bounces."},{"t":"AM, FM or SSB?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#mod","k":"AM varies the carrier's amplitude: longer range and narrower bandwidth but more static. FM varies its frequency: clearer but shorter range and wider bandwidth. The information lives in the sidebands; HF uses single sideband (J3E) — carrier and one sideband removed, restored by a BFO — for power, bandwidth and fading benefits. AM vs FM AM goes far, FM sounds clear, SSB saves power."},{"t":"Which emission is it?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#emission","k":"VHF voice is A3E (AM, double sideband, telephony), HF voice J3E (single sideband, suppressed carrier), NDBs N0N with A1A or A2A identification, VOR A9W and DME pulse P0N. Emission designators "},{"t":"HF, VHF, UHF or Satcom?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#comms","k":"HF (2–25 MHz, SSB) reaches across oceans via the ionosphere; pick about 0.85 of the maximum usable frequency and go lower at night. VHF (117.975–137 MHz, 8.33 kHz spacing, AM) is line of sight. INMARSAT's four geostationary satellites cover 80°N–80°S. HF, VHF, Satcom HF hops oceans; VHF stays in sight."},{"t":"Is this a valid SELCAL code?","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#selcal","k":"A SELCAL code is four letters from A to S, never I, N or O, with the first pair and the second pair each in ascending order (e.g. CD-AM). It lets the crew stop listening to HF until called. SELCAL "},{"t":"Transmitters, receivers and aerials","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#rx","k":"A superheterodyne receiver mixes the incoming signal with a local oscillator to a fixed intermediate frequency for amplification and filtering. A BFO makes an unmodulated carrier audible. Aerials are cut to half or a quarter wavelength; a Yagi uses a longer reflector and shorter directors to beam its signal. Receivers and aerials "},{"t":"VDF bearings and accuracy classes","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#vdf","k":"With VDF the ground station measures the bearing of the aircraft's own VHF transmission and passes QDM (magnetic course to the station) or QTE (true bearing from it). Class A is within ±2°, B ±5°, C ±10°. Accuracy suffers from reflections, aircraft attitude, the cone overhead and simultaneous transmissions. VHF direction finding A2, B5, C10."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Speed of radio waves: 3 × 10⁸ m/s","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"121.5 MHz wavelength: ≈ 2.47 m","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Double range: 4 × power","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VHF refraction bonus: ≈ 7%","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ducting: up to +40%","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sub-refraction: up to −20%","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sky wave: 2–30 MHz","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Peak ionisation: ≈ 1400 local","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"OWF: 0.85 × MUF","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VHF spacing: 8.33 kHz","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Satcom coverage: 80°N–80°S","s":"Handbook 24 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 24 · Radio Waves, Propagation and Communications","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Radio waves (7-3)","s":"Handbook 24 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Propagation (7-5)","s":"Handbook 24 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Modulation (7-6)","s":"Handbook 24 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Communications (7-7)","s":"Handbook 24 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"VDF (7-10)","s":"Handbook 24 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 24 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-24.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 25. NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","s":"The three workhorse en-route aids: the NDB and the ADF that points at it, the VOR that tells you which radial you are on by comparing two 30 Hz signals, and the DME that measures slant range by timing a radar-like reply.","h":"chapters/hb-25.html","k":"Loop, sense aerial, cardioid · ADF errors · VOR phase comparison · D-VOR, VOT · VOR accuracy and range · DME slant range · beacon saturation "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"What kinds of NDB are there?","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#ndb","k":"NDBs radiate vertically polarised AM in all directions on LF/MF. An A1A beacon is identified by keying its carrier, so the ADF needs a BFO to hear the Morse; an A2A beacon modulates a 1020 Hz tone, so it doesn't. Locators cover about 25 nm, holding NDBs 50 nm, en-route NDBs 200 nm. NDB types A1A: Add a BFO. A2A: Audio tone."},{"t":"NDB range","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#ndb-range","k":"NDB range is roughly 2√P nm over land and 3√P over sea (P in watts): a 1600 W beacon reaches about 120 nm over water. NDB range "},{"t":"NDB range","s":"Handbook 25 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#ndb-range","k":""},{"t":"How does the ADF find the beacon?","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#adf","k":"A loop aerial gives a figure-of-eight response with two nulls, so it knows the line but not which end. A sense aerial adds an equal circular response; combined they form a cardioid with a single null that points to the NDB. Modern ADFs do this electronically with fixed crossed coils. Loop and sense aerials Eight + circle = heart with one null."},{"t":"Which ADF error is it?","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#adf-errors","k":"At night sky waves mix with the surface wave and blur the null (up to 30°, worst at dawn and dusk); signals crossing a coastline at an angle are refracted; the airframe distorts the field most at 45° relative bearings; storms drag the needle towards lightning; and the ADF gives no failure warning at all. ADF errors Night, Coast, Quadrant, Storm, Mountain, Dip — and no flag."},{"t":"How does a VOR tell you your radial?","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#vor","k":"A VOR radiates a 30 Hz reference signal (FM on a 9960 Hz sub-carrier) that has the same phase everywhere, and a 30 Hz variable signal (AM from a pattern rotated 30 times a second) whose phase depends on direction. They are in phase towards magnetic north; the phase difference you receive is your radial. VOR principle Phase difference = radial."},{"t":"C-VOR, D-VOR, T-VOR or VOT?","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#vor-types","k":"A Doppler VOR swaps the roles — reference AM, variable FM from a rotating aerial's Doppler shift — and almost eliminates site error with no change to aircraft equipment. A VOT radiates radial 000 in every direction: tuned on the ground, the RMI should read 180° within ±4°. VOR types and VOT Doppler VOR swaps the signals, kills site error."},{"t":"VOR tolerances","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#vor-acc","k":"The ground transmission should be within ±3° and the whole system within ±5°, 95% of the time. Check the receiver to ±4° on a ground checkpoint or VOT and ±6° on an airborne checkpoint. The monitor shuts the beacon down if bearings shift 1° or power drops 15%. VOR accuracy Three on the ground, five for the system."},{"t":"VOR range and the CDI","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#vor-range","k":"VOR range is line of sight: 1.23 (√h_aircraft + √h_beacon) nm. On a 5-dot CDI each dot is 2°, full scale ±10°. VOR range and CDI 1.23 times the root heights."},{"t":"VOR range","s":"Handbook 25 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#vor-range","k":"2500 ft and 196 ft → 79 nm."},{"t":"CDI","s":"Handbook 25 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#vor-range","k":""},{"t":"TACAN and VORTAC","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#tacan","k":"TACAN is the military UHF pulse system giving bearing and distance from one beacon; a VORTAC co-locates a civil VOR with a TACAN so civil aircraft get VOR bearing and TACAN-DME distance. TACAN and VORTAC "},{"t":"How does DME measure distance?","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#dme","k":"The aircraft sends randomly spaced pulse pairs; the ground transponder replies 63 MHz away after a fixed 50 µs delay; the aircraft recognises its own pattern and times the round trip. The result is slant range: overhead the station it shows your height (about 1 nm per 6000 ft). DME principle DME measures the hypotenuse."},{"t":"DME numbers","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#dme-facts","k":"DME uses UHF 962–1213 MHz with 252 channels, searches at 150 pulse pairs a second and tracks at 25, serves about 100 aircraft before reducing gain to answer only the strongest, and is accurate to ±0.25 nm or 1.25% of range. DME facts 150 to search, 25 to track."},{"t":"Ground range from DME","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#slant","k":"Ground distance = √(DME² − height²), with height in nm (ft ÷ 6080). The error matters only close to the station. Slant-range sum "},{"t":"Ground range","s":"Handbook 25 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#slant","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"NDB band: 190–1750 kHz","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"A2A ident tone: 1020 Hz","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Night-effect error: up to 30°","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VOR reference: 30 Hz FM on 9960 Hz","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VOR system accuracy: ±5°","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VOT check: ±4°, reads 180°","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"CDI: 2° per dot","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DME band: 962–1213 MHz","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DME reply: ±63 MHz, 50 µs","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DME capacity: ≈ 100 aircraft","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DME accuracy: ±0.25 nm / 1.25%","s":"Handbook 25 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 25 · NDB and ADF, VOR and DME","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"NDB (7-11)","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ADF (7-12)","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ADF errors (7-13)","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"VOR (7-14)","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"VOR accuracy, range and monitoring (7-15 to 7-16)","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"DME (7-17)","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 25 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-25.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 26. ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","s":"Precision approach aids and the radars that watch the sky: how the ILS localiser and glidepath steer you down, the markers and monitors, MLS, how a primary radar measures range and what limits it, Doppler navigation, airborne weather radar, and secondary surveillance radar.","h":"chapters/hb-26.html","k":"Localiser and glidepath lobes · coverage · markers · false glideslope · MLS · PRF and range · Doppler · weather radar · SSR modes and codes "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How do the localiser and glidepath steer you?","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ils","k":"Each ILS element radiates two overlapping lobes: the localiser has 90 Hz on the left and 150 Hz on the right of the centreline (on approach); the glidepath has 90 Hz above and 150 Hz below the path. Where the two modulations are equal (zero difference in depth of modulation) you are on centreline or on the glidepath. Localiser and glidepath Ninety is left and high; one-fifty is right and low."},{"t":"Localiser vs glidepath on one card","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ils-table","k":"Localiser: odd tenths 108.1–111.95 MHz, ±10° to 25 nm and ±35° to 17 nm, full scale ±700 ft at the threshold. Glidepath: 329–335 MHz, ±8° to 10 nm, 0.45θ to 1.75θ vertically, crossing the threshold about 50 ft. ILS facts Localiser odd tenths; glidepath near 330 MHz."},{"t":"Outer, middle or inner marker?","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#markers","k":"Markers are 75 MHz fan beams pointing straight up. Outer: blue light, dashes, 3.5–6 nm (around the FAF). Middle: amber, dot-dash, about 3000–4000 ft (Cat I DH). Inner: white, dots, 1000–1500 ft (Cat II DH). Marker beacons Blue dashes, amber dot-dash, white dots — slower to faster as you get closer."},{"t":"False glideslopes and ILS errors","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#false-gs","k":"Reflections repeat the glidepath lobes at multiples of its angle; the first false glideslope sits near 6° and some have reversed sense, so always intercept the glideslope from below. Reflections from aircraft and vehicles, heavy precipitation and FM broadcasts also disturb the ILS, hence the critical and sensitive areas. False glideslope and errors Join the glideslope from below."},{"t":"Height and rate of descent on the glidepath","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ils-sums","k":"On a glidepath, height ≈ distance (nm) × angle × 100 ft — 300 ft per nm on a 3° path — and rate of descent ≈ groundspeed × angle × 100 ÷ 60, about 5 × groundspeed on 3°. Glidepath sums 3°: 300 ft a mile, five times the groundspeed."},{"t":"Height on the glidepath","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ils-sums","k":""},{"t":"Rate of descent","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ils-sums","k":"3° → ≈ 5 × GS."},{"t":"Gradient","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ils-sums","k":""},{"t":"What does MLS do better than ILS?","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#mls","k":"MLS sweeps time-referenced scanning beams at about 5 GHz: the time between the to and fro sweeps gives the angle. Its ±40° azimuth and 0.9°–15° elevation coverage allow curved and segmented approaches and chosen glide angles, with 200 channels, a precision DME and less trouble from terrain. MLS "},{"t":"PRF, range and resolution","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#radar","k":"Range is half the echo time × the speed of light. A lower PRF (longer gap between pulses) allows a longer maximum unambiguous range; a shorter pulse allows a shorter minimum range and better range resolution; a narrower beam gives better bearing resolution. Doubling range needs 16 times the power. Radar range sums Slow pulses see far; short pulses see close."},{"t":"Target range","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#radar","k":"500 µs → 75 km."},{"t":"Max unambiguous range","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#radar","k":""},{"t":"Minimum range","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#radar","k":""},{"t":"Beam width","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#radar","k":"3 cm, 45 cm dish → 4.7°."},{"t":"Doppler radar: groundspeed and drift","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#doppler","k":"A Doppler navigator beams energy down at about 67° and measures the frequency shift of the return to get groundspeed, and the direction of greatest shift to get drift. Janus systems look fore and aft to cancel pitch errors. Over a smooth sea it under-reads by 2–3% (sea bias), and moving water adds its own error. Doppler navigation Doppler: shift gives speed, its direction gives drift."},{"t":"How do you read a weather radar picture?","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#wx","k":"Weather radar uses 9375 MHz (3 cm), which reflects well from large drops and wet hail. Green is light rain, yellow medium, red heavy, magenta turbulence or extreme. The worst turbulence is where the colours change fastest; hooks, fingers, U-shapes and scalloped edges suggest hail or tornadoes, and a shadow behind a cell hides more weather. It cannot see clear-air turbulence. Reading the weather radar Steepest gradient = worst ride."},{"t":"Weather-radar settings and windshear","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#wx-facts","k":"A pencil beam (3–5°) is used for weather and a cosecant fan beam for ground mapping. Auto gain is normal; reduce gain slowly to find the strongest cells. Predictive windshear works automatically below about 2300 ft. Weather-radar facts Pencil for weather, fan for mapping."},{"t":"How high is that cloud?","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#wx-sum","k":"Height of a cloud top relative to the aircraft ≈ (tilt − half the beam width) × range (nm) × 100 ft. Cloud-top sum "},{"t":"Cloud top above the aircraft","s":"Handbook 26 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#wx-sum","k":"Tilt 3.5°, beam 5°, 45 nm → +4500 ft."},{"t":"SSR modes and pulse spacing","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ssr","k":"SSR interrogates on 1030 MHz and the transponder answers on 1090 MHz with 12 pulses between framing pulses 20.3 µs apart — 4096 codes using digits 0–7. Mode A gives identity, mode C pressure altitude in 100 ft steps, and mode S a unique address, 25 ft altitude and datalink. SSR modes 1030 asks, 1090 answers."},{"t":"Garbling, fruiting or ghosting?","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#ssr-err","k":"Garbling is overlapping replies from aircraft within 1.7 nm in range on a similar bearing; fruiting is replies triggered by another interrogator; ghosting comes from replies to side lobes, prevented by side-lobe suppression (the P2 pulse). SSR errors "},{"t":"Special SSR codes","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":" Special codes "},{"t":"Unlawful interference: 7500","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"Radio failure: 7600","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"Emergency: 7700","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"Entering cover, no code: 2000","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"VFR conspicuity: 7000","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"Aerobatics / display: 7004","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"Para-dropping: 0033","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#codes","k":""},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Localiser: 108.1–111.95 MHz odd","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Glidepath: 329.15–335 MHz","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Markers: 75 MHz","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"LOC coverage: ±10°/25 nm, ±35°/17 nm","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"GP coverage: ±8°/10 nm","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"False glideslope: ≈ 6°","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"MLS: 5030–5090 MHz, 200 ch","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Weather radar: 9375 MHz (3 cm)","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"SSR: 1030 / 1090 MHz","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"SSR codes: 4096","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Garbling: 1.7 nm","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"SSR range: ≈ 250 nm","s":"Handbook 26 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 26 · ILS, MLS, Radar and SSR","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"ILS (7-18)","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"MLS (7-20)","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Primary radar (7-21)","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Doppler radar (7-22)","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Weather radar (7-25)","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"SSR (7-27)","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 26 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-26.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 27. GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","s":"Satellite navigation and the safety nets built on it: how GPS fixes a position and how its integrity is checked and improved, its errors, the ground-proximity warning modes, airborne collision avoidance, and the surveillance and datalink systems that replace voice and radar over the oceans.","h":"chapters/hb-27.html","k":"GPS segments and pseudo-ranges · RAIM, FDE, GBAS, SBAS · GPS errors · GPWS modes 1–7 · EGPWS · TCAS TA and RA · ADS-B, ADS-C · CPDLC "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"The three GPS segments","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#gps","k":"GPS has 24 satellites in six orbital planes inclined at 55°, at 20,180 km with 12-hour orbits; a master control station at Colorado Springs and five monitor stations keep their orbits and clocks corrected; receivers measure pseudo-ranges to at least four satellites to solve for position and their own clock error. GPS segments 24 – 6 – 55 – 20,180 – 12."},{"t":"How does a receiver get a fix?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#fix","k":"Each satellite sends its position and an exact time; the receiver times the signal with its own cheap clock, so each range is a pseudo-range. Four satellites give four equations for four unknowns — x, y, z and the receiver clock error. Good geometry (one overhead, others spread around) keeps dilution of precision low. Why four satellites? Three for where, one more for when."},{"t":"How is GPS integrity checked and improved?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#integrity","k":"RAIM lets the receiver check itself and needs five satellites (or four plus baro-aiding); FDE also throws out a bad satellite and needs six (or five plus baro). GBAS sends local corrections by VHF from a surveyed airport station for precision approaches; SBAS (GAGAN in India) relays corrections through geostationary satellites. RAIM, FDE, GBAS, SBAS RAIM 5, FDE 6."},{"t":"Which GPS error is biggest?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#gps-err","k":"Poor satellite geometry (GDOP) can magnify errors up to about 20 m; of the physical errors the ionospheric delay is the largest (around 5 m, least with the satellite overhead), then satellite clock (~1.5 m), troposphere (~0.5 m) and receiver noise (~0.3 m). Selective availability was switched off in May 2000. GPS errors "},{"t":"What does each GPWS mode protect against?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#gpws","k":"Mode 1 excessive sink rate, 2 excessive terrain closure, 3 height loss after take-off or go-around, 4 too low with gear or flaps not set, 5 below the glideslope, 6 minimums call-outs and bank angle, 7 windshear. On a warning: wings level, maximum power, climb at best angle. GPWS modes Sink, Closure, Don't sink, Too low, Glideslope, Callouts, Windshear."},{"t":"What does EGPWS add?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#egpws","k":"Basic GPWS only knows the ground below; EGPWS adds GPS position and a terrain database to look ahead. Its terrain display colours ground red when it is well above the aircraft, yellow near its level and green below; the terrain clearance floor stops premature descent near runways. EGPWS GPWS looks down; EGPWS looks ahead."},{"t":"What do the TCAS symbols mean?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#tcas","k":"A hollow cyan diamond is other traffic; a solid cyan diamond is proximate traffic within 6 nm and 1200 ft; a solid amber circle is a traffic advisory (about 20–48 s from collision — look, don't manoeuvre); a solid red square is a resolution advisory (about 15–35 s) — fly the vertical manoeuvre within 5 seconds, even against ATC. TCAS display Cyan watch, amber look, red act."},{"t":"TCAS I, TCAS II and the RA rules","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#tcas-facts","k":"TCAS I gives traffic advisories only; TCAS II adds coordinated vertical resolution advisories. An RA overrides ATC; respond to the first within 5 seconds and to later ones within 2–3. TCAS sees only aircraft with transponders, and gives RAs only against those reporting altitude. TCAS rules An RA beats ATC: act within 5 seconds."},{"t":"Which warning wins?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#priority","k":"Book priority: stall warning, then windshear, then GPWS, then ACAS, then ATC instructions. Warning priority "},{"t":"ADS-B or ADS-C?","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#ads","k":"ADS-B broadcasts the aircraft's GPS position, velocity and identity about every second to anyone listening (1090 MHz extended squitter or 978 MHz UAT). ADS-C sends reports only to ground units holding a contract — periodic, on demand, or when an event happens — and is used for oceanic surveillance with CPDLC. ADS-B vs ADS-C B = Broadcast to all; C = Contract with one."},{"t":"TIS-B, FIS-B, TLS, FANS","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#others","k":"TIS-B uplinks radar-derived traffic to ADS-B-in aircraft; FIS-B uplinks weather; TLS uses the transponder to produce ILS-type guidance for one aircraft; FANS combines ADS, CPDLC and RNP/GPS for oceanic air traffic management. Other systems "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"GPS satellites: 24","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":"21 + 3"},{"t":"Orbital planes: 6 at 55°","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Orbit height: 20,180 km","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Orbit period: 12 h","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"L1: 1575.42 MHz","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Satellites for 3-D fix: 4","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RAIM / FDE: 5 / 6","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"SA switched off: 1 May 2000","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Proximate traffic: 6 nm, ±1200 ft","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TA / RA time: 20–48 s / 15–35 s","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"RA response: 5 s, then 2–3 s","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"ADS-B: 1090 ES / 978 UAT","s":"Handbook 27 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 27 · GNSS, GPWS, ACAS, ADS and Datalink","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"GNSS (7-29)","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Integrity and augmentation (7-30)","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"GPS errors (7-31)","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"GPWS and EGPWS (7-32)","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ACAS / TCAS (7-34)","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"ADS, ACARS, CPDLC and others (7-36 to 7-39)","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 27 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-27.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 28. Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","s":"How an aircraft's mass is built up from empty to take-off, which limits cap it, how the centre of gravity is found and why its position matters, how often aircraft are weighed, and the runway distances declared by the aerodrome versus those the aircraft requires.","h":"chapters/hb-28.html","k":"BEM, DOM, ZFM, TOM, LM · structural and performance limits · max traffic load · CG and %MAC · weighing · TORA, TODA, ASDA, LDA · TORR, TODR, ASDR, LDR "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How is take-off mass built up?","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#masses","k":"Basic empty mass plus the variable load (crew, catering, water) is the dry operating mass. Add the traffic load and you have the zero fuel mass; add take-off fuel and you have the take-off mass; burn the trip fuel and you have the landing mass. Building up the mass Empty → Dry → Zero-fuel → Take-off → Landing."},{"t":"Every mass definition","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#mass-defs","k":"DOM contains no usable fuel and no traffic load; ZFM is DOM plus traffic load; operating mass is DOM plus take-off fuel; useful load is traffic load plus usable fuel. Definitions Dry is no fuel, no load; zero fuel adds the load."},{"t":"What limits the take-off mass and the payload?","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#limits","k":"Regulated limits are the lower of structural and performance limits. The maximum take-off mass is the lowest of MZFM + take-off fuel, the regulated TOM, and the regulated landing mass + trip fuel; the maximum traffic load is the lowest of MZFM − DOM, RTOM − DOM − take-off fuel, and RLM − DOM − landing fuel. Max TOM and traffic load Three ceilings: zero-fuel, take-off, landing — take the lowest."},{"t":"Maximum take-off mass","s":"Handbook 28 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#limits","k":""},{"t":"Maximum traffic load","s":"Handbook 28 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#limits","k":""},{"t":"Load sheet and weighing rules","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#docs","k":"Every flight needs a mass and balance document signed by the commander, with a last-minute-change procedure. Aircraft are weighed on entry to service, every four years, after modifications, and whenever the DOM changes by more than 0.5% of MLM or its CG by more than 0.5% of MAC. Documents and weighing Weighed in, then every four years."},{"t":"Finding the CG and %MAC","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#cg","k":"Multiply each mass by its arm from the datum to get its moment, add the moments, and divide by the total mass to get the CG arm. Express it as a percentage of the mean aerodynamic chord from the MAC leading edge. CG sums CG = Σ moments ÷ Σ masses."},{"t":"CG position","s":"Handbook 28 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#cg","k":""},{"t":"CG as % MAC","s":"Handbook 28 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#cg","k":""},{"t":"Mass to move","s":"Handbook 28 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#cg","k":""},{"t":"What does CG position change?","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#cg-effects","k":"A forward CG makes the aircraft more stable and heavier to control, needs more tail down-force — so higher stall and reference speeds, more trim drag, more fuel and less range. An aft CG gives the opposite, down to neutral stability at the limit. Forward vs aft CG Forward: safe but thirsty. Aft: frisky but frugal."},{"t":"More mass: what goes up and what goes down?","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#heavier","k":"A heavier aircraft needs higher speeds and longer distances, stresses its structure more, burns more fuel, climbs worse and has a lower ceiling and less range; its minimum control speeds VMCG and VMCA are unaffected. Effects of more mass "},{"t":"What are the declared distances?","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#declared","k":"TORA is the runway available for the take-off run; TODA adds any clearway; ASDA adds any stopway; LDA is the runway available for landing. A stopway can bear an aircraft stopping in an emergency; a clearway is obstacle-free ground or water beyond the runway, no longer than half the TORA, over which the aircraft may climb to screen height. TORA, TODA, ASDA, LDA Clearway to Climb (TODA); Stopway to Stop (ASDA)."},{"t":"TORR, TODR, ASDR, LDR","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#required","k":"TORR and TODR are each the greater of the engine-failure-at-V1 distance and 115% of the all-engines distance (to the 35 ft midpoint for TORR, to 35 ft at V2 for TODR). ASDR stops without reverse thrust. LDR from 50 ft is multiplied by 1.67 for jets and 1.43 for turboprops, plus 15% if wet. Required distances Distances: the worse of engine-out or 115% all-engines."},{"t":"Take-off and landing rules of thumb","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":" Rules of thumb "},{"t":"Take-off mass +10%: TOD +21%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Take-off mass +21%: take-off speed +10%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Tailwind 10% of V_TO: TOD +21%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Headwind 10% of V_TO: TOD −19%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Landing mass +10%: LD +10%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Approach speed +10%: LD +20%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"50 ft high at threshold: LD ≈ +1000 ft","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Wet runway: LD ×1.4","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#thumb","k":""},{"t":"Runway alignment reduction","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#alignment","k":"Big aircraft use up runway turning to line up, so the take-off distances are reduced — TORA/TODA measured to the main wheels and ASDA to the nose wheel (a bigger reduction). Line-up allowance "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Flight / cabin crew: 85 / 75 kg","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Weighing: every 4 years","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Fleet weighing: every 9 years","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Reweigh trigger: 0.5% MLM / 0.5% MAC","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Clearway: ≤ ½ TORA","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Clearway plane: 1.25%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Screen height: 35 ft (A) / 50 ft","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"AEO factor: 1.15","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"LDR jets / props: × 1.67 / × 1.43","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Wet runway: +15%","s":"Handbook 28 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 28 · Mass and Balance, and Declared Distances","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Mass terms (9-3)","s":"Handbook 28 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Documentation and weighing (9-4)","s":"Handbook 28 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Centre of gravity (9-5)","s":"Handbook 28 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Declared distances (9-6)","s":"Handbook 28 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 28 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-28.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 29. V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","s":"The speeds that structure every take-off and landing, the performance rules that limit how heavy an aircraft may be on a given runway on a given day, the climb gradients it must achieve after an engine failure, and the manoeuvre margins at high altitude.","h":"chapters/hb-29.html","k":"VMCG, VMCA, V1, VR, V2 · Vx and Vy · VA, VNO, VNE · gross vs net · performance classes · field, climb and obstacle limits · balanced field · take-off segments · drift-down · coffin corner "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"In what order do the take-off speeds come?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#tospeeds","k":"On the roll: VMCG (rudder alone can hold an engine failure), then V1 (go/stop decision), VR (rotate), VLOF (lift-off) and V2 (safety speed at 35 ft). V1 must lie between VMCG and VR and not exceed the brake-energy limit; V2 is at least 1.2 Vs and 1.1 VMCA. The take-off speed ladder Control, Decide, Rotate, Lift, Safe."},{"t":"Every take-off speed","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#tospeed-table","k":"VMCG is controlled on the ground with rudder alone; VMCA in the air with up to 5° bank towards the live engine and aft CG; V1 is the go/stop decision speed; VR the rotation speed; V2 the take-off safety speed reached by 35 ft. Speed definitions VMCG, V1, VR, V2: control, decide, rotate, climb."},{"t":"What happens if V1 is raised or lowered?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#v1-effects","k":"A higher V1 means a longer accelerate-stop distance but a shorter accelerate-go distance and better obstacle clearance. The V1 at which the two are equal gives the balanced field length — the shortest runway for a given weight. Moving V1 Raise V1: harder to stop, easier to go."},{"t":"Vx, Vy, VREF, VA, VNO, VNE…","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#other-speeds","k":"Vx gives the steepest climb and Vy the fastest; they meet at the absolute ceiling. VREF is 1.3 Vs at 50 ft. VA is the highest speed for full abrupt control deflection and falls as weight falls. VNO tops the green arc and VNE is the red line. Climb, approach and structural speeds Vx steepest, Vy fastest."},{"t":"Gross or net? Class A, B or C?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#defs","k":"Gross performance is what an average aircraft and pilot achieve (certification); net performance is gross reduced by a safety margin and is what must clear obstacles. Class A covers all multi-engine jets and turboprops above 5700 kg or 9 passengers; class B the smaller propeller aircraft; class C large piston aircraft. Gross, net and classes Gross is the average; net is the safe."},{"t":"What can limit the take-off weight?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#limits","k":"The take-off weight is the lowest of the structural limits, the performance limits (field length, climb gradient, obstacle clearance), the landing-weight limits plus burn-off, and the tyre-speed and brake-energy limits. What limits TOW Take-off weight is the lowest limit."},{"t":"How do flap, slope, wind, heat and height change the limits?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#factors","k":"More flap raises the field-length limit but lowers the climb limit, so long runways use less flap and short runways more. Heat and height lower every limit. Headwind and downslope help the field limit; tailwind, heat and height lower the tyre and brake limits. Factor effects Short runway: more flap. Long runway, hot day: less flap."},{"t":"What is a balanced field length?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#balanced","k":"As V1 rises the accelerate-stop distance grows and the accelerate-go distance shrinks. Where the two curves cross, at the balanced V1, the runway needed is the least possible for that weight — or the weight is the most for that runway. Balanced field Where stop meets go."},{"t":"What are the four take-off segments?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#segments","k":"After an engine failure: segment 1 from 35 ft until the gear is up (positive gradient for a twin); segment 2 at V2 with take-off flap to at least 400 ft (2.4% for a twin — usually the limiting one); segment 3 level acceleration while the flaps retract; segment 4 clean at max continuous thrust to 1500 ft (1.2%). The net path, 0.8% shallower for a twin, must clear obstacles by 35 ft. Take-off segments Positive, 2.4, accelerate, 1.2."},{"t":"Required gradients","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#gradients","k":"Twin-engine minimum gradients: 2nd segment 2.4%, final segment 1.2%, approach climb 2.1% (one engine out), landing climb 3.2% (all engines). Net take-off path is gross minus 0.8%, net en-route gross minus 1.1%. Gradients table 2.4, 1.2, 2.1, 3.2."},{"t":"Climb gradient","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#gradient-calc","k":"Climb gradient ≈ (thrust − drag) ÷ weight; as a rate it is ROC ÷ TAS (×101.3 for fpm and kt). Heavier, hotter or higher means less gradient. Gradient sum "},{"t":"Gradient","s":"Handbook 29 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#gradient-calc","k":""},{"t":"ROC for a gradient","s":"Handbook 29 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#gradient-calc","k":""},{"t":"Engine failure in the cruise","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#enroute","k":"If an engine fails at cruise altitude the aircraft drifts down at the best L/D (drift-down) speed until it reaches the gross level-off altitude, where one-engine thrust equals drag. The net path must clear terrain within 5 nm by 1000 ft (2000 ft over mountains). Wind changes the drift-down distance, not the rate. En-route and drift-down Lose an engine high: drift down at best L/D."},{"t":"Why is there a ceiling the aircraft cannot exceed?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#buffet","k":"Low-speed buffet comes at a constant IAS, so its TAS rises with altitude; high-speed (shock) buffet comes at a Mach number whose TAS falls as it gets colder. They converge at the coffin corner — the aerodynamic ceiling. Airliners cruise only where there is buffet margin for at least 1.3 g (about 40° of bank). Coffin corner Stall from below, Mach from above: the corner squeezes."},{"t":"What shrinks the buffet margin?","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#buffet-factors","k":"Pre-stall buffet occurs sooner with more weight, bank, load factor, altitude and a forward CG, and later with flap; shock buffet is set near Mcrit with MMO below it. Buffet margins "},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"V2: ≥ 1.2 Vs, ≥ 1.1 VMCA","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VR: ≥ 1.05 VMCA, ≥ 1.1 Vs","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VREF: 1.3 Vs","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMCA bank: ≤ 5° to live engine","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"VMCG deviation: ≤ 30 ft","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Service ceiling: 100 / 500 fpm","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Class A/B split: 5700 kg, 9 seats","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"2nd segment (twin): 2.4%","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Final segment (twin): 1.2%","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Approach / landing climb: 2.1% / 3.2%","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Obstacle margin: 35 ft","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Buffet margin: 1.3 g","s":"Handbook 29 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 29 · V-Speeds and Aircraft Performance","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Take-off V-speeds (9-9 to 9-10)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Other speeds (9-11 to 9-13)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Performance definitions and classes (9-14)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Take-off limits (9-15 to 9-17)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Take-off flight path (9-19)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"En-route, approach and landing (9-20 to 9-21)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Manoeuvre margins and coffin corner (9-22)","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 29 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-29.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 30. Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","s":"How the pilot works as a system: the brain, heart and lungs, memory and decision-making, leadership and stress, sleep, the eye and ear, and how the body copes with acceleration.","h":"chapters/hb-30.html","k":"Brain and blood · Henry and Dalton · CO · memory · leadership grid · stress · sleep credit · rods and cones · the ear and balance · g-tolerance "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"Body numbers to know","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":" Body numbers "},{"t":"Brain: 2% weight, 20% energy","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Information via eyes: ≈ 70%","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Resting heart rate: 72 / min","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Cardiac output: ≈ 5 l/min","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Blood pressure: 120 / 80","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Tidal volume: ≈ 500 ml","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Residual volume: ≈ 1200 ml","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Breathing rate: 16 / min","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"CO affinity: 250 × O₂","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Human-factor accidents: ≈ 73%","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Body temperature: max 1700, min 0500","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"After blood donation: 24 h rest","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#body","k":""},{"t":"Henry's law or Dalton's law?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#gaslaws","k":"Henry's law: the gas dissolved in a liquid depends on that gas's pressure above it — so nitrogen bubbles out of the blood when pressure falls (decompression sickness). Dalton's law: total pressure is the sum of the partial pressures — so oxygen's share of a thinner atmosphere falls (hypoxia). Gas laws Henry: bubbles (bends). Dalton: shares (hypoxia)."},{"t":"What does each vibration band do?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#vibration","k":"Low-frequency vibration interferes with breathing (1–4 Hz), causes chest and abdominal pain (4–10 Hz), backache (8–12 Hz) and headache and eye strain (10–20 Hz). BMI is weight over height squared: under 25 healthy, over 30 obese. Vibration and BMI "},{"t":"How long does each memory last?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#memory","k":"Sensory stores hold an image for about a second (iconic) or a sound for 2–8 seconds (echoic). Short-term memory holds about 7 ± 2 items for 10–20 seconds and is easily interrupted; chunking and mnemonics help. Long-term memory stores knowledge (semantic), events (episodic) and skills (motor). Memory Seven plus or minus two."},{"t":"Which captain is ideal?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#grid","k":"Plotting concern for people against concern for the goal: the ideal captain (P+ G+) balances crew well-being with an efficient flight. Too democratic (P+ G−) neglects the task; too autocratic (P− G+) silences the crew; laissez-faire (P− G−) cares about neither. Leadership grid Both plus = best."},{"t":"Skill, rule or knowledge-based?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#decide","k":"Skill-based behaviour is automatic (handling the controls); rule-based behaviour recognises a situation and applies a learned procedure (checklists); knowledge-based behaviour is slow, effortful problem-solving for something new. Maslow's needs run from physiological through safety, belonging and esteem to self-actualisation. Decision levels "},{"t":"Stress: kinds and coping","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#stress","k":"Stress is cumulative: a moderate level sharpens performance, too much breaks it down. It can be physiological, cognitive, personal, imaginary or organisational. Deal with the cause (action coping) or rethink it (cognitive coping); symptom-directed coping with drugs or cigarettes is undesirable. Stress A little stress sharpens; too much breaks."},{"t":"Sleep credit","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#sleep","k":"The body clock free-runs on about 25 hours; one sleep cycle lasts about 90 minutes and dreams occur in REM sleep. Sleep credit: +2 for each hour asleep, −1 for each hour awake, up to a maximum of 16. Sleep Two up for sleep, one down awake."},{"t":"Sleep credit","s":"Handbook 30 · formula","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#sleep","k":""},{"t":"Rods or cones?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#eye","k":"Cones, packed in the fovea, give sharp colour vision in daylight. Rods, densest about 10° off-centre, give black-and-white night and peripheral vision and need about 30 minutes to adapt to the dark. At night look slightly to one side of an object. Hypoxia, alcohol, smoking and vitamin A shortage all spoil night vision. Rods and cones Cones for Colour and Centre; Rods for the Rim and the night."},{"t":"Cones or rods?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#eye-sort","k":"Cones: colour, daylight, fovea, one neuron each, sharp. Rods: night, periphery, 30-minute adaptation, many per neuron, no colour. Eye facts "},{"t":"Myopia, hypermetropia, presbyopia","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#eye-defects","k":"Short sight (myopia) — a long eyeball focusing in front of the retina — needs a concave lens; long sight (hypermetropia) a convex lens; presbyopia is age stiffening the lens. Colour blindness affects about 7% of men and 0.1% of women. Eye defects "},{"t":"What does each part of the ear do?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#ear","k":"The outer ear and eardrum pass sound to the three ossicles of the air-filled middle ear, which the Eustachian tube keeps at ambient pressure. The fluid-filled inner ear holds the cochlea for hearing and the vestibular organs for balance: three semicircular canals at right angles sense angular acceleration, the otoliths sense linear acceleration and gravity. Ear and balance Canals turn, otoliths tilt."},{"t":"Hearing limits and barotrauma","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#ear-facts","k":"We hear 20–20,000 Hz. Long exposure above 90 dB damages hearing; 140 dB hurts; above 160 dB can burst the eardrum. Ear and sinus barotrauma come from pressure the body can't equalise — worst in descent, so don't fly with a cold. Hearing and barotrauma 90 dB damages, 140 hurts, 160 bursts."},{"t":"Black-out or red-out?","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#g","k":"Positive g (head-to-foot) drains blood from the head: grey-out, tunnel vision, black-out and finally loss of consciousness; tolerance is about 4.5–6 g for 5 s, 8 g with a g-suit, helped by straining and crouching. Negative g floods the head: red-out — and nothing protects against it. g-effects Positive goes grey then black; negative goes red."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Iconic memory: ≈ 1 s","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Echoic memory: 2–8 s","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Short-term memory: 7 ± 2, 10–20 s","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Circadian cycle: ≈ 25 h","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Sleep cycle: 90 min","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Max sleep credit: 16","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Dark adaptation: 30 min","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Rod density peak: 10° off fovea","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Hearing range: 20–20,000 Hz","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Noise damage: > 90 dB","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"+Gz tolerance: 4.5–6 g; 8 g suit","s":"Handbook 30 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 30 · Human Performance: Body, Senses and Mind","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Brain, circulation and breathing (11-3)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Arousal, memory, leadership, decisions (11-4)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Stress (11-5)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Sleep (11-5)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The eye (11-6)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"The ear (11-7)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Acceleration (11-8)","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 30 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-30.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Handbook 31. Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","s":"When the senses lie and the air runs thin: how pilots become spatially disoriented, the vestibular, acceleration and visual illusions that cause it, decompression sickness, the four kinds and stages of hypoxia with their times of useful consciousness, and hyperventilation.","h":"chapters/hb-31.html","k":"Orientation triad · types 1–3 · somatogyral, Coriolis, leans, graveyard spiral · somatogravic · runway and black-hole illusions · DCS · hypoxia types and stages · TUC · hyperventilation "},{"t":"The whole chapter on one page","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#map","k":" Mind map "},{"t":"How do we stay oriented, and how do we lose it?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#triad","k":"Orientation comes from the eyes (about 90%), the vestibular organs and the seat-of-the-pants senses. Without a visual horizon the brain trusts the inner ear, which is easily fooled. Type 1 disorientation, where the pilot doesn't realise it, is the most dangerous; the cure is to believe the instruments. Orientation and types Believe the instruments, not your bottom."},{"t":"What makes disorientation more likely?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#predisp","k":"Inexperience, fatigue, illness, alcohol, medication, hypoxia, anxiety, high g, glare, poor weather or night, emergencies and unnecessary head movements all raise the risk. Predisposing factors "},{"t":"Which illusion is it?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#vestib","k":"Somatogyral: a sustained turn stops being felt and the roll-out feels like a turn the other way. Coriolis: moving the head in a turn makes the world tumble. Leans: a slow, unnoticed roll is corrected and the pilot feels banked the other way. Graveyard spiral: re-banking after a felt \"opposite turn\" tightens a descending spiral. Somatogravic: forward acceleration feels like pitching up. Vestibular illusions Head still in turns; distrust the after-feeling."},{"t":"How can a runway fool you?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#visual","k":"An up-sloping or narrow runway looks steep, so you feel high and fly too low; a down-sloping or wide runway looks flat, so you feel low and fly too high. On a black-hole approach — a lit runway beyond dark terrain at night — pilots fly a curved path well below the glidepath. Runway illusions Up or narrow → low. Down or wide → high. Black hole → very low."},{"t":"Autokinesis, empty-field myopia, white-out…","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#other-vis","k":"A fixed light stared at in the dark seems to wander (autokinesis); with nothing to look at the eyes focus 1–2 m ahead (empty-field myopia); snow removes depth cues (white-out); haze makes things look farther; sloping cloud tops make a false horizon. Other visual illusions "},{"t":"Name the illusion from what the pilot feels","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#illusion-cards","k":"Vestibular illusions come from the canals (somatogyral, Coriolis, leans, graveyard spiral), otolith illusions from linear acceleration (somatogravic: speeding up feels like pitching up), visual ones from runway shape, darkness and haze. Trust the instruments. Illusion cards Feel it, doubt it, check the instruments."},{"t":"Bends, creeps and chokes","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#dcs","k":"When pressure falls quickly, dissolved nitrogen forms bubbles (Henry's law): bends in the joints, creeps in the skin, chokes in the lungs, and nervous-system symptoms. It is likely above about 25,000–30,000 ft. Don't fly within 12 hours of scuba diving, or 24 hours after a dive deeper than 30 ft; treat with oxygen and descent. Decompression sickness Bends, creeps, chokes."},{"t":"Which type of hypoxia?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#hyp-types","k":"Hypoxic hypoxia — too little oxygen pressure at altitude — is the pilot's usual kind. Anaemic hypoxia is blood that can't carry oxygen (CO poisoning, anaemia, blood loss); stagnant hypoxia is blood that doesn't circulate (cold, high g); histotoxic hypoxia is cells that can't use oxygen (alcohol, drugs, cyanide). Types of hypoxia Air, blood, flow, cells."},{"t":"How fast does hypoxia work?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#hyp-stages","k":"Up to 10,000 ft hypoxia is barely noticeable; from 10,000 to 15,000 ft the body compensates with faster breathing and pulse; from 15,000 to 20,000 ft it can no longer cope; above 20,000 ft collapse is rapid. Time of useful consciousness is about 10 minutes at 20,000 ft, 3 at 25,000 ft and barely a minute at 30,000 ft. Stages and TUC 20k: 10 min; 25k: 3 min; 30k: about a minute."},{"t":"When is oxygen, 100% oxygen or pressure breathing needed?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#o2","k":"Supplementary oxygen from about 12,000 ft; 100% oxygen from about 34,000 ft; pressure breathing from about 41,000 ft; above about 63,000 ft body fluids boil (Armstrong line), so a sealed cabin or suit is essential. Oxygen requirements "},{"t":"Hypoxia or hyperventilation?","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#symptoms","k":"Hypoxia (too little oxygen) and hyperventilation (too little CO₂ after over-breathing) feel alike: dizziness, tingling, poor judgement. Hypoxia gives no reliable warning, may bring euphoria, and can kill; hyperventilation victims recover once breathing settles. At altitude always assume hypoxia and use oxygen; below 10,000 ft, slow your breathing. Hypoxia vs hyperventilation In doubt up high: it's hypoxia."},{"t":"Numbers to know","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":" Numbers "},{"t":"Visual share of orientation: ≈ 90%","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Straight and level: ≥ 60 s","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Somatogyral fade: ≈ 20 s","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"DCS likely: > 30,000 ft","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":"> 10 min"},{"t":"No flying after diving: 12 h / 24 h","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Supplementary O₂: ≈ 12,000 ft","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"100% O₂: ≈ 34,000 ft","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Pressure breathing: ≈ 41,000 ft","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Ebullism: ≈ 63,000 ft","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"TUC 20k / 25k / 30k: 10 min / 3 min / 70 s","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Smoker's hypoxia: 4000–5000 ft lower","s":"Handbook 31 · numbers","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#numbers","k":""},{"t":"Quick check","s":"Handbook 31 · Disorientation, Illusions, Decompression and Hypoxia","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#quiz","k":" Quick check "},{"t":"Spatial disorientation (11-9)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Vestibular (gyral) illusions (11-10)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Otolith (acceleration) illusions (11-11)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Visual illusions (11-11 to 11-12)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Decompression sickness (11-13)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hypoxia (11-14)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Hyperventilation (11-16)","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""},{"t":"Exam traps","s":"Handbook 31 · notes","h":"chapters/hb-31.html#points","k":""}];